2 DATE 9 SEP 1513
2 ADDR
3 CITY Branxton
3 STAE England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT John Dunn married Margaret RUTHVEN, daughter of Sir William RUTHVEN Lord Ruthven and Isabel LEVINGTON 'of Saltcoats', before 21 June 1508.1 (Margaret RUTHVEN was born about 1478 and died in 1548 1 3.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT # ERSKINE, John 5th (6th) of Dun+
2 CONT # ERSKINE, Katherine
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.patrickspeople.scot/ourfamily/6072.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT NUMBERING
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth laird of Dun, John Erskine, and his son, also John, both died at Flodden. In the Pedigree of Erskine of Dun in the article Erskine of Dun by A. W. Cornelius Hallen, the son, John Erskine married to Margaret Ruthven, is 
2 CONC numbered as '5th of Dun'. Most other sources reserve this number '5th of Dun' for John Erskine of Dun, his son, born about 1508-9, who married Elizabeth Lindsay as his first wife. This latter system of numbering is generally used 
2 CONC here, with a bracketed number given also, indicating that another numbering is possible if the second John Erskine who fell at Flodden is included in the numbering. 2
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT "1. Hon Margaret Ruthven (d. 1548), mar. (1) bef. 1499 as his second wife Alexander [Stewart], 2nd Earl of Buchan, (2) bef. 21 Jun 1508 Sir John Erskine, Yr. of Dun (d. 9 Sep 1513), (3) bef. 23 Dec 1518 James Stewart of Ryland (d.
2 CONC  bef. 11 Mar 1524/5), and (4) (annulled 1534) William Wood of Bonnyton, and had issue by her first husband ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT from cracroftpeerage website
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Flodden '''Battle of Flodden'''] From Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Flodden, Flodden Field, or occasionally Branxton (Brainston Moor[4]) was a military combat in the War of the League of Cambrai between the Kingdom of England and the Kingdom of Scotland, resulting in an English victo
2 CONC ry. The battle was fought in Branxton in the county of Northumberland in northern England on 9 September 1513, between an invading Scots army under King James IV and an English army commanded by the Earl of Surrey.[5] In terms of 
2 CONC troop numbers, it was the largest battle fought between the two kingdoms.[6] James IV was killed in the battle, becoming the last monarch from the British Isles to die in battle.
2 CONT
