1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://clanirwin.org/index.php '''Clan Irwin Association'''] A Not For Profit Corporation
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Alexander Irvine of Drum:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p44746.htm#i447453
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Irvine of Drum [1]
2 CONT *M, #447453
2 CONT *Last Edited=12 Sep 2010
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Irvine of Drum is the son of Sir Alexander Irvine of Drum.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Alexander Irvine of Drum
2 CONT *1. Alexander Irvine of Lonmay+[1] d. 10 Sep 1547
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2063. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Extract from "Short account of the family of Irvine of Drum in the county of Aberdeen", 1893, Page 14 (http://archive.org/details/shortaccountoffa00wimb)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Alexander, the l0th laird, eldest son of the preceding married in 1617 Magdalen, eldest daughter of Sir John Scrimgeour of Dudhope, Constable of Dundee, by whom he had issue five sons and six daughters, viz.:
2 CONT * 1. Alexander, younger of Drum.
2 CONT * 2. Robert.
2 CONT * 3. James.
2 CONT * 4. Charles.
2 CONT * 5. Francis.
2 CONT * All of whom, except Alexander, died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters—
2 CONT * 1. Marion,- married James, first Viscount Frendraught, at the Church of Drumoak, 8th November, 1642. Lord Frendraught was son of the laird whose name is connected with the burning of Frendraught.
2 CONT * 2.
2 CONT * 3.
2 CONT * 4. Jean, married George Crichton, brother of Lord Frendraught.
2 CONT * 6. Margaret, married Charles 1st Earl of Aboyne.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographical notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/w/i/l/Sharen-Williams-PA/GENE3-0010.html Descendants of William de Irvine]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander Irvine, the Xth Laird of Drum, was the forebear of the branch which came to America via Ulster and County Antrim, Ireland. The union of the Crowns of Scotland and England soon resulted in a serious neglect of Scotland. T
2 CONC he greater the size, population, and foreign relations of England were matters which eclipsed those of Scotland almost completely. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus, the interests and concerns of the smaller nation became centered in it's religion and its Parliament. Under King Charles I, both the Pope in Rome and the Archbishop of Canterbury in England threatened the Presbyterianism of 
2 CONC the Scots. The Solemn League and Covenant was formed for protection against this threat, and soon bacame prominent and powerful politically 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the Sherriff of County Aberdeen in 1634 and King Charles I planned to make him Earl of Aberdeen, but the outbreak of the "Great Rebellion" prevented it . He held extensive estates in teh counties f Aberdeen, Forfar, Baniff,
2 CONC  and Kincardine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In June of 1640, during Sir Alexanders absence from Drum Castle, it was besieged by teh forces of General Monroe and William, 7th Earl of Marichal but was defended by it's lady. Sir alexander gave himself up at Aberdeen 9 June 164
2 CONC 0 and was taken to Edinburg, where he was fined 10,000 Merks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander, the Xth Laird of Drum, having always been a Royalist, rejected the Covenent. But with the strength of the "Covenanters" and the weakness of the King and his few Royalist supporters, the Laird and his estate suffered bad
2 CONC ly. The Laird was imprisoned and Drum Castle was occupied by the Covenanter's army under Argyle. After months of plundering the estate, a decree was issued by the Covenanters to demolish the Tower and the Castle of Drum, but the d
2 CONC ecree was never carried out. 
