1 NAME William /Erskine/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Erskine
2 _MARNM Erskine
1 NAME William Erskine of Auchnagray
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1510
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1560
1 OCCU parson of Duthell
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Erskine-1238
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Agnes (Guthrie) Erskine — married about 1544 (to about 1552) [location unknown]
2 CONT Husband of Mariot (Douglas) Erskine — married about 1553 [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born at the House of Dun about 1510, second son to Sir John Erskine Fiar of Dun and his spouse Margaret Ruthven daughter of Sir William Ruthven, first Lord Ruthven. Sir John fell at the Field of Flodden in 1513 along w
2 CONC ith his father and uncle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Ruthven had married Sir John Erskine in 1508 having been previously married to Alexander earl of Buchan who died in 1505. She was to enter a further two marriages but kept a very close link with her Erskine family.
2 CONT
2 CONT From her first marriage, Margaret retained lands in the baronies of Auchterhouse and Kinalty. At Edinburgh on 3 October 1526, a Precept of Sasine was granted to Margaret Ruthven, Countess of Buchan in liferent and William Erskine,
2 CONC  her son, in fee of a quarter part of Inshewan extending to the 5 merk lands and the brewery and the croft of Kinalty,the forty shilling lands of Auchnagray and the five merk lands of Easter Auchleuchrie in the barony of Kinalty t
2 CONC hat formerly belonged to the late James (Stewart) earl of Buchan.[1] On 3 February 1526/27, a discharge was made to them for the maills of Auchnagray and a quarter of Inshewan. A few months later on 14 June 1527 an Obligation was 
2 CONC made to Margaret and her son William anent the lands of Hatton and Ryeland.[2] He had gained these while a youth and after graduating from university, about 1530, he was known as Master William Erskine of Auchnagray.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was parson of Duthell in the episcopacy of Moray for a number of years[3] during which time it seems that he made an extended visit to foreign lands. King James V granted a licence on 10 May 1537 to John Erskine, laird of Dun, 
2 CONC his son John, his cousin Thomas Erskine and Master William, parson of Duthell for these three young men and the boy to travel to France, Italy and other places beyond the sea in order to make a pilgrimage and carryout out business
2 CONC  for the space of three years, during which time their estates would be under the King’s protection. Perhaps Master William acted as tutor to the young John Erskine, fiar of Dun who was then just entering his teens.
2 CONT
2 CONT They returned safely to Scotland and on 8 August 1540, Master William was presented with the rectory of the church of Duthell on the demission of Dr George Clapperton. [4] On 10 Sept 1541 at the Tron in Aberdeen John Frisall baili
2 CONC e gave possession of the hereditary 15 silver pennies of the Great Customs of Aberdeen to Master William Erskine who was acting as attorney for John Erskine of Dun.[5] In 1542 as parson of Duchell, Mr William set a tack of the fru
2 CONC its of his church to James Grant of Freuchy. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Master William again acted as procurator to his brother german, John Erskine of Dun, when Janet Stewart, lady of Lecks (Leoch Easter & Wester) granted a Precept of Sasine to John in the lands of Lecks in the barony of Auchterhouse
2 CONC  on 17 April 1550. Janet Stewart was probably sister uterine to the Erskine brothers. At some stage, Master William gained these lands of Leoch from his elder brother as mentioned in his younger daughter’s marriage contract. [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Master William married and had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Margaret who married John Ogilvy son of Clova
2 CONT # Jane who married Thomas Ogilvy son of Inverquharity
2 CONT
2 CONT Following on a Procuratory note by Master William for the resignation of Auchnagray[8], on 21 June 1553, Queen Mary confirmed a charter to Master William Erskine of Auchnagray and Mariot Douglas his spouse in the lands of Auchnagr
2 CONC ay, Easter Auchleurchie, and the west quarter of Inshewan with its brasina (brewery) and croft of Kinalty in the barony of Kinalty which Master William had resigned for them to be jointly infeft.[9] These lands lie just east of Ki
2 CONC rriemuir in the south part of the parish of Tannadice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Master William was alive in May 1554 but died within the following ten years, leaving his daughters, Margaret and Jane as his heirs portioner.[10]
