1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun02pauluoft/scotspeeragefoun02pauluoft/page/547/mode/1up?view=theater p547
2 CONT
2 CONT http://clanforrester.org There was a Stirlingshire branch of the clan, the Forresters of Garden who were heritable keepers of the Torwood (a Royal forest and hunting ground).[3] They owned the barony of Garden as well as Torwood a
2 CONC nd the ruins of Torwood Castle still stand.[3] Sir Duncan Forrester, 1st of Torwood was Comptroller of the Royal Household for James IV of Scotland.[3] No fewer than eight Forresters of Torwood were Provosts of Stirling burgh.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Forrester, seventh chief of Clan Forrester was killed at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh in 1547, as was Sir David Forrester, 4th of the Torwood branch of the clan.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT A cadet of the Forresters of Garden was the first of the Fifeshire Chieftains, the Forresters of Strathendry.[3] The Forresters of Strathendry built Strathendry Castle, a 16th-century tower house where both Mary, Queen of Scots an
2 CONC d Oliver Cromwell stayed.[3] It is the only Forrester stronghold that is still inhabited today.[3] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clan_Forrester
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Pinkie Cleugh,[5] took place on 10 September 1547 on the banks of the River Esk near Musselburgh, Scotland. The last pitched battle between Scottish and English armies, it was part of the conflict known as the Rough 
2 CONC Wooing, and is considered to be the first modern battle in the British Isles. It was a catastrophic defeat for Scotland, where it became known as Black Saturday.[6]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Pinkie_Cleugh
