2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1599-1626: Discharges by David Lindsay, bishop of Ross, Mr. Jeremy Lindsay in Leith, his son, Robert, lord Lindsay, and John, lord Lindsay, to Ninian MacMoran, merchant, burgess of Edinburgh, and Ninian McMoran, younger, of tei
2 CONC nds of lands of Kingsbarns. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD158%2f636&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=david+lindsay&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1530&dt=1614&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers
2 CONC  of the family of Hume of Polwarth, Berwickshire, Earls of Marchmont, reference GD158/636]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''12 November 1604: Feu Charter by Mr. David Lyndesay, bishop of Ross, whereby, for augmentation of his rental, he granted in feu-farm to Alexander Moresoun, indweller in Leyth, that piece of land of the churchyard of Nig (presen
2 CONC tly occupied by the said Alexander Moresoun) measuring in length 52 feet and in breadth 24 feet, on which a dwelling house and stable are now being built, lying between the burn of Pitcany on the east, the common highway on the so
2 CONC uth, the passage of the church of Nig on the West, and the said churchyard on the north, within the parish and barony of Nig and sheriffdom of Inverness: To be held de nobis in feu-farm for payment of 6/8d Scots yearly: With prece
2 CONC pt of Sasine addressed to Finlay Mansoun, minister at Nig. At Letham 12th February 1604. Witnesses, John Fowlertoun, feuar of Kimaber [Kinnaber], Mr. Hugh Dowglas and Alexander Montgumrie, servitors of the granter, Thomas Davidsou
2 CONC n in Edinburgh, George Norvell, notary, and Mr. Jerome Lyndsay, Admiral Depute, son of the granter. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD86%2f350&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=bishop+of+ros
2 CONC s&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1600&dt=1614&di=n National Records of Scotland, Sir William Fraser Charters, reference GD86/350]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''13 July 1607: Feu Charter by David, bishop of Ross, with consent of the dean, chapter and canons of the cathedral church of Ross, granting to Andrew Monro of Culnald, son and heir of the late David Monro of Culnald (ancient and
2 CONC  native feuar and tenant of the lands underwritten) and his heirs male and assignees whomsoever (1) the town and lands of Nyg, with the brewhouse and brewhouse-croft thereof, extending to a davoch of land (2) the houses or "girnal
2 CONC lis", back and fore under and above, with the pertinents, adjoining the wall of the cemetery of Nyg, with the custody of the place and manor of Nyg for the reception and convenience of the granter, his successors and officers, at 
2 CONC their own expense, whenever they should happen to stay there and (3) the east half of the lands and town of Pitcalzeane extending to a half davoch of land (formerly possessed by Alexander Ferne and his predecessors) with the brewh
2 CONC ouse and brewhouse-croft thereof, all lying within the diocese of Ross and sheriffdom of Innerness, and declaring that a sasine taken at the said "girnallis" of Nyg should be sufficient for the whole lands above conveyed: To be he
2 CONC ld de nobis, rendering for the lands of Nyg, £12:11/- Scots, a mart, 5 "muttones", 4 kids, 80 eggs, 18 capons, 18 poultry, a chalder barley, 10 bolls "custume et suggeroun aittis", 40 loads of "turffis" with arriages and services 
2 CONC used and wont; for the houses or "girnallis", 4 shillings Scots; for the east half of Pitcalzeane, £4:16/- Scots, half a mart, 2 "muttones", 2 kids, 40 eggs, 6 capons, 6 poultry and 1 boll "custume aittis" with arriages and servic
2 CONC es used and wont; for the brewhouse and brewhousecroft of Pitcalzeane, 21/4 Scots, 1"mutton", 3 capons and 3 poultry; with whatever other duties are contained in the ancient rentals and in use to be paid for all the above lands: M
2 CONC oreover the grain growing upon said lands shall be ground at the mill of Morwech paying therefor the customary multures services and aids, and the grantee, or the possessors of the said lands of Pitcalzane, shall lead 40 loads of 
2 CONC "peittis or turvis" (which are to be prepared by the granter) to the manse of Nyg when required to do so: Further the grantee is bound to furnish 4 horses with their necessaries for 3 days for leading the teind sheaves of the pari
2 CONC shes of Nyg or Tarbett within the bishopric of Ross, beyond their side of Ross (extra latere Rossen. eorum), whenever required to that effect, and the tenants of said lands shall assist and labour to build repair and upkeep the "z
2 CONC air" of Kincardin proportionately to the rate and quantity of the plough and davoch lands of the barony of Nyg. The grantee and the tenants of said lands are to attend the courts of the granter to be held at the Chanonry of Ross a
2 CONC nd Nyg; the grantee and his heirs are to render three suits at the three head courts to be held at the Chanonry of Ross; and the heirs of the grantee are to pay a double of the above duties in the first year of their entry to said
2 CONC  lands: With precept of sasine addressed to Finlay Mansoun, minister of Nig. At Letham 13 July 1607. Witnesses, John Corbett of Arkboll, Mr. Jerome Lyndsay of Dynnynnow, son of the granter, John Monro of Lemlaer and George Monro, 
2 CONC son and heir apparent of George Monro of Mekill Tarrell. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD86%2f373&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=bishop+of+ross&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1600&dt=1614&di=n Nationa
2 CONC l Records of Scotland, Sir William Fraser Charters, reference GD86/373]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''16 July 1606: Discharge by Mr. David Spens, minister at Kirknaldie, to William, Earl of Morton, of a half year's feu-duty of Bischopschyre assigned to him and Mr Nicol Dagleische, minister at Pittinweme, in terms of a decree ag
2 CONC ainst Mr. Jeremy Lindsay, son of Mr David Lyndsay, Bishop of Ross. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD29%2f1225&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=bishop+of+ross&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1600&dt=1614&d
2 CONC i=n National Records of Scotland. Papers of the Bruce family of Kinross (Kinross House Papers), reference GD29/1225]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''13 August 1609: David, Bishop of Ross to Mr John Drummond, brother to Sir Alexander Drummond of Medhope, knight and collector clerk for   benefices in Scotland. Witnesses: Mr Jeremy Lindsay of Downynno (?); George Smeaton, indw
2 CONC eller in Leith; Mr David Thomson, servant. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD1%2f1149%2f15&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=bishop+of+ross&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1613&dt=1614&di=n National Records
2 CONC  of Scotland, Mackenzie of Coul Papers, reference GD1/1149/15]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     6
2 CONT
2 CONT '''14 January 1613: Charter by David, bishop of Ross, with consent of the chapter of the cathedral church thereof, narrating that whereas the lands of Kilterne, with mill multures, and fishings thereof, lying in the earldom of Ros
2 CONC s, and sheriffdom of Innernes, have been held of him and his predecessors, bishops of Ross, as part of the patrimony of the bishopric, beyond the memory of man, by Robert Monro of Foullis and his predecessors, in feufarm, and wher
2 CONC eas the Act of Annexation of kirklands to the Crown was rescinded by Act of Parliament held at Perth in Jul 1606, and the bishops required to re-infeft their former vassals, therefore and for certain sums of money and many benefit
2 CONC s rendered to said bishop by said Robert Monro said bishop grants him said lands and others as formerly to be held of said bishop and his successors in feufarm for the yearly sum of 14 marks payable by equal portions at Whitsunday
2 CONC  and Martinmas in name of feufarm, with duplicand thereof on the entry of heirs; with precept addressed to Robert Monro Hectorsone, in Drummond, as bailie, to give sasine. Witnesses, Mr Jeremy Lindsay of Dynninno, the bishop's son
2 CONC , Andrew Moresoun, collector-depute, James Wod and John Low, the bishop's servants, and Robert Monro, fiar of Aldie. At Leith. Signed by the bishop and members of the chapter. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogu
2 CONC e/details.aspx?reference=GD93%2f154&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=bishop+of+ross&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1600&dt=1614&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Munro family of Foulis, reference GD93/154]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/fastiecclesiu01scotuoft/page/n8/mode/1up Fasti Ecclesiae Scoticanae. The Succession of Ministers in the Church of Scotland from the Reformation. By Hew Scott, D.D. New Edition. Volume I. Synod of 
2 CONC Lothian and Tweedale (Oliver and Boyd, Edinburgh, 1915), 452 pp. including indices]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/fastiecclesiaesc00scot/page/n6/mode/1up Fasti Ecclesiae Scoticanae. The Succession of Ministers in the Church of Scotland from the Reformation. By Hew Scott, D.D. New Edition. Volume VII. Synods o
2 CONC f Ross, Sutherland and Caithness, Glenelg, Orkney and of Shetland. The Church in England, Ireland and Overseas (Oliver and Boyd, Edinburgh, 1928), 800 pp. including indices]'''
2 CONT # '''[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Lindsay_(d._1613) Wikipedia: David Lindsay]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Lindsay_(d._1613) Biographical Summary by Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''David Lindsay (1531–1613) was one of the twelve original ministers nominated to the "chief places in Scotland" in 1560. In 1589 as one of the recognised leaders of the Kirk and as chaplain of James VI of Scotland, Lindsay accom
2 CONC panied James to Norway to fetch home his bride. He was appointed bishop of Ross and a privy councillor in 1600. He was five times Moderator of the General Assembly: 1577, 1582, 1586, 1593 and 1597.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lindsay was the son of Alexander Lindsay and wife Rachel Barclay, and nephew of David, 9th Earl of Crawford. He was said to have been a student of the University of St Andrews, though his name does not appear as such in any of 
2 CONC the university records. He appears to have been an associate of John Knox in Geneva.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''During travels in France and Switzerland Lindsay imbibed Reformation principles, and he was one of the twelve original ministers nominated in July 1560 to the "chief places in Scotland", the town assigned him being Leith. He wa
2 CONC s present in December following at the first meeting of the general assembly of the kirk, and thenceforth was one of its recognised leaders. He was moderator of the assembly which met in February 1568, and subsequently held the sa
2 CONC me office on five occasions. He visited Knox on his deathbed in 1572, and at Knox's request, though "he thought the message hard", went to the castle of Edinburgh to warn William Kirkcaldy of Grange that unless he gave it up he "s
2 CONC hould be brought down over the walls of it with shame and hang against the sun".'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lindsay visited Kirkcaldy after his condemnation, and was sent by him to Morton to intercede for his life, being empowered to offer Kirkcaldy's whole estate as a ransom. The intercession having failed, Lindsay, at Kirkcaldy's s
2 CONC pecial request, attended him on the scaffold, and thus, according to Calderwood, became witness of the literal fulfilment of the doom pronounced by Knox. Always inclined to moderate counsels, Lindsay in 1579 took part in the succe
2 CONC ssful mediation between Morton and the dissentient lords. On the arrival shortly afterwards of Esmé Stuart, the secret catholic emissary from France, Lindsay, at the king's request was, on account of his knowledge of French, appoi
2 CONC nted by the kirk to attend on him with a view to his conversion to protestantism.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By Lindsay's nominal success, he became the unconscious tool of Stuart in his designs against Morton. After the banishment of those concerned in the Ruthven raid, Lindsay endeavoured to obtain the co-operation of Robert Bowes, 
2 CONC the English ambassador, to bring about a reconciliation between the two factions, but his endeavours were unsuccessful. He had gradually won considerable influence with the king, and acquired the reputation of being "the minister 
2 CONC whom the court liked best". On this account he was in May 1584 selected by the ministers in and around Edinburgh to induce the king to delay his assent, until a meeting of the assembly, to certain acts circumscribing the authority
2 CONC  of the kirk; but as he entered the palace gate he was apprehended and lodged in Blackness Castle. Here he had a remarkable dream, recorded at length by Calderwood. On the fall of Arran shortly afterwards, he was set at liberty. L
2 CONC indsay was the only one of the ministers of the kirk – with the exception of the "king's own minister" – who complied with the request of the king to pray for Queen Mary before her execution.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As chaplain of the king Lindsay accompanied him in October 1589 when he set sail for Norway to bring home his bride, Anne of Denmark. Lindsay preached a sermon at Tønsberg on 16 November, which is commemorated on a surviving pa
2 CONC inted board, and on 23 November he married them at Oslo. He and Robert Bruce crowned them in the abbey kirk, Edinburgh, on 12 May 1590. At the baptism of the young Prince Henry at Stirling Castle, 23 August 1594, Lindsay delivered
2 CONC  a learned speech to the ambassadors in French.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lindsay came to Edinburgh from Falkland Palace in 1600 in order to assure the clergy of the truth of the official version of the Gowrie House conspiracy of 5 August 1600. When the clergy declined to order a general service of t
2 CONC hanksgiving for the king's deliverance, a service was conducted by Lindsay at the market cross. On the arrival of the king at Leith, 16 August, Lindsay also preached a thanksgiving sermon in his own church. Soon afterwards he rece
2 CONC ived a special mark of royal favour by his crown provision on 5 November 1600, in accordance with the act for the establishment of a modified episcopacy, to the bishopric of Ross. On 30 September he was also admitted a member of t
2 CONC he privy council. His provision was renewed on 22 December 1604, on both occasions the honour was purely titular until the restoration of regular episcopal succession in 1610. He received consecration as bishop on 24 February 1611
2 CONC  in his home church of South Leith.'''
