1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adrian Benjamin Burke, The Livingston Ancestry of the Duncanson Sisters of New Netherland, Part II: Identifyng their maternal great-grandmother Margaret Forrester," in The Genealogist, 2013, pp. 172-74.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Livingston-187
2 CONT
2 CONT >The fact that Mr. Henry Livingston is described as the brother of Mr. Alexander Colville, Iconomus of Melrose, can only mean they were uterine brothers, i.e., their mother was Margaret Forrester, widow of Sir James Colville of Ea
2 CONC st Wemyss. Robert Milne the younger's manuscript pedigree is thus proved correct: Sir James Colville's widow married Henry Livingston of Falkirk and was the mother of Mr. Henry Livingston, Minister of St. Ninian's.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261286089_Recent_Literature_on_the_Duncanson_Sisters_of_New_Netherland Mary Loockermans, Anne Powell, Margaret Telller, and Katherine Glen were sisters. They were daughters of the Reverend
2 CONC  James Duncanson, minister of Alloa and Tullibody, Clackmannanshire, Scotland, by his wife Helen Livingston. Grand daughters of the Reverend John Duncanson, minister and to the household of King James VI,probably (now known to be 
2 CONC certainly) by John's first wife, Janet Watson. Helen Livingston was a daughter of the Rev. Helen Livingston, minister of St. Ninian’s, Stirlingshire, by his wife Agnes Gray, and a granddaughter of Henry Livingston of Falkirk and M
2 CONC argaret Forrester ...
