2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Sir Thomas Craig from Electric Scotland Website CRAIG, SIR THOMAS, of Riccarton, a distinguished lawyer and writer on the feudal law, was born at Edinburgh about 1538. It is uncertain whether his father was Robert Craig, a merchan
2 CONC t in Edinburgh, or
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Thomas's son, Sir James Craig of Craig C ===
2 CONT Thomas's son, Sir James Craig of Craig Castle and Craigston County, Aberdeen, became one of the Scottish undertakers of the Ulster Plantation in 1610.  His descendant, James Craig, became the first Prime Minister of Ireland in 192
2 CONC 1, having been an organizer of the Ulster Volunteer Force in the struggle against Home Rule. He was then elevated to the Peerage, taking the title Viscount Craigavon.  The new town of Craigavon in County Armagh was named for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas acquired the estate of Riccarton, Edinburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT Lord Advocate and Judge. Educated at St. Leonard's College, St. Andrews University, B.A. 1555, called to Bar, 1563, appointed Justice Depute, 1564; acquired a crown charter of the estates to himself and his heirs male, 16 April 15
2 CONC 76.  Sir Thomas Craig accompanied King James I to England in 1608 and acquired celebrity from his "Treatise on Feudal Law", 1603, and "Treatise on Union of Two Kingdoms", 1605.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === !#218-v2-p347; ===
2 CONT !#218-v2-p347;
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === St. Andrews University ===
2 CONT Thomas attended St. Andrews where he completed his law degree. "An Account of the Life and Writings of Sir Thomas Craig"
2 CONT
