1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John Sinclair, Master of Caithness''' (b. circa 1543, d. September 1575) was the son of George Sinclair, 4th Earl of Caithness and Elizabeth Graham. He married Lady Jean Hepburn, daughter of Patrick Hepburn, 3rd
2 CONC  Earl of Bothwell and Agnes Sinclair, from 10 December 1565 to 16 January 1566/67. He and Lady Jean Hepburn were divorced on 17 July 1575. He died in September 1575, in captivity.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sinclair, Master of Caithness was styled as Master of Caithness. Between 1569 and 1575 he was imprisoned in Girnigo Castle by his father following a quarrel.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was imprisoned by his father at Girginoe Castle for making peace with the Morays. He died a raving lunatic. One account says that his brother, in order to hasten his death, fed him heavily salted meat without giving him water t
2 CONC o drink.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children of John Sinclair, Master of Caithness and Lady Jean Hepburn===
2 CONT
2 CONT # George Sinclair, 5th Earl of Caithness+ b. 1566, d. Feb 1642/43 m. Jean Gordon 
2 CONT # Agnes Sinclair b. 1573
2 CONT # Sir James Sinclair+ b. a 1567, 1st of Murchil (Murkle), m. Elizabeth Stewart
2 CONT # Sir John Sinclair+ b. a 1567, d. b 21 Dec 1627 m. Janet Sutherland
2 CONT # Marie Sinclair d. a 20 Feb 1582 m. Sir John Home, of Cowdenknows
2 CONT # dau. Sinclair m. William Gunn, of Kilearnan, 3rd Mackeamish
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Sinclair is derived from Saint-Clair-sur-Elle in Normandy. William de Sancto Claro, whose father had come over with William the Conqueror in 1066, came to Scotland with his wife. There were, however, other members of the 
2 CONC St. Clair family who came north also. They became established near Edinburgh and were granted the barony of Rosslyn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William de St Clair was involved in negotiating the marriage of Yolande de Dreux with King Alexander III. Sir William signed the "Ragman Roll" of King Edward I of England in 1296. His heir, Henry, was initially loyal to the En
2 CONC glish king but then supported Robert the Bruce and fought at the Battle of Bannockburn. The king granted him lands in the Pentlands, south of Edinburgh. Sir Henry Sinclair died in Spain with Sir James Douglas as they took the hear
2 CONC t of Robert the Bruce on a crusade. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Through marriage, the earldom of Orkney was obtained. Henry de St Clair was a notable seaman and probably reached North America a 100 years before Columbus. His son surrendered the Orkney earldom and was created Earl of Caithness 
2 CONC in 1445 by King James II. It was Henry who constructed Rosslyn Chapel with its ornate carvings and it was around this time that the spelling "Sinclair" came into use, usually pronounced in Scotland as "Sinkler". 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sinclair clan motto is "Commit thy work to God". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Surnames regarded as septs (sub-branch) of the Sinclair clan include Caird, Clyne, Linklater, Lyall and Mason. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sinclair was the 79th most frequent surname at the General Register Office in 1995 - but in Orkney it is easily the most frequently found surname.. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Sinclair, Master of Caithness1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #20723, b. circa 1543, d. September 1575
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=4 Aug 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.09%
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sinclair, Master of Caithness was born circa 1543.2 He was the son of George Sinclair, 4th Earl of Caithness and Lady Elizabeth Graham.3
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Lady Jean Hepburn, daughter of Patrick Hepburn, 3rd Earl of Bothwell and Agnes Sinclair, from 10 December 1565 to 16 January 1566/67.1 He and Lady Jean Hepburn were divorced on 17 July 1575.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in September 1575, in captivity.2
2 CONT
2 CONT He was styled as  Master of Caithness.1 Between 1569 and 1575 he was imprisoned in Girnigo Castle by his father following a quarrel.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of John Sinclair, Master of Caithness
2 CONT * 1.unknown Sinclair+
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Sinclair, Master of Caithness and Lady Jean Hepburn
2 CONT * 1.Marie Sinclair3 d. a 20 Feb 1582
2 CONT * 2.George Sinclair, 5th Earl of Caithness+3 b. 1566, d. Feb 1642/43
2 CONT * 3.Sir James Sinclair+3 b. a 1567
2 CONT * 4.Sir John Sinclair+3 b. a 1567, d. b 21 Dec 1627
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - The Master of Caithness was kept chained as prisoner at Girnigoe Castle from 1571 till he died in 1578, by his own father.
2 CONT
2 CONT He strangled his brother with the chains before dying of thirst in the dungeon.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - There is great doubt that the story of the Master of Sinclair dying in a Girnigoe Dungeon. It was written by Gordon who had an axe to grind with the Sinclairs as his Chief was Sutherland and at that time Sinclair and Sutherlan
2 CONC d were not getting along. There is much spurious history attributed to the North because of Gordon. There is ample evidence to show the Master of Sinclair (oldest son) lived his last years in Knockinnon Castle, staying out of the 
2 CONC way of his father. He was a gentle soul and his father was not. The Master is buried in the Sinclair Aisle in the Wick Churchyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Source: [ http://sinclair.quarterman.org/who/george5caithness.html ]
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 - John, Master of Caithness, the eldest son of the fifth Sinclair, Earl of Caithness, who is said to have been imprisoned here because he had wooed and won the affections of the daughter of a neighbouring laird, marked out by hi
2 CONC s father, at that time a widower, for himself. He was confined in that old dungeon for more than six long years before death released him from his inhuman parent.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his imprisonment John had three keepers appointed over him: Murdoch Roy and two brothers named Ingram and David Sinclair. Roy attended him regularly, and did all the menial work, as the other two keepers were kinsmen of the
2 CONC  earl, his father, who had imprisoned him. Roy was sorry for the unfortunate nobleman, and arranged a plot to set him at liberty, which was unfortunately discovered by John's brother William, who bore him no good will. William tol
2 CONC d his father, the earl, who immediately ordered Roy to be executed. The poor wretch was accordingly brought out and hanged on the common gibbet of the castle without a moment being allowed him to prepare for his final account.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon afterwards, in order to avenge the death of Roy, John, who was a man of great bodily strength and whose bad usage and long imprisonment had affected his mind, managed to seize his brother William on the occasion of his visit 
2 CONC to the dungeon and strangle him. This only deepened the earl's antipathy towards his unhappy son, and his keepers were encouraged to put him to death. The plan adopted was such as could only have entered the imagination of fiends,
2 CONC  for they withheld food from their prisoner for the space of five days, and then set before him a piece of salt beef of which he ate voraciously. Soon after, when he called for water, they refused to give him any, and he died of r
2 CONC aging thirst. Another account said they gave him brandy, of which he drank so copiously that he died raving mad. In any case, there is no doubt whatever that he was barbarously done to death.
2 CONT
2 CONT >From John O'Groats to Land's End by Robert Naylor and John Naylor pub 1916 [ http://www.gutenberg.org/files/14415/14415-h/14415-h.htm ] [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
