1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John, Earl of Mar and Lord Erskine (1562 - 14 December 1634)'''
2 CONT s/o  John Erskine, 18th Earl of Mar, Regent of Scotland and Annabelle Murray
2 CONT x Anna or Agnes Drummond, 2nd d/o David, Lord Drummond.
2 CONT * John, Earl of Mar.
2 CONT xx 7 December 1592, Marie Stewart, s2nd d/o Esme, first Duke of Lennox.
2 CONT * James, Earl of Buchan
2 CONT * Henry 
2 CONT * Mr. Alexander, colonel of Horse
2 CONT * Sir Arthur Erskine x Margaret Buchanan. 
2 CONT * Sir John Erskine x  Margaret Inglis.
2 CONT * Sir Charles Erskine, x Mary Hope xx Helen Skene.
2 CONT * Mr. William, Cupbearer to King Charles I
2 CONT * Mary x William, Master of Marischal.
2 CONT * Anna x John, sixth Earl of Rothes.
2 CONT * Margaret x John, second Earl of Kinghorn.
2 CONT * Annabella, died young.
2 CONT * Catherine x Thomas, second Earl of Haddington.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =John,  Earl of Mar and Lord Erskine (1562 - 14 December 1634)
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. John, who succeeded as Earl of Mar and Lord Erskine, was perhaps the most prominent of his family, and remarkable in his long friendship with King James VI. He appears also as a man of much common-sense and with a good grip o
2 CONC f affairs. He is believed to have been born in 1562, and was therefore a few years older than King James VI., with whom he was educated, and who had ever a strong affection and regard for him. The well-known epithet applied by the
2 CONC  King to the Earl of 'John Slates,' or, 'John Slaitis,' is said by a family genealogist to have been given because, when boys together playing at some game, the King alleged the Earl had 'slaited' him ('which is an old Scots word 
2 CONC signifying he had outwitted him'), and ever after gave him the sobriquet.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 March 1572 he was, by a jury sitting at Stirling, retoured heir to his father in the lands and barony of Alloa, co. Clackmannan, and also in the whole earldom of Mar, including Strathdon, Braemar, Cromar, and Strathdee. He wa
2 CONC s then under age, and his first prominent appearance in public life was later, being the forcible seizure of Stirling Castle from his uncle Alexander, who had held it since 1572. He did this on 26 April 1578, it is said, at the in
2 CONC stigation of the Regent Morton. He was, however, formally made Captain of the castle, which he kept faithfully, though he fell for a time under the severe displeasure of the King by joining the party of Morton and Angus, and after
2 CONC wards of the Ruthven Raiders. In the end of 1583 he had licence to go abroad for three (or five) years with provision to his son in case of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he and the other banished lords returned to Scotland in 1585, Mar was made a Privy Councillor, and was reappointed Keeper of Stirling Castle. From this time his career is to be traced in the public history and records of Scot
2 CONC land, and is too long to detail, but a few items of personal history may be dwelt upon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1592, and again in 1595, he was a candidate for the office of Lord Chancellor in succession to Lord Thirlestane, but the Earl of Montrose was appointed. Mar, however, had a most important charge conferred upon him, the care of 
2 CONC the infant Prince Henry, an office intrusted to him with special instructions from King James himself. The appointment led to quarrels and intrigues at court, but the King firmly resisted all efforts by the Queen and others to wit
2 CONC hdraw the Prince from Mar's custody.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1601 he was one of two ambassadors sent to England on a most important mission, to press upon the English Parliament the claim of King James to the English crown. They returned in the following May and were honourably 
2 CONC discharged. It is said that Queen Elizabeth received the Earl very graciously, and among other things presented him with a very fine bason and laver of mother-of-pearl with several rubies and pearls set therein.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1603, after the King's departure to England, the Queen made another determined effort to obtain the custody of Prince Henry, and to embroil Mar with his master, but without success. At the King's request, however, the Princ
2 CONC e was delivered to the Duke of Lennox to be taken to England, while the Earl and his family received special thanks for their services. Mar soon followed his master to England, and was, on 25 June 1603, elected a Knight of the Gar
2 CONC ter, and installed on 9 July. On 13 September 1603 he received a royal grant conferring on him the rights and privileges of a subject of England, to himself and his heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT English air, however, was prejudicial to his health, as appears from a will he made in April 1608 at Stirling, on the eve of his departure for England to attend the King, where he had several times been visited with great sickness
2 CONC , to the danger of his life. In 1605 King Henry IV of France wrote to the Earl beseeching his good offices in the preservation of friendship with King James, and sent Mar a jewel valued at 15,000 livres. In 1606 the abbacies of Dr
2 CONC yburgh and Cambuskenneth and the priory of Inchmahome were erected in his favour into the lordship of Cardross, a grant repeated to his son; and on 10 June 1610 he was created LORD CARDROSS, the charter containing powers by which 
2 CONC he assigned the dignity in favour of his son Henry and his heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609 King James applied to his old and reliable friend for help and advice in the difficult affair of the Marquess of Huntly and the Earl of Erroll, who were still suspected of papist leanings and intrigues. On 9 December 1616 
2 CONC the Earl received an appointment under the Great Seal as High Treasurer of Scotland, and eight days later was formally invested with the white rod.
2 CONT
2 CONT His correspondence with the King thenceforth deals almost entirely with the finances of the kingdom, which were not in a too flourishing state, but in one letter, in October 1621, the King asks his Treasurer to send fir seed and y
2 CONC oung firs to the Marquess of Buckingham, who wished to have fir trees planted about his house at Burleigh-on-the-Hill, where 4000 or 5000 plants were to be sent, with instructions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King's death in March 1625 brought changes in the government of Scotland, and the Earl's place was specially affected by what was called a Commission of Exchequer, a body which much abridged his office of Treasurer. So much di
2 CONC d he feel the new proposal that he addressed a letter of remonstrance to the young King. In January 1625-26 he and others of the Scottish Privy Council had a long and important interview with King Charles, when the differences bet
2 CONC ween them were discussed, and in this Mar played a straightforward part, although the meeting did not change the King's policy. Mar continued the same steady course in his later dealings with the King, though increasing years and 
2 CONC troublesome subordinates made his way difficult. He demitted his office in March 1630, and the Earl of Morton was appointed his successor, although Mar continued to take interest in public affairs until his death a few years later
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT A few words may be devoted to the great private purpose to which this Earl, apart from his public duties, bent all his energies, the recovery of the actual possession of the earldom of Mar granted to his father. The earldom during
2 CONC  its being in the hands of the Crown had been granted to other owners against whom the Earl now directed the force of law to compel restitution. His first step, apart from the usual service after his father's death, already cited,
2 CONC  was to obtain from Parliament an Act in his favour setting forth his rights and claims, ratifying these and guarding them against being lost by prescription. Various formal protests were made, which showed that the importance of 
2 CONC the Act was recognised. He next, before a special jury, was served heir-general to Isabel, Countess of Mar, on 20 March 1588-89, thus establishing his right as her heir of blood. This was followed by sasine on 7 November 1589.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first action the Earl brought to enforce his legal rights was in 1593 against William Forbes of Corse. He revived the process in 1620, and had a decree in his favour on 23 June 1621. The Earl then turned his attention to Lord 
2 CONC Elphinstone, whose ancestor had received grants of the lands and barony of Kildrummy. The progress of the action need not be detailed, but the decision of the Court was in favour of Mar upon all the points contested. The charter o
2 CONC f 12 August 1404, granting the earldom to Alexander Stewart and his heirs, was declared invalid, the charter of 9 December 1404 was upheld, and others declared void. The title of Lord Elphinstone was thus imperfect. The Earl was f
2 CONC inally put in possession, but the result was not declared in his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 14 December 1634, but his funeral apparently did not take place till April 1635, at Stirling. His last will is dated at Stirling 1 March 1634, in which he nominates Mary Stewart, his Countess, as tutrix to their younges
2 CONC t son William, and he appoints his grandson John Erskine, afterwards Earl of Mar, as his executor. He made a special legacy to his wife 'in contentation' of her third of his moveables, the 'Jewell of diamonds' which he had receive
2 CONC d from the King of France. He concludes his testament with a special injunction to his eldest son to care for his stepmother and his brothers and sisters, especially the 'lytell ons wha can [nocht] do for thamselffis.' He also com
2 CONC mends his family to the protection of King Charles. His personal estate, deducting debts, amounted to £75,971, 17s. 6d. Scots.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Earl of Mar, married:
2 CONT
2 CONT First, Anna or Agnes Drummond, second daughter of David, Lord Drummond.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second, on 7 December 1592, Marie Stewart, second daughter of Esme, first Duke of Lennox.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by first marriage:
2 CONT * John, Earl of Mar.
2 CONT Children by second marriage:
2 CONT * James, Earl of Buchan
2 CONT * Henry (see title Cardross)
2 CONT * Mr. Alexander, colonel of Horse, killed in the explosion at Dunglass Castle on 30 August 1640. Had a natural son, Alexander, by Anna Bothwell.
2 CONT * Sir Arthur Erskine, married Margaret Buchanan. Killed at Worcester 3 September 1651. Issue: John and William.
2 CONT * Sir John Erskine, married Margaret Inglis.
2 CONT * Sir Charles Erskine, ancestor of the Erskines of Alva. Married (1) Mary Hope, (2) Helen Skene.
2 CONT * Mr. William, Cupbearer to King Charles I., died unmarried.
2 CONT * Mary, married William, Master of Marischal.
2 CONT * Anna, married John, sixth Earl of Rothes.
2 CONT * Margaret, married John, second Earl of Kinghorn.
2 CONT * Annabella, died young.
2 CONT * Catherine, married Thomas, second Earl of Haddington.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun05paul/page/592/mode/2up James Balfour Paul, ed., ''The Scots Peerage'', vol. 5 (Edinburgh: D. Douglas, 1904), p615-22]
