1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===SIR JOHN BUCHANAN OF SCOTSCRAIG===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Buchanan of Scotscraig is the second son of Robert Buchanan of Leny. He purchased the lands of Sound and Shapinshaw in Orkney from James Tullo. Sir John was dead in 1642, and his son in-law Harry Erskine sold the properti
2 CONC es to Thomas Buchanan sherrif of Orkney. [http://www.buchanan-heraldry.net.nz/body/sound.html Buchanan Heraldry: Arthur Buchanan of Sound]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT 11 November 1663: Charter by '''Arthur Buchannan of Sound, in favour of Margaret Buxtoun, his spouse,''' of the liferent of the 36d. land of Sound, manor place and teinds thereof, the 8d. land called Elwick, with the mill thereof 
2 CONC called the mill of Shapinsha and the holm called Elginhome, and teinds thereof, the 1½d. land called How between the Hills, and teinds thereof, the 1½d. land called Horraldisgarth, whereof 1d. land is udal land and ½d. land someti
2 CONC me pertained to St. Katharine's prebendary and thereafter to the bishopric of Orkney, the 8d. land of Weyland and the quoy called Laverock, the 3¼d. and half farthing land called the Kingisland of Nes with the quoy called Liaquoy,
2 CONC  and the 5¾d. land in Holland, all in island of Shapinsha, the 6d. land called Grind and the 1d. land called Foirland, in parish of St. Ola, the 3 merk udal land in Grimbuster, in parish of Firth, the lodging and tenement of house
2 CONC s on both sides of the close presently possessed by Mathew McCaill, apothecary, and Andrew Young, indweller in Kirkwall and said '''Arthur Buchannan,''' lying in the Midtoun of Kirkwall and built into one great lodging by the '''d
2 CONC eceased Sir John Buchanan of Scottiscraig, kt., and Dame Margaret Hartsyde, his spouse,''' which thereafter belonged to the '''deceased Thomas Buchanan of Sound, father of said Arthur, thereafter to the deceased George Buchannan, 
2 CONC eldest son of said deceased Thomas Buchannan, who disponed the same to said Arthur,''' the yard opposite said great lodging, tenement of land formerly pertaining to the deceased Hugh Halcro of Aickers and the tenement of land now 
2 CONC possessed by William Linkletter, merchant, and Andrew Corner, notary public, lying in the Laverick in Kirkwall [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD31%2f78&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=hart
2 CONC syde&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1600&dt=1700&di=y National Records of Scotland, Papers of the family of Fea of Clestrain, Orkney, reference GD31/78]
