1 NAME William /Livingston/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Livingston
1 NAME Rev. William Livingston, of Monyabroch
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1576
2 PLAC Kilsyth Castle
2 ADDR
3 CITY Monyabroch
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 SEP 1641
2 PLAC Lanark, Lanarkshire, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Reverend, Univ. of Glasgow, Scotland graduate.
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} His birth date is calculated from a writing by his son John stating "he(his father) died in the year 1641, being sixty-five years old: His Autobiography states the following: My father was Mr. William Livingston, f
2 CONC irst minister at Monyabroch, where he entered in the year 1600, and thereafter was transported about the year 1614 to be minister at Lanark, where he died in the year 1641, being sixty-five years old. His father was Mr. Alexander 
2 CONC Livingston, minister also at Monyabroch, who was near relation to the House of Callendar, his father, who was killed at Pinkie Field, Anno Christi 1547, being a son of the Lord Livingston's which house thereafter was dignified to 
2 CONC be Earls of Linlithgow. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. William, born at Monyabroch (Kilsyth) 1576, completed his education at Glasgow where he was laureated in 1595; on leaving college he was ordained to preach privately on Jan. 13, 1595; licensed Jan 27th, 1595; instituted July 
2 CONC 10th, 1595 and ordained July 13th 1595. He had been permitted by the Glasgow Presbytery to have temporary charge of the parish of Monyabroch on his father's deposition; and his conduct having given satisfaction to the church autho
2 CONC rities, they recommended Feb. 20, 1598-99, that he should be appointed to fill the vacant ministry permanently; whereupon he was duly presented to the living, upon the following July 1, by the patron, Alexander, 7th Lord Livingsto
2 CONC n, admitted by the 10th, and inaugurated and instituted upon the 15th of the same month. The Privy Council, in carrying out the commands of the king detained Rev. William (no date specified) as a prisoner within the bounds of his 
2 CONC parish until his deposition 6 years later, for opposing the restoration of Episcopacy and not submitting to the canons and ceremonies. Whether the king considered this bold minister had been sufficiently punished by being deprived
2 CONC  of the right of ministering to the spiritual needs of has native parish, or whether family influence had been brought to bear upon James, who was always well disposed towards the Livingstons for their unselfish loyalty to his unh
2 CONC appy mother, it is still a rather remarkable fact that within a few weeks of his being deposed from the ministry of Monyabroch, he was presented to the living of Lanark by no less a person than King James himself. If however that 
2 CONC crafy monarch had entertained any hope that by this act of grace or policy he would not be troubled further with opposition from this quarter, he was soon doomed to be disappointed. For a very few years after receiving this appoin
2 CONC tment, one of the most outspoken of the preachers who denounced the legality of the General Assembly that passed a so-called Five Articles of Perth was this very minister of Lanark.
2 CONT
2 CONT This conduct naturally brought him to the notice of the High Commission Court, a tribunal which had been recently specially appointed for the trial of such offences. Rev. William was deposed from his ministry and sentenced to be i
2 CONC mprisoned, but the Court allowed him to return to Lanark before proceeding to his place of confinement. For what length of time the people of Lanark were deprived of their pastor is not stated, but he had been restored to them pri
2 CONC or to Sept. 1624 But in the summer of 1635 he was again summoned before this tribunal to answer, this time, to the charge of having employed his son, the Rev. John Livingston, who had been also deposed for nonconformity in Ireland
2 CONC , to assist him in the dispensing of the communion. Four months later he was a member of the General Assembly which met at Glasgow to enquire into the evils that distressed the country, and to provide suitable remedies; and his na
2 CONC me was placed, with four other, on the list for moderator, tho he was not the one finally chosen to fill the post. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.robertsewell.ca/livingston.html#gen18 "Generation Twelve  {Reverend} William Livingston, M.A. 
2 CONT  "When only twenty years old William Livingston was presented by his kinsman, Alexander, 7th Lord Livingston, afterwards first Earl of Linlithgow, to the family living of Callendar in Perthshire, but, finding that his ignorance of
2 CONC  the Gaelic language stood in the way of his properly discharging the duties of this Highland parish, he was eventually transferred, by the same patron, to the rectory of Monyabroch in succession to his father." (Sir Hector Living
2 CONC ston Duff, The Sewells of the New World, William Pollard and Co., 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Kilsyth Castle, circa 1576  Kilsyth Castle is halfway between Glasgow and Stirling in North Lanarkshire, Scotland. From earliest recorded times Kilsyth was one of the main routes between Glasgow, Falkirk and Edinburgh, and i
2 CONC s very close to the Roman Antonine Wall. Died: before October 1641, Lanark  Married: January 6, 1601 at Falkirk to Agnes Livingston, daughter of Alexander Livingston of Falkirk, and Marlan (or Marion) Bryson of Falkirk. Agnes and 
2 CONC William were 5th cousins once removed, their common ancestor being Sir Alexander Livingston of Callendar. She was described by her son Rev. John Livingston in his Autobiography 'she was a rare pattern of piety and meekness.'
