2 CONT
2 CONT * John O'Hart, "[https://archive.org/stream/irishpedigreesor02ohar#page/414/mode/2up Vance No.1, No.2 and No. 3]" in ''Irish Pedigrees; or, The origin and stem of the Irish nation'' (1892).
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Patrick Vans, Lord Barnbarroch (died 22 July 1597), or Patrick Vaus, was a Scottish judge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Vans was the second son of Sir John Vans of Barnbarroch by Janet, only child of Sir Samuel MacCulloch of Myreton, keeper of the palace of Linlithgow. He was educated for the church, and became rector of Wigton. In 1568 he succeede
2 CONC d to the family estates on the death of his elder brother, and on 1 January 1576 he was appointed an ordinary lord of session on the spiritual side with the title of Lord Barnbarroch. He was knighted in 1583 and made an ambassador
2 CONC  to Denmark in 1587.[1] On 21 January 1587 he was admitted a member of the privy council. In May of the same year he was sent, along with Peter Young, ambassador to Denmark, to arrange for a marriage between James VI and Anne, pri
2 CONC ncess of Denmark, and, having arrived home in August, he was on 1 October exonerated for his proceedings in Denmark. When the ships conveying the princess to Scotland in October 1589 were driven back by storm, and the king resolve
2 CONC d to send a special embassy to fetch her, Vans was named one of the principal ambassadors for that purpose, and, when the king resolved himself to embark, was especially chosen to accompany him. After witnessing the marriage, he, 
2 CONC on the king's resolving to remain in Denmark until the spring, returned to Scotland to report the marriage to the council, arriving in Scotland on 15 December. In 1592 he was elected a lord of the articles, and in June of the same
2 CONC  year received an annual pension of 200l. He was again chosen a lord of the articles on 16 July 1593, and at the same time was appointed to a commission for the provision of ministers and augmentation of stipends. He died on 22 Ju
2 CONC ly 1597, and was succeeded by his son, Sir John Vans, one of the gentlemen of the chamber to King James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though the name of Sir Patrick Vans has not by any ballad editor been associated with the old ballad of "Sir Patrick Spens," the supposition that he is the hero of it is at least as probable as any other theory as to the origin of
2 CONC  the ballad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Attribution
2 CONT  This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: Henderson, Thomas Finlayson (1899). "Vans, Patrick". In Lee, Sidney. Dictionary of National Biography. 58. London: Smith, Elder & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn 6/23/18 (Christina leaving tomorrow for college in Virginia)
