2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Carnegie,_Lord_Kinnaird?
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Sir Robert Carnegie, Lord Kinnaird, 5th Laird of Kinnaird (c.1490–1566) was a 16th-century Scottish landowner, diplomat, judge and Senator of the College of Justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT He was born at Kinnaird Castle near Brechin around 1490, the son of John Carnegie and his wife Euphame Strachan. His father was killed at the Battle of Flodden (9 September 1513) and his mother died a month after (possibly grief-s
2 CONC tricken).[1] Robert inherited Kinnaird Castle at this point and extended it with a kitchen and servants wing around 1520.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1547 he was elected a Senator of the College of Justice and took the title Lord Kinnaird.[2] He replaced Henry Balnaves who was removed due to suspicion of complicity in the murder of Cardinal Beaton.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the autumn of 1548 Carnegie was the diplomat, sent with an armed guard, to deliver the ransom for the George Gordon, 4th Earl of Huntly who had been captured at the Battle of Pinkie Cleugh and was held in the Tower of London. A
2 CONC fter leaving London he went to Blois in France with Archbishop Gavin Hamilton and the David Panter, Bishop of Ross to create the Regent Duke of Chatelherault. After two years in France he returned to Scotland in the summer of 1551
2 CONC .[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Carnegie was clerk of the treasury in 1549.[4] He was knighted sometime after 1551 and prior to 1554 (as he then appears as "Sir Robert Carnegie”).
2 CONT
2 CONT Diplomat
2 CONT He was involved with other law lords in the debates over the Scottish-English border and especially the issue of who should have Berwick-upon-Tweed. In 1553, when Mary of Guise became Regent, he was appointed Clerk to the Treasure
2 CONC r. Later that year, he was re-appointed, along with Sir Robert Bellenden, to the task of settling legal issues relating to the border. Carnegie was sent to present Border complaints to Mary I of England. He joined with the French 
2 CONC ambassador, François de Noailles, in London, which added to English irritation.[5] Carnegie next attended a meeting at Carlisle with James MacGill of Nether Rankeillour, the Earl of Cassilis, and the Bishop of Orkney, with an Engl
2 CONC ish delegation including the Bishop of Durham and the Earl of Westmorland.[6] This long-winded negotiation resulted in an initial agreement in December 1557.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT His final appearance as a member of the Privy Council appears in December 1565.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at either Leuchars Castle or nearby Earlshall Castle in Fife on 5 July 1566 and is buried in the parish churchyard at Leuchars in central Fife.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT He was married to Margaret Guthrie of the Guthries of Lunan. They were parents to Sir John Carnegie (d.1595) and at least 12 other children including David Carnegie of Colluthie and Helen Carnegie whose third husband John Gordon b
2 CONC uilt Glenbuchat Castle.[7]
