1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == GENERAL THOMAS DALYELL OF THE BINNS ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Commander-in-Chief of the King's Army in Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''General Thomas Dalyell of the Bins, here treated, is the son and heir of https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Dalyell-of-the-Binns/6000000008946591165. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotu
2 CONC oft/page/38/mode/1up The Binns Papers: 216]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4-7 August 1677: Ante-nuptial Marriage Contract between David Beatson of Powguild with consent of his mother, Janet Boswell, relict of David Beatsone of Cardon, on one part, and Marie Dalzell, daughter of General Thomas Dalzell
2 CONC  of Binns, with consent of her said father, on the other, whereby said Marie is to be infeft in annualrent of 1000 merks from lands of Powguild and Dundonald after decease of said David, her tocher being 4000 merks. [http://catalo
2 CONC gue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD1%2f49%2f26&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=janet+boswell&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=1700&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Clephane Family of Carslogie, Kinross
2 CONC -shire, reference GD1/49/26]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the Binns Papers ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''23 April 1642: Precept of Sasine (Clare Constat) by James, Earl of Abercorn, superior of the lands underwritten, in favour of Thomas Dalyell, now of Bynnis, son and heir of the late Thomas Dalyell of Bynnis, of the lands of Man
2 CONC erstonn, Bynnis, and others, with reservation of Duntnivie Myre and the loanings within the bounds of the said Myre. Dated at Cannongaitt, 23 April 1642. Witnesses, Beter Algeo, writer, Mr. John Algeo, his son (writer of the prece
2 CONC pt), Robert Baillie and Charles Lennox, servants of the granler. (Signed) "Abercorne" and the witnesses. Seal attached. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/38/mode/1up The Binns Pa
2 CONC pers: 216]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 June 1671: John Arbuckle, John Aikenhead, John Couper, John Arbuckle, John Aikenhead, John Couper, John Arbuckle, John Aikenhead, John Couper, Jessie and Janet Couper, Andrew Thomsone and John Warnuik, all of whom were porti
2 CONC oners of Wester Kittochside (Nether Kettucksyde), promised to compear at three head courts to be holden by General Thomas Dalyell, their superior, within his barony of Knockmead. The deed was executed at Binns. It was signed by th
2 CONC e parties except John Couper and John Warnuik could not write. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/64/mode/1up The Binns Papers: 64]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''7 September 1695: Marriage Contract between James Bell, younger, tenant in Easter Auldcathie, and Margaret Dalyell, natural daughter of General Thomas Dalyell of Jjinns, with consent of Sir Thomas Dalyell of Binns, her brother,
2 CONC  whereby the said James Bell binds him to provide 200 merks to each child of the marriage, and the said Sir Thomas binds him to pay 1000 merks as tocher with another 1000 merks, being half of the 2000 merks left by the will of the
2 CONC  said late General to the late Jean Drummond, sister of the laird of Riccartoune, and left in legacy by the said Jean to the said Margaret Dalyell. Dated at Binns, 7 September 1695, and written by Robert Masterton, writer in Edinb
2 CONC urgh. Witnesses, William Dalyell, younger son of the said Sir Thomas, James Allan, his gardener, and the said Robert Masterton. (Signed) "James Bell, Tho. Dalyell, Margaret Dalyell (could not write)," and the witnesses. [https://a
2 CONC rchive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/93/mode/1up The Binns Papers II: 447]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/n2/mode/1up The Binns Papers 1320-1864. Part I. 1320-1685( Scottish Record Society, Edinburgh, 1936), pp. pp. 1-80]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/n86/mode/1up The Binns Papers 1320-1864. Part II. 1686-1864. (Scottish Record Society, Edinburgh, 1936), pp. 81-146]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/dd/dalzell01.php#lklo Stirnet: Dalzell 01]
2 CONT # [http://www.thepeerage.com/p33874.htm#i338732 Darryl Lundy's Peerage]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tam_Dalyell_of_the_Binns Biographical Summary Copied From Wikipedia] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT General Tam (Thomas) Dalyell ( Dalziell, Dalziel or Dalzell) (1599 – 1685) was a Scottish Royalist General in the Wars of the Three Kingdoms. Also known by the soubriquets, Bluidy Tam and the Muscovite de'il.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dalyell was born in Linlithgowshire; the son of Thomas Dalyell of the House of the Binns, Linlithgowshire; head of a cadet branch of the family of the Earls of Carnwath, and of Janet, daughter of the 1st Lord Bruce of Kinloss, Mas
2 CONC ter of the Rolls in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears to have accompanied Charles I's expedition to La Rochelle in 1628 (to aide the Huguenots of La Rochelle during the Siege of La Rochelle) at the age of 13. Latterly as a colonel, served under General Robert Munro and Gen
2 CONC eral Leslie in Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hearing of the execution of Charles I on 30 January 1649 it is said that he refused to shave his beard as a penance for the behaviour of his fellow countrymen. He was taken prisoner at the capitulation of Carrickfergus in August 1
2 CONC 650, but was given a free pass, and having been banished from Scotland, remained in Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at the Battle of Worcester (3 September 1651), where his men surrendered, and he himself was captured and imprisoned in the Tower of London. In May he escaped abroad, and in 1654 took part in the Highland rebellion 
2 CONC and was exempted from Oliver Cromwell's act of grace, a reward of 200 guineas being offered for his capture, dead or alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dalziel evaded capture and fled to Russia. There he entered the service of the Tsar, Alexei Mikhailovich, and distinguished himself as general in the wars against the Turks and Tatars as well as in the Russo-Polish War (1654–1667)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to Britain upon The Restoration of Charles II. By 19 July 1666 he was appointed commander-in-chief in Scotland, with orders to subdue the Covenanters. Dalziel defeated them at the Battle of Rullion Green, in the Pentla
2 CONC nd Hills, south of Edinburgh. He treated the defeated with great cruelty, imprisoning some 1200 covenanters in Greyfriars Kirkyard amongst other things, earning him the nickname Bluidy Tam. Legends of his cruelty were probably exa
2 CONC ggerated by anti-royalists. The General obtained several of the forfeited estates of his opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 January 1667 he was made a privy councillor, and from 1678 till his death he represented Linlithgow in the Scottish parliament. He was incensed by the choice of the Duke of Monmouth as commander-in-chief in June 1679, and was
2 CONC  confirmed in his original appointment by Charles, but in consequence did not appear at the Battle of Bothwell Brig till after the close of the engagement .
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 November 1681, a commission was issued authorizing him to enroll the regiment afterwards known as the Scots Greys. His commission was confirmed by James VII of Scotland, but died soon after the latter's accession in August 1
2 CONC 685. He married Agnes, daughter of John Ker of Cavers, by whom he had a son, Thomas, created a baronet in 1685, whose only son and heir, Thomas, died unmarried. The baronetage apparently became extinct, but it was assumed about 17
2 CONC 26 by James Menteith. The Dalyell Baronetage today is represented by Tam Dalyell a.k.a. Sir Thomas Dalyell of the Binns, 11th Baronet, the veteran British politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legend has it that "Bluidy Tam" enjoyed on occasion a hand of cards with the Devil. During one of these games, the Devil losing threw the card table at the General. The Devil missed and the Table flew threw through the window and 
2 CONC ended up in a pond on the grounds of the House of the Binns. This tale was passed down through generations of inhabitants of the Binns. In 1870 following a particularly hard drought, a Marble topped Card table was seen poking thro
2 CONC ugh the low waters of the pond. In 1930 the Mother of the present Tam Dalyell asked a local joiner to repair the legs on the table, to find out that the about to be retired tradesman's first job was to retrieve said table from the
2 CONC  pond.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT # Strange Tales of the Lothians. Lang Syne, Midlothian 1978
2 CONT
