1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Munro,_14th_Baron_of_Foulis
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married Margaret Dunbar, only daughter of Sir Alexander Dunbar, Sheriff of Morayshire. They had seven children who produced many of the important branches of the Clan Munro.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  '''George Munro, 1st of Katewell'''. According to historian Alexander Mackenzie this George Munro was killed alongside his father in battle in 1547.[4] However the evidence is against this because the same historian quotes a c
2 CONC harter granted to Robert Mor Munro, 15th Baron in 1563 which was witnessed by his brother George, proving that George was still alive years after the battle.[7] James Monroe, fifth president of the United States was descended from
2 CONC  the Munro of Katewell branch of the clan.[8] DNA testing of a living male relative of President James Monroe has confirmed the president's ancestral connection to the Munro chiefs.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT I. George Munro, fifth son of Robert Munro, fourteenth  Baron of Fowlis, killed at the battle of Pinkie in 1547, was 
2 CONT the first of this family. He married with issue, among  others — 
2 CONT
2 CONT n. George Munro, who received the lands of Katewell, and married, first, Catherine, daughter of Hector 
2 CONT Mackenzie, IV. of Fairburn, with issue — 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *4. Mackenzie, Alexander. (1898). History of the Munros of Fowlis. pp. 40 - 43.
2 CONT *5.  Fraser, James. (1674). Chronicles of the Frasers: the Wardlaw manuscript entitled Polichronicon seu policratica temporum, or, The true genealogy of the Frasers, 916-1674. Re-published in 1905 by William Mackay pp. 132 - 135.
2 CONT *6. The Scottish Clans and Their Tartans. (1885). pp. 79. Library Edition. Published by W. & A. K. Johnston, Limited. Edinburgh and London.
2 CONT *7. Mackenzie, Alexander. (1898). History of the Munros of Fowlis. pp. 46.
2 CONT *8. Monroe, Horace, Canon of Southwark. (1929). Foulis Castle and the Monroes of Lower Iveagh.
2 CONT *9. Munro DNA project Clan Munro DNA Project clanmunro.org.uk. Retrieved 7 September 2014. [http://www.clanmunro.org.uk/dna.htm link]
2 CONT *10. Munro DNA Project familytreedna.com. Retrieved 7 September 2014 [http://www.familytreedna.com/public/MunroDNA/default.aspx?section=yresults link]
2 CONT * "History of the Munros of Fowlis" by A. Mackenzie - Inverness (1898) - p. 391-392 [https://archive.org/details/historyofmunroso00mack/page/391 Archive.Org] 
2 CONT *Genealogies of Virginia Families from the William and Mary College Quarterly. Vol. III Heale-Muscoe.  “The Monroe Family.”  Page 742.  [https://www.ancestry.com/interactive/48643/VAFamiliesIII-002932-742?pid=434866&backurl=https:
2 CONC //search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?indiv%3D1%26dbid%3D48643%26h%3D434866%26tid%3D103015317%26pid%3D190048253534%26usePUB%3Dtrue%26_phsrc%3DXFX3%26_phstart%3DsuccessSource&treeid=103015317&personid=190048253534&hintid=&usePUB=tr
2 CONC ue&_phsrc=XFX3&_phstart=successSource&usePUBJs=true AncestryImage]
