1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Anne Drummond (alternatively recorded as Lady Anne Drummond) was a 17th-century Scottish noblewoman belonging to the prominent House of Drummond and the Earldom of Perth. Born circa 1583, she was the daughter of Patrick Drummond, 
2 CONC 3rd Lord Drummond, and Elizabeth Lindsay. As a member of the high Scottish aristocracy, she navigated the strategic marital and territorial alliances required of noble families in Perthshire and Aberdeenshire.
2 CONT Anne was married twice over her lifetime. She entered into her first marriage contract in 1593 with Patrick Barclay, Laird of Towie, with whom she had children and managed the Barclay estates until his death. Following a period of
2 CONC  widowhood, she married her second husband, Andrew Fraser, 6th Laird of Muchalls, who would shortly thereafter be elevated to the peerage as the 1st Lord Fraser. She served as the lady of Castle Fraser during her husband's final y
2 CONC ears and elevation, passing away in Scotland at an advanced age after 1636. Her lineage and marital timeline are explicitly detailed in peerage records, cleanly differentiating her from unverified profiles generated by online data
2 CONC base hints.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Patrick Drummond, 3rd Lord Drummond (born circa 1550 – died before 1600).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Elizabeth Lindsay (born circa 1555 – death date unrecorded).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: Circa 1583 in Perthshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: After 1636 in Scotland (documented via surviving estate arrangements and dowager provisions active at the time of her second husband's death).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriages & Issue===
2 CONT Marriage 1: Patrick Barclay, Laird of TowieAnne Drummond married Patrick Barclay under a marriage contract dated April 2, 1593, in 1602. Primary Scottish peerage registries and contemporary estate charters verify that this first u
2 CONC nion produced documented children:
2 CONT 1. Christian Barclay: Born circa 1603; married William Ruthven before 1603.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Walter Barclay of Towie: Born after 1605; succeeded to the Towie estate and died before 1643.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Francis Barclay of Towie: Born circa 1605; died in October 10, 1621 as the younger of Towie.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. George Barclay of Seggat: Date of birth unrecorded; inherited the Seggat family branch assets.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Jean Barclay: Date of birth unrecorded; married into adjacent regional families.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Elizabeth Barclay: Date of birth unrecorded; married John Gordon of Ardlogie.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Alexander Barclay of Towie Mill: Date of birth unrecorded; held property rights at the family mill tract.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 2: Andrew Fraser, 1st Lord Fraser
2 CONT Following the death of her first husband, Anne Drummond married Andrew Fraser after May 20, 1624. This secondary marriage produced no documented issue prior to Lord Fraser's death in 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Documentation===
2 CONT 1593 Barclay Marriage Contract: Documented in Scottish legal registries, explicitly recording her initial marital alliance with the House of Barclay of Towie.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1624 Fraser Marriage Contract: Preserved in historical Aberdeenshire estate charters, verifying her second union to Andrew Fraser and detailing her updated dower rights at Castle Fraser.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots Peerage Registries: Explicitly records her parentage and both of her marriages, confirming that she had no children with Lord Fraser.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Historical Sources===
2 CONT Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, Vol. IV (Fraser) and Vol. VII (Perth), David Douglas Publishing.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Malcolm, A Genealogical History of the House of Drummond, 1808 (Extensively documenting the lineages, marriages, and descendants of the Drummond family).
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Charters of the Lands of Towie and Muchalls, National Records of Scotland (NRS).
