3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Second son of David McGill, Lord Cranstonriddell, by Mary, daughter of Sir William Sinclair of Herdmanston, succeeded his elder brother in 1619, was created a baronet in 1627, and admitted an Ordinary Lord on the 3
2 CONC d November 1629, in place of Sir Alexander Napier, Lord Laurieston; his father, grandfather, and great grandfather having also been senators. On the 29th November, the Court elected him their collector of the taxation of forty shi
2 CONC llings Scots on every pound land of old extent, lately granted to them by the king and the estates. He was reappointed <em>ad vitam aut culpam</em> in November 1641 by the king, with the approbation of Parliament, and was named on
2 CONC e of the committee of exchequer, 1st February 1645. Lord Cranstonriddell was created a peer, by the title of Viscount Oxfurd, on the 19th April 1651; but the troubles of the time preventing him from assuming the title, he was agai
2 CONC n appointed a judge at the Restoration, as Sir James McGill of Cranston, but never took his seat. His patent of nobility was read and allowed in Parliament on the 13th March 1661. His Lordship died on the 5th May 1663.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=grI2AAAAIAAJ An Historical Account of the Senators of the College of Justice: From Its Institution in MDXXXII]; by George Brunton, David Haig; 1832; Page  283
