1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===DAME JANET LAWSON (aka DAME JONET LAWSOUN)===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dame Janet Lawson, here treated, is the daughter of James Lawson of Humbie and his wife Elizabeth Bellenden. [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ee/edmonstone3.php Stirnet: Edmonstone 3]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''23 and 26 November 1609: REX confirmavit cartam factam per Jac. Fawsyde apparentem de eodem proprietarium, et per M. Rob. F. de eodem ejus patrem liberetenementarium infrascriptorum, — [qua, pro perimpletione litere obligatorie
2 CONC  de data presentium, — cum consensu D. Joannis Cokbume de Ormestoun miltis clerici justiciarie et unius senatorum supremi senatus Sootie, et dicti Rob., quibus dictus Jac. interdictus fuit, necnon Jonete Lawsoun spouse dicti Jac, 
2 CONC — vendiderunt M. JOANNI LAYNG de Spittellis, custodi signeti regis, et REBECCE DENNEISTOUN ejus sponse in vitali redditu, et D. ANDREE HAMMYLTOUN de Reidhous militi, uni senatorum supremi seuatus Scotie, et Doniine JEANNE LAYNG ej
2 CONC us sponse in conjnncta infeodatione, et heredibus inter eos legit, procreatis, quibus deficieutibus, heredibus et assignatis dicti And. quibuscunque, irredimabiliter, — imam suarum 2 terrarum husbandiarum in villa et territorio de
2 CONC  Ballincreiff nuncupatam lie Wester Bissetleyis (inter Spittellis et Gosfurde), per dictum M. Jo. occupatam, in constab. de Hadingtoun, vic. Edinbrughe: — Tenend. de rege :- cum precepto sasine : — Test. Samuele Fawsyde filio dict
2 CONC i Roberti, Antonio Quhyte scriba signeti regii, Jo. Thomesoun servitors dicti Rob., Tho. Layng servitore dicti M. Jo. : — carta scripta per Wil. Kynloch servitorem dicti Antonii : — Apud Edinbrughe, 23 et 26 Nov. 1609] :— Test, ut
2 CONC  in aliis cartis &c. xlvi. 239. [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi07scot/page/103/mode/1up RMS, 1609-1620: number 280]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 November 1633: Complaint by Sir Thomas Hope of Craighall, King's Advocate, Sir William Murray of Touchadame, Sir Alexander Seatoun of Graden, Sir John Stirline of Carden, and Wlliam Stirline of Ardoch, as follows : On 3rd Oc
2 CONC tober last John Fawside of that Ilk became cautioner in their Lordships' books for JANET LAWSOUN, widow of Sir John Edmistoun of Ednam, that she would remove from the place of Ednem and lands of the same, and also take her goods o
2 CONC ff the meadows thereof and repone the complainers therein, except the particular farms of which she was in possession before the riot committed by her against them, and that within six days after the date of the act of caution und
2 CONC er a penalty of 3000 merks. Nevertheless upon the said 3rd of October the said DAME JANET directed her brother-in-law, Mr. William Cockebume, to the town of Ednem, and by her order he commanded the tenants to lead in their corns u
2 CONC nteinded and in no wise to permit the complainers or their servants to lead the teinds, nor to furnish carts to them for this purpose, which formerly they were in use to do. Further, the said Mr. William Cockebume, understanding t
2 CONC hat the complainers were coming to Ednem to receive possession, " to ludifie and mocke thame sent to Williame Cranstoun in Morestoun, who formerlie possest the saids lands, in thair name to come and receave possessioun upoun the m
2 CONC orne thereafter, being the fourt day of October, bot that same day he strake aff the lockes of the chamber doore, tooke the same with him, left the doores opin, drave the ladeis goods over ane little burne, and so, as if he had sa
2 CONC tisfied the said act, tooke instruments that he had offered possessioun to the compleaners, who wer not there to receave the same ; bot within a short apace thereafter the same wer brought backe agane to the Maynes of Ednem, where
2 CONC  they pastoured ; and when the compleaners come to receave possessioun the said Mr Williame absented himselfe and departed so as the compleaners could find nane to give thame possessioun, bot found the ladeis goods pastouring upon
2 CONC  the ground ; and having past throw the ground they found the tennents leading thair cornes unteinded," stating that they did so by order of Mr William Cockeburne in the lady's name. Further, the said William Cranstoun having brou
2 CONC ght his goods and oxen to the lands of Ednem on the said 4 th of October to take possession, ALEXANDER FAWSYDE, son to the said lady, George Fortoun, her servant, with a great number of shearers, about three score persons, armed w
2 CONC ith forkes and great staves, at the said lady's instigation, pursued the said William Cranstoun's servant and goods for their lives with stones and forks, drove them back to the lands of Stitchell and threatened to " fell " them i
2 CONC f they came there. The complainers complained to James Cockeburne of Eysla, sheriff of Berwick, and craved redress at his hands, but he replied that " he could not helpe thame, becaus he wes not shireff in that part, and the shear
2 CONC ers wer all the ladeis servants." Thereupon the complainers went out of the town and caused the goods to be brought back so as to take possession and see if any responsible person would hinder them, when the said son and servant, 
2 CONC seeing this, came, accompanied by the company of shearers foresaid, from the place where they were shearing about a quarter of a mile away, and with swords, staves and stones, pursued them for their lives, wounded several of their
2 CONC  servants, and barbarously injured some of the cattle. Thereafter Thomas Broun in Ednem " caused ring the bell of Ednem, and when the ministers wife stayed him he upbraided her with contumelious speeches and thereafter come furth 
2 CONC with Williame Dicksoun, younger, Williame Broun, and Charles Robertsoun in Ednem, with others thair complices, and with convocatioun of his Majestis lieges to the nomber of ane hundreth persons boddin with swords, forkes and lang 
2 CONC speiris, all of the speciall causing and hounding out of the said Ladie Ednem, and of new sett upon the compleaners, hurt and woundit thair servants, slew ane of thair oxin, hurt the rest with forkes, and drave thame away be the s
2 CONC pace of ane myle, ever striking thame with thair forkes ; and within ane houre thereafter the said DAME JONET her selfe come to Ednem, and finding her directiouns in the violences foresaids to be satisfied, she allowed of the same
2 CONC ." However, to take away all pretexts and excuses, the complainers sent George Stirling to her to ask if she would give them possession and suffer them to lead the teinds, which she refused, saying that she would do no more than s
2 CONC he had done. So she still keeps her goods upon the ground, and discharges the tenants to attend the complainer's courts or pay their rents, whereby she has contraveened the said act. Her cautioner should therefore be decerned to p
2 CONC ay the penalty of 3000 merks, and her ladyship and other persons named should be punished for the insolencies foresaid. Charge having been given to the said DAME JANET LAWSOUN, JOHN FAWSYDE OF THAT ILK AND ALEXANDER FAWSYDE, HER S
2 CONC ONS, George Fortoun, his servant, Thomas and William Broun, Charles Robertson and William Dicksoun, younger, and the pursuers not compearing, but DAME JANET LAWSOUN being personally present and producing an instrument under the si
2 CONC gn and subscription of James Mader, notary, dated at Ednem, 22nd November instant, to the effect that William Broun in Ednem, as procurator for her, went to the two barns of Ednem Maynes called " the peis barn and the oatt barn at
2 CONC  nyne houres in the morning or thereby and opened the barn doores," and declared that he was ready to repone Sir William Murrey of Touchadame, Sir Alexander Seatoun of Kilcreuche, Sir John Stirline of Carden, William Stirline of A
2 CONC rdoch and William Cranstoun in Moreistoun to the possession thereof, and was willing to deliver the keys thereof to them, and because none of these persons, nor any one in their name, attended to receive the same, he passed to the
2 CONC  personal presence of Marion Seatoun, wife of the said William Cranstoun, who was in the manor place of Ednem for the time, and delivered to her four keys, being the keys of the whole doors, back and fore, of the said barns, which
2 CONC  he desired her to receive so that the persons foresaid should enter into possession, and that the said Marion Seatoun took the said keys, therefore the Lords assoilzie the said lady and remanent defenders and also her cautioner f
2 CONC rom the conclusions of this summons, and declare them free thereof. [https://archive.org/details/registerprivyco07coungoog/page/n206/mode/1up RPS 1633-35: pp. 152-4]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Printed Evidence===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/registerprivyco07coungoog/page/n9/mode/1up The Register of the Privy Council of Scotland. Edited by P. Hume Brown, MA., LL.D. Second Series. Vol. V. A.D. 1633-1635. (H. M. General Register House, Edinb
2 CONC urgh, 1904), 1851 pp.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
