2 CONT '''     James Gray was the son of''' Patrick Gray, 4th Lord Gray and Marion Ogilvy.1,2 '''He married Elizabeth Bethune''', daughter of Sir John Bethune, 2nd of Creich and Janet Hay, i'''n June 1573.1 He and Elizabeth Bethune were 
2 CONC divorced on 10 June 1581 on the grounds of James' adultery with Elizabeth's niece, Isabel Bethune.1 He died in 1585/86, slain.'''1
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume VIII, page 140. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 1642. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10835.htm#i108349
2 CONT _______
2 CONT Patrick Gray, 4th Lord Gray (c. 1518 -1584) was a Scottish landowner and Sheriff of Angus, active during the war of the Rough Wooing as a supporter of the Scottish Reformation.
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Patrick Gray was the son of Egidia Mercer and Gilbert Gray of Buttergask (half-brother of Patrick Gray, 3rd Lord Gray), and the grandson of Andrew Gray, 2nd Lord Gray (d. 1514). Patrick became Lord Gray in April 1541, after the de
2 CONC ath of his uncle. In order to succeed to the Gray lands, as heir of his grandfather, he had to pay 10,000 marks to the Treasury of King James V of Scotland, and was confirmed Lord Gray on 14 September 1542. He was still paying Jam
2 CONC es Hamilton, Regent Arran, in 1543.[1]
2 CONT Patrick Gray firstly married Marion Ogilvy in 1537. Their son was Patrick Gray, 5th Lord Gray (1538–1608). '''They also had another son, James Gray, who was the second husband of Elizabeth Bethune, a mistress of King James V of Sc
2 CONC otland'''. Their daughter Elizabeth Gray married Laurence Bruce of Cultmalindie, who moved to Shetland and built Muness Castle on Unst.[2]
2 CONT Patrick secondly married Margaret Ker, daughter of Sir Walter Ker of Cessford, in 1557. The chief residence of the Grays was Fowlis Castle, near Dundee, Scotland, and they also lived as well at Castle Huntly, nearby. In 1583, an E
2 CONC nglish list of the Scottish nobility described Patrick with an interesting but inaccurate English pedigree;
2 CONT *"an aged man, esteemed to come of English bloode, that came into Scotlande with the Lady Somerset, wyef to King James the Firste. In religion suspected; of no greate power or frendes. His eldest sonne married th'erle of Gowrie's 
2 CONC father's sister, and his other the daughter of Lord Glamis."[3]
2 CONT Rough Wooing
2 CONT Lord Gray was captured by the English at the Battle of Solway Moss and was held by the Archbishop of York.[4] When he returned to Scotland he made a band of friendship or 'manrent' with Cardinal David Beaton at St Andrews on 22 Oc
2 CONC tober 1544.[5]
2 CONT On 11 March 1547, during the siege at St Andrews Castle, Gray made a pledge to Edward VI of England. This contract stated that for English assistance in re-instating his rights over Perth, and the return of his brother, who was a 
2 CONC hostage (called a "pledge" in the language of the time) in England; Patrick Gray would further the marriage of Mary, Queen of Scots to Edward VI, and deliver Broughty Castle and the Spey Tower at Perth to the English army. This in
2 CONC strument was witnessed by Norman Leslie, James Kirkcaldy of Grange, Henry Balnaves, and Alexander Whitelaw of Newgrange.[6]
2 CONT Gray asked to be compensated for his fishing rights at Broughty; he was duly paid £1000 on 14 November 1547 by Sir Andrew Dudley, an English soldier and brother of the Duke of Northumberland.[7] At the same time, the Master of Rut
2 CONC hven, whose father was Provost of Perth and Gray's rival, offered to deliver Perth to the English.[8] In June 1548, Thomas Fisher brought him the Lord Protector's thanks, a gold chain, a pension of 1000 crowns and a gift of 300 cr
2 CONC owns.[9]
2 CONT In January 1548, an English commander, Thomas Wyndham, placed a garrison of 20 "tall men" in the Steeple at Dundee, with the help and advice of Andrew Dudley and Lord Gray. Their armaments included cannon described as "a saker and
2 CONC  a falcon and four double bases" and small guns called "hackbuts of crook".[10]
2 CONT Gray made a compromise with Mary of Guise in March 1548, promising to serve her and Mary, Queen of Scots and accepting a pension of 500 merks.[11]
2 CONT Gray was captured by French troops in November 1548 and summoned for treason on 18 December 1548.[12] D'Essé, the French commander, wanted him executed, but Regent Arran and the other Scottish lords protested.[13]
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT During the crisis of the Scottish Reformation, Mary of Guise wrote to Lord Gray on 11 May 1560 to inform him of the defeat of an English assault at the Siege of Leith. In August 1560, Lord Gray attended the Scottish Reformation Pa
2 CONC rliament, and signed the commission for the marriage of James Hamilton, 3rd Earl of Arran to Elizabeth I of England, (which had no effect).[14] Some years before, Gray had been captured by the English at a border incident called t
2 CONC he Raid of Swinton. In 1562 he was detained in England, on account of his old bail conditions – he had been, "lattin hame upoun ane band." Mary, Queen of Scots, wrote to Elizabeth to complain at his ill-treatment.[15]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Gray,_4th_Lord_Gray
2 CONT ______
2 CONT '''Elizabeth Bethune, or Beaton (died after 1581), was one of the mistresses of King James V of Scotland. Their daughter''', Lady Jean Stewart, married Archibald Campbell, 5th Earl of Argyll.[1]
2 CONT '''Elizabeth was the daughter of''' Sir David Beaton of Creich, a nephew of James Beaton, Archbishop of St Andrews, and Keeper of Falkland Palace.
2 CONT '''As an infant, Elizabeth's daughter''' Jean Stewart was brought up in the household of Mary of Guise (the Queen of Scotland as wife of King James V), '''and then briefly in the nursery of her legitimate half-brother''', Prince J
2 CONC ames, the Duke of Rothesay, the infant son of King James V.[2]
2 CONT '''Subsequently, Elizabeth was married to John Stewart, 4th Lord Innermeath, by whom she had two sons''', James Stewart, 5th Lord Innermeath and the poet John Stewart of Baldynneis. '''Elizabeth and her husband John were granted l
2 CONC ands in the parish of Inverkeilor in May 1544.'''[3]
2 CONT '''In 1572 she married secondly James Gray''', son of Patrick Gray, 4th Lord Gray. '''In 1578 Gray fathered a child by her niece''', Isobel Beaton. '''The subsequent quarrel led to sieges of Redcastle in Angus, and several court c
2 CONC ases.[4] She got her divorce in 1581.'''[5]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Bethune
2 CONT ______
