2 CONT *https://en.m.wikisource.org/wiki/Gibson,_Alexander_(d.1693)_(DNB00)_ “GIBSON, Sir ALEXANDER (d. 1693), clerk of session, was eldest son of Sir John Gibson of Pentland and Addiston, co. Edinburgh (knighted circa 1647), by Jean, da
2 CONC ughter and heiress of Alexander Hay of Kennet, Clackmannanshire. Sir John was second son of Sir Alexander Gibson, the first lord Durie (d. 1644) [q. v.] Douglas states that Sir John was a distinguished royalist, and was created a 
2 CONC knight-banneret at the battle of Worcester, but there seems no other evidence than his assertion. ....”
2 CONT *https://digital.nls.uk/histories-of-scottish-families/archive/95078654?mode=transcription JOHN HAY OF EASTER KENNET was the eldest son and heir of Sir Alexander Hay, Lord Easter Kennet, by his spouse Mariot Farquhar, descended fr
2 CONC om an ancient family of Gilmerscroft,  Britain. He was living A. D. 1600, and married Mariot (or Marian) Drummond. In continuation of our extract introduced into the last section from Mr.  Bain's sketch of "The Hays of Lochloy," w
2 CONC e quote : —  "Sir Alexander Hay of Easter Kennet died in 1594. https://www.geni.com/people/Alexander-Hay-Lord-Easter-Kennet/6000000017893705244?through=6000000017894292175 * * *  * * * His eldest son, John, married iMariot Drummon
2 CONC d and  left two sons : —  Mr. John Hay of Easter Kennet by his spouse Mariot Drummond had  two sons: — ("The Bruces and the Cumyns," and Mr. Bain.)  i . Mr. Alexander Hay, one of the Clerks of Session, who left daughters : — Marga
2 CONC ret, Anna, and Ellen, provided for out of Easter Kennet, '''one of whom married Sir John Gibson'''.  ii. Mr. David Hay of Woodcockdale, Easter Kennet, Craigtown, and Kennet Paus, sold to Bruce of Clackmannan and of Wester Kennet, 
2 CONC in 1638, for 16,000 merks.
