1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Jean Colquhoun1,2
2 CONT *F, #13657
2 CONT *Last Edited=9 Feb 2011
2 CONT *'''     Jean Colquhoun was the daughter of''' Sir Alexander Colquhoun of Luss and Helen Buchanan.3,4 '''She married, firstly, Alan Cathcart, 5th Lord Cathcart''', son of Alan Cathcart, Master of Cathcart and Isabel Kennedy, '''on
2 CONC  29 October 1626.5 She married, secondly, Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt.''', son of Sir Dugald Campbell of Auchinbreck, 1st Bt. and Mary Erskine, '''before 1645.1 She married, thirdly, Hon. Sir William Hamilton, 1st a
2 CONC nd last Bt.''', son of James Hamilton, 1st Earl of Abercorn and Marion Boyd, '''between 1645 and 1 April 1656.1,6
2 CONT *'''     From 29 October 1626, her married name became Cathcart. As a result of her marriage, Jean Colquhoun was styled as Countess Dowager Cathcart.1 From before 1645, her married name became Campbell.1 From between 1645 and 1 Ap
2 CONC ril 1656, her married name became Hamilton.1
2 CONT *'''Child of Jean Colquhoun and Alan Cathcart, 5th Lord Cathcart
2 CONT **Alan Cathcart, 6th Lord Cathcart+4 b. 1628, d. 13 Jun 1709
2 CONT *'''Children of Jean Colquhoun and Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt.
2 CONT **Alexander Campbell4 d. 1685
2 CONT **Archibald Campbell of Knockamillie+4 b. c 1632, d. b 1679
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S15] George Edward Cokayne, editor, The Complete Baronetage, 5 volumes (no date (c. 1900); reprint, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 1983), volume II, page 322. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Baronetage.
2 CONT *[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinct
2 CONC  or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume III, page 104. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *[S15] George Edward Cokayne, The Complete Baronetage, volume II, page 340.
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT *[S323] Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: founded on Wood's edition of Sir Robert Douglas's The Peerage of Scotland (Edinburgh, Scotland: David Douglas, 1904), volume I, page 47. Hereinafter cited as The Scots Peerage.
2 CONT *From: http://thepeerage.com/p1366.htm#i13657 ____________________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Duncan Campbell (1597–1645), 2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck''', was commander of the Marquess of Argyll, Archibald Campbell's troops, (Covenanters) in Ireland.
2 CONT *He was a son of Sir Dugald Campbell, 1st Baronet of Auchinbreck and Mary Erskine. He was recalled by Archibald Campbell to Scotland and placed in command of the Covenanter troops at the Battle of Inverlochy (1645).
2 CONT *Titles
2 CONT **2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck[1]
2 CONT **Governor of Rathlin Island[1]
2 CONT **Member of Parliament for Argyllshire Scotland between 1639 and 1641[1]
2 CONT **Member of Parliament for Argyllshire Scotland in 1643[1]
2 CONT *Battles
2 CONT **Irish Rebellion of 1641
2 CONT **Wars of the Three Kingdoms
2 CONT **Battle of Inverlochy (1645)
2 CONT *'''Sir Duncan Campbell, 2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck'''[1] (1597–1645) was commander of Argyll’s troops in Ireland in 1641 and taking of Dunluce Castle, County Antrim. He held the office of Governor of Rathlin Island. 
2 CONC He married first Margaret Blair, who was born about 1575. She was the daughter of the Lord of Blair, and died without issue. He married second, Miss Maxwell, daughter to the Lord of Newark. They had issue:
2 CONT **a) Dugald, Third Baronet, born abt 1629 in Auchinbreck. He succeeded his father in 1645 and sat in Parliament for Argyllshire in 1649. He died unmarried about 1662 and was succeeded by his nephew, Sir Duncan, son of Archibald Ca
2 CONC mpbell of Knockumilie.
2 CONT **b) Miss Campbell, b. 1627, md. Lord of Lechnel.
2 CONT *'''He married third, 1628, Dame Jean Coloquhoun, Lady Cathcart, of Luss, Scotland. She was born about 1610. Duncan and Jean had issue:
2 CONT **a) Archibald, born abt 1629, of Knockamillie, who married 16 Mar 1678 Margaret Campbell, daughter to Colin, Tutor of Calder by whom he had a son, Sir. Duncan.
2 CONT **b) Sir Duncan: 4th Baronet eldest son of Archibald of Knockemelie; Member of Parliament for Argyllshire, 1689–1698; m. 1679/80 dau. of 1st Earl of Balcarres; d. abt 1700.
2 CONT **c) James: born about 1630 of Knockumilie. He married Janet McLoed about 1650.
2 CONT **d) William Campbell, of Wester Kaimes.
2 CONT **e) Alexander of Strondour, born about 1635 was married to Jean, eldest daughter to the Lord of Ottir by whom he had issue. He was murdered after the surrender of Carnassarie Castle in June 1685.
2 CONT *On 2 February, '''Sir Duncan Campbell''' led Argyll’s troops at the Battle of Inverlochy (1645) where he was taken prisoner and murdered by Alasdair Mac Colla.
2 CONT *One prisoner who would receive no mercy was '''Duncan Campbell, Lord Auchinbreck''', the functional commander of the covenanters that day. Auchinbreck had ravaged the lands of Alasdair Mac Colla's Irish cousins in Antrim as a com
2 CONC mander in the Scottish army in Ireland. Also he had plotted to assassinate Alasdair when Alasdair tried to reconcile with the Scottish army following the defeat at Glen Maquin. Now Alasdair had Auchinbreck brought before him. Alas
2 CONC dair offered Auchinbreck two choices, to be made longer, that is hanged, or made shorter, that is decapitated. Auchinbreck reportedly replied, "da dhiu gun aon roghain," which could be roughly translated as two worst alternatives 
2 CONC without one choice. At this point Alasdair swung his two handed sword, and took off the top of Auchinbreck's head above the ears like the top of a soft-boiled egg.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Duncan_Campbell_of_Auchinbreck ______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
