1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary
2 CONT "<strong>Sir James Skene</strong> (d. 1633), the eldest son, was admitted advocate on 6 July 1603, and on 12 June 1612 became a lord of session. On 12 June 1619 he was summoned before the privy council for endeavoring, at Easter, 
2 CONC to evade one of the five articles of Perth, requiring that the communion should be taken kneeling, by failing to attend; but excused himself on the ground that he was examining witnesses by direction of the lords at the time of th
2 CONC e preparation sermon, and his excuse was accepted (Reg. P. C. Scotl. xi. 595–6; Calderwood, vii. 383). ‘Some, however,’ says Calderwood, ‘ascribed his not conforming, not to conscience, but to the dissuasions of his mother-in-law 
2 CONC and her daughter, a religious woman’ (ib.). His wife was Janet Johnston, [daughter of Sir John Johnston of Hilton. SIC]. On 14 Feb. 1626 he succeeded Thomas, earl of Melrose, as president of the court of session, and on 16 Jan. 16
2 CONC 30 he was created by Charles I a baronet of Nova Scotia. He died on 25 Oct. 1633 at his own house in Edinburgh, and was buried in the church of the Grey Friars."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Dictionary_of_National_Biography_volume_52.djvu/348 Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 52], Page 348
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed Wife
2 CONT
2 CONT She was https://www.geni.com/people/Janet-Johnston-of-Hilton/6000000017425170940 according to the Skene book, but the Johnston diary has her as daughter of Archibald Johnston.
2 CONT
