2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to navigationJump to search Christina Stewart Douglas, Countess of Buchan (c. 1548 – 20 September 1580), also known as Christian,[1] was a Scottish noblewoman, the suo jure Countess of Bu
2 CONC chan. She succeeded her grandfather John Stewart, 3rd Earl of Buchan in 1551. In that year she was, as heir to her father, enfeoffed in the lands, jurisdictions, and offices enumerated in her grandfather's charter of 1547. She was
2 CONC  only three years of age at her mother's death, and was placed under the guardianship of Margaret Erskine, wife of Robert Douglas of Lochleven.[2] In January 1549/50, though Christina was only a young child, a contract of marriage
2 CONC  was arranged between her and James Stewart, afterwards Earl of Moray and Regent, which would give him possession of her lands. He was the son of Margaret Erskine by King James V. Notwithstanding this contract he married another l
2 CONC ady, Agnes Keith. Christina was married instead to Moray's half-brother, Robert Douglas, second son of Margaret Erskine and Robert Douglas of Lochleven. In his wife's right, Robert Douglas became Earl of Buchan and Sheriff of Banf
2 CONC f.[2] When Mary, Queen of Scots was imprisoned in Lochleven Castle in July 1567, Robert Douglas, Earl of Buchan, was one the few people allowed to visit her.[3] In 1574, they obtained a charter of the family estates, heritable off
2 CONC ices, and the Earlshill in conjunct fee, with remainder to the heirs-male of the marriage, heirs female, heirs of the body of the Countess, and finally, to the nearest lawful heirs of the Earl. Her husband, as Earl of Buchan, took
2 CONC  a prominent part in the political movements made in the minority of James VI, and he supported his brother Regent Moray in opposition to the Loyalists who favored Mary, Queen of Scots. After the Regent's assassination in 1570, he
2 CONC  was one of the four lords to whom the government of the country was committed in name of the King.[2] They had one son and three daughters: James Douglas, 5th Earl of Buchan. Christian, married to Richard Douglas, brother of Will
2 CONC iam Douglas of Whittinghame. Mariota, married to Alexander Irvine of Drum. Elizabeth, married, in 1592, to Andrew Fraser of Muchals, and had issue. The Earl of Buchan died at Mills of Drum, near Aberdeen, 18 August, and the Counte
2 CONC ss at Aberdeen 20 September 1580.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/details/LZRC-C15
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stewart-3764
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/261781757/christina-stewart
2 CONT
2 CONT Christina (also spelt Christian) Stewart was the (suo jure) 4th Countess of Buchan. As was Scottish tradition at the time Christina did not take her husband's surname.
2 CONT
2 CONT She succeeded her grandfather John Stewart, 3rd Earl of Buchan in 1551. She was only three years of age at her mother's death, and was placed under the guardianship of Margaret Erskine, wife of Robert Douglas of Lochleven.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christina married Robert Douglas (second son of Margaret Erskine and Robert Douglas of Lochleven). Their children included one son and three daughters :
2 CONT
2 CONT James Douglas, 5th Earl of Buchan.
2 CONT Christian, who married Richard Douglas.
2 CONT Mariota, who married Alexander Irvine of Drum.
2 CONT Elizabeth, who married, in 1592, Andrew Fraser of Muchals.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Buchan died at Mills of Drum, near Aberdeen, 18 August, and the Countess at Aberdeen 20 September 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT C.Smith
2 CONT Gravesite Details
2 CONT Possibly buried in the Holy Rood Kirkyard
