1 NAME David /Carnegie of Kinnaird/
2 GIVN David
2 SURN Carnegie of Kinnaird
2 _MARNM Carnegie of Kinnaird
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1533
2 ADDR
3 CITY Colluthie
3 STAE Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 APR 1598
2 ADDR
3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU He held the office of Commissioner of the Treasury [Scotland] in 1595, an 'Octavian.'
1 TITL Rt. Hon.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Courtesy of The Peerage:-
2 CONT
2 CONT "Rt. Hon. David Carnegie of Kinnaird was the son of Sir Robert Carnegie of Kinnaird and Margaret Guthrie.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Elizabeth Ramsay, daughter of Henry Ramsay and Isabel Kinloch.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married by contract, secondly, Eupheme Wemyss, daughter of Sir John Wemyss of Wemyss and Margaret Otterburn, on 4 October 1568.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 19 April 1598.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Scotland].
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived at Colluthie, Fife, Scotland, which he acquired through his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Commissioner of the Treasury [Scotland] in 1595, an 'Octavian.' He lived at Kinnaird, Perthshire, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Rt. Hon. David Carnegie of Kinnaird and Elizabeth Ramsay
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  Elizabeth Carnegie
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Margaret Carnegie d. 4 Sep 1589
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Rt. Hon. David Carnegie of Kinnaird and Eupheme Wemyss
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  (Sir) Robert Carnegie d. Dec 1632
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  Alexander Carnegie of Vayne and Balnamoon d. Oct 1657
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  Jean Carnegie
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  Katherine Carnegie
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  Agnes Carnegie d. 8 Dec 1634
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.  Eutheme Carnegie
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.  John Carnegie, 1st Earl of Northesk b. c 1569, d. 8 Jan 1667
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Carnegie_of_Colluthie?
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT David Carnegie of Colluthie (1559–1598) was a Scottish landowner and administrator.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT David Carnegie was the younger son of Sir Robert Carnegie of Kinnaird (d. 1565) and Margaret Guthrie (d. 1571). Colluthie is near Leuchars in the parish of Moonzie.[2] David Carnegie had lands at Panbride in Forfarshire. When his 
2 CONC older brother John Carnegie died in 1595, he became laird of Kinnaird.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1593, Carnegie was appointed to a committee to audit the account of money spent by the Chancellor, John Maitland of Thirlestane, on the royal voyages. The funds in question came from the English subsidy and the dowry o
2 CONC f Anne of Denmark.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an auditor of the Scottish exchequer in 1595 and joined the government finance committee known as the Octavians in 1596.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He took down the old kirk of Cookstoun to build a new church at Kinnaird.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1583 he acquired Cruivie Castle in Logie, Fife from his daughter Margaret.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT His niece Catherine Carnegie (d. 1597) was abducted with a view to a forced marriage with James Gray, a son of Patrick Gray, 5th Lord Gray, in Edinburgh in 1593. She was taken from the house of the merchant Robert Jousie by the co
2 CONC urtiers John Wemyss of Logie and Sir James Sandilands of Slamannan.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 19 April 1598.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and family
2 CONT David Carnegie married (1) Elizabeth Ramsay, the heir of Henry Ramsay of Colluthie and Leuchars who was killed at the battle of Pinkie in 1547,[10] (2) Euphame Wemyss (d. 1593), daughter of John Wemyss of Wemyss, (3) Janet Henriso
2 CONC n.[11] His children included:
2 CONT
2 CONT David Carnegie of Kinnaird, Lord Carnegie, and Earl of Southesk
2 CONT John Carnegie, Earl of Northesk
2 CONT Alexander Carnegie
2 CONT Robert Carnegie
2 CONT Katherine Carnegie, who married John Aytoun of Kinnaldie, brother of the poet Robert Aytoun
2 CONT Elizabeth Carnegie, daughter of Elizabeth Ramsay, who married John Inglis younger of Inglistarvit in 1579
2 CONT Jean Carnegie
2 CONT Margaret Carnegie (d. 1589), younger daughter of Elizabeth Ramsay, who married William Dundas of Fingask in 1582
2 CONT Agnes Carnegie, who married Alexander Falconer of Halkertoun, and was the mother of Sir Alexander Falconer, 1st Lord Falconer of Halkerton
2 CONT Euphame Carnegie, who married Robert Graham, younger of Morphie
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Carnegie-70
