1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == COLONEL THOMAS DALZIEL OF TICKNEVIN==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===He served as Captain in Colonel James Douglas's Regiment under the Prince of Orange), known as such from 1680 to 1685, and formerly as Colyear's Regiment of Scots Dutch===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Colonel Thomas Dalyell, here treated, is not the same person as https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Thomas-Dalyell-1st-Baronet-of-the-Binns/6000000008947218574, although both men were sons of https://www.geni.com/people
2 CONC /Thomas-Dalyell-of-the-Binns-Bluidy-Tam/6000000008947008540?through=6000000222300534873. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 385]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == The Question of Identity ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Colonel Thomas Dalyell, here treated, is the second son of https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Dalyell-of-the-Binns-Bluidy-Tam/6000000008947008540?through=6000000222300534873. The name of his mother has not been identified. With
2 CONC in the family he was commonly known as Black Tom. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 383]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the Binns Papers ==
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''27 September 1676: Testament of General Thomas Dalyell of Binns, in which he nominates Thomas Dalyell. his eldest son, as his sole executor and directs him to pay to Thomas Dalyell, younger, and to Charles Dalyell, the portions
2 CONC  to be paid to them under a Bond of Provision of the date of the will ; and declares that the said will shall not be prejudicial to the dispositions granted to his said eldest son, of date 27 September 1676. Dated at Binns, 27 Jul
2 CONC y 1678. Witnesses, James Easson, servant of the said General, and James May, W.S. in Edinburgh, writer of the will. (signed "T. Dalyell," and the witnesses. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54s
2 CONC cotuoft/page/72/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 372]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''17 April 1682: Marriage Contract between Captain Thomas Dalyell, younger of Binns, and Agnes Murray, widow of Mr. John Ellies, younger of Eliestoun, whereby the said Agnes assignes to the said Captain Thomas an annualrent of 18
2 CONC 00 merks on the lands of Eliestoun, lying in the lordship of Kilpunt and sheriffdom of Linlithgow, and further the said Agnes renounces any terce that may fall to her on the death of the said captain Thomas, unless he provide one 
2 CONC willingly during his lifetime. Dated at Edinburgh 17 April 1682: Witnesses, John Elies of Eliestoun, younger, John Murray, brother to the laird of Levingsloun, Captain Thomas Dalyell [Note] in Colonel James Douglas's Regiment unde
2 CONC r the Prince of Orange, James Hay, W.S., and John Gourlay his servitor (writer of the contract). (Signed) "Thomas Dalyell. Agnes Murray," and the witnesses. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54s
2 CONC cotuoft/page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 383]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note: Second son of the General. Commonly called in the family " Black Tom."'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3 August 1682: Bond or Provision by General Thomas Dalyell of Binns, wherein under a disposition to his grandchild, Thomas Dalyell, eldest son born of the marriage of Thomas Dalyell, the said General's eldest son, and the late 
2 CONC Katherine Drummond, he made his said grandson liable for the payment of these portions to the General's other children : To Thomas Dalyell, his second son 8000 merks ; to John Dalyell, his third son, 6000 merks ; to Charles Dalyel
2 CONC l, his fourth son, 4000 merks; to Marie Dalyell, his eldest daughter, 3000 merks ; to Anna Dalyell, his daughter, 3000 merks ; and to Margaret Dalyell, his daughter by Marion Abercrombie, 3000 merks. Dated at Edinburgh, 3 August 1
2 CONC 682. Witnesses, George Clellland, servant of the said General. James Hay, W.S., and Harry Hoome. his servitor. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 3
2 CONC 85]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                    3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3 August 1682: By his Disposition and Bond of Taillie of the same date General Dalyell disponed all his estates to his said grandson, and the heirs male of his body ; whom failing to the heirs female of his body ; whom failing 
2 CONC to his grandson William Dalyell, the second son born of the said marriage, and his heirs male and female ; whom failing to any person hereafter nominated by the General ; failing which nomination to his grand-daughter, Magdalen Da
2 CONC lyell, the eldest daughter born of the said marriage, and heirs male and female ; whom failing to his grand-daughter, Janet Dalyell, the youngest daughter born of the said marriage, and her heirs male and female : whom failing suc
2 CONC cessively to his second son, Thomas Dalyell, to his third son, John Dalyell, to his fourth son, Charles Dalyell, to his eldest daughter, Mary Dalyell, to his daughter, Anna Dalyell, and to his daughter, Margaret Dalyell, daughter 
2 CONC of Marion Abercrombie, and their heirs male and female. Place and witnesses as above. Registered in the Books of Council and Session, 24 March 1701. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/
2 CONC page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 385]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3 January 1685: Will of General Thomas Dalyell. I, General Thomas Dalyell of Binns, Considering that it is the deutie of all Christians so to order their affairs in their own tyme that all differences which may fall out theiran
2 CONC ent after their death may be obviat and prevented, Doe therfor being at present sick in bodie but of sound judgement and memorie Make my last Will and Testament as follows : In the first place I committ my Soule to God Almightie m
2 CONC v Creator Hoping to be saved by the merits of Jesus Christ my only Lord and Redeemer, And I desyre my freinds when it pleases God to call me to cause decentlie to interr my bodie. And for my worldlie estate I nominal Thomas Dalyel
2 CONC l, my grandchild, procreat of the marriage betwixt Thomas Dalyell, my son, and Katherine Drummond, his spouse, And failying of him be deceis the next heir of tailyie and provisione appointed to succeed after him and the aires of h
2 CONC is bodie, to my estate To be my only executor and universall Legator and intromettor with my haill goods, geir, debts, soumes of money and others perteining, or which shall happen to pertein, and be resting to me at the tym of my 
2 CONC deceis and falling under testament, To whom I leav and bequeath the same to be used and disposed upon at my said executoris pleasure. Which goods, geir, money and debts I leav to my said executor with the burden of all debts that 
2 CONC shall be resting be me at the tym of my deceis. And with the burden of the portions and provisions I have granted to Thomas Dalyell, my second sone, John Dalyell and Charles Dalyell and to Marie (here the words 'and Anna Dalyell' 
2 CONC are crossed out) my children, conform to a band daited the third of August imvic eighty two, And also with the burden of the provisions of my younger grandchildren, procreat betwixt the said Thomas my son and Katherine Drummond, A
2 CONC nd with the burden of Two thousand merks, to my neece, Mrs. Jean Drummond, and I have appointed Anna's name to be delet and hers and Margaret's out of the said band of provision. And I remitt and referre to Mr. John Arthur, minist
2 CONC er of the Gospel, and James Hay what they think to allow to my soune Thomas yearlie during his lifetyme out of his sounes estate. ..........
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/77/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 389]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''18 February 1686: Obligation by Captain Thomas Dalyell, second son of the late General Thomas Dalyell of Binns, to repay, in the event of his having no lawful children of his own, to the said General's grandson, eldest son of S
2 CONC ir Thomas Dalyell and the late Katherine Drummond, the sum of 8000 merks now paid to the said Captain Thomas under his father's Bond of Provision in No. 385. Obligation dated at Edinburgh, 18 February 1686. Written by Robert Ged a
2 CONC s in No. 397. Witnesses, Mr. John Arthour, minister of the Gospel, and the said James Hay, W.S. (Signed) "T. Dalyell," and the witnesses. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/81/mod
2 CONC e/1up The Binns Papers II: 398]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     6
2 CONT
2 CONT '''16 February 1694: Bond or Provision by General Thomas Dalyell of Binns, wherein under a disposition to his grandchild, Thomas Dalyell, eldest son born of the marriage of Thomas Dalyell, the said General's eldest son, and the la
2 CONC te Katherine Druminond, he made his said grandson liable for the payment of these portions to the General's other children : To Thomas Dalyell, the said General's second son, 8000 merks ; to John Dalyell, his third son, 6000 merks
2 CONC  ; to Charles Dalyell, his fourth son, 4000 merks; to Marie Dalyell, his eldest daughter, 3000 merks ; to Anna Dalyell, his daughter, 3000 merks ; and to Margaret Dalyell, his daughter by Marion Abercrombie, 3000 merks. Dated at E
2 CONC dinburgh, 3 August 1682. Witnesses, George Clelland, servant of the said General, James Hay, W.S., and Harry Hoome. his servitor. (Written by John Robertson, also servitor of the said James Hay (signed) " T. Dalyell," and the witn
2 CONC esses. Registered in the Sheriff Court Books of Linlithgow, 16 February 1694. [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 385]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     7
2 CONT
2 CONT '''14 March 1701: By his Disposition and Bond of Taillie of the same date General Dalyell disponed all his estates to his said grandson, and the heirs male of his body ; whom failing to the heirs female of his body ; whom failing 
2 CONC to his grandson William Dalyell, the second son born of the said marriage, and his heirs male and female ; whom failing to any person hereafter nominated by the General ; failing which nomination to his grand-daughter, Magdalen Da
2 CONC lyell, the eldest daughter born of the said marriage, and heirs male and female ; whom failing to his grand-daughter, Janet Dalyell, the youngest daughter born of the said marriage, and her heirs male and female : whom failing suc
2 CONC cessively to his second son, Thomas Dalyell, to his third son, John Dalyell, to his fourth son, Charles Dalyell, to his eldest daughter, Mary Dalyell, to his daughter, Anna Dalyell, and to his daughter, Margaret Dalyell, daughter 
2 CONC of Marion Abercrombie, and their heirs male and female. Place and witnesses as above. Registered in the Books of Council and Session, 24 March 1701.  [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft
2 CONC /page/76/mode/1up The Binns Papers I: 385]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     8
2 CONT
2 CONT '''18 July 1707: Letter from James Hamilton to Sir Thomas Dalyell . . . . . [Note 1] [https://archive.org/details/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/100/mode/1up The Binns Papers II: 491] 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note 1: Endorsed (in another hand) : Mr. Hamilton's Letter in Relation to Black Colonel Dalyells Settlement. Editors' notes : — (1) The " Black Colonel" was Colonel Thomas Dalyell of Ticnevin in Ireland [Note 2], second son of 
2 CONC General Thomas Dalyell of Binns. (2) He served as Captain in Colonel James Douglas's Regiment, known as such from 1680 to 1685, and formerly as Colyear's Regiment of Scots Dutch. [Letter from the Ross Herald (the late Mr. Andrew R
2 CONC oss), dated 17 December 1921]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note 2: Ticknevin is a townland in the parish of Carbury in County Kildare, Ireland. [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticknevin Wikipedia: Ticknevin]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 September 1743: Letter from John Lindsay in Dublin to Mrs. Magdalen Dalyell.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Madam, — The present situation of Affairs with respect to the family of Collonel Thomas Dalyell, a near relation of yours, late of the County of Kildare in this kingdom, forces me to acquaint you that pursuant to an Agreement m
2 CONC ade between the Coll. in his life time and Sir Thomas Dalyell. your good Brother (sic). Your Brother, Sir Thomas Dalyell, did by Deed by him duly executed under his Hand and Seal settle his estate in Scotland in manner following. 
2 CONC That is to say, in case in failure of Issue Male in himself. To the Issue Male of the said Coll. Thomas Dalyell, And in default of such Issue Male To the Right Heirs of the said Coll. Thomas Dalyell. In consideration whereof and p
2 CONC ursuant to the said Agreement the said Coll. Thomas Dalyell made and published his last Will and Testament in Writing under his Hand and Seal, whereby he Devised his said Estate of Inheritance in the said County of Kildare as foll
2 CONC ows.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''First he devised his said estate to his eldest son Thomas then living, and since dead without Issue, And for want of Issue Male in him to his second son John and his Issue Male, and for want of Issue Male in him to his third so
2 CONC n James and his Issue Male, and for want of such Issue, to his fourth son Charles and his Issue Male according to Seniority of age and Priority of birth, and in default of such Issue, he devised his said Estate in the County of Ki
2 CONC ldare To the Use of the said Sir Thomas Dalyell and his heirs male, and for want of Issue Male in him, To Christian Dalyell his daughter (who is my wife) and to the heirs male of her body.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sometime after the death of the said Coll. Dalyell I intermarried with the said Christian by whom I have had several children and particularly one son called Charles now about Eighteen years old, and after our Intermarriage, Jo
2 CONC hn the second son upon the death of his brother Thomas who died without Issue, being Intitled to the said Estate and being then a Minor I was obliged to become his Guardian and he having died without issue, the Estate came to Jame
2 CONC s, the third son, to whom one William Watt was appointed Guardian by the Lord Chancellor. This James during his Life was a very easy, supine, Indolent Man, subject to many Extravagancies, and led a Course of Life much beneath the 
2 CONC Dignity of the Worthy Family he descended from. And particularly for some time kept company with one Jane Doyle a woman of a very Infamous Character and Daughter to one Henry Doyle who was a tenant on the Estate and a man of a ver
2 CONC y base Character.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I must further beg leave to acquaint you that for many years from the time I intermarried with Christian the Colonel's daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT . . . . . . . . . . . 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I beg pardon for this long but necessary epistle and am Madam, your most Humble Servant. (Signed) "John Lindsay."''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Direct for me to the Hen and Chickens in Castle Street, Dublin. Addressed : — To Mrs. Magdalen Monteith, widow at her House at the Binns, or at her son's the Bart. Monteith at the Binns near Edinburgh. [https://archive.org/deta
2 CONC ils/scottishrecords54scotuoft/scottishrecords54scotuoft/page/119/mode/1up The Binns Papers II: 579]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
