2 CONT *From: http://thepeerage.com/p1100.htm#i10991
2 CONT *________________
2 CONT *''''''John Cecil, 5th Earl of Exeter (c. 1648 – 29 August 1700), known as Lord Burghley until 1678, was a British peer and Member of Parliament. He was also known as the Travelling Earl.
2 CONT *Exeter was the son of John Cecil, 4th Earl of Exeter, (1628–1678) and Lady Frances Manners. He was educated at Stamford School and St John's College, Cambridge.[1] He was elected to the House of Commons for Northamptonshire in 16
2 CONC 75, a seat he held until 1678 when he succeeded his father in the earldom and entered the House of Lords.
2 CONT *He was a notable Grand Tourist and filled his family home, Burghley House, with treasures purchased on his travels in Italy.
2 CONT *''''''Lord Exeter married Lady Anne, daughter of William Cavendish, 3rd Earl of Devonshire, in circa 1670, they had nine children. He died in August 1700 and was succeeded in his titles by his son John Cecil, 6th Earl of Exeter.
2 CONT *See also
2 CONT *List of deserters from James II to William of Orange
