1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The younger sons of David Brodie were : —
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Alexander Brodie, who became the founder of the Lethen Family, having purchased the barony of Lethen, in 1622, from Sir John Grant ; with the Abbey lands of Kinloss, from Thomas, Lord Bruce of Kinloss, also the lands of Pitgave
2 CONC nie, in 1630, fi-om Alexander Hay of Ejnudie, and also during the rule of the English in the North, the Laird of Letiiien in 1651 and 1652 sold the stones of Kinloss Abbey to be employed in building the citadel of Inverness.* Subs
2 CONC equent dilapidation for the purpose of building dykes and cottages have left the rains a sad spectacle of a venerable ecclesiastical building, apparently used as a farm-steading for keeping poultry and swine!
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Mr. John Brodie, was educated for the Church, and his name occurs as a student in King's College, Aberdeen, in 1606. He became, in 1624, on the death of his maternal grandfather, minister of Auldeme and Dean of Murray. He died,
2 CONC  as stated In his Nephew's Diary, on the 7th of January, 1655.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Mr. Joseph Brodie was also educated for the Church, in Eing^s College, Aberdeen, where he took his degree as Master of Arts, in 1620. He was instituted as minister of Keith, 27th June, 1631. He was present at the memorable Glas
2 CONC gow Assembly in 1638; and was translated to Forres, in December, 1646. He died 27th of October, 1656. The Laird of Brodie speaks of these two ministers with great affection, when they joined together, with other relations, at Leat
2 CONC hen, in January, 1654, in renewing their solemn Covenant engagements on two days set apart for private humiliation and devotional exercises.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Francis Brodie, designed of Miltoun and Inverlochtie, whose death is recorded by his nephew in 1676.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. William Brodie, designed of Coltfield, who, according to the same authority, died in 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Brodie of Brodie, the eldest son, was born in the year 1586. Before 1616 he married Katherine, daughter of Mr. Thomas Dunbar* of Grange, Dean of Murray, by Qrizzell, daughter of Sir Robert Crichton of Eliock and Cluny, and s
2 CONC ister of the Admirable Crichton.  The Laird of Brodie died 22d September, 1632, aged 46, leaving three sons and one daughter.
