1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Muster Roll (c. 1630) Results
2 CONT
2 CONT Muster Roll (c. 1630) Results
2 CONT The Muster Rolls of c. 1630 (Lieutenant William Graham's Muster) list Protestant male tenants over 16 years old who were capable of bearing arms. This is a crucial primary record and a census substitute for the period.
2 CONT
2 CONT The search results included an excerpt from the 1630 Muster Roll for the City and Liberties of Londonderry, which contains 599 names. The following similar surnames appear:
2 CONT
2 CONT COGHERAN, Robert (listed with "Sword only")
2 CONT
2 CONT COGHERAN, George (listed with "Sword only")
2 CONT
2 CONT COCHRAN, John (listed with "Sword only")
2 CONT
2 CONT COOGHERON, Robert (listed with "Sword only")
2 CONT
2 CONT Significance of the Muster Roll Findings
2 CONT Legal Authentic Record: The Muster Roll is a primary government record, not a user-created family tree, making the presence of the names legally authentic evidence of their residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Location and Date Match: The roll is from the correct time period (c. 1630) and the correct location (Londonderry).
2 CONT
2 CONT Potential Matches to Sons: The names Robert, George, and John perfectly match three of the sons listed in the online family tree data (born c. 1596, 1601, and 1607, respectively). By 1630, they would all be 23 to 34 years old, pla
2 CONC cing them squarely in the age range for the roll.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: While this does not list James, it is strong primary evidence that three men with the surname Cochran/Cogheran, matching the names and ages of his reported sons, were residents and armed Protestant settlers in the Lond
2 CONC onderry area by 1630. This confirms the family tradition laid out in the "Old COCHRAN Manuscript" regarding their move to Ulster.
2 CONT The above is from Collaboration in Family Search 
