1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == SIR JOHN SPOTTISWOOD OF DAIRSIE ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''15 November 1644: Contract between Sir John Spottiswood of Dairsie, kt, on the one part, Mr Andrew Bruce, principal, Mr George Weames [Wemyss], Mr John Sinklare [Sinclair], Mr David Nevoy [Nevay] and Mr James Schearpe [Sharp], 
2 CONC regents of St Leonard's College in St Andrews, on the second part, and Mr Archibald Wilkie, eldest son of deceased Mr James Wilkie, nephew and heir of deceased Mr Robert Wilkie, sometime principal of said College, on the third par
2 CONC t, narrating Contract dated 1 June 1598, between deceased James Lermounthe [Learmonth] of Dairsie, with consent of deceased William Lermounthe, his eldest son and apparent heir, and the said deceased Robert Wilkie, whereby for £20
2 CONC 00 the said deceased James Lermounthe, with consent foresaid, bound himself to infeft said deceased Robert Wilkie in an annualrent of £200 furth of the lands of Dairsie, Instrument of Sasine following thereon, and Charter of Confi
2 CONC rmation thereof by the deceased George, Archbishop of St Andrews, in which annualrent the said deceased Mr James Wilkie was infeft on 13 August 1611 as heir to the said deceased Robert Wilkie, his uncle, following on precept of cl
2 CONC are constat by the deceased Sir John Lermounthe of Beltoune [Belton], kt, and also narrating contract, dated 4 December 1627, between the principal and regents of St Leonard's College and the said deceased James Wilkie whereby the
2 CONC  said annualrent, with the arrears thereof from 1717 to 1726, was given to the said principal and regents to found six bursaries in the said College thus fulfilling the desire of the said deceased Robert Wilkie, and decreet dated 
2 CONC 16 November 1643, obtained by said Mr Andrew Bruce, principal, and regents of said College, and said Mr Archibald Wilkie against said Sir John Spottiswood of Dairsie, kt, Dame Marie Irving, his spouse, and John Spottiswood, their 
2 CONC eldest son, for apprising of the lands of Dairsie for payment of principal sum and arrears of annualrent now amounting to 10,000 merks, and by the present contract the said Sir John binds himself to pay an annualrent of 700 merks 
2 CONC from said lands, the money to be used to provide seven bursaries in said College. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD59%2f169&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=john+spottiswood&ko=p&r=&ro=s&d
2 CONC f=&dt=1650&di=y National Records of Scotland, Wilkie of Foulden Writs, reference GD59/169]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir John Spottiswoode, who, upon his father's resignation had a charter written in Latin, 1616. He was appointed one of the gentlemen of the bed-chamber to the king, when a young man, and had the honor of knighthood conferred u
2 CONC pon him, by his majesty, which appears by another charter under the great seal, June 5, 1621.  Sir John had only one son, John Spottiswoode, Esq., a youth of  extraordinary parts, a most noble spirit, and a staunch loyalist, who h
2 CONC aving joined the great Montrose, was taken prisoner with him, and executed immediately after him, anno 1650, in the flower of his age, to the great grief of all beholders and every body else who knew him. His father Sir John, died
2 CONC  also before the restoration of King Charles II, and having no surviving issue, the representation devolved on the children of his brother, Sir Robert, before mentioned.'''
