2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1677
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir John, sixth of Dunipace</strong>, married Annabella Young, succeeded his father in 1634, and that same year sold the estate to Sir Robert Spottiswoode. He is said to have had a daughter, Elizabeth, who married David B
2 CONC arclay of Mathers, who obliged to sell his estate in consequence of having become surety for the debts of the Livingstone family."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/landslairdsoflar00gibs#page/96/mode/2up Lands and lairds of Larbert and Dunipace parishes], 1908, page 96
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ir John Livingston III aka 6th Lord of Dunipace
2 CONT Born about 1613 in Falkirk, Stirlingshire, SCT
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of David Livingston and Barbara (Forrester) Livingston
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Margaret Livingston [half], William Livingston [half], Alexander Livingston [half] and David Livingston	
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Annabella Abigail Young — married about 1644 in New Kent, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of John Livingston IV and George Livingston	
2 CONT
2 CONT Died after 1704 in King and Queen Co VA
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir David Livingston, 5th of Dunipace, married Barbara Forrester, sister to Sir James Forrester of Garden. Abt 1613 Sir David and Barbara had a son, John Livingston. John married Annabella Young.
2 CONT
2 CONT A DNA study APPEARS TO CONFIRM Sir John Livingston is the immigrant ancestor of the documented Livingston line which became established in the southern American colonies prior to the founding of the USA. Historical Documentation
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1606 King James granted a joint charter to two companies to settle Virginia. One of the original investors in the London Company, which was authorized to settle southern Virginia, was Sir John Levison (Livingston). This John Li
2 CONC vingston is believed to be Livingston-986, the father of Sir David Livingston (Livingstone-688) and grandfather of John (Livingstone-670).
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 3, 1633, King James granted 1/5 of marsh of Sir Henry Watton and Sir Edward Dymock to Sir John Livingstone. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1634 Sir David Livingstone died, and John succeeded his father as Sir John, 6th of Dunipace. Sir David had amassed enormous debts before his death and John was forced to sign an agreement with his father's creditors that grante
2 CONC d him one year to come up with the owed money or forfeit his inheritance. Apparently he failed to do this as in 1635 the lands of Dunipace were granted to Sir Robert Spotswood.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 9, 1641, there was a lawsuit involving Sutton Marsh, William Wise, and Sir James Livingstone over 3,500 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1650, Wingfield Webb & Richard Pate transported 23 persons into tidewater Virginia. One passenger was John Livingston. They gave him 2 receipts for which they had not collected headrights. (A headright consisted of 50 
2 CONC acres) They were given a headright for each person they brought into Virginia. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The road which turns off to the left, coming up the county, at Plainview, leads to the plantations which were along Poropotank Creek. This road was close to the line of the Lewis Plantation in 1716. The straight road that continue
2 CONC s to the creek bisects a tract which was owned by the Andersons. This land was bought of John Beverly Whiting, from the early Lewis plantation, from the Livingstons and Carys. John Livingston had been in Virginia but a short time 
2 CONC when he had possession of this land (See description below), for his name appears as a headright of Richard Pate in 1650. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1650 the population of Virginia was estimated at 20,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1652-1654 (approximately), John Livingston II was born in New Kent County.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1653, John Livingston I was granted 400 acres on west side of Poropotank Creek behind the land of John Thomas. [5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT John Livingston transported 6 persons (Anne Silke, Jno Backster, Barbary Scott, Ralph Bottock, James ______, Joane _____, Rose Allen and William Lamb) and added to the 2 receipts from Webb & Pate, he was able to acquire 400 acres.
2 CONC  John found an ideal location - by water. There was access to the Chesapeake Bay and all the great rivers that gave ingress to the inland ports of Virginia. Down the coastline was Jamaica with its lucrative trade in sugar and rum.
2 CONC  One had only to sail around the southern tip of Maryland to head for the northern markets and the wide Hudson with its prosperous harbor. [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1654, New Kent County was created. The sliver of land on the northwest banks of Poropotank Creek (where the Livingstons settled) was a part of York County. New Kent was created mostly out of York County, but had that sliver of 
2 CONC land formerly in Gloucester County (with the Livingston plantation) included in what lands it got. [8][9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1660, Charles II comes to power in England with the overthrow of Cromwell. British Parliament promptly passes new Navigation Act requiring all Virginia trade to pass through English ports with payment of high duties. The move d
2 CONC evastates the tobacco trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664, John Livingston transported 2 more people to add 50 acres adjacent to the 400 he already had. [11]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664, John Livingston I added another 452 (or 453) acres to his first tract. This land of Livingston's was stated to be three miles from the York River. (Old New Kent County, page 271, 271) Land patent book no. 5, page 348: Joh
2 CONC n Leviston, 453 acres, New Kent County, 30 August 1664 on the northwest side of Poropotank Creek adjoining Ty Thomas' land running southwest by south & _____ to Timothy Lond Dell's (Lowdell) land and 400 acres granted him 16 Decem
2 CONC ber 1653, and 53 acres for transporting two persons: Joane Brown and Henry Prat. [12]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1669, John Livingston held the following land patent:
2 CONT
2 CONT         "Mr. John Leviston 780 acres New Kent County 5 November, 1669. Three hundred eight acres on the northeast side and near the road to the mill adjacent Mr. John Lewis to Cattail Branch, a branch of Mattasup Creek Swamp and a
2 CONC djacent land where sd Leviston now liveth, crossing the path to the church, etc. Four hundred acres was granted him 16 December 1653, and three hundred eighty acres for the transporting of eight persons, John Freegrave, George Hun
2 CONC t, Abra. Mayes, Wm. Turner, Jone Wilkins, Peter Arrowes, Mary Wise, Jno Neaves. [13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1670 the population of Virginia was estimated to be 40,000 (about double the amount listed for 1650).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 June 1670, John Leviston (Livingston) was appointed with John Harwell and Captain Wm. Jones to appraise the estate of Richard Roberts. [14]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1691 King and Queen County was created out of part of New Kent County in order to honor the new Joint Regents of thre throne of England, William of Orange and Mary, who took over after William's army defeated Charles II in East
2 CONC ern Ireland in 1690. [15]
2 CONT
2 CONT April 29, 1693: John William of King and Queen County petitioned for 710 acres escheated land on north side of York River near Poropotank Creek, adjoining lands of Roger Shackelford, John Major and John Livingstone (Levistone). [1
2 CONC 6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1699 the College of William and Mary was founded at Middle Plantation (Williamsburg). The seat of government was moved to Middle Plantation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1704, John Livingston was charged with two tracts: one of 600 acres and one of 750 acres, which he had bought of Sowell. The lines of the Lewis land given in his patent for 1664 fixes the Livingston land next to Lewis, on Porop
2 CONC otank Creek. [17]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704: Quit Rent rolls for King & Queen County list Sam'l Livingston 800 acres) and John Livingston. [18] 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sources
2 CONT
