1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Genealogical Account of the Barclays of Urie; for Upwards of Seven Hundred Years: With Memoirs of Colonel David Barclay, and His Son Robert Barclay-also Letters that Passed Between Him, the Duke of York ... and Ot
2 CONC her Distinguished Characters":   https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=vTsWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA26&lpg=PA26&dq=robert+barclay+of+urie&source=bl&ots=cEI-xn-Bld&sig=Dv6J5Yf4yHLDdpXGy8fRTUvIUbE&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwij7tTPzfLMAhWHKsAKHVsyBWEQ
2 CONC 6AEISDAH#v=onepage&q=robert%20barclay%20of%20urie&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Barclay_%28Quaker%29 David Barclay] (Quaker) From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. David Barclay (1610 – October 12, 1686), of Mathers, St Cyrus, Kincardineshire in Scotland, was 1st Laird of Urie near Stonehaven, Kincardineshire, Scotland, and father of Robert Barclay, the eminent Quaker apologist. Followi
2 CONC ng active service in Europe as a professional soldier under Gustavus Adolphus, King of Sweden, he attained the rank of Colonel and fought in the English Civil War on the side of the King. In 1647 he purchased the lands and barony 
2 CONC of Urie in Kincardineshire from William Keith, 7th Earl of Marischal.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a known associate of the Earl Marischal he was subsequently confined in Edinburgh Castle where he was converted to Quakerism in 1665 by the celebrated laird of Swinton, who was confined in the same prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. David Barclay was the son of David Barclay (1580–1660), 11th of Mathers , of Kincardineshire, Scotland and Elizabeth Livingstone, daughter of John Livingston of Dunnipace.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Katherine Gordon, daughter of Sir Robert Gordon (1580–1656), 1st Baronet., founder of Gordonstoun School (NO, it was founded in 1934)
2 CONT  and Louisa Gordon, daughter of John Gordon of Glenluce, Dean of Salisbury on 24 Dec 1647 in Kincardine.
2 CONT
2 CONT By Katherine he had two daughters, Lucy and Jean, and three sons, Robert, John, and David.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy died unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean married Sir Ewen Cameron of Lochiel, to whom she bore eight children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, the eldest son, who became celebrated as the apologist for the Quakers.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, the second son, settled in East Jersey in America, where he married and left issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT David died unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of the Surname Barclay
2 CONT
2 CONT Further reading
2 CONT
2 CONT ODNB article by Brian M. Halloran, ‘Barclay, Robert (1611/12–1682)’, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, Oxford University Press, 2004 [1], accessed 3 Dec 2007.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir David Barclay, Baron of Callairnie and Third Bailie of Lindores, Callairnie Castle, Fife, Scotland, and of Urie in Kincadine, and other lands, united them into the Barony of Urie. His oldest son, Robert, was appointed by the K
2 CONC ing as Governor of East Jersey in 1683. Sir David's wife, mother of Robert, was Lady Katherine Gordon, known as "The White Rose of Scotland", to whom he was married Dec. 24, 1647.
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. David Barclay of Urie, who purchased the estate in 1648 from William, Earl Marishal. He served with dis-tinction in the army of Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden, and in the civil wars at home. In 1666 he joined the Quak-ers, and s
2 CONC uffered persecution, imprisonment, and various indignities on account of his religious beliefs. He is de-scribed as a "proper, tall, personage of a man as could be seen among thousands. His hair was white as flax, but quite bald u
2 CONC pon the top of his head, which obliged him to wear commonly a black satin cap under his hat". He died in 1686, and was buried 12th Oct. in a place selected by himself upon his grounds about a mile to the northwest of Urie.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Barclay_%28Quaker%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. David Barclay (1610 – October 12, 1686), of Mathers, St Cyrus, Kincardineshire in Scotland, was 1st Laird of Urie near Stonehaven, Kincardineshire, Scotland, and father of Robert Barclay, the eminent Quaker apologist. Followi
2 CONC ng active service in Europe as a professional soldier under Gustavus Adolphus, King of Sweden, he attained the rank of Colonel and fought in the English Civil War on the side of the King. In 1647 he purchased the lands and barony 
2 CONC of Urie in Kincardineshire from William Keith, 7th Earl of Marischal.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a known associate of the Earl Marischal he was subsequently confined in Edinburgh Castle where he was converted to Quakerism in 1665 by the celebrated laird of Swinton, who was confined in the same prison. Family
2 CONT --------------------
