2 CONT DEACON CALEB FOBES FORBES
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Fobes-37
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 3 Jan 2021 | Created 30 Jul 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb Fobes Sr (abt. 1655 - 1710)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Caleb Fobes Sr aka Forbes===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1655 in Bridgewater, Plymouth, Massachusetts, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of John Fobes and Constant (Mitchell) Briggs
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Edward Fobes, William Fobes and Elizabeth (Forbes) Russell
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Mary Huntington — married [date unknown] [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Sarah (Gager) Fobes — married 30 Jun 1681 in Norwich, New London, Connecticut, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Sarah (Fobes) Bishop, Caleb Forbes, Mary (Fobes) Gates and John Fobes
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 25 Aug 1710 in Preston City, New London, Connecticut, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon Caleb Fobes of Norwich and Preston, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Frances Manwaring Caulkins in her History of Norwich, Connecticut refers to Caleb as "Forbes", I have yet to find a single instance in the town or church records where it is spelled with an "r". Generally, it is spelled f
2 CONC fobes. The double "ff" is used to distinguish it from "f" which is actually our modern "s".
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:
2 CONT Caleb Fobes was the son of John and Constant (Mitchell) Fobes of Duxbury and Bridgewater, Mass., where it is assumed that he was born, about 1655, which seems to be the currently accepted date. I would put it a few years earlier, 
2 CONC because he owned land in Norwich in 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages 1st: Sarah Gager, daughter of John Gager of Norwich. They were married June 30, 1681 in Norwich.[1] Caleb is listed in his father-in-laws will dated 1695, although John Gager didn’t die until 1703. 2nd: Married Marah by 
2 CONC 1699. An entry in the Preston Church records establishes his marriage to Marah before Jan 1699. It lists Marah the wife of Caleb Fobes and others as having "adjoined by renewal of their covenant" on the 1st of Jan 1699.[2] It is b
2 CONC elieved by many researchers that the surname of Marah was Huntington.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: He died August 25, 1710 in Norwich.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb was the owner of land in Norwich as early as 1672. In 1684 and 1685 he was constable east of the river. He was one of the petitioners for the incorporation of the town of Preston in Oct 1686. He is named among the fifteen la
2 CONC ndholders on the Plantation Act, dated Jan. 15, 1686 and became a settled inhabitant of the new town. He had several grants of land from Owaneco, chief of the Mohegans, one of which dated Dec 10, 1683, was for 110 acres of upland 
2 CONC and meadow “south of Connoughtug brook.”[4] He was one of the first twelve members and a deacon of the Preston Congregational Church. Caleb was frequently elected selectman or townsman held the office of town treasurer for many ye
2 CONC ars, and was often called upon to lay out lands and highways and to transact other public business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death his estate was inventoried at £625. He left a widow, Marah/Mary, and five children, Sarah, Caleb, Mary, John, and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children, so far as known, were
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah, b. in Norwich June 24, 1684;[1]< m. Samuel Bishop Jan. 2, 1705/6.[1] Caleb Fobes b. about 1688 (1728-40); d. May 7, 1728; m., May 21, 1713, Abigail Gates, who d. July 10, 1774, having borne (i) Abigail, b. May 25, 1714, (2)
2 CONC  Joshua, b. Sept. 22, 1715, (3) Sarah, b. Sept. 13, 1717, m. Zebulon Walbridge, (4) Caleb, b. June 20, 1719, m. Edith Walbridge, (5) Simon, b. Feb. 2, 1721-2, (6) Bethiah, b. Feb. 2, 1725-6.[5] Mary, m., June 6, 1716 Caleb Gates (
2 CONC both of Preston), and had ten children.[5] John Ffobes, d. Feb. 18, 1738/9, m., Jan. 14, 1718/9, Ruth Bruster (Brewster), had eleven children.[5] Elizabeth.[5] Because there are no records of Sarah's death, Caleb's marriage to Mar
2 CONC ah, or birth records for the children after Sarah, it is difficult to determine which of these children belong to which wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ 1.0 1.1 1.2 Vital Records of Norwich, 1659-1848, Part I. Hartford, Society of Colonial Wars in the State of Connecticut, 1913. ↑ 2.0 2.1 First Congregational Church of Preston, Connecticut 1698-1898. Together with statistics of 
2 CONC the church taken from church records. Preston, Conn.: The Society, 1900. ↑ Torrey, Clarence Almon. New England Marriages Prior to 1700. Baltimore, MD.: Genealogical Publishing Co., 2004. TEXT AS FOLLOWS: Caleb (-1710) & Mary/Marah
2 CONC  [HUNTINGTON?] (1657-1710+); by 1699; Preston, CT {Backus Anc. 91 (Forbes); Hale (1952) 651; Preston Ch. Rec. 129; TAG 11:105; Granberry 218; Brainerd Anc. 169}. ↑ See Preston, Connecticut Deeds 1687-1736. at New London County CTG
2 CONC enWeb http://www.ctgenweb.org/county/conewlondon/deeds/PrestonDeeds1.html ↑ 5.0 5.1 5.2 5.3 Descendants of Henry Wallbridge, who married Ann Amos, December 25th, 1688, at Preston, Conn, with some notes on the allied families of Br
2 CONC ush, Fassett, Dewey, Fobes, Gager, Lehman, Meech, Stafford. Scott (1898]). Wallbridge, William Gedney (compiler) Litchfield, Conn. 1898 Caulkins, Frances Manwaring. History of Norwich, Conn. From its possession by the Indians to t
2 CONC he Year 1866. Friends of the Author, 1874. First Congregational Church of Preston, Connecticut, 1698-1898 : together with statistics of the church taken from church records.. Preston, Conn.: The Society, 1900 p 84,85 Descendants o
2 CONC f Henry Wallbridge, who married Ann Amos, December 25th, 1688, at Preston, Conn, with some notes on the allied families of Brush, Fassett, Dewey, Fobes, Gager, Lehman, Meech, Stafford. Scott (1898]). Wallbridge, William Gedney (co
2 CONC mpiler) Litchfield, Conn. 1898 Vital Records of Norwich, 1659-1848, Part I. Hartford, Society of Colonial Wars in the State of Connecticut, 1913. Roberts, Gary B., and William A. Reitwiesner. American ancestors and cousins of the 
2 CONC Princess of Wales: the New England, Mid-Atlantic, and Virginia forebears, near relatives, and notable distant kinsmen, through her American great-grandmother, of Lady Diana Frances Spencer, now Her Royal Highness the Princess of W
2 CONC ales. Baltimore: Genealog. Publ. Co., 1984. Print. Find a grave memorial No stone, no sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/126209968/caleb-forbes
2 CONT
2 CONT Aged 57 years, the son of John Forbes and Constant Mitchell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb married 30 June 1681 in Norwich, New London County, Connecticut to SARAH GAGER, born February 1651 in New London, and died about 1702, age 50 years. Sarah was a daughter of John Gager. John was born 25 May 1620 in Little Wal
2 CONC dingfield, Suffolk County England and died 10 Dec 1703 in Norwich, New London, Connecticut, age 83, the son of William Gager (1590/2 Eng-1630 MA), a Surgeon. John Gager married before 1647 in England to Elizabeth Gore (1618- )
2 CONT
2 CONT Caleb Fobes was a Constable on the east side of Norwich, Connecticut in 1684-85; First Deacon in Church of Preston; Selectman; Town Treasurer; Committee on Land and Highways; was a signer of the Preston, Connecticut petition to th
2 CONC e Legislature for incorporation of the town in October 1686 and named among the fifteen landholders on the Plantation Act, dated 15 January 1686, on record at Preston. He was a Connecticut volunteer in the Narragansett Campaign of
2 CONC  King Philip's [Indian] War and an original proprietor of Voluntown, Ct. At the time of his death, his estate was valued at $3,125.
2 CONT
2 CONT A qualifying ancestor for the Society of Colonial Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Caleb Fobes and Sarah Gager:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Sarah Fobes (1684-1759).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Caleb Fobes (ii) (1689-1728).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Mary Fobes (1694- ).
