1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Life summary===
2 CONT
2 CONT John 'of Brookeborough' Armstrong was born in 1642, probably in Ederney, County Fermanagh.  He was one of several sons of William Armstrong of Hightower, the original Armstrong immigrant from Scotland to County Fermanagh.  His fat
2 CONC her was a Captain in the British Army under James I, in 1641.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no question that John had many male siblings, some older than him; there are marriage records we can reference today which demonstrate that by 1685, eastern County Fermanagh was full of Armstrongs.  We know for certain of
2 CONC  one brother (Francis) who emigrated to Maryland, as well, and there are many Armstrongs who likely came from County Fermanagh who are currently bereft of accurate placement within the County Fermanagh families.  DNA and Irish nam
2 CONC ing traditions give us some clues, however, as to who was a part of John's family, and who was not.
2 CONT
2 CONT First, and most importantly, John married Jennet Corry in Enniskillen on August 13, 1667.  She was the daughter of Matthew Corry and Christine Kincaid.  He settled near the town of of Brookeborough, which is considerably to the so
2 CONC utheast of Enniskillen and close enough to County Tyrone for some of his descendants to settle on the border.  The church that was used for these eastern Armstrongs was the main church of the Diocese of Clogher.  The marriage reco
2 CONC rds for this church for Armstrongs begins in 1702, and is a valuable resource.
2 CONT
2 CONT From both accounts of children that immigrated, as well as inference from Clogher records and DNA, as well as pedigree information (some accurate, most not), these are the children of John and Jennet:
2 CONT
2 CONT # William, b. c. 1669.  William married Jane Gaffney and raised their family in County Fermanagh.  Eventually he and Jane emigrated to the New World in 1734, along with son William, son William's wife Mary Caldwell, brother James,
2 CONC  and James's wife Janey, and two of their children (William and Jennet).
2 CONT # Matthew, b. c. 1670.  Matthew's wife was Catherine Martin.  Matthew did not emigrate, as far as we can tell; he settled in the east of Fermanagh and his children extensively intermarried with a Johnston family there headed by Sa
2 CONC muel Johnston.  Most of those marriages were recorded at Clogher.  Many of Matthew's children did emigrate to Pennsylvania as well, but they are relatively unknown.
2 CONT # Christine, b. c. 1673.  I have no evidence that Christine ever married.
2 CONT # Isabella, b. c. 1675.  Isabella married William Campbell, and the couple moved to Asbury, County Fermanagh.
2 CONT # Jennet, b.c. 1678.  Jennet married an unknown McGuire; we have two of their sons identified - William, and Philip.
2 CONT # Margaret, b. c. 1681.  Margaret married William Forster, and the marriage was recorded at Clogher.
2 CONT # John 'Joseph', b. 1683.  John married Susanna Johnson in about 1704 and settled at "Longfields", which is near Five Mile Town, County Tyrone.  John did not emigrate but almost all of his children did.
2 CONT # James, b. c. 1685.  James married Jane around 1711, and there were at least four children.  Two of those children, William and Jennet, accompanied James and "Janey" to Pennsylvania in 1734 along with brother William and part of 
2 CONC his family.
2 CONT # Joseph, b. 1686.  Joseph married Mary unknown around 1728, and emigrated to Philadelphia in 1731.  The evidence that Joseph was a son of John is because his second son Simon married Jane Armstrong a granddaughter of his brother 
2 CONC Matthew, in Philadelphia.  The marriage otherwise wouldn't make sense; Jane was a resident of Bedminster, Bucks County, at the time.  There is also a hint that both Simon and Jane had renounced Presbyterianism and joined the Quake
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT # Mary, b. c. 1687.  Married Robert Simpson, stayed in Ireland
2 CONT # Thomas, b. 1689.  Thomas married twice; his second wife was Dorothy Johnston, and the marriage was recorded at Clogher.  Thomas and his family emigrated to Dumfriesshire, Scotland.
2 CONT # Unknown, b. c. 1690, the wife of John McClure.  One of her children, John "William" McClure, relocated to Londonderry, and at least one of his children emigrated to the US.  Further research may yield the given name of John's mo
2 CONC ther.
2 CONT # Andrew, b. c. 1692.  Andrew married Dorcas Finlay; the marriage was recorded at Clogher.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note: I have removed Archibald from this family because no marriage record for him or later history for him was found, and worse, he seems to have been borrowed from an Archibald who emigrated to Delaware from County Fermanagh 
2 CONC with different parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Warning: the above family summary is subject to change as more information becomes available and more research is done.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Analysis===
2 CONT
2 CONT The Armstrong pedigree (attached as a source) acknowledges the existence of John of Brookeborough, and probably correctly attributes him to father William of Hightower, supposedly the original Armstrong settler of County Fermanagh
2 CONC .  It lists some sons, but these seem to be wrong for the most part, e.g. Martin, who died in battle in 1689, could not realistically have been the son of John because we DO have a solid marriage date for him: August 13, 1667, whe
2 CONC n he married Jeanette Corry.  (This date I do not have an independent source for from the Clogher records, so I presume it comes from Enniskillen church records, which are not available on line in full.)
2 CONT
2 CONT John's first children therefore are not at all described by pedigree; we have to look at other sources for that.  Those sources include immigration accounts, DNA, and consistent existing trees.  The immigration account of William 
2 CONC Armstrong, which by Irish naming tradition would have been John's first son, gives us some evidence, albeit slim, that we have the right parents for him.  Specifically:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''William arrived in America in 1734 with wife Jane Gaffney, Brother James & wife Janey, Niece Jenny, Nephew William, and son William age 22 & his wife Mary Caldwell age 19.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Source-Citations include WLA Book, added after the history was written in 1901.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Source-Chron-368, Margaret Armstrong of Mason Texas; NSSAR 93, Price.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Source-From the Rutledge Files. The family settled in Augusta Co., Va.and resided there a few years, then William moved with his wife, to South Carolina and resided in Honea Path, Anderson Co., S.C. until his death in1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT Irish naming traditions would be satisfied if "niece Jenny" was the second-born daughter of "brother James".  It nevertheless has implications, because it means that James left behind older children - at least two - who had either
2 CONC  died or married in Fermanagh.  We don't know who they were, but their names would have been John and whoever William of Hightower married.  Similar logic can been applied to William, since we are pretty certain he would have been
2 CONC  the first born son by naming tradition, and yet he was traveling without his eldest son, and possibly without his second son as well.  (William would be the name of the second or third son - John was the first, the second would h
2 CONC ave been the father of Jane Gaffney.  If that was also William, then the second son would have been William, and not the third.)  Marriage records hint that he may also have been without his eldest daughter, because there is a Joh
2 CONC nston/Armstrong marriage that could realistically belong nowhere else that probably represented her.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Johnston family and the Armstrong family both appear heavily in the Clogher, County Tyrone, marriage license records.  Clogher is on the border of easternmost County Fermanagh and northernmost County Monaghan, and not too far 
2 CONC from Brookeborough, so it seems likely that some of John's children, and especially his grandchildren, who may have settled farther east, would have married there.  And that too would hold for the Johnstons, who had settled (based
2 CONC  on their marriages) between Clogher and Clones, Monaghan, a bit to the south.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Johnston/Armstrong marriages form the Johnstons into a coherent family, and it is likely that they married early children of John because of both familial association and locational association.  It is tempting to attribute th
2 CONC e Armstrongs that married Johnstons all to one family of Armstrongs.  Although that doesn't quite work for all such marriages, because of dates and one duplicated name, it does work out remarkably well and gives us a clue as to wh
2 CONC o John of Brookeborough's second son might have been: Matthew.  Matthew's third son was named Matthew and he married a Johnston at Clogher (see sources attached to https://www.geni.com/people/Matthew-Armstrong/6000000191442021886 
2 CONC ).  I have guessed at the marriage for eldest son John based solely on dates, but the second son https://www.geni.com/people/William-Armstrong/6000000007791959267 we have more to go on: there is a marriage record, as well as evide
2 CONC nce that William and his Johnston wife emigrated to Bedminster, Bucks County, Pennsylvania, along with an extended family group that included his likely sister Rebecca Hughes, many of his children (but once again, not all), and tw
2 CONC o of his nephews, apparently from another Armstrong/Johnston marriage.  So we can pretty confidently claim that Matthew was the third son, and his father would have been the second son, born c. 1671.  (That, by the way, hints stro
2 CONC ngly that Jeanette Corry's father's name was Matthew, and that how it got incorporated into the Armstrongs in the first place.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The third son, John Joseph of Five Mile Town, Fermanagh, is documented elsewhere apparently (? I don't have the original reference) as being a son of John of Brookeborough.  It is probable he was called "John" and not "John Joseph
2 CONC " but this I cannot confirm.  He was, however, the third son and therefore was named after his father.  Many of his known children who emigrated to the US described him as their father, which is how we know about him.  John himsel
2 CONC f never emigrated, and there is strong DNA evidence that he's my direct Armstrong ancestor (through many relationships that converge on him and his wife Susanna, who I believe is a Martin, also based on DNA evidence).  John of Fiv
2 CONC e Mile Town's first-born son married Jane Crozier (we have the record from Clogher) and emigrated to Morristown, New Jersey, around 1742.  Strong DNA evidence ties this John with father John Joseph through multiple descendants of 
2 CONC the Morristown family, and the naming of the Morristown family is perfectly consistent with parents John and Jane, and with grandparents as stated.
2 CONT
2 CONT We've already discussed son James, who was born c. 1685, who emigrated to the US along with first son William.  I suspect, but cannot yet prove, that James and his wife Janey settled in Paxtang, Dauphin County, PA, and later heade
2 CONC d to western Pennsylvania.  His two known children that came with them, Jenny and William, likely married in Pennsylvania and their subsequent fate is unknown, at least by me.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: The Paxtang Armstrongs have been assumed by many to be a single family.  I think it is more likely than not that this is untrue.  For the Armstrongs, the family groups consisted of aunts and uncles, siblings, as well as clo
2 CONC se cousins.  It would be a worthwhile exercise re-examining the Paxtang Armstrongs in this light.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Next up for children we have two later children that come from the Clogher marriage records, and thus we cannot guarantee parentage, although later naming choices hint that they are placed correctly.  Margaret Armstrong was born a
2 CONC round 1681, if her marriage record is any indication, and she married William Forster at Clogher in 1702.  And Andrew Armstrong, b.c. 1690, married Dorcas Finlay in 1715.  The name Andrew also appears later as a son of John Joseph
2 CONC  Armstrong of Five Mile Town, and there seems to have been a daughter of John Joseph who also married a Finlay - Rachel Armstrong.  For that reason I believe Andrew is properly placed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources whose basis I am unaware of state that John had a son Thomas Armstrong, who was a military officer.  If that is true, I have found that he married in Clogher as well, to Dorothy Johnston, in 1727.  The Johnston assoc
2 CONC iation does convince me that the placement is correct here as well.  He emigrated to Dumfriesshire, Scotland, as did Archibald Armstrong, who married Ann Armstrong (whose Armstrong parentage is unknown).  Both of the Scottish immi
2 CONC grants need work as to their life in Scotland and children, although both marriages were recorded at Clogher.
