1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/James-of-Paxtang-Armstrong-l/6000000191471950835
2 CONT
2 CONT as of October 2021, there is no proof for the origins of https://www.geni.com/people/Brig-General-John-Armstrong-Sr-Continental-Army/6000000000889006665?through=6000000032191758008.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Armstrong''' was born About 1695 in Brooksboro Parish, Fermanagh, Ulster, Ireland, and died there on 1 May 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT > “Here lies the body of James Armstrong, son to Edward, also son to Margaret, who died Sunday, May 1745, aged 50 years.3“
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1645: In this year died James Armstrong of Brook boro. He was son of Edward from the Border and grandson of Christie's Will (see 1650). His sons Edward of Terwinney, George, William, John, and his daughter Margaret went to Pennsyl
2 CONC vania, his son Andro and another daughter, Mrs. Graydon, re- mained in Fermanagh. He is buried in Agahvea, County Fermanagh, Ireland. The inscription on the stone could be read in 1896. (Terwinney Records. Agahvea Monument. See 16
2 CONC 30. Letter from Ann Buchannan to General John Armstrong, dated May loth, 1809. Records of John Armstrong Herman, Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Armstrong, James Lewis. ''Chronicles of the Armstrongs'' (New York: The Marion Press, 1902).  Page 329-330.  < [https://archive.org/details/chroniclesofarms00arms/page/329/mode/2up?view=theater Archive.Org] >
2 CONT
2 CONT The following statement was made by Edward Arm- strong, the present possessor of Terwinney:
2 CONT
2 CONT >”Edward from the Border, son of Christie's Will, came from Brookboro to Terwinney about 1650 to take possession of land that was left either to him or his father for military service. After clearing the land and building the hous
2 CONC e which is standing to-day, he went to Brookboro, the place of the first homestead.  His son, '''James of Brookboro''', succeeded him in the possession of Terwinney. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >James of Brookboro had the following children of whom we know: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # Edward of Terwinney, 
2 CONT # Andro, 
2 CONT # a sister who married Lieutenant Graydon, 
2 CONT # John, afterwards Major-General John Armstrong of Pennsylvania, [disputed] 
2 CONT # William, and 
2 CONT # George. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >John's oldest brother, who was heir-apparent to Terwinney, died in 1744, fighting in Pennsylvania, leaving a young son in Ireland called Gentle James. <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT >James of Brookboro, the father of Gen- eral John Armstrong, '''died at Terwinney in 1745''', and was buried at Agahvea, where the inscription upon the monument erected to his memory may still be easily discerned. Upon this slab i
2 CONC s the complete and correct coat-of-arms of the family. <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT >The boy Gentle James be- came heir to Terwinney, Mrs. Graydon, his aunt, became his guardian, and she and her husband moved to Ter- winney. When Gentle James became of age he came into full possession of Terwinney, and Lieutenant
2 CONC  and Mrs. Graydon moved away soon afterwards. They are also buried in Agahvea, where their graves and the monument with its inscription may still be seen.  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://gardnerlibrary.org/journal/family-john-armstrong-sr-1717-1795-carlisle-pennsylvania “The Family of John Armstrong, Sr. (1717-1795) of Carlisle, Pennsylvania”] Journal Issue: 1991 Winter, Volume 8, Issue 2 Author: Bell, Ra
2 CONC ymond Martin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT AIthough the record of John Armstrong, Senior, is fairly complete,1 and biographies of his sons James and John are available because, like their father, they both served as Congressman,2 that of his wife and her family, his father
2 CONC , brothers and sisters are sketchy. This paper undertakes an examination of the family with emphasis on those members.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his tombstone in the Old Carlisle Cemetery, John was born in 1717 at Brookeborough, County Fermanagh, in the Ulster portion of Ireland. His father was James Armstrong; his mother's name is not known. In 1895 one James
2 CONC  Lewis Armstrong copied a gravestone inscription in Aghavea Parish Cemetery, one mile from Brookeborough:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Here lies the body of James Armstrong, son to Edward, also son to Margaret, who died Sunday, May 1745, aged 50 years.3“
2 CONT
2 CONT It may well represent the grave of the father of Colonel John Armstrong of Carlisle, who seems to have had five brothers and five sisters:
2 CONT
2 CONT BROTHERS AND SISTERS
2 CONT *1. Edward, the oldest, named for his grandfather, died in Ireland before his father, James. Some descendants came to America.4
2 CONT * 2. James, who died .in Juniata County, Pennsylvania c. 1774, was a private in the militia company captained by James Patterson in 1758. Indians captured his wife and two children on 27 January 1756, but she was released a year l
2 CONC ater at Fort Augusta.5 After his death by drowning, letters of administration were issued 23 April 1774. The younger of the ten surviving children went under the guardianship of their uncle, John Lyon.  
2 CONT *3. George lived in Bedford in 1769. Colonel John mentioned "my brother George" as a militia captain in 1758 and "my late brother George" in 1785. He rose to the rank of colonel in the militia.6 In 1758 he was commissioned to lay 
2 CONC out a road to Fort Duquesne. On 2 April 1766 he received a Pennsylvania license to many Martha Turner, probably a sister of his sister Rebecca's husband. George's last years seem to have been spent in Bedford.
2 CONT *4. William was a surveyor in Mifflin County, where his home near the site of Fort Granville by 1773 was a Presbyterian preaching point. He died late in 1784. Colonel John in 1755 referred to "my brother William. " He later became
2 CONC  a lieutenant in the militia.7 His wife was named Rebecca.
2 CONT *5. Andrew, living in Ireland in 1795, was named in Colonel John's will.8   See https://www.geni.com/people/Andrew-Armstrong/6000000032191493116?through=6000000000889006665
2 CONT *6. Margaret married John Lyon in Ireland and came to Pennsylvania in 1763. They were the great-great-grandparents of James G. Blaine of Maine, an unsuccessful presidential candidate in 1884.9
2 CONT *7. Ann married William Graham and lived in Juniata County.10
2 CONT *8. Rebecca married James Turner 1 November 1759 and lived in Chester County.  See https://www.geni.com/people/Rebecca-Turner/6000000041604530304?through=6000000000889006665
2 CONT *9. _ _ _ _ _ married a Lieutenant Graydon and died in Irelancl.
2 CONT *10. _ _ _ _ _ married Thomas McCord, of Mifflin County
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lineage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Terwinney Armstrong and Margret Mary Maguire - 9th 
2 CONT James Armstrong I and Jane Ross Beatye - 8th 
2 CONT James Armstrong II and Catherine Jane Campbell - 7th 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT Edward ‘of Terwinney’ Armstrong was born in 1604 in Gilnockie, Dumfriesshire, Scotland.[1] His parents were William ‘Christie’s Will’ Armstrong and Margaret Elliot.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Margret ‘Mary’ Maguire, daughter of Thomas Maguire, about 1630. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan Armstrong (29 Jun 1622 - 1701),
2 CONT James Armstrong (abt 1645 - 1 May 1745),
2 CONT Francis Armstrong (1648 - 1726),
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1650 in Ederney, Terwinney, County Fermanagh, Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
