1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===SIR DUNCAN CAMPBELL OF AUCHINBRECK, BARONET===
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Baronet was born before 1610. He was the son of Sir Dugald Campbell of Auchinbreck, 1st Baronet and Mary Erskine. He married, firstly, Margaret Blair, daughter of Brice Blair of Blair, in 16
2 CONC 20. He married, secondly, a daughter --?-- Maxwell of Newark, in 1628. He married, thirdly, Jean Colquhoun, daughter of Sir Alexander Colquhoun of Luss and Helen Buchanan, before 1645. He died in 1645 at Inverlochy, Invernessshire
2 CONC , Scotland, murdered with Argyll's troops, after being taken prisoner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] between 1628 and 1633. He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] between 1639 and 1641
2 CONC . He was commander of the Argyll's troops in Ireland in 1641 taking Dunluce Castle, County Antrim. He succeeded to the title of 2nd Baronet Campbell of Auchinbreck in 1641. He held the office of Governor of Rathlin Island. He held
2 CONC  the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] in 1643.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524382#page/n359/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1900; Vol. II; page 340
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00214181&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck==
2 CONT '''Sir Duncan Campbell (1597–1645), 2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck, was commander of the Marquess of Argyll, Archibald Campbell's troops, (Covenanters) in Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was a son of''' Sir Dugald Campbell, 1st Baronet of Auchinbreck and Mary Erskine. '''He was recalled by Archibald Campbell to Scotland and placed in command of the Covenanter troops at the Battle of Inverlochy (1645).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Titles
2 CONT *2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck[1]
2 CONT *Governor of Rathlin Island[1]
2 CONT *Member of Parliament for Argyllshire Scotland between 1639 and 1641[1]
2 CONT *Member of Parliament for Argyllshire Scotland in 1643[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Battles
2 CONT *Irish Rebellion of 1641
2 CONT *Wars of the Three Kingdoms
2 CONT *Battle of Inverlochy (1645)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Duncan Campbell, 2nd Baronet and 6th Lord of Auchinbreck[1] (1597–1645) was commander of Argyll’s troops in Ireland in 1641 and taking of Dunluce Castle, County Antrim. He held the office of Governor of Rathlin Island. He m
2 CONC arried first Margaret Blair''', who was born about 1575. She was the daughter of the Lord of Blair, and died '''without issue. He married second, Miss Maxwell''', daughter to the Lord of Newark. '''They had issue:
2 CONT *a) Dugald, Third Baronet, born abt 1629 in Auchinbreck. He succeeded his father in 1645 and sat in Parliament for Argyllshire in 1649. He died unmarried about 1662 and was succeeded by his nephew, Sir Duncan, son of Archibald Cam
2 CONC pbell of Knockumilie.
2 CONT *b) Miss Campbell, b. 1627, md. Lord of Lechnel.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He married third, 1628, Dame Jean Coloquhoun, Lady Cathcart, of Luss, Scotland'''. She was born about 1610. '''Duncan and Jean had issue:
2 CONT *a) Archibald, born abt 1629, of Knockamillie, who married 16 Mar 1678 Margaret Campbell, daughter to Colin, Tutor of Calder by whom he had a son, Sir. Duncan.
2 CONT *b) Sir Duncan: 4th Baronet eldest son of Archibald of Knockemelie; Member of Parliament for Argyllshire, 1689–1698; m. 1679/80 dau. of 1st Earl of Balcarres; d. abt 1700.
2 CONT *c) James: born about 1630 of Knockumilie. He married Janet McLoed about 1650.
2 CONT *d) William Campbell, of Wester Kaimes.
2 CONT *e) Alexander of Strondour, born about 1635 was married to Jean, eldest daughter to the Lord of Ottir by whom he had issue. He was murdered after the surrender of Carnassarie Castle in June 1685.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On 2 February, Sir Duncan Campbell led Argyll’s troops at the Battle of Inverlochy (1645) where he was taken prisoner and murdered by Alasdair Mac Colla.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''One prisoner who would receive no mercy was Duncan Campbell, Lord Auchinbreck, the functional commander of the covenanters that day. Auchinbreck had ravaged the lands of Alasdair Mac Colla's Irish cousins in Antrim as a command
2 CONC er in the Scottish army in Ireland. Also he had plotted to assassinate Alasdair when Alasdair tried to reconcile with the Scottish army following the defeat at Glen Maquin. Now Alasdair had Auchinbreck brought before him. Alasdair
2 CONC  offered Auchinbreck two choices, to be made longer, that is hanged, or made shorter, that is decapitated. Auchinbreck reportedly replied, "da dhiu gun aon roghain," which could be roughly translated as two worst alternatives with
2 CONC out one choice. At this point Alasdair swung his two handed sword, and took off the top of Auchinbreck's head above the ears like the top of a soft-boiled egg.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Duncan_Campbell_of_Auchinbreck
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt.1
2 CONT *'''M, #110311, d. 1645
2 CONT *Last Edited=9 Feb 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.02%
2 CONT *'''     Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt. was the son of''' Sir Dugald Campbell of Auchinbreck, 1st Bt. and Mary Erskine.2,3 '''He married, firstly, Margaret Blair''', daughter of Brice Blair of Blair, '''in 1620.1 He m
2 CONC arried, secondly, unknown daughter Newark, daughter of unknown Maxwell of Newark, in 1628.1 He married, thirdly, Jean Colquhoun''', daughter of Sir Alexander Colquhoun of Luss and Helen Buchanan, '''before 1645.4 He died in 1645 a
2 CONC t Inverlochy, Inverness-shire, Scotland, murdered with Argyll’s troops, after being taken prisoner.1
2 CONT *'''     He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] between 1628 and 1633.1 He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] between 1639 and 1641.3 He was commander of
2 CONC  the Argyll’s troops in Ireland 1641 in 1641 taking Dunluce Castle, County Antrim.1 He succeeded to the title of 2nd Baronet Campbell, of Auchinbreck [N.S., 1628] in 1641.1 He held the office of Governor of Rathlin Island.1 He hel
2 CONC d the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Argyllshire [Scotland] in 1643.3
2 CONT *'''Child of Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt. and unknown daughter Newark
2 CONT **Sir Dugald Campbell of Auchinbreck, 3rd Bt.2 d. c 1662
2 CONT *'''Children of Sir Duncan Campbell of Auchinbreck, 2nd Bt. and Jean Colquhoun
2 CONT **Alexander Campbell2 d. 1685
2 CONT **Archibald Campbell of Knockamillie+2 b. c 1632, d. b 1679
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 666. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT *[S15] George Edward Cokayne, editor, The Complete Baronetage, 5 volumes (no date (c. 1900); reprint, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 1983), volume II, page 340. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Baronetage.
2 CONT *[S15] George Edward Cokayne, The Complete Baronetage, volume II, page 322.
2 CONT *From: http://thepeerage.com/p11032.htm#i110311
