1 OCCU 1st Earl of Stirling, Scottish courtier, statesman, and poet who founded and colonized the region of Nova Scotia in Canada., Earl of Stirling, Lord of Stirling, Knight, Secretary of Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "ALEXANDER, WILLIAM, Earl of Stirling, remembered in the land of his birth as a scholar, poet, courtier, and the favourite of James I and Charles I of England in their dealings with Scotland; and on this side of th
2 CONC e Atlantic as the putative founder of a new Scotland under the aegis of both monarchs; b. c. 1577; d. 1640. Though his colonizing interest is the chief concern of cisatlantic readers, it cannot be understood without reference to h
2 CONC is poetical works, which brought him first to royal notice and subsequently to royal favour and collaboration.
2 CONT William Alexander was born in the village of Menstrie. He received a thorough classical education in the grammar school of Stirling, under Dr. Thomas Buchanan, nephew of George Buchanan, tutor of James VI of Scotland; and probably
2 CONC  attended the University of Glasgow. He made “the grand tour” (France, Spain, Italy, and Holland) at the end of the century, as the companion of his kinsman, the seventh Earl of Argyll, who later introduced him to court.
2 CONT Before going to court in London he had made some reputation as a poet, with his Tragedie of Darius, published at Edinburgh in 1603 and dedicated to James VI. This was reprinted in London in 1604, together with another tragedy, Cro
2 CONC esus. Both reflect his classical education and foreshadow the main character of his poetical works. (Aurora, a sonnet sequence published in the same year, was obviously an earlier work; and A Paraenesis to the Prince was obviously
2 CONC  a bid for royal favour.) These were followed in 1605 by The Alexandrean Tragedy; and in 1607 by a collected edition of his tragedies, including Julius Caesar, written in the interval. In this edition the author is described as a 
2 CONC gentleman of the Prince’s Privy Chamber.
2 CONT In this year, 1607, he was granted the rights to the mines and minerals in the barony of Menstrie; and he shared equally with his father-in-law an annuity of £200. (He had married in 1601 Janet, daughter of Sir William Erskine, a 
2 CONC relative of the Earl of Mar, who in due course bore him ten children, seven sons and three daughters; and while he was climbing the ladder of fame Providence concealed from him the fact that a grandchild eight years of age was to 
2 CONC enjoy for a few months only the heritage of an empty title by primogeniture.) In 1608, he and a relative were made agents for collecting debts due the Crown in Scotland from 1547 to 1588, on a 50 per cent basis; he was knighted in
2 CONC  1609.
2 CONT In 1612 he wrote An Elegie on the Death of Prince Henrie and was appointed gentleman usher in the household of Prince Charles. Two years later, he published Doomes-Day, or, The Great day of the Lords Judgement, his last poetical w
2 CONC ork of importance. He had already won on his previous works and, from this date, would continue to win the warm praise of contemporary writers, above all that of Drummond of Hawthornden. Moreover, he had been chosen by King James 
2 CONC as collaborator in translating The Psalms of King David.
2 CONT It was, therefore, as a well-known literary figure, having close associations with both England and Scotland, that he was appointed in 1614 master of requests for Scotland – whose chief duty was to ward off needy Scots from the En
2 CONC glish court – and, in 1615, a member of the Scottish Privy Council, the highest advisory authority on Scottish affairs. It was in these two positions that he was most intimately associated with King James and, having won his compl
2 CONC ete confidence, was enabled to obtain his ardent support for his colonial adventures.
2 CONT During his residence at court, though as a poet Sir William still maintained the futility of human ambition, nonetheless he mingled with those who were promoting the expansion of England overseas, became aware of Scotland’s lack o
2 CONC f any part in it, and, as a patriotic Scot, began to dream of making a name for himself by diverting the constant stream of Scottish manhood from the continental wars into a colony that should bear the name of Scotland.
2 CONT He first approached Capt. John Mason, governor of Newfoundland, 1615–21, and author of A briefe discourse of the New-found-land for aid in getting room for a plantation there. Through Mason’s influence he obtained a grant of the n
2 CONC orthwestern part of that island, from the Bay of Placentia to the Gulf of St. Lawrence, but, although he named it Alexandria, he made no use of it, because of a grander vision that emerged from the suggestion of Governor Mason tha
2 CONC t he consult Ferdinando Gorges, treasurer of the recently formed Council of New England. (In 1620 the London and Plymouth companies, which in 1606 were granted the territory between latitudes 34° and 45°N under the name of Virgini
2 CONC a, were reorganized and the northern part, extended to 48°, was granted to the Council of New England.)
2 CONT Acting on Mason’s advice, Alexander persuaded King James that the only way to get Scots to emigrate was to give them a new Scotland comparable to New France and New England; and the king conveyed the royal wish to the Council of N
2 CONC ew England and obtained from the latter the surrender of all their territory north of the Sainte-Croix. Thereupon the king immediately instructed the Scottish Privy Council to prepare a grant of this territory for Sir William Alex
2 CONC ander. The grant was signed on 10 Sept. 1621 (o.s.), making Sir William, on paper at least, lord proprietor of what are now known as the three Atlantic Provinces and the Gaspé peninsula – to be called for all time New Scotland or 
2 CONC Nova Scotia.
2 CONT Unfortunately, this grant included the territory that had been claimed and nominally occupied by the French as Acadia. The much neglected Charles de Biencourt and a little band of Frenchmen and their Indian allies were the unoffic
2 CONC ial representatives of French claims in the entire area; this same band under Charles de Saint-Étienne de La Tour was to play an important role in the later revival of French claims and in the final failure of Sir William’s coloni
2 CONC zing projects. Meanwhile in the six years during which his title was practically unchallenged, notwithstanding the continued favour of King James and his son Charles, Alexander was unable to plant permanently a single colonist in 
2 CONC his far-flung domain.
2 CONT This is perhaps not a matter of surprise when one considers his previous lack of experience with practical affairs, the difficulty of combining the roles of a dreamer and a man of action, and the magnitude of the task which he had
2 CONC  undertaken single-handed; for he alone had to remould the Scottish national outlook and create a favourable public opinion, whereas the promoters of Virginia, New England, and Newfoundland were numerous and had the English nation
2 CONC  behind them.
2 CONT Though Sir William lacked experience he did not lack either courage or tenacity. In 1622 he hired a ship in London and sent it to Kirkcudbright to pick up settlers and supplies; but it was delayed by the reluctance of artisans to 
2 CONC enlist and the scarcity of supplies, encountered bad weather near Cape Breton, left the colonists in St. John’s, Newfoundland, and returned to London for more supplies. In 1623 he sent out another ship to pick up the colonists, bu
2 CONC t this party found that some of them had died, that others were out fishing, and that too few remained to found a colony. However, ten of these decided to go along with the ship to locate a fit site for a future settlement. They e
2 CONC xplored as far as Cap Nègre, landed at Port-Joli and Port-au-Mouton, formed a favourable opinion of the natural beauty and resources of the country, but returned to Newfoundland, whence they took passage to England with some of th
2 CONC e West Country fishermen, leaving the ship to get its own load of fish. The remainder of the original party apparently became absorbed into the population of Newfoundland.
2 CONT The net gain of Sir William’s first venture, at an alleged cost of £6,000, was a rather vague but flowery description of New Scotland, which he embodied in his pamphlet An encouragement to colonies, published in 1624 and accompani
2 CONC ed by a map on which he sprinkled a number of Scottish names. Thus the Sainte-Croix becomes the Tweed; the Saint John, the Clyde; and the whole is divided into two provinces Alexandria and Caledonia.
2 CONT In this pamphlet he traces colonization from Abraham through the Greeks and Romans to the Spanish, French, and British, recounts the experience of his first venture, paints a glowing picture of its advantages to gentry and commone
2 CONC r, merchant and missionary, and concludes with an appeal to the king to further the project by making it appear a work of his own and thereby encouraging public “helps, such as hath beene had in other parts, for the like cause” – 
2 CONC an obvious reference to the help given by the king to the plantation of Ulster, by the creation of knight-baronets, which brought £225,000 to the royal exchequer.
2 CONT In response to this appeal, King James informed the Privy Council of Scotland that he had decided to confer the dignity of knight-baronet upon any worthy Scot who would undertake to furnish a number of settlers in return for a por
2 CONC tion of New Scotland and instructed the council to prepare a proclamation to that effect. This they did on 30 Nov. 1624, offering a barony and the title of knight-baronet in return for setting forth six men fully armed, clothed, a
2 CONC nd provisioned for two years, within a year and a day after accepting the honour, at the cost of 2,000 merks and 1,000 merks to Sir William for resigning his interest in the barony. As there was no response to this offer, on 23 Ma
2 CONC rch 1624/25 the king wrote the council offering the baronies for a cash payment of 3,000 merks to Sir William, who would use the 2,000 merks for furnishing the settlers. Four days later, King James died without having seen a baron
2 CONC et created or a colonist set forth; but his death did not mean the withdrawal of royal favour: Charles I was equally well disposed to Sir William’s project and for the next five years did everything in his power to induce the Scot
2 CONC tish nobility and gentry to support it. By the end of May 1625 he had created eight baronets on the cash basis, with land three miles wide and six long, and on 12 July he renewed the charter of 1621 with additional provisions for 
2 CONC a total of 150 baronets and for the incorporation of Nova Scotia into the kingdom of Scotland, in order that Sir William and the baronets could take seisin of their distant lands in the castle of Edinburgh without the necessity of
2 CONC  going to Nova Scotia.
2 CONT Despite the promising beginning, the response to the new charter was very disappointing. There was opposition on the part of the lesser barons to the special privileges offered to the knight-baronets. To meet this Charles demoted 
2 CONC the secretary of state for Scotland, who had approved the protest of the lesser barons, and appointed Sir William in his stead. But by 25 July 1626 only 28 baronets had been created and, even if Sir William had received the full s
2 CONC um of 3,000 merks for each, he would have had only £4,666 13s. 4d. to finance his projected colony, considerably less than the £6,000 that he had already expended on the first attempt, for which he had a warrant from King James, s
2 CONC till unpaid.
2 CONT Accordingly, on 25 July 1626 King Charles authorized a committee of the council to meet frequently at stated times to see that the petitioners for the dignity of knight-baronet had satisfied Alexander and that he was prepared to s
2 CONC urrender the lands specified, and to award the dignity forthwith. In the same commission the king awarded armorial bearings to the province. (It is from this commission that Nova Scotia derives its flag and present coat of arms.)
2 CONT As only one baronet had responded by 3 March 1626/27, Charles wrote the council again to speed up matters and stated that unless the full number of baronets were created Sir William would be utterly undone, as he had already spent
2 CONC  more on the two ships he was preparing than he had received.
2 CONT The story of later creations may be summarized thus. Despite repeated urgings only 14 came forward in 1627, 21 in 1628, 7 in 1629, and even after the special badge was offered (27 Nov. 1629), 11 in 1630, and 4 in 1631. That is, 85
2 CONC  baronies only had been disposed of from 1625 to 1631 when Sir William was ordered to give up his colony at Port-Royal to the French and the creation of knight-baronets had degenerated into a scheme of raising money by the sale of
2 CONC  titles. In fact 25 baronets were created between 1633 and 1637 after Port-Royal had been surrendered.
2 CONT Until 1627, Sir William had to contend only with the problems of finance and the reluctance of the Scots to emigrate. But in the spring of that year he had to meet the competition of the powerful Compagnie des Cent-Associés, which
2 CONC  Cardinal Richelieu had organized in Paris to control the destinies of New France and to challenge Sir William’s claims to Nova Scotia. The outbreak of war between France and England also brought new adventurers into the field, no
2 CONC tably the Kirke brothers, whose brilliant achievements now threatened to supplant Alexander for the time being in the public eye. In 1628, after threatening Quebec, the Kirkes captured the supply ships of the Compagnie de la Nouve
2 CONC lle-France near Gaspé, brought Claude de Saint-Étienne de La Tour, who was returning to Acadia after seeking help for the French against the British, a prisoner to England, and applied to the king for a grant of the monopoly of tr
2 CONC ade in Canada, under the Crown of England.
2 CONT Meantime in February 1627/28 Sir William, whose eldest son, William, was already in command of an expedition towards New Scotland, had induced the king to direct that all prize money collected in Scotland should be turned over to 
2 CONC him and to grant him the lordship of Canada – an area of 50 leagues on each side of the St. Lawrence from its mouth to the mythical South Sea. He opposed the application of the Kirke brothers and, supported by the Privy Council of
2 CONC  Scotland, obtained a compromise whereby the rival interests were united and agreed to operate under the crowns of England and Scotland. Accordingly, on 4 Feb. 1628/29 a commission was issued to Sir William Alexander the younger a
2 CONC nd others for a monopoly of the trade of the St. Lawrence, with power to confiscate the goods and ships of any interlopers, to make prizes of all French or Spanish ships, and to displant the French (Royal letters, 47).
2 CONT It was under this commission that the Kirkes took the fort and trading post of Quebec and brought Champlain and other Frenchmen prisoners to England. It was under the same commission that Sir William the younger helped Sir James S
2 CONC tewart, Lord Ochiltree, to plant a short-lived colony at Baleine in Cape Breton – it was uprooted two months later by Charles Daniel – and that under the guidance of Claude de La Tour he planted a colony at Port-Royal (now Annapol
2 CONC is Royal, N.S.), which had a precarious existence until 1632.
2 CONT The younger Sir William decided to remain in Port-Royal over the winter of 1629–30 and to send home his ships for supplies and reinforcement and Claude de La Tour with a draft agreement for his father to sign, whereby Claude and h
2 CONC is son Charles were to receive a large barony (from Yarmouth to Lunenberg) in exchange for their allegiance and assistance. He also sent home the Indian chief Segipt and his wife and son to do homage to King Charles.
2 CONT During the winter of 1629–30 Sir William managed to get together supplies and reinforcements for his colony, confirmed the agreement with Claude de La Tour, who married a Scottish lady, made him a knight-baronet, and sent him back
2 CONC  in May with a patent for his son Charles. However, his own son, returning in the autumn of 1630, had to report that 30 of the 70 colonists had died during the winter, that Charles de La Tour had refused to accept the title of kni
2 CONC ght-baronet, and that Claude de La Tour had therefore lost face with both those whom he brought and the original settlers at Port-Royal. He in turn found his father deep in a struggle to resist the surrender of Nova Scotia to Fran
2 CONC ce in accordance with the Treaty of Susa, 23 April 1629, by which the British and French had agreed on mutual restoration of all territory and shipping taken subsequent to that date. Negotiations dragged on for two years. The Brit
2 CONC ish admitted that Quebec had been taken since that treaty but maintained that Port-Royal had been settled in unoccupied territory to which they had good title by discovery, strengthened by the charter of Virginia, the conquest of 
2 CONC Argall, the charters of 1621 and 1625, and the homage of the Indian chief. The French insisted that both Quebec and Port-Royal be restored to their original condition or the possessor would have an unfair advantage in negotiation.
2 CONC  Finally, King Charles, in dire financial straits, at outs with his Parliament, and lured by the prospect of receiving the half of his wife’s dowry still unpaid by the French, agreed to instruct Sir William to withdraw his colonis
2 CONC ts with their effects and to destroy the fort and other habitations.
2 CONT Though these instructions were drafted in 1631, they were not given to the French for submission to the colonists at Port-Royal until a year later, owing to the king’s insistence that the marriage portion must first be paid to his
2 CONC  banker. They sounded the death knell of Sir William’s colonizing efforts (notwithstanding his later association with the Council of New England) and his hope of solvency, for by the end of 1632 the colony had been withdrawn and t
2 CONC he king’s warrant for £10,000 in compensation for his losses had been dishonoured. The monopoly of minting copper coins in Scotland, given at the time, failed to restore his finances. He died so heavily in debt that his creditors 
2 CONC surrounded his death-bed in London, and denied him a peaceful burial in the church of Stirling.
2 CONT He had enjoyed royal favour to the end so far as the bestowal of titles was concerned (Viscount Stirling, Lord Alexander of Tullibody, 1630; Earl of Stirling, 1633; and Earl of Dovan [Devon], 1639), but this favour did not extend 
2 CONC to saving his colony when put in the balance with the queen’s marriage portion. On the other hand his close association with Charles in his attempt to foist episcopacy upon Scotland cost him the affection of many Scots who were ne
2 CONC ither his debtors nor his creditors.
2 CONT As to his chief colonial venture, although two centuries were to elapse before unassisted Scottish emigration made New Scotland a reality, it cannot be regarded as a complete failure so long as the name Nova Scotia survives, and i
2 CONC ts citizens treasure their armorial achievement and their flag.
2 CONT D. C. Harvey"
2 CONT [https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/alexander_william_1577_1640_1E.html?fbclid=IwY2xjawF6EyBleHRuA2FlbQIxMAABHeiWh7W-dLhwHsNSq20czT2a89dKYuIaxj5qmhQ8ZZF7cEU99nRY9zTdeA_aem_0yK57BJd_Xs_Ts9xGKmOSA]
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT ===Sir William Alexander (c.1577-1640), 1st bt. and 1st Earl of Stirling
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Alexander''' was the only son of Alexander Alexander (d. 1581) and Marion Graham, daughter of Gilbert Graham of Gartavertane. His father died while he was young and his grand uncle James was appointed his tutor. Paul be
2 CONC lieves he received his early education at the Grammar School of Stirling and University of Leyden. He was chosen as travelling companion to Archibald, seventh Earl of Argyll, and went with him to France, Spain and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://landedfamilies.blogspot.com/2013/10/80-alexander-of-menstrie-earls-of.html
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, 1601, Janet (fl. 1649), daughter of Sir William Erskine and had issue: 
2 CONT *(1) Sir William Alexander (c.1604-38), Lord Alexander (q.v.);  
2 CONT * (2) Hon. Sir Anthony Alexander (d. 1637), educated at Glasgow University; licenced to travel abroad, 1626-29; Master of Works in Scotland, 1629-37 (jointly with James Murray until the latter's death in 1634); knighted, 1635; mar
2 CONC ried Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Henry Wardlaw, bt. of Pitreavie but died without issue, 17 September 1637;  
2 CONT * (3) Henry Alexander (d. 1647), 3rd Earl of Stirling (q.v.);  
2 CONT * (4) Hon. John Alexander of Gartmore (d. 1641/2), Master of Minerals and Metals in Scotland (jointly with his father), 1635; Master of the Mint in Scotland, 1635-41; married Agnes (d. c.1636), daughter of Robert Graham of Gartmor
2 CONC e and had issue one daughter;  
2 CONT * (5) Hon. Charles Alexander (d. 1664), married, before 1645, Anna Drurie and had issue two sons;  
2 CONT * (6) Lt-Col. Hon. James Alexander (d. 1671), married first, 16 August1656, Margaret (d. 1662), daughter of Capt. Hon. David Scrimgeour and second, c.1668, Grizel, daughter of Hon. James Hay and had issue one daughter;  
2 CONT * (7) Lady Jean Alexander (d. 1670), married first, 1623, Hugh Montgomery, 2nd Viscount of the Ards (d. 1642) and second, Maj-Gen. Robert Munro, son of Obsdale Munro of Foulis;  
2 CONT * (8) Lady Margaret Alexander, married 1620 Sir William Murray (d. 1646), 1st bt., of Dunearn;  
2 CONT * (9) Hon. Robert Alexander (d. by 1638);  
2 CONT * (10) Hon. Ludovic Alexander, died young;  
2 CONT * (11) Lady Elizabeth Alexander (d. 1642); died unmarried, December 1642. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited Menstrie Castle (Clacks) from his father in 1581, and purchased Argyll's Lodging, Stirling and Tullibody House in 1629 and Tillicoultry House in 1634. He died 12 February 1640, aged about 64.  He left debts of £136,00
2 CONC 0.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ref: “Memorials of the Earl of Stirling and of the House of Alexander ... “ By Charles Rogers.  Page 29.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=dRw8byhG5fkC&lpg=PA29&ots=7R1Y76xv4H&dq=William%20Cowan%2C%20of%20Stirling%20janet%20alex
2 CONC ander&pg=PA29#v=onepage&q=William%20Cowan,%20of%20Stirling%20janet%20alexander&f=false GoogleBooks]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.undiscoveredscotland.co.uk/usbiography/a/williamalexander.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Alexander, 1st Earl of Stirling, lived from 1567 to 12 September 1640. He was a Royal courtier and poet who went on to establish Nova Scotia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander was born in the village of Menstrie in Clackmannanshire. The family home of Menstrie Castle had been built in about 1560, possibly on the site of an earlier castle owned by the family. As a very junior member of 
2 CONC the aristocracy, the young Alexander was appointed tutor to the Earl of Argyll and spent time touring with him abroad. He subsequently became a gentleman usher to Prince Charles, the son of James VI. When the royal court moved to 
2 CONC London in 1603 on James' accession to the English crown as James I, William Alexander moved with it, becoming Gentleman of the Privy Chamber and Master of the Household.
2 CONT
2 CONT Increasingly known for his poetry and his rhymed tragedies, Alexander assisted the King in the preparation of "The Psalms of King David, translated by King James", and was later appointed by the King to be their sole printer. In 1
2 CONC 614 he was knighted. In 1621 James VI/I granted Alexander vast tracts of land in North America. He christened his new lands "Nova Scotia" and set to work colonising them with Scots to whom he sold hereditary baronetcies (on behalf
2 CONC  of the King) at £150 each, with considerable success.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1626, James appointed Sir William to the post of Secretary for Scotland, with the power to govern north of the border on the King's behalf. In 1628 Sir William was granted the lands and barony of Menstrie. In 1630 Sir William b
2 CONC ecame Viscount of Stirling and Lord Alexander of Tullibody, and in 1633 he was further promoted through the ranks of the aristocracy to become the 1st Earl of Stirling. This may have been a consolation prize awarded to him by Jame
2 CONC s VI/I, because in 1632 much of Nova Scotia had been lost to the French. In the process much of Sir William's fortune was lost and he spent the remainder of his life in reduced circumstances, despite continuing to hold various gov
2 CONC ernment appointments. Not so reduced, however, that he couldn't afford to relocated his home to the newly renovated and very grand Argyll's Lodging in Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William continue to write throughout his life. He specialised in "closet dramas" not intended to be performed publicly, and is remembered particularly for a series of classical tragedies entitled "Croesus", "Darius", "The Alex
2 CONC andrean", and "Julius Caesar". A number of editions of his collected plays were published between 1604 and 1637. Sir Alexander died in London in 1640. There is an exhibition about Sir William in the small museum in Menstrie Castle
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Canadian History Sir William Alexander'''
2 CONT ==
2 CONT
2 CONT https://electricscotland.com/history/canada/william_alexander.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander's first years were spent in the parish of Logie, but following his father's death he was brought up in Stirling by a granduncle who was a merchant there. He was educated at the universities of Glasgow and Leiden,
2 CONC  and became tutor to the Earl of Argyle. He joined the court of James VI in Edinburgh as tutor to Prince Henry, and was one of a number of poets whom the King surrounded himself with, known as the Castalian Band. Following the Uni
2 CONC on of the Crowns in 1603, James moved his court to London. Alexander was one of the favoured courtiers who accompanied him, and his subsequent involvement in English politics made Alexander unpopular in Scotland. He was knighted i
2 CONC n 1609, granted the plantation of Nova Scotia in 1621 and made Viscount of Canada. He was appointed Secretary of State for Scotland in 1626, and was created Earl of Stirling in 1633. Despite such political achievements, however, h
2 CONC e met with severe financial difficulties during his last years, and he died in poverty. His literary work is now regarded as an historical curiosity; it includes sonnets and songs (collected in "Aurora"); four tragedies written fo
2 CONC r Prince Henry; and "Doomesday", a poem of over 10,000 lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Windsor Castle on September 10, 1621 King James signed a grant in favour of Sir William Alexander covering all of the lands "between our Colonies of New England and Newfoundland, to be known as New Scotland " (Nova Scotia in La
2 CONC tin), an area larger than Great Britain and France combined. On October 18, 1624 the King announced his intention of creating a new order of baronets to Scottish "knichts and gentlemen of cheife respect for ther birth, place, or f
2 CONC ortounes ". James I died on March 27, 1625 but his heir, Charles I, lost no time in implementing his father’s plan. By the end of 1625, the first 22 Baronets of Nova Scotia were created and, as inducements to settlement of his new
2 CONC  colony of Nova Scotia, Sir William offered tracts of land totalling 11,520 acres "to all such principal knichts & esquires as will be pleased to be undertakers of the said plantations & who will promise to set forth 6 men, artifi
2 CONC cers or laborers, sufficiently armed, apparelled & victualled for 2 yrs ". Baronets could receive their patents in Edinburgh rather than London, and an area of Edinburgh Castle was declared Nova Scotian territory for this purpose.
2 CONC  In return, they had to pay Sir William 1000 merks for his "past charges in discoverie of the said country ".
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.archive.org/stream/memorialsofearlo02rogeuoft/memorialsofearlo02rogeuoft_djvu.txt
2 CONT Chapter XXV. 
2 CONT ''note to Curators and other interested persons;'' ''''''This link below,provides citation to 8 children of Sir William Alexander and Lady Janet Erskine'''The link also provides chronological order to these 8, proven children's bi
2 CONC rths, as well as pertinent marriages, and other information.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.cracroftspeerage.co.uk/online/content/stirling1633.htm]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that the entire Alexander line prior to William b. 1749 is terribly terribly weak.  But it is just barely plausible, so I am keeping it for now as I try to get better info.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander, Earl of Stirling (c. 1570, Menstrie, Clackmannanshire – 12 September 1640) was a Scotsman who was an early developer of Scottish colonisation of Nova Scotia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1609-
2 CONT ===Knighted===
2 CONT - [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Alexander,_William_(1567%3F-1640)_(DNB00)_]
2 CONT
2 CONT  1630-elevated to: 
2 CONT ===Lord Alexander of Tullibody and Viscount Stirling=== 
2 CONT [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Alexander,_William_(1567%3F-1640)_(DNB00)]
2 CONT
2 CONT  1631-appointed: 
2 CONT ===Extraordinary Judge of the Court of Session===
2 CONT [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Alexander,_William_(1567%3F-1640)_(DNB00)]
2 CONT
2 CONT When a young man he was appointed tutor to the Earl of Argyll and accompanied him abroad. At a later date he received the place of gentleman usher to Prince Charles, son of James VI of Scotland, and continued in favour at court af
2 CONC ter the king became James I of England. He attained reputation as a poet and writer of rhymed tragedies, and assisted the king in preparing the metrical version known as "The Psalms of King David, translated by King James," and pu
2 CONC blished by authority of Charles I. He was knighted in 1614. In 1621 King James I granted him a royal charter appointing him governor of a vast territory in North America which was erected into a lordship and barony of Nova Scotia 
2 CONC (New Scotland); the area now known as Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, and part of the northern United States. The creation of Baronets of Nova Scotia was used to settle the plantation of the new province.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed Secretary for Scotland in 1626 and held that office for the rest of his life. In 1630, King Charles rewarded his service by creating him Viscount of Stirling and in 1633 he became Earl of Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Stirling’s efforts at colonisation were less successful, at least in monetary terms. He briefly established a Scottish settlement at Annapolis Royal, led by his son William Alexander (the younger). However the effort cost him
2 CONC  most of his fortune, and when the maritimes were returned to France in 1632, it was lost. He spent his later years with limited means, and died in London on September 12, 1640. However Alexander's settlement provided the basis fo
2 CONC r British claims to Nova Scotia and the other Maritime Provinces and his baronets provided the Coat of arms of Nova Scotia and Flag of Nova Scotia which are still in use today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stirling also wrote closet dramas: classical tragedies titled Croesus, Darius, The Alexandrean, and Julius Caesar. His plays were published in several editions (1604, 1607, 1616, 1637).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Canadian Coast Guard has named the CCGS Sir William Alexander in his honour
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ALEXANDER, WILLIAM, Earl of Stirling, remembered in the land of his birth as a scholar, poet, courtier, and the favourite of James I and Charles I of England in their dealings with Scotland; and on this side of the Atlantic as the
2 CONC  putative founder of a new Scotland under the aegis of both monarchs; b. c. 1577; d. 1640. Though his colonizing interest is the chief concern of cisatlantic readers, it cannot be understood without reference to his poetical works
2 CONC , which brought him first to royal notice and subsequently to royal favour and collaboration.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander was born in the village of Menstrie. He received a thorough classical education in the grammar school of Stirling, under Dr. Thomas Buchanan, nephew of George Buchanan, tutor of James VI of Scotland; and probably
2 CONC  attended the University of Glasgow. He made “the grand tour” (France, Spain, Italy, and Holland) at the end of the century, as the companion of his kinsman, the seventh Earl of Argyle, who later introduced him to court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before going to court in London he had made some reputation as a poet, with his Tragedie of Darius, published at Edinburgh in 1603 and dedicated to James VI. This was reprinted in London in 1604, together with another tragedy, Cro
2 CONC esus. Both reflect his classical education and foreshadow the main character of his poetical works. (Aurora, a sonnet sequence, published in the same year was obviously an earlier work; and A Paraenesis to the Prince was obviously
2 CONC  a bid for royal favour.) These were followed in 1605 by The Alexandrean Tragedy; and in 1607 by a collected edition of his tragedies, including Julius Caesar, written in the interval. In this edition the author is described as a 
2 CONC gentleman of the Prince’s Privy Chamber.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this year, 1607, he was granted the rights to the mines and minerals in the barony of Menstrie; and he shared equally with his father-in-law an annuity of £200. (He had married in 1601 Janet, daughter of Sir William Erskine, a 
2 CONC relative of the Earl of Mar, who in due course bore him ten children, seven sons and three daughters; and while he was climbing the ladder of fame Providence concealed from him the fact that a grandchild eight years of age was to 
2 CONC enjoy for a few months only the heritage of an empty title by primogeniture.) In 1608, he and a relative were made agents for collecting debts due the Crown in Scotland from 1547 to 1588, on a 50 per cent basis; he was knighted in
2 CONC  1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1612 he wrote An Elegie on the Death of Prince Henrie and was appointed gentleman usher in the household of Prince Charles. Two years later, he published Doomes-Day, or, The Great day of the Lords Judgement, his last poetical w
2 CONC ork of importance. He had already won on his previous works and, from this date, would continue to win the warm praise of contemporary writers, above all that of Drummond of Hawthornden. Moreover, he had been chosen by King James 
2 CONC as collaborator in translating The Psalms of King David.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was, therefore, as a well-known literary figure, having close associations with both England and Scotland, that he was appointed in 1614 master of requests for Scotland – whose chief duty was to ward off needy Scots from the En
2 CONC glish court – and, in 1615, a member of the Scottish Privy Council, the highest advisory authority on Scottish affairs. It was in these two positions that he was most intimately associated with King James and, having won his compl
2 CONC ete confidence, was enabled to obtain his ardent support for his colonial adventures.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his residence at court, though as a poet Sir William still maintained the futility of human ambition, nonetheless he mingled with those who were promoting the expansion of England overseas, became aware of Scotland’s lack o
2 CONC f any part in it, and, as a patriotic Scot, began to dream of making a name for himself by diverting the constant stream of Scottish manhood from the continental wars into a colony that should bear the name of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He first approached Capt. John Mason, governor of Newfoundland, 1615–21, and author of A briefe discourse of the New-found-land for aid in getting room for a plantation there. Through Mason’s influence he obtained a grant of the n
2 CONC orthwestern part of that island, from the Bay of Placentia to the Gulf of St. Lawrence, but, although he named it Alexandria, he made no use of it, because of a grander vision that emerged from the suggestion of Governor Mason tha
2 CONC t he consult Ferdinando Gorges, treasurer of the recently formed Council of New England. (In 1620 the London and Plymouth companies, which in 1606 were granted the territory between latitudes 34° and 45° N under the name of Virgin
2 CONC ia, were reorganized and the northern part, extended to 48º, was granted to the Council of New England.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Acting on Mason’s advice, Alexander persuaded King James that the only way to get Scots to emigrate was to give them a new Scotland comparable to New France and New England; and the king conveyed the royal wish to the Council of N
2 CONC ew England and obtained from the latter the surrender of all their territory north of the Sainte-Croix. Thereupon the king immediately instructed the Scottish Privy Council to prepare a grant of this territory for Sir William Alex
2 CONC ander. The grant was signed on 10 Sept. 1621 (o.s.), making Sir William, on paper at least, lord proprietor of what are now known as the three Atlantic Provinces and the Gaspé peninsula – to be called for all time New Scotland or 
2 CONC Nova Scotia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, this grant included the territory that had been claimed and nominally occupied by the French as Acadia. The much neglected Charles de Biencourt and a little band of Frenchmen and their Indian allies were the unoffic
2 CONC ial representatives of French claims in the entire area; this same band under Charles de Saint-Étienne de La Tour was to play an important role in the later revival of French claims and in the final failure of Sir William’s coloni
2 CONC zing projects. Meanwhile in the six years during which his title was practically unchallenged, notwithstanding the continued favour of King James and his son Charles, Alexander was unable to plant permanently a single colonist in 
2 CONC his far-flung domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is perhaps not a matter of surprise when one considers his previous lack of experience with practical affairs, the difficulty of combining the roles of a dreamer and a man of action, and the magnitude of the task which he had
2 CONC  undertaken single-handed; for he alone had to remould the Scottish national outlook and create a favourable public opinion, whereas the promoters of Virginia, New England, and Newfoundland were numerous and had the English nation
2 CONC  behind them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though Sir William lacked experience he did not lack either courage or tenacity. In 1622 he hired a ship in London and sent it to Kirkcudbright to pick up settlers and supplies; but it was delayed by the reluctance of artisans to 
2 CONC enlist and the scarcity of supplies, encountered bad weather near Cape Breton, left the colonists in St. John’s, Newfoundland, and returned to London for more supplies. In 1623 he sent out another ship to pick up the colonists, bu
2 CONC t this party found that some of them had died, that others were out fishing, and that too few remained to found a colony. However, ten of these decided to go along with the ship to locate a fit site for a future settlement. They e
2 CONC xplored as far as Cap Nègre, landed at Port-Joli and Port-au-Mouton, formed a favourable opinion of the natural beauty and resources of the country, but returned to Newfoundland, whence they took passage to England with some of th
2 CONC e West Country fishermen, leaving the ship to get its own load of fish. The remainder of the original party apparently became absorbed in the population of Newfoundland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The net gain of Sir William’s first venture, at an alleged cost of £6,000, was a rather vague but flowery description of New Scotland, which he embodied in his pamphlet An encouragement to colonies, published in 1624 and accompani
2 CONC ed by a map on which he sprinkled a number of Scottish names. Thus the Sainte-Croix becomes the Tweed; the Saint John, the Clyde; and the whole is divided into two provinces Alexandria and Caledonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this pamphlet he traces colonization from Abraham through the Greeks and Romans to the Spanish, French, and British, recounts the experience of his first venture, paints a glowing picture of its advantages to gentry and commone
2 CONC r, merchant and missionary, and concludes with an appeal to the king to further the project by making it appear a work of his own and thereby encouraging public “helps, such as hath beene had in other parts, for the like cause” – 
2 CONC an obvious reference to the help given by the king to the plantation of Ulster, by the creation of knight-baronets, which brought £225,000 to the royal exchequer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In response to this appeal, King James informed the Privy Council of Scotland that he had decided to confer the dignity of knight-baronet upon any worthy Scot who would undertake to furnish a number of settlers in return for a por
2 CONC tion of New Scotland and instructed the council to prepare a proclamation to that effect. This they did on 30 Nov. 1624, offering a barony and the title of knight-baronet in return for setting forth six men fully armed, clothed, a
2 CONC nd provisioned for two years, within a year and a day after accepting the honour, at the cost of 2,000 merks and 1,000 merks to Sir William for resigning his interest in the barony. As there was no response to this offer, on 23 Ma
2 CONC rch 1624/25 the king wrote the council offering the baronies for a cash payment of 3,000 merks to Sir William, who would use the 2,000 merks for furnishing the settlers. Four days later, King James died without having seen a baron
2 CONC et created or a colonist set forth; but his death did not mean the withdrawal of royal favour: Charles I was equally well disposed to Sir William’s project and for the next five years did everything in his power to induce the Scot
2 CONC tish nobility and gentry to support it. By the end of May 1625 he had created eight baronets on the cash basis, with land three miles wide and six long, and on 12 July he renewed the charter of 1621 with additional provisions for 
2 CONC a total of 150 baronets and for the incorporation of Nova Scotia into the kingdom of Scotland, in order that Sir William and the baronets could take seisin of their distant lands in the castle of Edinburgh without the necessity of
2 CONC  going to Nova Scotia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the promising beginning, the response to the new charter was very disappointing. There was opposition on the part of the lesser barons to the special privileges offered to the knight-baronets. To meet this Charles demoted 
2 CONC the secretary of state for Scotland, who had approved the protest of the lesser barons, and appointed Sir William in his stead. But by 25 July 1626 only 28 baronets had been created and, even if Sir William had received the full s
2 CONC um of 3,000 merks for each, he would have had only £4,666 13s. 4d. to finance his projected colony, considerably less than the £6,000 that he had already expended on the first attempt, for which he had a warrant from King James, s
2 CONC till unpaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Accordingly, on 25 July 1626 King Charles authorized a committee of the council to meet frequently at stated times to see that the petitioners for the dignity of knight-baronet had satisfied Alexander and that he was prepared to s
2 CONC urrender the lands specified, and to award the dignity forthwith. In the same commission the king awarded armorial bearings to the province. (It is from this commission that Nova Scotia derives its flag and present coat of arms.)
2 CONT
2 CONT As only one baronet had responded by 3 March 1626/27, Charles wrote the council again to speed up matters and stated that unless the full number of baronets were created Sir William would be utterly undone, as he had already spent
2 CONC  more on the two ships he was preparing than he had received.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of later creations may be summarized thus. Despite repeated urgings only 14 came forward in 1627, 21 in 1628, 7 in 1629, and even after the special badge was offered (27 Nov. 1629), 11 in 1630, and 4 in 1631. That is, 85
2 CONC  baronies only had been disposed of from 1625 to 1631 when Sir William was ordered to give up his colony at Port-Royal to the French and the creation of knight-baronets had degenerated into a scheme of raising money by the sale of
2 CONC  titles. In fact 25 baronets were created between 1633 and 1637 after Port-Royal had been surrendered.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until 1627, Sir William had to contend only with the problems of finance and the reluctance of the Scots to emigrate. But in the spring of that year he had to meet the competition of the powerful Compagnie des Cent-Associés, which
2 CONC  Cardinal Richelieu had organized in Paris to control the destinies of New France and to challenge Sir William’s claims to Nova Scotia. The outbreak of war between France and England also brought new adventurers into the field, no
2 CONC tably the Kirke brothers, whose brilliant achievements now threatened to supplant Alexander for the time being in the public eye. In 1628, after threatening Quebec, the Kirkes captured the supply ships of the Compagnie de la Nouve
2 CONC lle-France near Gaspé, brought Claude de Saint-Étienne de La Tour, who was returning to Acadia after seeking help for the French against the British, a prisoner to England, and applied to the king for a grant of the monopoly of tr
2 CONC ade in Canada, under the Crown of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meantime in February 1627/28 Sir William, whose eldest son, William, was already in command of an expedition towards New Scotland, had induced the king to direct that all prize money collected in Scotland should be turned over to 
2 CONC him and to grant him the lordship of Canada – an area of 50 leagues on each side of the St. Lawrence from its mouth to the mythical South Sea. He opposed the application of the Kirke brothers and, supported by the Privy Council of
2 CONC  Scotland, obtained a compromise whereby the rival interests were united and agreed to operate under the crowns of England and Scotland. Accordingly, on 4 Feb. 1628/29 a commission was issued to Sir William Alexander the younger a
2 CONC nd others for a monopoly of the trade of the St. Lawrence, with power to confiscate the goods and ships of any interlopers, to make prizes of all French or Spanish ships, and to displant the French (Royal letters, 47).
2 CONT
2 CONT It was under this commission that the Kirkes took the fort and trading post of Quebec and brought Champlain and other Frenchmen prisoners to England. It was under the same commission that Sir William the younger helped Sir James S
2 CONC tewart, Lord Ochiltree, to plant a short-lived colony at Baleine in Cape Breton – it was uprooted two months later by Charles Daniel – and that under the guidance of Claude de La Tour he planted a colony at Port-Royal (now Annapol
2 CONC is Royal, N.S.), which had a precarious existence until 1632.
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger Sir William decided to remain in Port-Royal over the winter of 1629–30 and to send home his ships for supplies and reinforcement and Claude de La Tour with a draft agreement for his father to sign, whereby Claude and h
2 CONC is son Charles were to receive a large barony (from Yarmouth to Lunenberg) in exchange for their allegiance and assistance. He also sent home the Indian chief Segipt and his wife and son to do homage to King Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the winter of 1629–30 Sir William managed to get together supplies and reinforcements for his colony, confirmed the agreement with Claude de La Tour, who married a Scottish lady, made him a knight-baronet, and sent him back
2 CONC  in May with a patent for his son Charles. However, his own son, returning in the autumn of 1630, had to report that 30 of the 70 colonists had died during the winter, that Charles de La Tour had refused to accept the title of kni
2 CONC ght-baronet, and that Claude de La Tour had therefore lost face with both those whom he brought and the original settlers at Port-Royal. He in turn found his father deep in a struggle to resist the surrender of Nova Scotia to Fran
2 CONC ce in accordance with the Treaty of Susa, 23 April 1629, by which the British and French had agreed on mutual restoration of all territory and shipping taken subsequent to that date. Negotiations dragged on for two years. The Brit
2 CONC ish admitted that Quebec had been taken since that treaty but maintained that Port-Royal had been settled in unoccupied territory to which they had good title by discovery, strengthened by the charter of Virginia, the conquest of 
2 CONC Argall, the charters of 1621 and 1625, and the homage of the Indian chief. The French insisted that both Quebec and Port-Royal be restored to their original condition or the possessor would have an unfair advantage in negotiation.
2 CONC  Finally, King Charles, in dire financial straits, at outs with his Parliament, and lured by the prospect of receiving the half of his wife’s dowry still unpaid by the French, agreed to instruct Sir William to withdraw his colonis
2 CONC ts with their effects and to destroy the fort and other habitations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though these instructions were drafted in 1631, they were not given to the French for submission to the colonists at Port-Royal until a year later, owing to the king’s insistence that the marriage portion must first be paid to his
2 CONC  banker. They sounded the death knell of Sir William’s colonizing efforts (notwithstanding his later association with the Council of New England) and his hope of solvency, for by the end of 1632 the colony had been withdrawn and t
2 CONC he king’s warrant for £10,000 in compensation for his losses had been dishonoured. The monopoly of minting copper coins in Scotland, given at the time, failed to restore his finances. He died so heavily in debt that his creditors 
2 CONC surrounded his death-bed in London, and denied him a peaceful burial in the church of Stirling.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had enjoyed royal favour to the end so far as the bestowal of titles was concerned (Viscount Stirling, Lord Alexander of Tullibody, 1630; Earl of Stirling, 1633; and Earl of Dovan [Devon], 1639), but this favour did not extend 
2 CONC to saving his colony when put in the balance with the queen’s marriage portion. On the other hand his close association with Charles in his attempt to foist episcopacy upon Scotland cost him the affection of many Scots who were ne
2 CONC ither his debtors nor his creditors.
2 CONT
2 CONT As to his chief colonial venture, although two centuries were to elapse before unassisted Scottish emigration made New Scotland a reality, it cannot be regarded as a complete failure so long as the name Nova Scotia survives, and i
2 CONC ts citizens treasure their armorial achievement and their flag.
2 CONT
2 CONT Why do I bother:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT The First Nova Scotian" It is generally believed that Williamreceived his first formal ed
2 CONT
2 CONT ucation at the grammar school in Stirlingwhere he would have had strict rules and a
2 CONT
2 CONT strident round of studies conditioned by dominant church teaching. Histeacher was probably Thomas Buchanan nephew of the celebrated scholarand historian George Buchanan who had been tutor to Prince James, sonof Mary Queen of Scots
2 CONC  and future king of Scotland and England.With it's ornamental interiors and its magnificent hall and chapel,
2 CONT
2 CONT Stirling Castle was a Renaissance Palace.
2 CONT
2 CONT After being crowned king, James VI of Scotland made Stirling hishome, so during Alexander's early years the city was place of pomp,pageantry and international influence. Early Scots were encouraged toread, write and speak classica
2 CONC l Latin, study Greek and Roman cultures.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Scotland's zealot, John Knox, fundamentalism gave rise to thatunique form of church worship and government, ScottishPresbyterianism. Although enrollment records of the period arelost, it is certain he attended Glasgow Universit
2 CONC y as the fact ismentioned in several contemporary sources. He later took a particularinterest in the welfare of that
2 CONT
2 CONT university and all but one of his sons attended Glasgow University.Also references that he attended Leyden, the Dutch University.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point he formed a friendship with Alexander Hume, a Pariseducated Scottish lawyer who later turned to theology and became aminister in the parish of Logie. Hume was also a writer and may haveacted as mentor to young Alexan
2 CONC der. Writers such as Philip Sidney,Edmund Spenser, Christopher Marlowe , William Shakespeare, and BenJohnson probably influenced young Alexander who was reputed to havebegun composing poetry in Stirling at age fifteen and aspired 
2 CONC to bepart of the Elizabethan literary Renaissance.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young man in his early twenties, having gained a reputationas one both scholarly and talented, he was chosen by Campbell cousinsto accompany their son, the seventh Earl of Argyle on the customaryeducational Grand Tour of Euro
2 CONC pe taken by many sons of nobility. Hissojourn through France, Italy and Spain prompted his Celtic soul intocomposing passionate love poems, earning him a reputation as asonneteer. Once back home in Scotland Alexander turned his ha
2 CONC nd towriting plays in the Senecan form for which he gained quite areputation.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1598 his erstwhile traveling companion, the Earl of Argyleinvested Alexander with additional land in Menstrie "in considerationof among other things, services rendered to the Earl in foreignnations and at home" Alexander's inte
2 CONC llectual and creativeabilities were much admired by the Scottish King James VI and hewelcomed young Alexander to his court in Edinburgh. He was given theinfluential position of tutor to Prince Henry, the King's eldest sonand heir 
2 CONC to the throne. When King James VI of Scotland moved onto themore powerful English throne as James I in 1603 Alexander followed.London with its thriving literary scene seemed to prompt him to writeand publish his major works.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young Prince James, son of Mary Queen of Scots, became king atage 13 months when his mother was dethroned. While still a boy Jameswas placed under the guardianship of the Erskines, hereditary keepersof Stirling Castle and the 
2 CONC family William Alexander would later marryinto. After a palace coup in 1581 in which the palace was invaded andthe King
2 CONT
2 CONT imprisoned, Alexander wrote his first prose piece entitled " A ShortDiscourse of the Good Ends of Providence in the Late Attempt Againsthis Majesty's Person, exhorting the King to pursue the perpetrators ofhis imprisonment, a grou
2 CONC p of radical Protestants and disgruntlednobles. Soon after his return to "Scotland from abroad he successfullywooded the
2 CONT
2 CONT young , musically talented and aristocratic Janet Erskine whose familyhad been keepers of Stirling Castle and protectors of princes of therealm. They married in 1601. She mothered seven sons and 3 daughtersand outlived him after 4
2 CONC 0 years of married life. Alexander turnedto play writing. From his education abroad, he imitated in theme andstructure the classical playwrights of the past, particularly theRoman Seneca. His first published play appeared in 1603 
2 CONC the year JamesVi
2 CONT
2 CONT moved south to become James I of England. Entitled the Tragedy ofDaraius,
2 CONT
2 CONT which told the tale of the Persian King and dealt with trials oftribulations
2 CONT
2 CONT of kings in general. This work was dedicated to James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his appointment as tutor to Prince Henry he wrote atract
2 CONT
2 CONT entitled A Paraenesis to the Prince, a poem of 84 verses including awarning
2 CONT
2 CONT that misbehaving princes can be dethroned. Prince Henry died ofpneumonia in
2 CONT
2 CONT Nov 1612.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1604 Alexander published another Greek-style poetic drama,entitled Cro
2 CONT
2 CONT esus. Again the theme dealt with the tragedy that befalls those whoput their
2 CONT
2 CONT faith in vane ambition, the pursuit of power and banal accumulation of
2 CONT
2 CONT material wealth. In 1605 he published The Alexandrean about Alexanderthe
2 CONT
2 CONT Great. and in 1607 a play about Julius Caesar which one source statesmay
2 CONT
2 CONT have provided Shakespeare with a line of dialogue for his play.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1612 Alexander also busy with commercial and state matters andas
2 CONT
2 CONT Gentleman to the Privy Chamber of Prince Henry he wrote his longestpoetic
2 CONT
2 CONT composition, some 11,000 lines entitled Dooms-day or The Great Day ofthe
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord's Judgment, a Biblical epic which extends from the Creation tothe Book
2 CONT
2 CONT of Revelation. It is one of two great religious works of his , theother a
2 CONT
2 CONT metrical translation of the Psalms of David. His translation of thePsalms so
2 CONT
2 CONT shocked the Presbyterian clergy of Scotland by its secular languagethat
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was looked upon as a heathen and traitor.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1616 he finished (deceased )Sir Philips's masterpiece, Arcadia.His
2 CONT
2 CONT tract entitled An Encouragement to Colonies was published a few yearsafter
2 CONT
2 CONT he received his Nova Scotia land charter from the King in 1621.Heavily in
2 CONT
2 CONT debt after giving much of his own personal fortune besides his timeand
2 CONT
2 CONT attention, Alexander went into deep background chronicling earlyhistory of
2 CONT
2 CONT colonization. The motivation was that in North America they couldbuild a New
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT One last time he retired to Sterling Castle and wrote andpublished,
2 CONT
2 CONT Recreations with the Muses
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's poetry and plays were in keeping with literary stylesof the
2 CONT
2 CONT times and dealt with themes both international and universal. In 1603the
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish tongue was almost non-existent. He could converse and writein both
2 CONT
2 CONT Latin and English.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1607 the King granted Alexander the right to mine minerals andmetals
2 CONT
2 CONT of all types in his home barony of Menstrie and 1/10th to go to theroyal
2 CONT
2 CONT coffers. In 1611 Alexander applied for and received an additionalroyal grant
2 CONT
2 CONT to set up a silver refinery in the area.. He was later given the rightto
2 CONT
2 CONT mine gold and silver at Crawford Muir in Lanarkshire and at Hilderstonin
2 CONT
2 CONT Linlithgowshire which later failed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1608 Alexander was knighted by the King. When Prince Henry diedin
2 CONT
2 CONT 1612 William composed An Elegy on the Death of Prince Henry and wasappointed
2 CONT
2 CONT aide and tutor to Prince Charles, Henry's younger brother. In 1615Alexander
2 CONT
2 CONT was appointed to office of Master of Requests for Scotland. In 1614he was
2 CONT
2 CONT appointed to Scottish Privy Council, the highest advisory body onScottish
2 CONT
2 CONT affairs short of Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1621 he became one of the Lords of the Articles who prepared billsfor
2 CONT
2 CONT submission to Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain John Mason who had been running a successful fishingoperation
2 CONT
2 CONT out of Newfoundland, on returning to England in 1620, published ABrief
2 CONT
2 CONT Discourse on Newfoundland which may have sparked Sir Alexander'sinterest who
2 CONT
2 CONT had had earlier dealings with Mason. Alexander invited Mason to ameeting at
2 CONT
2 CONT his Covent Garden residence in London. where Alexander "declared his
2 CONT
2 CONT affection to Plantation and wished the Captain to be the means toprocure him
2 CONT
2 CONT a girth of the planters thereof for a portion of the land waithin."In
2 CONT
2 CONT consequence of this meeting, the land west of Placentia Bay was givento
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander which he gave the name Alexandria. Alexander was describedby
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish historian Charles Rogers as "fertile in device and expert in
2 CONT
2 CONT execution and of an unswerving tenacity of purpose"
2 CONT
2 CONT The territory of New Scotland embraced Nova Scotia and also NewBrunswick,
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Edward Island, part of Quebec and Man. Alexander's settlementwas
2 CONT
2 CONT financed solely from his own coffers. A warrant for Alexander'scharter was
2 CONT
2 CONT issued at Windsor Castle on Sept 10 1621 and on Sept 29th the charteritself
2 CONT
2 CONT passed under the great Seal. His unique charter gave the 54 year oldpoet,
2 CONT
2 CONT playwright and courtier powers unequaled by any other colony inEurope or
2 CONT
2 CONT the new world. King James made him virtual King of a vast new domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was his own Lord of the Admiralty, Chief Justice andMinister of
2 CONT
2 CONT Trade and Chief of the Exchequer.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first settlers despite provision problems and shortfall ofwilling
2 CONT
2 CONT immigrants set sail for New Scotland in June 1622 arriving in themiddle of
2 CONT
2 CONT September at St John's harbor to meet one of the coldest winters inyears. A
2 CONT
2 CONT second ship, St Luke embarked to the new world and surveyed the land.To help
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander King James charged the cost to the royal exchequer, but itwas
2 CONT
2 CONT never paid out. He never recovered from his financial misfortunes. Tospark
2 CONT
2 CONT more interest in colonization he wrote the highly interestingdocument, An
2 CONT
2 CONT Encouragement to Colonies issued in London in 1624. Also, Alexanderproposed
2 CONT
2 CONT the creation of an order of Baronets of Nova Scotia. Each baron wouldbe at
2 CONT
2 CONT least 16,000 acres. Each baronet would have to send "6 suffience menfor two
2 CONT
2 CONT years". By July of 1627 only about 35 Baronets had been conferred andmany
2 CONT
2 CONT had not paid their money down.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander's oldest son William had by now graduated from Glasgow
2 CONT
2 CONT University, presented at court and knighted and was ready to commandthis
2 CONT
2 CONT next expedition. However hostilities with France and Spain made theopen seas
2 CONT
2 CONT too dangerous . War with France also began in 1627. Finally on March26 1628
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander jr. in command of four vessels left London forScotland (He
2 CONT
2 CONT was now known as Lord William Alexander) and 60-70 settlers leftDunbarton
2 CONT
2 CONT for Canada. Young Alexander returned in triumph to England. Churchrecords in
2 CONT
2 CONT Stirling indicated that when Lord Wm returned he gave the poor ofStirling 58
2 CONT
2 CONT pounds
2 CONT
2 CONT A new patent was issued in the name of Lord William Alexander.The
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexanders were at this point perhaps the most powerful landholders inthe
2 CONT
2 CONT whole of North America. However Charlesfort, established by LordWilliam fell
2 CONT
2 CONT to the French by 1632 by the Treaty of St Germaine. The area now iscalled
2 CONT
2 CONT Annapolis Royal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Williams's final years were filled with tragedy. September 1637his
2 CONT
2 CONT second son Anthony an architect and Kings Master of Works and Head ofAll
2 CONT
2 CONT Masons died. A few months later Lord William died, his Nova Scotiaproject in
2 CONT
2 CONT ruins and no hope of financial compensation. Ailing and aging andmercilessly
2 CONT
2 CONT hounded by creditors,he died impoverished in London. Sept. 1640, andwas
2 CONT
2 CONT buried in a family crypt in High Church of Stirling. Few of his fellow
2 CONT
2 CONT Scotsmen paid him homage. His wife Janet applied and lived on a Crown
2 CONT
2 CONT pension in her remaining years. Sir William was considered by some an
2 CONT
2 CONT opportunist, others a traitor and many an impractical idealist andfailure.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE FIRST NOVA SCOTIAN " William Alexander was likely born in1567 or soon afterwards, the only son of Alexander Alexander and hiswife Marion. Birth records of the parish of Logie, of which Menstriewas part have been lost. Little o
2 CONC r nothing is known of his earliestyears spent in Menstrie Castle. When his father died in 1581 he andhis two sisters were left in charge of his grand uncle, JamesAlexander , a well-placed Burgess of Sterling
