1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===MR. JOHN EDMONSTONE OF BROICH===
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''Tutor of Duntreath; Commissioner for War for the County of Stirling; afterwards Mr. John Edmonstone of Bailliebentrone in County Antrim, Ireland'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>'''It should not be regarded as being certain but it is possible that John Edmonstone of Broich, here treated, was a lawyer. As much is suggested by the fact that he is referred to as Mr. John Edmonstone of Broich in a
2 CONC  charter witnessed by his son William on 29 April 1677 [https://archive.org/details/calendaroflaing00edin/page/646/mode/1up Laing Charters: 2770] and, post mortem and more significantly, perhaps, in the testament dative and invent
2 CONC ory given up in his name by his daughter Katherine, [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000138105855839& Image of Testament] It was not usual at this period in Scottish history to award the abbreviated title Mr. (Magis
2 CONC ter in Latin, Master in English) to men of ordinary status. It is true that it was sometimes used as an honorary title, more often than not for writers or lawyers, but more frequently it was used to identify someone who held the d
2 CONC egree of Master of Arts. One of  Mr. John Edmonstone's grandsons was a lawyer or writer in Edinburgh, also named Mr. John Edmonstone. He is noticed using this style when he acted himself as cautioner for his aunt Katherine, when s
2 CONC he give up her deceased father's testament. [Ibidem] [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000138105855839& Image of Testament]'''</blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mr. John Edmonstone of Broich is the third son of William Edmonstone of Duntreath. [https://archive.org/details/parishofstrathbl00smit/page/96/mode/1up Strathblane: 96] Following the death of his older brother James in 1644 he 
2 CONC was appointed tutor to his nephews, William Edmonstone and James Edmonstone, the sons of his eldest brother Archibald Edomstone of Duntreath, who were still minor children at this time. He was appointed to attend the Scottish Parl
2 CONC iament of 1649, who nominated him as Commissioner for War for the County of Stirling. [https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalacco00inedmo#page/49/mode/1up Duntreath: 49]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===An Account of His Family===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''‘The seventh Laird of Duntreth called, William Edmonston, married Issobel Hadden, Daughter to the Laird of Glennegies, and had by her five Sons and two Daughters, . . . . . . . . . . John Edmonston third Son to said William Edm
2 CONC onston of Duntreth, married Eliz. Edmonston, Daughter to John Edmonston of Broich, and got with her both the Lands of Broich in Scotland, and the Lands of Bellibantro in Ireland; and had by her four Sons and five Daughters; Willia
2 CONC m his Eldest Son, James his second Son, Robert his third Son, and [blank space] Edmonston his fourth Son: His eldest Daughter called Issobel, married to Crawfurd of Walston, and had issue; his second Daughter called, Jean; his thi
2 CONC rd; his fourth Helen; and his fifth Agnes, married to Andrew Adair, and had issue. Secondly the said John Edmonston married Catherine Cunningham, of the family of Glengarnock, and had by her five Sons and two Daughters: The Eldest
2 CONC  Son called Archibald; the second John; the third Son James; the fourth son Robert; the fifth Son Alexander: His Eldest Daughter called, Katherine, and Anna his second Daughter. William Edmonston Eldest Son of the said John Edmons
2 CONC ton by his first Wife, married Jean Buchanan, of the Family of Buchanan, and had by her six Sons and four Daughters; William, John, Archibald, Robert, James; his Daughters, Eliz. Mary Jean & Anna-Helena: James Edmonston the third 
2 CONC Son of the said John Edmonston by his second Wife, married Margaret Lindsay, Daughter to the Laird of Dow-hill in Fife, and had by her two Sons and five Daughters; John and Robert, Katherine, Anna-Helena, Margaret, Eliz, and Letti
2 CONC ce’ [https://drive.google.com/file/d/1n8MLsVdxL72BKPF67Ed_FBAwP0AyuReU/view Duntreth: pp. 13-4] Link supplied by Tamás Flinn Caldwell-Gilbert'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The testament dative of Mr. John Edmonstone is defective and does not identify the date of his death, which is recorded there as 16 - -.  All that can reasonably be deduced from this is that he probably died before 31 December 
2 CONC 1699. Neither is his place of death identified in his testament dative but it seems that he may have been the heritable proprietor of Bailliebentrone in County Antrim, Ireland, and it seems possible that he died there. [National R
2 CONC ecords of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Dative and Inventory of Mr. John Edmonstoun, sometime of Broich, thereafter of Bailliebentrone, Co. Antrim, Ireland, reference CC8/8/87]''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Testament Dative and Inventory===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Testament Dative and Inventory of Mr. John Edmonstoun, sometime of Broiche, afterwards of Bailliebentrone in County Antrim, Ireland, was given up by his daughter Katherine on 16 March 1721. At the time of his death, her fat
2 CONC her, John Edmonstoun of Bailliebentrone, was owed one thousand merks Scots money by Archibald Edmonstoun of Duntreath. The Commissary Court of Edinburgh recognised her as the only executor dative and nearest of kin to her deceased
2 CONC  father. Thereafter, John Edmonstoun, writer in Edinburgh, who identified himself as the eldest son of Mr. James Edmonstoun of Bailliebentrone, acted himself as cautioner for Katherine Edmonstoun, and confirmation was granted to h
2 CONC er on 14 February 1721. [National Records of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Dative and Inventory of Mr. John Edmonstoun, sometime of Broich, thereafter of Bailliebentrone, Co. Antrim, Ireland, reference CC8/8/
2 CONC 87] [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000138105855839& Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===First Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Edmonstone of Broich married Elizabeth Edmonstone, daughter of James Edmonstone and Elizabeth Edmonstone, with who he got the lands of Ballbantry in Ireland. They are said to have had several children, [https://archive.org
2 CONC /details/genealogicalacco00inedmo/page/47/mode/1up Duntreath: 47]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children of the First Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/William-Edmonstone-of-Broich/6000000138010278878
