1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page on Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 2nd Baronet:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p18515.htm#i185149
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 2nd Bt.1
2 CONT *M, #185149, 
2 CONT *b. 19 August 1621
2 CONT *Last Edited=5 Feb 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 2nd Bt. was born on 19 August 1621.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Helen Douglas, daughter of William Douglas of Cavers.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 1st Bt. and Rachel Johnston.[1] He succeeded to the title of 2nd Baronet Bruce, of Stenhouse [N.S., 1628] in February 1630.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He gained the rank of Colonel in 1649 in the service of the county of Stirling.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 2nd Bt. and Helen Douglas
2 CONT *1. Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, 3rd Bt.+[1] d. Mar 1682
2 CONT *2. unknown daughter Bruce [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1. [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 547. Hereinafter cited as Burke's P
2 CONC eerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *2. [S15] George Edward Cokayne, editor, [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524382#page/n377/mode/2up The Complete Baronetage], 5 volumes (no date (c. 1900); reprint, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 1983), volume II, p
2 CONC age 358. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Baronetage.
2 CONT *Diary of Sir Archibald Johnston, Lord Wariston Author: Scottish History Society; Constable, T. and A., Edinburgh; Warriston, Archibald Johnston, Lord, 1611-1663; Mar, John Erskine, earl of, 1675-1732; Grant, Anne MacVicar, 1755-1
2 CONC 838. Publisher: Edinburgh, Printed at the University Press by T. and A. Constable for the Scottish History Society 1896. Page: 1 to 33. [https://archive.org/stream/diaryofsirarchib26scot#page/n19/mode/2up Archive.Org] When Sir Jam
2 CONC es Skene, President of the Court, failed, notwithstanding the King's orders, to be present at the Kirk of Edinburgh on Easter Day 1619 to receive the Communion kneeling as prescribed by one of the Articles of Perth, his absence wa
2 CONC s ascribed by some ' not to conscience, but to dissuasions of his mother-in-law' (Rachel Arnot) ' and her daughter, his wife "* (Jonet Johnstoun), 'a religious gentlewoman.''^ The other daughter, Rachel, was no doubt of the same w
2 CONC ay of thinking. Her eldest son. '''Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse''', was a ruling elder in James Guthrie's separate Presbytery, which was composed of the most extreme or Remonstrant members of the party.- And Burnet wrote of his 
2 CONC own mother, ' Guthry, the ' chief of their preachers, was hid in my mothers house, who ' was bred to her brother Waristoun's principles, and could ' never be moved from them.' ^ The steadfastness of some of Wariston's own children
2 CONC  to his principles will be afterwards noticed. - Page n26
