1 NAME Sir Alexander /Bruce/
2 GIVN Sir Alexander
2 _MARNM Bruce
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT SEP 1655
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} " That samyn yere the XIX day of Januare, James the thrid [sic] helde his first Parliament at Edinburgh, unto the quhilk Parliament thar was forfaltit Schir Alexander Levingstoun, lord Kalendar, and James Dundas of
2 CONC  that ilk, and Robert Bruce the lard of Clackmanan's brother, and James of Levingstoun, sone and air to the said Alexander, was put to deid, and Robyne of Levingstoune of Lithgu, that tyme comptroUar was put to deid, baith togidde
2 CONC r on the Castel hill thar heidis stricken of"
2 CONT
2 CONT From these extracts I think there can be no doubt that a Robert Bruce was executed on this occasion, but who he was seems very doubtful ; if, as Crawford thinks, he was Robert Bruce of Airth and consequently son to Edward Bruce an
2 CONC d Agnes de Erth, he could not have been, as Asloan states, the laird of Clackmannan's brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also stated ' that in a charter of James ii. to Alexander Bruce of Stanehous, 26 December 1451, Alexander Bruce is. styled sou of Bobert Bruce of Airth, but this is not the case, the charter not mentioning Alexander's parent
2 CONC age at all. In fact, of this Robert, as well as of his reputed father Edward, we know nothing, nor have we any proof of his existence even, unless he is the Robert mentioned by Buchanan and Asloan as having been executed in Januar
2 CONC y 1449-50, of which there is no proof
2 CONT
2 CONT It would appear then that the first of the family of Bruce of Airth, of whom we have any authentic account, is Alexander Bruce of Stanehouse ; that he was a son of Robert Bruce who was executed, January 7, 1449-50, and grandson of
2 CONC  Edward Bruce and Agnes de Erth, seems from the following facts to be most improbable.
2 CONT
2 CONT We find Alexander Bruce of Stanehous obtaining a charter from Lawrence, lord Abernethy, 21 January 1449-50 [just two days after his reputed father was beheaded], of a piece of land in Stanehous.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then in December 1451 he gets a charter from James ii. of the lands of Lethbertscheillis, etc. Again in 1452, 1456, 14G4, he gets charters from the Abbot of Holyrood and James II. of portions of Airth, which was probably the first
2 CONC  footing of the Bruce family in that place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander is witness to a charter 12th February 1463-64, from Agnes de Erth to Alexander Forrester and Agnes Levynston ; this is the only transaction in which Alexander Bruce of Stenhous and his supposed grandmother Agnes de Erth,
2 CONC  are mentioned together, as far as we know.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is called Sir Alexander by Mrs. Gumming Bruce, but there does not appear to be any proof that he was a knight. His wife's name is proved to have been " Jonete," but there is no evidence [except a 17th century document " found a
2 CONC mongst the Blair writs, giving the genealogy of the Bruces of Airth from this Alexander downwards, but not tracing it further than to him] that she was a daughter of the Lord Livingston, as stated by Mrs. Cumming Bruce. The geneal
2 CONC ogy'' before alluded to states that by Lord Livingston's daughter Alexander had no issue, and that he married secondly a daughter of Malcohn Forrester of Torwoodhead, and by her had six sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander was dead in 1487.This is all we know for certain of Alexander Bruce of Stanehous, and '''from him the pedigree of the Bruces of Airth is complete down to Alexander, the last of the line, who died in 1665.'''
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/brucesofairththe00arms/page/n29/mode/1up?q=janet+livingston&view=theater The Bruces of Airth and their cadets by Armstrong, William Bruce p 14-6]
