1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===JOHN FAWSYDE OF THAT ILK===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Fawsyde of that Ilk, here treated, is the son of Dame Janet Lawson,  [https://archive.org/details/registerprivyco07coungoog/page/n206/mode/1up RPC 1633-35: pp. 152-4] and, since her first husband was James Fawsyde of that 
2 CONC Ilk, it follows that he must be their son.  [https://archive.org/details/registrummagnisi08scot/page/14/mode/1up RMS 1620-33: 43]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Fawsyde of that Ilk, here treated, died intestate
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Testament Dative===
2 CONT
2 CONT Fawsyd
2 CONT Johne
2 CONT 1/3/1642 of that Ilk, parish of Tranent, constabulary of Haddington Testament Dative and Inventory Edinburgh Commissary Court CC8/8/60
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Additional Testament Dative and Inventory===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Confirmation was granted on 25 July 1646. [National Records of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Dative and Inventory of Johne Fawsyde of that Ilk, parish of Tranent, constabulary of Haddington, reference CC8/
2 CONC 8/62]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===His Additional Testament Dative and Inventory===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Confirmation was granted on 15 October 1646. [National Records of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Dative and Inventory of Johne Fawsyde of that Ilk, parish of Tranent, constabulary of Haddington, reference C
2 CONC C8/8/62]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/James-Fawsyde/6000000176307526887?through=6000000176172139869
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Evidence from the National Records of Scotland===
2 CONT
2 CONT                     1
2 CONT
2 CONT '''27 June 1620: REX,— cum consensu &c. (207. 175.), — confirmavit cartam D. Joannis Cokburiie de Ormestoun militis, unius senatorum collegii justitie, — [qua, cum consensu Domine Eliz. Bellenden conjugis sue, — pro iinpletione li
2 CONC terarum suarum dispositionis, et pro 8000 mercarum persolutis, — vendidit JONETE LAWSONE relicte Jacobi Fawsyde feodatarii de eodem, heredibus ejus et assignatis quibuscunque, irredimabiliter, — terras de Bowscheilhill, cum tenent
2 CONC ibus &c, in constab. de Hadingtoun, vic. Edinburgh : — Reddend. regi 6 lib. feudifirme ; cum 3 sectis ad 3 placita capitalia apud locum de Kelso ; necnon duplum feudi- firme in introitu heredum : — cum precepto sasine: — Test. M. 
2 CONC Tho. Henrysoun uno commissariorum de Edinburgh, M. Pat. Bannatyne scriba, Jo. Cas scriba signeti regii (scriptore carte) : — Apud Edinburgh, 27 Jun. 1620] : — Test, ut in aliie cards &c. xlix. 253. [https://archive.org/details/reg
2 CONC istrummagnisi08scot/page/14/mode/1up RMS 1620-33: 43]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     2
2 CONT
2 CONT '''13 August 1630: Precept of sasine by George, Earl of Wintoun, for infefting JOHN FAWSYD OF THAT ILK, AS HEIR TO HIS GRANDFATHER, MR. ROBERT FAWSYD OF THAT ILK, in the £10 lands of old extent of Wester Fawsyd in the barony of Tr
2 CONC anent, constabulary of Hadingtoun, sheriffdom of Edinburgh. Seal (good impression) on tag. [http://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD1%2f402%2f24&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=fawsyd&ko=p&r=&ro=s
2 CONC &df=&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Wester Falside and Grant-Suttie Writs, reference GD1/402/24]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT                     3
2 CONT
2 CONT '''28 November 1633: Complaint by Sir Thomas Hope of Craighall, King's Advocate, Sir William Murray of Touchadame, Sir Alexander Seatoun of Graden, Sir John Stirline of Carden, and Wlliam Stirline of Ardoch, as follows : On 3rd Oc
2 CONC tober last JOHN FAWSIDE OF THAT ILK became cautioner in their Lordships' books for JANET LAWSOUN, widow of Sir John Edmistoun of Ednam, that she would remove from the place of Ednem and lands of the same, and also take her goods o
2 CONC ff the meadows thereof and repone the complainers therein, except the particular farms of which she was in possession before the riot committed by her against them, and that within six days after the date of the act of caution und
2 CONC er a penalty of 3000 merks. Nevertheless upon the said 3rd of October the said DAME JANET directed her brother-in-law, Mr. William Cockebume, to the town of Ednem, and by her order he commanded the tenants to lead in their corns u
2 CONC nteinded and in no wise to permit the complainers or their servants to lead the teinds, nor to furnish carts to them for this purpose, which formerly they were in use to do. Further, the said Mr. William Cockebume, understanding t
2 CONC hat the complainers were coming to Ednem to receive possession, " to ludifie and mocke thame sent to Williame Cranstoun in Morestoun, who formerlie possest the saids lands, in thair name to come and receave possessioun upoun the m
2 CONC orne thereafter, being the fourt day of October, bot that same day he strake aff the lockes of the chamber doore, tooke the same with him, left the doores opin, drave the ladeis goods over ane little burne, and so, as if he had sa
2 CONC tisfied the said act, tooke instruments that he had offered possessioun to the compleaners, who wer not there to receave the same ; bot within a short apace thereafter the same wer brought backe agane to the Maynes of Ednem, where
2 CONC  they pastoured ; and when the compleaners come to receave possessioun the said Mr Williame absented himselfe and departed so as the compleaners could find nane to give thame possessioun, bot found the ladeis goods pastouring upon
2 CONC  the ground ; and having past throw the ground they found the tennents leading thair cornes unteinded," stating that they did so by order of Mr William Cockeburne in the lady's name. Further, the said William Cranstoun having brou
2 CONC ght his goods and oxen to the lands of Ednem on the said 4 th of October to take possession, AL TO THE SAID LADY, George Fortoun, her servant, with a great number of shearers, about three score persons, armed with forkes and great
2 CONC  staves, at the said lady's instigation, pursued the said William Cranstoun's servant and goods for their lives with stones and forks, drove them back to the lands of Stitchell and threatened to " fell " them if they came there. T
2 CONC he complainers complained to James Cockeburne of Eysla, sheriff of Berwick, and craved redress at his hands, but he replied that " he could not helpe thame, becaus he wes not shireff in that part, and the shearers wer all the lade
2 CONC is servants." Thereupon the complainers went out of the town and caused the goods to be brought back so as to take possession and see if any responsible person would hinder them, when the said son and servant, seeing this, came, a
2 CONC ccompanied by the company of shearers foresaid, from the place where they were shearing about a quarter of a mile away, and with swords, staves and stones, pursued them for their lives, wounded several of their servants, and barba
2 CONC rously injured some of the cattle. Thereafter Thomas Broun in Ednem " caused ring the bell of Ednem, and when the ministers wife stayed him he upbraided her with contumelious speeches and thereafter come furth with Williame Dickso
2 CONC un, younger, Williame Broun, and Charles Robertsoun in Ednem, with others thair complices, and with convocatioun of his Majestis lieges to the nomber of ane hundreth persons boddin with swords, forkes and lang speiris, all of the 
2 CONC speciall causing and hounding out of the said Ladie Ednem, and of new sett upon the compleaners, hurt and woundit thair servants, slew ane of thair oxin, hurt the rest with forkes, and drave thame away be the space of ane myle, ev
2 CONC er striking thame with thair forkes ; and within ane houre thereafter the said Dame Jonet her selfe come to Ednem, and finding her directiouns in the violences foresaids to be satisfied, she allowed of the same." However, to take 
2 CONC away all pretexts and excuses, the complainers sent George Stirline to her to ask if she would give them possession and suffer them to lead the teinds, which she refused, saying that she would do no more than she had done. So she 
2 CONC still keeps her goods upon the ground, and discharges the tenants to attend the complainer's courts or pay their rents, whereby she has contraveened the said act. Her cautioner should therefore be decerned to pay the penalty of 30
2 CONC 00 merks, and her ladyship and other persons named should be punished for the insolencies foresaid. Charge having been given to the ssdd DAME JANET LAWSOUN, JOHN FAWSYDE OF THAT ILK and ALEXANDER FAWSYDE, HER SONS, George Fortoun,
2 CONC  his servant, Thomas and William Broun, Charles Robertson and William Dicksoun, younger, and the pursuers not compearing, but DAME JANET LAWSOUN being personally present and producing an instrument under the sign and subscription 
2 CONC of James Mader, notary, dated at Ednem, 22nd November instant, to the effect that William Broun in Ednem, as procurator for her, went to the two barns of Ednem Maynes called " the peis barn and the oatt barn at nyne houres in the 
2 CONC morning or thereby and opened the barn doores," and declared that he was ready to repone Sir William Murrey of Touchadame, Sir Alexander Seatoun of Kilcreuche, Sir John Stirline of Carden, William Stirline of Ardoch and William Cr
2 CONC anstoun in Moreistoun to the possession thereof, and was willing to deliver the keys thereof to them, and because none of these persons, nor any one in their name, attended to receive the same, he passed to the personal presence o
2 CONC f Marion Seatoun, wife of the said William Cranstoun, who was in the manor place of Ednem for the time, and delivered to her four keys, being the keys of the whole doors, back and fore, of the said barns, which he desired her to r
2 CONC eceive so that the persons foresaid should enter into possession, and that the said Marion Seatoun took the said keys, therefore the Lords assoilzie the said lady and remanent defenders and also her cautioner from the conclusions 
2 CONC of this summons, and declare them free thereof. [https://archive.org/details/registerprivyco07coungoog/page/n206/mode/1up RPC 1633-35: pp. 152-4]'''
2 CONT
