1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Gibson,_Lord_Durie_II]
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>GIBSON, Sir ALEXANDER, Lord Durie</strong> (d. 1656), Scottish judge, was the eldest son of Sir Alexander Gibson (d. 1644), by Margaret, eldest daughter of Sir Thomas Craig of Riccarton. He was made a clerk of session con
2 CONC jointly with his father upon the latter's promotion to the bench in 1621. He opposed Charles I's policy respecting the service-book, protested against the royal proclamations of 1638, and petitioned the presbytery of Edinburgh aga
2 CONC inst the bishops, November 1638. He was commissary-general of the forces raised to resist Charles I in 1640, but was afterward knighted 15 March 1641, and made lord clerk register 13 Nov. 1641. He was made a commissioner of the ex
2 CONC chequer 1 Feb. 1645, and sat on the committee of estates (1645–8). He became lord of session in 1646, when he took the title of Lord Durie. He was deprived of his offices in 1649 by the act of classes, after joining ‘the engagemen
2 CONC t.’ He was one of the Scottish commissioners chosen to attend the English parliament in 1652 and 1654. Lamont writes in 1650, ‘Both Durie and his lady were debarred from the table because of their malignancie.’ He died in June 165
2 CONC 6. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was twice married; 
2 CONT
2 CONT first to Marjory Hamilton, by whom he had one daughter; 
2 CONT
2 CONT secondly to Cecilia, daughter of Thomas Fotheringham of Powrie, by whom he left Sir Alexander Gibson of Durie, knt., commissioner to parliament in England for Fife and Kinross 1656–9, and for Fife 1659, who died at Durie 6 Aug. 16
2 CONC 61."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: 'Gibson, Alexander (d.1656) (DNB00)', <em>Wikisource, The Free Library</em>, 14 September 2011, 19:10 UTC, <http://en.wikisource.org/w/index.php?title=Gibson,_Alexander_(d.1656)_(DNB00)&oldid=3439086> [accessed 1 Septembe
2 CONC r 2012]
2 CONT
