2 CONT
2 CONT Jonet/Janet Johnstone  married (1) James Skene, (2) James Inglis.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Johnston-3305
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Janet Johnston was born about 1580 in Edinburgh Scotland, [the daughter of Archibald Johnston and Rachel Arnot. The sister of Joseph Johnston, James Johnston and Rachel (Johnston) Bruce. [1] ??]
2 CONT
2 CONT Janet married Sir James, First Baronet Skene. They were the parents of:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Sir John (Skene) Skene of Curriehill 2nd Bt,
2 CONT # Rachel (Skene) Douglas,
2 CONT # Euphame (Skene) Inglis and
2 CONT # Helen (Skene) Erskine
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT “ His wife was Janet Johnston, daughter of Sir John Johnston of Hilton.”
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT *Diary of Sir Archibald Johnston, Lord Wariston Author: Scottish History Society; Constable, T. and A., Edinburgh; Warriston, Archibald Johnston, Lord, 1611-1663; Mar, John Erskine, earl of, 1675-1732; Grant, Anne MacVicar, 1755-1
2 CONC 838. Publisher: Edinburgh, Printed at the University Press by T. and A. Constable for the Scottish History Society 1896. Page: 1 to 33. [https://archive.org/stream/diaryofsirarchib26scot#page/n19/mode/2up Archive.Org] When Sir Jam
2 CONC es Skene, President of the Court, failed, notwithstanding the King's orders, to be present at the Kirk of Edinburgh on Easter Day 1619 to receive the Communion kneeling as prescribed by one of the Articles of Perth, his absence wa
2 CONC s ascribed by some ' not to conscience, but to dissuasions of his mother-in-law' (Rachel Arnot) ' and her daughter, his wife "* (Jonet Johnstoun), 'a religious gentlewoman.''^ The other daughter, Rachel, was no doubt of the same w
2 CONC ay of thinking. Her eldest son. Sir William Bruce of Stenhouse, was a ruling elder in James Guthrie's separate Presbytery, which was composed of the most extreme or Remonstrant members of the party.- And Burnet wrote of his own mo
2 CONC ther, ' Guthry, the ' chief of their preachers, was hid in my mothers house, who ' was bred to her brother Waristoun's principles, and could ' never be moved from them.' ^ The steadfastness of some of Wariston's own children to hi
2 CONC s principles will be afterwards noticed. - Page n26
2 CONT *peerage.com Janet Johnston, #527940
2 CONT * “Memorials of the family of Skene of Skene, from the family papers, with other illustrative documents.”  Page 113. [http://archive.org/stream/memorialsoffamil00skenuoft#page/112/mode/2up Archive.Org]
