1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Alexander Home''', 1st Earl of Home and 6th Lord Home (c. 1566 – 5 April 1619), was a Scottish nobleman and Lord Warden-general of all the March. He succeeded as the 6th Lord Home, a Lord of Parliament in the Peerage of Scotlan
2 CONC d, in 1575, and he was created Earl of Home in the Peerage of Scotland in 1605.  Alexander, Earl of Home died in London on 5 April 1619.[1][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT The newly created title of Earl of Home was counted in 1605 as a title in the English peerage.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Home,_1st_Earl_of_Home
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT he was son of Alexander Home, 5th Lord Home, by his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1586, Lord Home married Christian, daughter of the 6th Earl of Morton and widow of Laurence, Master of Oliphant.[22] She died without surviving issue by Home. Some records of her household survive including a bill from an
2 CONC  Edinburgh merchant for textiles for her gowns in 1589 and 1590, made by an Edinburgh tailor, Peter Sanderson.[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Home and Christian had some disagreements, according to the English diplomat George Nicholson, who wrote of a separation in October 1595 when Christian carried plate and household stuff from Dunglass Castle to a house in Fife
2 CONC  belonging to the Oliphant family.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife was Mary Dudley Sutton, eldest daughter of the 5th Baron Dudley, the son of the English keeper of Home Castle in 1547 during the Rough Wooing. They married at Bedford House on the Strand on 11 July 1605. Their chil
2 CONC dren included:
2 CONT
2 CONT # James Home, 2nd Earl of Home
2 CONT # Margaret Home, who married the 4th Earl of Moray
2 CONT # Anne Home, who married the 1st Duke of Lauderdale.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow, Mary, Dowager Countess of Home, built Moray House in Edinburgh's Canongate.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
