1 OCCU 8th Baron of Wedderburn; M.P., Sheriff of Berwickshire in 1625; Colonel of Horse and Foot, 6th Baron of Wedderburn
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir David 1st Bart'''. succeeded 1617. Was named principal Sheriff of the county of Berwick in 1625, and created a Baronet by K. Charles I. between 1631 and 1640. He was an old man, killed at the battle of Dunba
2 CONC r in 1650, fighting against Cromwell. A great many of the Scottish prisoners taken in this battle were sold as slaves in Barbadoss. Married Margaret, daughter of Sir John Home of Cowdingknows, widow of Sir Mark Ker of Dolphinston 
2 CONC or, Elizabeth, daughter of Sir James Home of Whiteriggs, Printed case.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT * Drummond's Histories of Noble British Families, William Pickering, London, 1844, Part VI., Dunbar, Hume and Dundas Families, page 24
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~hume/tree/1358.htm
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524382#page/n461/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1900; Vol. II; page 442
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT @R853374904@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid
2 CONC =51268345&pid=924
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''Sir David Home, of Wedderburn, 1st Baronet'''
2 CONT * https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I29468&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes:''' 
2 CONT  	 1 - George had an only son, Sir David Home of Wedderburn, slain at the battle of Dunbar in 1650, with his son, George Home, whose son, also named George, inherited the estate. [http://www.electricscotland.com/history/nation/hom
2 CONC e.htm]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - 1650 - Battle Of Dunbar
2 CONT
2 CONT The Calvinists were in support of Charles II when he became King and signed both the Covenants in 1649. Cromwell, however, was not. With sixteen thousand men he invaded Scotland in July and headed for Edinburgh, expecting to rende
2 CONC zvous with his supply ships at Leith. David Leslie and the Scots army did not let him. By August Cromwell had back-tracked to Dunbar. As the Scots followed, Covenanting ministers dismissed from among them any who did not meet thei
2 CONC r godly standards. Least satisfactory were the professional soldiers, most of whom were told to leave, and Leslie's force dropped by five thousand, leaving what was described by an appalled officer as 'an army of clerks and minist
2 CONC ers' sons'. Even so, by September Cromwell's men were pushed up against the sea at Dunbar with no sign of their supplies, and with their premium position on Doune Hill above, the Scots had only to wait for the already weak enemy t
2 CONC o surrender.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patience was not among the fervent ministers' virtues. Ignoring Leslie and knowing nothing of military tactics, they sent his twenty thousand men down the hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was the consummate military blunder. Three thousand were killed and ten thousand captured. Edinburgh Castle belonged to Cromwell by December.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources:'''  	
2 CONT * [S6] Stirnet Genealogy, Peter Barns-Graham, Home03.
2 CONT * [S6] Stirnet Genealogy, Peter Barns-Graham, Home02.
