2 DATE 20 JUL 1679
2 ADDR
3 STAE Scotland
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p34926.htm#i349260
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Laurence Oliphant, 2nd of Gask''' was the son of Laurence Oliphant, 1st of Gask and Lilias Graham.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT A contract for the marriage of Sir Laurence Oliphant, 2nd of Gask and Lilias Oliphant was signed on 19 February 1634.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 20 July 1679.1
2 CONT
2 CONT He had four other sons (who died unmarried) and two other daughters.1
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.clanmacfarlanegenealogy.info/genealogy/TNGWebsite/getperson.php?personID=I14618&tree=CC
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Laurence Oliphant, 2nd of Gask and Lilias Oliphant, (married 1634): 
2 CONT
2 CONT # 	Patrick Oliphant, of Williamstown,   d. 1694. Married Margaret Murray.  Disinherited.
2 CONT #  	 Laurence Oliphant, 3rd of Gask,   d. Aug 1672, Coldingham, Berwickshire, Scotland.  apprenticed in 1656 to Robert Wallace, W.S. He died before his father on 20th August 1772, and is buried at Cask. He married, July 1661, Anna
2 CONC , daughter of Sir George Preston of Valleyfield, and left three sons and two daughters.
2 CONT #  	Lilias Oliphant,   d. Yes, date unknown.  married November 1668, to James Graeme of Orchill. They had six children, of whom four survived their parents. James Graeme died June, 1707, and his wife Lilias survived him.
2 CONT #  	Elizabeth Oliphant,   d. Bef 1733.  married May 1676, to Gavin Drummond of Belliclone. Both were dead in 1733.
2 CONT #  	Anna Oliphant,   d. 17 Oct 1732.  married April 1672, to Colin Campbell2 of Monzie.
2 CONT #  	Andrew Oliphant, WS,   d. Bef 15 Mar 1666. in a document dated 1659 designed a Writer to the Signet,1 who was dead 15th March 1666. 
2 CONT #  	Archibald Oliphant,   b. Bef 1646,   d. Bef 1686, Dsp - Died Without Children.  (Age ~ 40 years) who was apprenticed to David Scott, apothecary in Edinburgh, in December 1664. He was of age before 1667, and was afterwards a ch
2 CONC emist and burgess of Edinburgh. He married at Edinburgh, 1669, Anna, daughter of John Drummond, in Fintloch, who survived him and died 1694. He died without leaving children before 1686.
2 CONT #  	Charles Oliphant,   d. Yes, date unknown
2 CONT #  	David Oliphant,   d. 2 Nov 1707, Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland. who was a merchant burgess of Perth, and was in the Perthshire Militia in 1680. He died at Edinburgh, 2nd November 1707, and was buried in the Greyfriars.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 - Oliphant of Gask: A few days after the battle [of Tippermuir 1644], Oliphant of Gask deserted [the Covenanter side] and joined Montrose.
2 CONT [ http://www.alternative-perth.org/tippermuir.htm ]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - Laurence Oliphant was not the "Cask" who is said to have caused the loss of the battle of Tippermuir to the Covenanters by deserting and going over to Montrose. Gask was one of the Atholl titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT (THE OLIPHANTS OF CASK RECORDS OF A JACOBITE FAMILY BY E. MAXTONE GRAHAM) (E. BLAIR OLIPHANT) PUBLISHED 1910 IN LONDON BY JAMES NISBET & CO., LTD. 22 BERNERS STREET, W. )
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 - Laurence, the eldest son of Laurence and Lilias Oliphant, was established as second Laird of Gask in August 1632. His Great Seal charter is dated 9th March 1633. He married eighteen months after his father's death Lilias, the 
2 CONC eldest daughter of his cousin Patrick, sixth Lord Oliphant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In view of the part subsequently taken by his descendants, it is strange to learn that Laurence Oliphant of Cask signed the Covenant; but then so did his brilliant kinsman across the Strath, Montrose, and so did many who were afte
2 CONC rwards distinguished Royalists. There were circumstances which made it not strange that some of the best blood and the bravest hearts in Scotland should be on the side of what appeared to be liberty and justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Covenant was the charter of all that was dearest to the national character it was to safeguard the royal interests ; and when Montrose first drew his sword in its defence, numberless gentlemen rose with him in a tumult of enth
2 CONC usiasm, without seeing whither the Covenant was going to lead. It was not easy to see issues clearly in those days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurence Oliphant probably signed the Covenant at Perth, when it was "read sworne and subscryvit be the town of Perthe be standing up and everie man upholding their hands ; the women also were movit to stande and sweir."
2 CONT
2 CONT If his mother Lilias, and his wife Lilias, were among these enthusiasts, when Laurence Oliphant signed the Covenant, will never be known. His wife must have had a busy life among her children; to her and her husband were born in c
2 CONC ourse of time nine children. The dates of their births have not come to light. ….
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a known adherent of the Royal Family, and was subjected to the enormous penalty of twenty thousand merks as a fine by Oliver Cromwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laurence Oliphant was not among the rigid Presbyterians led by Argyle ; but neither was he in the gallant ranks of the absolute Royalists the followers of Montrose. In a less daring degree he had the same aim, though he lacked the
2 CONC  fiery ambition and steady purpose of the King's great champion. It is to his credit that he signed the Engagement, and doubtless held himself ready to support it, and disappointing to find him only a few months afterwards apologi
2 CONC sing to the Presbytery of Auchterarder for having subscribed to this treaty. The Church chose to declare it "ane unlawful bond," and charged those who had signed with sin. Oliphant explained that
2 CONT "in the day of temptationne, being threatened with loss of life and fortune, he did put his hand to that bond, yet he did then protest before the Committee of the shire that he did it only in so farre as it agreed with the Covenan
2 CONC t."
2 CONT
2 CONT He said he was " sorrowfull and grieved at his soull," but that there was no public Act of the Kirk to prevent his action. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That Sir Laurence had parties of soldiers quartered at Gask during the Troubles is proved by the following account:
2 CONT " Item : for ye quarteris of 60 horse with ye Byden of my lord Lanerkis owne troupe ye space of thrie days ye 25 May, 1646.
2 CONT "Item: for assisting quarteris to ye troupe of ye Marques Argyles Lievegaird in ye month of May. " Item : to four trowpers of Dalhousie's Regiment fra the 8 of Marche to the 14 thairof, 17 bollis aittis, 2 bollis peas with 40 thre
2 CONC aves of straw."
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2nd January 1651 Charles II. was at Perth with the Committee of Estates. His affairs were desperate enough. Cromwell held Edinburgh Castle, and the King was firmly in the toils of the Covenanters. Worst of all, the great Montro
2 CONC se was dead. Laurence Oliphant went over to Scone, where the King was, with other gentlemen, and there received the honour of knighthood at the King's hand. Perth, itself, was soon to fall into the destructive hands of the enemy, 
2 CONC when the ancient tomb of the Oliphants was destroyed, and the monastery walls pulled down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gask children were born into the " Great Troubles," and through life must have carried the recollection of early years passed in an atmosphere of stormy contentions, and the passion of causes that seemed to change from good to
2 CONC  bad. These were the first " bairnies" of the "Auld Hoose," the first little voices that sounded on the stair, where from generation to generation the
2 CONT light footsteps were to fall, and the voices call in the garden, as group after group through the long years were born, and lived, and died within the old walls. …
2 CONT
2 CONT The mania for destroying witches was at its height during the early years of the Oliphant children. Under the reign of the clergy the superstition grew and flourished, and caused the death of numberless wretched men and women. All
2 CONC  social life was under the control of the clergy and their theological ideas ; " the more vividly the torments of hell are realised" says a writer "the more callous do men become to human sufferings in this world." ….
2 CONT
2 CONT Not till 1690, however, were the civil consequences of the Church's sentence of excommunication done away, and the claws of the Church cut. Meanwhile, social life had been made as dreary as possible, and amusements strongly discou
2 CONC raged. Both music and dancing were banned, and only regained their lost ground quite at the end of the seventeenth century. The consequences of the cutting off of all beauty and light in social life was peculiarly disastrous to th
2 CONC e Celtic temperament. The evils of drink and immorality grew to great heights in the absolute reign of the Church. That the spirit of intolerance stalked unchecked all through the land in the swayings of public thought, and was eq
2 CONC ually virulent on both sides, there is abundant evidence in the numerous pasquils and satires of the day …. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (THE OLIPHANTS OF CASK RECORDS OF A JACOBITE FAMILY BY E. MAXTONE GRAHAM) (E. BLAIR OLIPHANT) PUBLISHED 1910 IN LONDON BY JAMES NISBET & CO., LTD. 22 BERNERS STREET, W. )
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
