1 NAME James /Rollo/ 2nd Lord Rollo
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Rollo
2 NSFX 2nd Lord Rollo
2 _MARNM Rollo
1 NAME James Rollo, 2nd Lord Rollo of Duncrub
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 11 DEC 1600
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 JUN 1669
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>James, second Lord Rollo</strong>, was born on 11 December 1600. He had the honour of knighthood conferred on him by Charles I. on his visit to Scotland in 1633, and upon his father's resignation obtained from that King a
2 CONC  charter of the barony of Duncrub, etc., 18 March 1642. 10 In 1622 he received a licence to go abroad with his father for three years. On 18 September 1634 he was appointed a Justice of the Peace for Perthshire, and in 1638 he was
2 CONC  appointed to superintend the subscribing of the King's Covenant in that county. Sir James was closely connected with the two great rivals in the fight between King and Covenant ; his first wife was a sister of Montrose, and his s
2 CONC econd wife was a sister of Argyll. Thus he was brother-in-law to both Caesar and Pompey. He sided with the Covenant, and was a colonel of horse in Perthshire, and served on Committees of War 1646-49. In May 1643 'Sir James Rollok 
2 CONC and Sir Mungo Campbell were, by the Marquess of Argyll and the rest, commissionated to go to Montrose, and to make offer that, if he would leave off his counter-working and comply with them, all his debts should be discharged, and
2 CONC  himself preferred to the highest place of command next to General Lesley.' Shortly after, at the interview between Mr. Alexander Henderson and Montrose, Sir James was also present. He was with Argyll during Montrose's campaign, a
2 CONC nd, on the day before the battle of Inverlochy, accompanied him on his barge. He was a Commissioner for Revaluation of Clackmannanshire 1649 ; a Commissioner of Supply for Perthshire 1655, 1656, 1659 ; a Commissioner of Excise 166
2 CONC 1. In 1661 an Act of Parliament was passed ratifying to him the barony of Duncrub and burgh of Doning. He took a principal part in the obsequies of the remains of Montrose in 1661." In 1662 he was fined 6000 for his part in the tr
2 CONC oubles beginning in 1637. In 1663 he was appointed a Justice of the Peace. He attended  Parliament 1661-63, 1665. 'The Lord Rollocke depairted out of this life att his dwelling-house, and was interred att Dinnen or thereby, the 12
2 CONC  of June [1669] att night.'
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, first, on 24 April 1628, Dorothea Graham, third daughter of John, Earl of Montrose, by his wife Margaret Ruthven, daughter of William, Earl of Gowrie. The great Marquess was present, and 'the feasting upon this occasio
2 CONC n . . . was scarcely less than at the funeral of her father, and lasted from 22 to 29 April.' ' She died s.p. 16 May 1638, and was interred at Holyrood the 8 of June. He married, secondly, on 20 March 1642, Mary Campbell, daughter
2 CONC  of Archibald, seventh Earl of Argyll, by his second wife Anne, fourth and youngest daughter of Sir William Cornwallis of Broome (by Lucy, his wife, third daughter and coheir of John Nevil, Lord Latimer), by whom he had issue : 
2 CONT * Andrew, third Lord Rollo. 
2 CONT * Archibald, a major in the Army. He was alive 7 September 1682.
2 CONT * Margaret, married (contract 7 September 1682) to Sir George Oliphant of Newton.
2 CONT * Mary."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft#page/200/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. VII, page 200
