1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir Robert Moray (alternative spellings: Murrey, Murray)</strong> (1608 or 1609 – 4 July 1673) was a Scottish soldier, statesman, diplomat, judge, spy, freemason and natural philosopher. He was well known to Charles I and
2 CONC  Charles II, and the French cardinals Richelieu and Mazarin. He attended the meeting of the 1660 committee of 12 on 28 November 1660 that led to the formation of the Royal Society, and was influential in gaining its Royal Charter 
2 CONC and formulating its statutes and regulations...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...Moray was the elder of two sons of a Perthshire laird, Sir Mungo Moray of Craigie. His grandfather was Robert Moray of Abercairney (near Crieff), and his mother was a daughter of George Halket of Pitfirran, Dunfermline. An uncl
2 CONC e, David Moray, had been a personal servant of Henry Frederick, Prince of Wales...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...In Scotland, Moray became Lord Justice Clerk, a Privy Councillor, and a Lord of Session in 1651. He married Sophia Lindsay, daughter of David Lindsay, 1st Lord Balcarres, but she died in childbirth on 2 January 1653 and the chi
2 CONC ld was stillborn. Moray joined a Scottish uprising in 1653 which was suppressed by Cromwell, and Moray returned to the continent in 1654. Moray spent time in Bruges in 1656, then in Maastricht until 1659, when he joined Charles in
2 CONC  Paris. Following the restoration of Charles II, Moray was one founders of the Royal Society at its first formal meeting on Wednesday 28 November 1660, at the premises of Gresham College on Bishopsgate, at which Christopher Wren, 
2 CONC Gresham Professor of Astronomy, delivered a lecture. The twelve in attendance were an interesting mix of four Royalists (William Brouncker, 2nd Viscount Brouncker, Alexander Bruce, 2nd Earl of Kincardine, Sir Paul Neile, William B
2 CONC alle) and six Parliamentarians (John Wilkins, Robert Boyle, Jonathan Goddard, William Petty, Lawrence Rook, Christopher Wren) and two others with less fixed (or more flexible) views, Abraham Hill and Moray. Moray was influential i
2 CONC n gaining the new society its Royal Charter and formulating its statutes and regulations."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'Robert Moray', <em>Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia</em>, 31 August 2012, 11:48 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Robert_Moray&oldid=510098237> [accessed 24 October 2012]
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * "<em>[http://www.westminster-abbey.org/our-history/people/robert-moray Sir Robert Moray]</em>", Westminster Abbey
2 CONT * Hogg, Bruce; [http://www.freemasonry.london.museum/os/wp-content/resources/frs_freemasons_complete_jan2012.pdf Freemasons and the Royal Society ed 2]; Library and Museum of Freemasonry; January 2012; page 82
