1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == JAMES GRAHAM Marquess of Montrose ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Genealogy ==
2 CONT
2 CONT ’’[https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun06pauluoft/page/n6/mode/1up The Scots Peerage : founded on Wood's ed. of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland; containing an historical and genealogical account of the nobility of
2 CONC  that kingdom. Edited by Sir James Balfour Paul, Lord Lyon King of Arms. Volume VI. (David Douglas, Edinburgh, 1909), 601 pp.]’’’
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose (25 October 1612 – 21 May 1650) was a Scottish nobleman and soldier, who initially joined the Covenanters in the Wars of the Three Kingdoms, but subsequently supported King Charles I as the 
2 CONC English Civil War developed. From 1644 to 1646, and again in 1650, he fought a civil war in Scotland on behalf of the King and is generally referred to in Scotland as simply "the Great Montrose".
2 CONT
2 CONT His "spectacular" victories, which took his opponents by surprise, are remembered in military history for their tactical brilliance."
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Graham,_1st_Marquess_of_Montrose
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=35386505
2 CONT
2 CONT http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I77719&tree=Nixon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p2815.htm#i28147
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations / Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003),  volume 1, page 1427. volume 2, page 2753. Hereinafter
2 CONC  cited as Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S21] #226 The Peerage of Scotland: Containing an Historical and Genealogical Account of the Nobility of That Kingdom, from Their Origin to the Present Generation (2nd edition, 1813), Douglas, Sir Robert, (2nd edition. 2 volumes. 
2 CONC Edinburgh: A. Constable, 1813 NOTE: Caution should be taken with this peerage, and compared with other peerages to obtain accurate information about the families. Some of the lineages are confused, but can be used for supplemental
2 CONC  information.), FHL book Q 941 D22d; FHL microfilm 1,440,956 items., vol. 2 p. 240, 243, 515.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.angus.gov.uk/history/features/people/marquismont.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Graham, 5th Earl and 1st Marquis of Montrose,''' was born in October 1612, a long-awaited son and heir for the wealthy Graham family. Sources disagree over the place of his birth, but it is likely to have been at either t
2 CONC he family mansion at Old Montrose, or their town house at Castlestead, Montrose. James must have spent much of his childhood at both these homes, as well as at the main family residence of Kincardine Castle in Perthshire. Shortly 
2 CONC before his death, however, his father sold Castlestead. James succeeded him as Earl at the tender age of fourteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Montrose was one of the most romantic figures in seventeenth century Scotland, a poet and as well as a dashing general of Charles I. In 1644 he raised the Highland clans for the King and won a series of victories but was defeated 
2 CONC at Philiphaugh, near Selkirk. He fled to the continent but returned after the execution of Charles I. He was captured at the battle of Carbisdale and hung and quartered in the Grassmarket of Edinburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT About the artwork:
2 CONT
2 CONT Title:James Graham, 1st Marquess of Montrose, 1612 - 1650. Royalist
2 CONT Accession number:PG 2418 Artist:Unknown Studio of:Sir Anthony van Dyck Depicted:James Graham Gallery:Scottish National Portrait Gallery(On Display) Object type:Painting Subject:Military and naval Writing and literature Aristocracy
2 CONC  Materials: Oil on canvas Date created:About 1636 Measurements:75.60 x 58.40 cm (framed: 85.00 x 73.50 x 4.50 cm) Credit line:Bequeathed by Lady Malise Graham 1978 Photographer:Antonia Reeve
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: National Galleries of Scotland Added by Janet Milburn 11/12/19
