1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == JAMES DUNLOP OF THAT ILK ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Dunlop of that Ilk, here treated, died in March 1683. [NRS: CC8/8/77]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == His Testament Dative and Inventory ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''On 7 April 1683 the Testament Dative and Inventory of the deceased James Dunlop of that Ilk was given up by his second son https://www.geni.com/people/John-Dunlop/6000000223360462880, who is identified as the only executor dati
2 CONC ve decerned as creditor to him. Confirmation was granted to https://www.geni.com/people/John-Dunlop/6000000223360462880 on the same date. [National Records of Scotland, Edinburgh Commissary Court, The Testament Dative of James Dun
2 CONC lop of that Ilk, reference CC8/8/77]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000223535891870 Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT # '''[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000223535850859 Image of Testament]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6 May 1667: Assignation by Antonia Broune [Brown], daughter of deceased Sir John Broune [Brown] of Fordell, with consent of Alexander Dunlope [Dunlop], fiar of that Ilk, her future spouse, to James Dunlope [Dunlop] of that Ilk,
2 CONC  father of said Alexander, of GD26/4/134 [ Note ] in part payment of her tocher as contained in marriage contract dated as above. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD26%2f4%2f144&st=1&t
2 CONC c=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=Dunlop+of+that+ilk&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=1601&dt=1700&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Leslie family, Earls of Leven and Melville, reference GD26/4/144]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note: 10 February 1663: Bond by George, Lord Melvill [Melville], to Antonia Broune [Brown], only daughter of deceased Sir John Broune [Brown] of Fordell, for £720 Scots as the price of a gold chain of 186 links, and 18 ounces w
2 CONC eight. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD26%2f4%2f134&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=&ko=p&r=GD26%2f4%2f134&ro=m&df=1650&dt=1700&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Leslie fa
2 CONC mily, Earls of Leven and Melville, reference GD26/4/134]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Commentary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dunlop's belonged to the Covenanters, those heroic Scots who bound themselves in solemn covenant to uphold their Presbyterian faith and resist to the death all those who tried to force them to change it. During the reign of Ch
2 CONC arles II (1660-1685) Covenanters who gathered secretly in glens and caves to worship God were hunted like animals with bugle and bloodhound. When caught they were hanged or drowned without mercy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Dunlop, 15th of that Ilk, was imprisoned in 1665 along with other Ayrshire lads for active resistance to the Church of England. After two years Dunlop was freed. He then set about securing his property by making over part of
2 CONC  it to the Earl of Dundonald to avoid forfeiture. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander,16th of that Ilk, son of James Dunlop, also suffered imprisonment for the Cause. After being freed, Alexander emigrated to America, that haven of the persecuted, to escape the danger that threatened every staunch Covenan
2 CONC ter during the "Killing Times." In the year 1684, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Dunlop, 17th of that Ilk, Baronet, and son of Alexander, secured the lands that his father had settled on him before leaving for America. In the following year, John received back the lands previously conveyed to the Earl
2 CONC  of Dundonald. These large estates were consolidated into the Barony of Dunlop. On the old site of the Norman Castle, Sir John built the stately mansion known as Dunlop House. '''[https://www.seekingmyroots.com/members/files/G0015
2 CONC 86.pdf The House of Dunlop, p 33 & p. 46]
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Genealogy Tab of the Clan Dunlop website (accessed: Oct. 20, 2017):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clandunlop.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunlop Generation, submitted by Hugh NanKivell of Australia
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleventh Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT 31. James DUNLOP, 15th. of that Ilk.   Born in about 1620 in Dunlop, Ayrshire, Scotland, James died in 1670; he was about 50 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation:   15th. of that Ilk
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 1645, when James was about 25, he married Elizabeth CUNYNGHAME of Corsehill, daughter of Alexander CUNYNGHAME, 5th. of Corsehill & Mary HOUSTOUN of Houston.   She was born in about 1620 in Corsehill, Stewarton, Ayrshire, 
2 CONC Scotland.   Elizabeth died in 1674; she was about 54 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT *39            i.          Alexander (1645-1687)
2 CONT *40           ii.          John (~1655-)
2 CONT *41          iii.          Jean (~1657-)
2 CONT *42          iv.          Marion (~1659-)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT www.clandunlop.com/dunlop_genealogy.htm               Item #32
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rev. Alex.   married twice. 1. Jean Mure 2. Elizabeth Mure.     
2 CONT     4 children born to her and Alex.  the eldest son was William [on linked profile]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
