1 NAME Samuel /Bitfield/ of Boston
2 GIVN Samuel
2 SURN Bitfield
2 NSFX of Boston
2 _MARNM Bitfield
2 NICK Bitsfield
2 NICK Bidsfield
2 NICK Bidfield
2 NICK Bydfield
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 21 FEB 1602
2 ADDR
3 CITY Wrington
3 STAE England
1 BURI
2 DATE SEP 1660
2 ADDR
3 CITY Boston
3 STAE MA
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 SEP 1660
2 ADDR
3 CITY Boston
3 STAE Massachusetts Bay Colony
1 OCCU Cooper
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} SAMUEL BITFIELD.1, a cooper, is thought to have lived first in Braintree, Mass., subsequently moving to Boston where he was granted twenty acres of land February 24, 1640, and was sworn a freeman June 2, 1641. In B
2 CONC oston, October 18, 1648, Thomas Venner, John Mileham, Samuel Bedfield, James Mattuck "and the rest of the coopers of Boston and Charlestowne" were granted leave to "meete together" for the purpose of incorporating into a guild for
2 CONC  mutual protection and for the benefit of the public and to prevent abuses in trade. Coopers represented the most important class of artisans in the Colony's foreign and domestic commerce. They made and repaired barrels, hogsheads
2 CONC , butts, tubs, and casks of some variety, and to them was assigned the responsibility for the safe shipping of liquids and perishables. The ability of shipments to endure long voyages and rough handling depended upon the cooper's 
2 CONC skill. Perhaps their principal products were hogsheads for rum, molasses and fish.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1652, Samuel Bitfield was a constable in Boston. Ruth Bidfield, probably his younger sister, married ca 1643, William Sawyer of Newbury. Samuel died September 10, 1660, his will of May 12, 1659, providing for his wife Elizabeth
2 CONC  and two daughters. His inventory of September 13, 1660, showed a good estate, œ512.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Samuel Bitfield of Boston and his Unknown 1st wife:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT #Elizabeth (Bitfield) (Stevens) Titcomb b "say" 1624; mar 1) William Stevens, 2) as his 2nd, William Titcomb
2 CONT #Mary (Bitfield) Plummer b "say" 1625; mar Samuel Plummer
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Plummer, both the wills of Samuel and his 2nd wife, Elizabeth, imply the daughters were her step-children, and as he says, "it is striking that she did not name Samuel Bitfield's sons-in-law her executors."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Estate 
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Bidfield, cooper of Boston, signed a will on 12 May 1659.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"I give unto my wife my dwelling and yard with the out house intheyard during her life, p'rided shee in this Cuntry and keepe the saydehousein repare, and after her or going out of the Cuntrie intoEngland, my willis, saide house 
2 CONC fall to Samuel Plumer, my grandchild,and to his heirs. Ifhee dye oute issue, it shall goe to the next sonnand his heirs, and soe tonext successively. Unto my wife #40, andhalfe the househould goods; other halfe to bee devided to m
2 CONC y tow[2]dau's children to each of them Alike, that is to say, Samuel Plummer,John Plumer, Ephraim Plumer, Plumer, John Stevens, Samuel Stevens ...to William Dinsdale, my axes, odersand all my other working tooleswith on coat and a
2 CONC  pare of britches."
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=ryder10&id=I10236
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Occupation:  cooper. June 2 1641, freeman; 1652, constable of Boston.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT “SAMUEL BITFIELD .. was baptized at Wrington, Somersetshire, England, on 21 February 1601/2. He died at Boston, Massachusetts, on 1 September 1660...
2 CONT Samuel Bitfield’s widow was ELIZABETH _____, who died, according to the inventory of her estate, on 30 7m [September] 1669. Her will, however, was probated on 23 September 1669..., and the inventory itself was taken on 20 7m [Sept
2 CONC ember] 1669. Her death date may have been 30 August 1669. The wills of both Elizabeth and Samuel Bitfield imply that she was a later wife and not the mother of his children. Samuel thought it possible that she might decide to retu
2 CONC rn to England, despite the fact that his children were in Massachusetts Bay. Her will emphasized her sisters and the children of a cousin who were probably in England. Even though she called Mary Plummer and Elizabeth Titcomb her 
2 CONC daughters, this usage was frequently used for stepdaughters; it is striking that she did not name Samuel Bitfield’s sons-in-law her executors. Samuel Bitfield’s 1616 apprenticeship record in Bristol, Gloucestershire, is given abov
2 CONC e. On 27 March 1622 he was discharged from his apprenticeship because his master was in prison... On 24 February 1639/40, Samuel Bitfield of Mount Woollystone” [i.e., Wollaston] was, for five heads, allotted twenty acres of land, 
2 CONC apparently in that section of Boston, Massachusetts.... On 2 June 1641, he was made a freeman of Massachusetts Bay Colony ... A complaint was registered on 7 August 1646 by Sampson Lane against Mr. Nathaniel Duncan and four others
2 CONC , including Samuel Bitfield, for taking away pipes of wine, a ring, and some money from his cellar; they replied that they had been carrying out an order of the General Court, Mr. Duncan being an officer thereof, and one of the me
2 CONC n a Constable... Samuel Bitfield served as a constable in Boston himself from 8 March 1651/2... Samuel “Bidfield” executed his will on 12 3m [May] 1659. It was probated on 20 September 1660; the inventory had been taken seven days
2 CONC  earlier. He gave his wife [not named] his dwelling house, etc., ‘pr[o]vided shee stay in this cuntry and keepe the sayde house in repare.” At her death or “going out of the cuntrie into England,” the property was to go to testato
2 CONC r’s grandchild Samuel Plumer. To wife £40 and half the household goods; other half to be divided to his two daughters’ children, i.e., Samuell Plumer, John Plumer, Epharim [sic] Plumer, Mary Plumer, John Stevens, and Samuell Steve
2 CONC ns. To John Stevens and Samuell Stevens (not yet twenty-one) £10 each, etc., To testator’s son Samuell Plumer, all wearing cloths. To Mr. Tompson £5, to Mr. Willson 40s., to Mr. Norton £3, to James Penn 40s., to John Seirch 20s., 
2 CONC to William Dinsdale, jointer, axes, oders, etc. Wife was to be executrix; overseers: James Penn and Samuell Plumer. Wits.: Nathaniell Williams, Madett Enges
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
