1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Elizabeth Douglas (alternatively recorded as Lady Elizabeth Douglas) was a 16th-century Scottish noblewoman belonging to the historic house of Douglas of Lochleven and the Earldom of Buchan. Born circa 1574, she was the youngest d
2 CONC aughter of Robert Douglas, Earl of Buchan, and Christina Stewart, Countess of Buchan. Growing up within the highest echelons of the Scottish peerage, her life was defined by the political and territorial alliances typical of the l
2 CONC ate Renaissance nobility in northeastern Scotland.In 1592, Elizabeth entered into a marriage contract with Andrew Fraser, 6th Laird of Muchalls, who would later be elevated to the peerage as the 1st Lord Fraser. She relocated to t
2 CONC he Fraser family holdings in Aberdeenshire, where she served as the primary matriarch during the extensive architectural remodeling of Castle Fraser. Over her marriage, Elizabeth mothered five sons, securing the primary male linea
2 CONC ge of the Lordship of Fraser. She passed away in Aberdeenshire prior to 1624. Her authentic lineage remains highly stable in peerage records, explicitly distinguishing her from the unproven or fictional maternal placeholders frequ
2 CONC ently manufactured by online family tree algorithms.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Robert Douglas, Earl of Buchan (born circa 1540 – died August 18, 1580).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Christina Stewart, Countess of Buchan (born 1547 – died September 20, 1580).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: Circa 1574 in Scotland (calculated based on her marriage contract date and the birth timeline of her elder siblings).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Before May 1624 in Aberdeenshire, Scotland (documented via her husband's remarriage to Anne Drummond after this date).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage & Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Andrew Fraser, 1st Lord Fraser, married under a formal marriage contract dated 1592. Primary Scottish peerage registries and contemporary estate charters verify that she was the biological mother of five chronological sons
2 CONC :
2 CONT 1. Andrew Fraser, 2nd Lord Fraser: Born circa 1593; died before 1657. He succeeded his father as the 2nd Lord Fraser and married Margaret Elphinstone and Anne Haldane.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Francis Fraser of Kinmundie: Date of birth unrecorded; died before 1676. He became a Burgess of Aberdeen and married Marjory Elphinstone.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. William Fraser of Faichfield: Date of birth unrecorded; became a Burgess of Aberdeen in April 1627 and married Jean Barclay.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Thomas Fraser: Date of birth unrecorded; died in November 1617.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. James Fraser: Date of birth unrecorded; died in November 1621.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Documentation===
2 CONT 1592 Marriage Contract: Preserved in Scottish legal registries, explicitly detailing the dowry and territorial alliances established between the House of Douglas and the Laird of Muchalls.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots Peerage Registries: Officially records her maternal relationship to all five of Andrew Fraser’s surviving sons, leaving no room for unproven or algorithm-generated alternative children.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Historical Sources===
2 CONT Sir James Balfour Paul, The Scots Peerage: Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, Vol. II (Buchan) and Vol. IV (Fraser), David Douglas Publishing.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Fraser, The Douglas Book, 1885 (Extensively documenting the lineages, charters, and marriage contracts of the Douglas family).
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Charters of the Lands of Muchalls and Stoneywood, National Records of Scotland (NRS).
2 CONT
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun02pauluoft#page/270/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. II, page 270
