1 NAME John (Ean) John-Mor or Big John /Mor (Mackay)/
2 GIVN John (Ean) John-Mor or Big John
2 _MARNM Mor (Mackay)
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Mackay married Margaret, daughter of Thomas, Lord Lovat, who succeeded to the chieftainship of Fraser in 1501. In the Wardlaw MS., Vol. 34 of the Scottish History Society Publications, she is designed " Margar
2 CONC et Lady McKay," and must have been the wife of John, from the period at which her father flourished. By her he had two daughters, but no male issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He had also an illegitimate son, John Mor, of whom we shall hear more afterwards. The  said John Mor had known issue five sons, viz., Neil; Rory, constable of Borve Castle in Farr during its siege in 1554 ; Murdo, whose son Donald
2 CONC  macMurdo macEan Mor lies buried within the old church of Durness ; John ; and Tonnat : — Rcay Papers. These rive brothers are also mentioned in Pitcairn's Criminal Trials, Vol. I. p. 352.  pg. 95
2 CONT
2 CONT  THE BOOK OF MACKAY BY ANGUS MACKAY, M.A. (St. Andrews University) Minister at Westerdale, Caithness EDINBURGH : NORMAN MACLEOD, -25 GEORGE IV. BRIDGE CANADA : EDWARD MACKAY, ST. PETER'S MANSE, MADOC, ONT. M DCCCC VI
