2 CONT FAMILY OF JAMES AUSTINE AND JEAN ROSS:
2 CONT (All data as above except Batch is C113876 -  all christened at Perth, Perth).
2 CONT MARIONE   c:  22 Jul 1694
2 CONT WILLIAM    c:  17 Jan 1697
2 CONT JOSEPH       c:  21 June 1698
2 CONT ANNA          c:  16  Nov 1701
2 CONT BEATRICH  c:  18  Jan  1705
2 CONT MARRIAGE:  JOSEPH AUSTINE  AND JEAN DRUMMOND  11 Apr 1718 at Errol, Perth,
2 CONT Scotland. Batch M113516, Source 1040070, 102722, Printout 6900660.
2 CONT Their family:
2 CONT ADAM       c:  24 Feb 1719 at Perth, Perth. Batch C113876, Source 1040333.
2 CONT THOMAS   c:  29 June 1721      "         "         "     C113877,     " 1040383.
2 CONT
2 CONT FROM JAMES MACGREGOR'S LETTER OF 31 JULY 1883  TO HIS SOLICITOR BAIN:
2 CONT Bain's letter of 27 July had advised James that a Miss AUSTIN "recently died intestate at Perth leaving such an enormous fortune".  James said the AUSTINS  were of English extraction, the first who came to Scotland at the time of 
2 CONC the Republic being an officer in the Army of Oliver Cromwell. (This would be between 1648 and 1660 - OVL.)  He became later a merchant and settled in Perth where he was made Provost in 1710 and on two subsequent occasions (term of
2 CONC  office was one year). James believed the last of the male descendants named Austin was "my great grandfather DR ADAM AUSTIN",  who had one son who died early in India  (HUGH AUSTIN a judge who died in 1784 - OVL), and several dau
2 CONC ghters, including James's grandmother.  James believed her to be the eldest daughter, and said the second daughter married GENERAL ROBERTSON of Lawers and had no issue. All the other daughters died unmarried.  He also stated that 
2 CONC Sheriff GLOAG was a relative of the AUSTINS and might have more information.  A later note in the SRO records indicated that the sum in Chancery was much less than the "enormous fortune" earlier mentioned - I think it was perhaps 
2 CONC £50,000 but am unsure of the figure and where this report came from. Possibly it was from Bain, who from Gloag's letter seems to have been about to write to the Probate Office on the subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT DR  ADAM AUSTIN  -   HIS BALLAD 'FOR LACK OF GOLD'.
2 CONT See my file 11 under "Descent of Dr Adam Austin" recounting how he lost the hand of his beloved JEAN DRUMMOND of Megginch, his beautiful and accomplished cousin, to JAMES MURRAY,  the Duke of Atholl.  He was heartbroken as a resul
2 CONC t and wrote a ballad which became very popular, entitled "For Lack of Gold".  I have now found the words of this ballad in the Edinburgh Book of Scottish Verse by W MacNeil Dixon, in the Edinburgh Central Library.  The book shows 
2 CONC  Adam's year of death as 1774. JAMES MURRAY was the 2nd Duke. My file 14 under "Lords of Man & the Isles" shows that he was the one who issued his own coinage as Sovereign Lord of the Isle of Man in 1758. Later Dukes disposed of t
2 CONC heir sovereignty to the British Crown for vast sums in 1765 and 1828. All this is also in file 14.  Here is the ballad:
2 CONT FOR LACK OF GOLD
2 CONT For lack of gold she's left me, O,
2 CONT And of all that's dear bereft me, O;
2 CONT She me forsook for Athole's duke,
2 CONT And to endless woe she's left me, O.
2 CONT
2 CONT A star and garter have more art
2 CONT Than youth, a  true and faithful heart;
2 CONT For empty titles we must part,
2 CONT And for glitt'ring show she's left me, O,
2 CONT
2 CONT No cruel fair shall ever move
2 CONT My injured heart again to love;
2 CONT Thro' distant climates I must rove
2 CONT Since Jeanie she has left me, O,
2 CONT
2 CONT Ye Powers above, I to your care
2 CONT Resign my faithless, lovely fair;
2 CONT Your choicest blessings be her share,
2 CONT Tho' she's for ever left me, O.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam eventually recovered from his grief, marrying ANNE SEMPILL, the daughter of General Lord Hugh Sempill, on 15 Sep 1754 (my file 03).
2 CONT -  OV Lundin  October 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT SEE ALSO THESE NOTES.  Further research is needed to establish correct relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SURNAME GLOAG.   RELATIONSHIPS WITH AUSTINS  & MACGREGORS.
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Famous Grouse" Scottish whisky bears the label signed by a Matthew Gloag which reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Named after the Red Grouse, Scotland's national game bird, THE FAMOUS GROUSE  is testimony to the unsurpassed blending skills of Matthew Gloag  and Son, a family business founded in 1800.   Finest malt whiskies, such as The Macal
2 CONC lan and Highland Park, are married with exceptional grain whiskies for an unusually long period in fully seasoned oak casks, thus creating a blend of the smoothest possible character.  Raised in the Highlands, THE FAMOUS GROUSE is
2 CONC  proud to be Scotland's favourite whisky".
2 CONT
2 CONT The signature Matthew Gloag also appears several times on the cork foil.
2 CONT
2 CONT The partial descent of the Gloag family was given by W E  Gloag who lived in April 1854 at 72 Northumberland Street, Edinburgh. He then mentioned he had a brother John Gloag living in 1853.  W E Gloag years later in August 1883 wr
2 CONC ote from Dunkeld about the Austin family of Kilspindie or Kilspundie  in the Carse of Gowrie, near Perth, and said his grandfather was also called  John Gloag.  W E Gloag said that he was a descendant of  Captain Joseph Austin, so
2 CONC n of Thomas Austin  of Kilspundie. Captain Joseph's brother was Dr Adam Austin, the ancestor of James MacGregor.
2 CONT W E Gloag was described as Sheriff Gloag and as Advocate Gloag.  He said  that his grandfather John Gloag married Cecilia Austin born 1739, one of nine children of Captain Joseph Austin of Kilspundie.   These are listed in Chapter
2 CONC  11 of Book I of Our Family History.
2 CONT -   OV Lundin 27 Jan 2003
2 CONT
