1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Some living descendants claim descent from William de la Haye who came from Normandy in 1160, married a Celtic heiress and became Baron of Erroll.
2 CONT
2 CONT There’s a parish called Yester in the East Lothians containing the village of Gifford, the hamlets of Long-Yester, Long-Newton in Haddingtonshire – and a great many people called  Hay. In about 1650 one of them separated from the 
2 CONC pack – John Hay was born and, when the time was right, took up with Hester Scott. In 1670 they had a son, William, but both mother and child evidently died in the process. With all the tact and grace of the average sex starved Sco
2 CONC tsman John rapidly bedded Miss Mary Maitland and, second time lucky, produced the requisite William in 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was destined to be grandfather to rather a lot of Hays but it took him, too, a couple of tries before he got it right. Will turned first to Grissal Fletcher and then in the fullness of time to Margaret Watson – having sett
2 CONC led on thelatter the two of them relocated to Kirkmichael, a small town of about 1000 people in the neighboring parish of Banff. What drew them there is not clear – neither of them had relatives  there – but to Kirkmichael they ca
2 CONC me in time to blessthe arrival of a son, James, with a christening on 5th September 1736 and, I hope, appropriate ceremony. James appears to be the only child they had.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably inevitable that the young fella would end up with one of the Riach girls – they were everywhere. The family crept in slowly through the 1720’s then exploded in a riot of Riach’s all through the following forty year
2 CONC s. Over sixty of them were born between 1730 and 1760, in a parish of scarcely 1000 people.
2 CONT
2 CONT Young James was the only Hay in the village.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was looking around for a wife he had quite a choice of Riach’s; there was Katerin, Janet, a Jean or two, several Margarets, an Isobel, Elizabeth, four Anns and one lonely Elspet. He took one of the Anns and together they p
2 CONC roceeded to breed as much as they could.
2 CONT
2 CONT James and Ann produced eight surviving children in a twenty-three year period. All were dragged across the field to St. Michaels to be baptized, the onslaught of Hay babies apparently weakening the church’s infrastructure. It was 
2 CONC demolishedonce the two of them stopped breeding and rebuilt the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, fourth son of five, grew into a fine young lad. He was educated with a fluid, practiced hand, literate, quick witted and even quicker tempered, a likeable, energetic bustle of a man. He wood Katherine Ogilvy from Forfar wi
2 CONC th a Brigadoon like intensity and, in the first week of November 1797, when the snow lay deep on the ground, took her to his bed. They were making babies, though neither of them knew it at the time. She was nineteen, he twenty-thr
2 CONC ee and, for a few brief minutes they were in love.
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT It became apparent in the New Year that Katherine Ogilvy was in a predicament. Amidst much rejoicing the two lovebirds were married in Katy’s home town on 21st April 1798 then moved up North to Alves, Morayshire, site of yet anoth
2 CONC er branchof the Hay clan . Barbara, the result of all that November huff and puff was born in the first week of August – but the Hays had the taste for more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was a volatile man.  There was something about the set of his jaw, the glint in his eye that made grown men nervous but, in reality, he was just a big pussy cat. Robert adored fatherhood – for a burly bad-tempered Scotsman 
2 CONC he could turn to mush at the mere sight of his baby girl. His eyes would fill with tears when held her in his sunburnt arms. There was no question but there would be more. He embraced fatherhood with the same passion he held for w
2 CONC omen – one was justan extension of the other and the two of them resolved to have a hundred more children. They tried and tried and tried again until Anne was conceived in June 1800. She was a February baby. They were firmly set o
2 CONC n the path to many more.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob was a carrier and work was in short supply. There was more of it down south so, after a month or so staring at his new baby and wondering what to do he packed up, leaving his   in the care of relatives in Alves and followed th
2 CONC e work toAlyth – all of forty miles away. Forty miles doesn’t seem like much but, for Robert, it may as well have been from here to the moon. He was twenty-six and like a million men before him and a million more to come felt the 
2 CONC weight of a dependant  family on his ample shoulders. He travelled back home every chance he got to see his daughters and try hard for the next but the visits became fewer as the months went by until, in October 1801 they stopped 
2 CONC altogether.
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Perth Court of Justiciary, on October 23rd he was charged and found guilty of the theft of some sheep. On the same day he was convicted on a second charge of burglary and theft; £10 from a locked chest, the property of Jose
2 CONC ph Dryburnat Boat of Bardmoney, Alyth. Lords Cullen and Dundennan heard his trial and, with not a moment’s hesitation, sentenced him to transportation for fourteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Temptation had the better of him. Of his own future he cared not a jot but for his wife and baby girls he was gutted. He was plunged into the Tolbooth, Perth, and sat, largely mute, staring at the wall, there to wait his fate. Six
2 CONC  months went by, then, with little warning, he was loaded on a cart and sent the 450 miles south to Woolwich where a rundown hulk awaited him. He never saw those two daughters again.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a man with an eye for the main chance – now fate had turned around and bitten him on his white Scottish arse. As he embarked the Prudentia and settled below stairs Robert allowed himself just a moment’s reflection on all he
2 CONC  had leftbehind. From that day till he died he kept a secret part of him in reserve for those babies, forever wondering what their lives had been like. They, in return, forgot he even existed.
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT James Hay (1736 – 1795) and Anne/Ann Riach (1741 – unknown) – Scottish Highlanders
2 CONT
2 CONT James Hay was baptised on the 5th September 1736 in the village of Tomintoul, parish of Kirkmichael in the county of Banffshire (also known as Banff).  James was the son of Lewis Hay and Margaret Watson.  His parents were both fro
2 CONC m the county of Banffshire.  James siblings were Barbara (1732) , William (1735),  Alexander (1735) and Margaret (1741).
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomintoul Tomintoul
2 CONT
2 CONT James aged 25 married Anne Riach aged 20, another native of Banffshire in the parish of Kirkmichael on the 8th December 1761.  Their children were a baby girl born on the 17th October 1762 who died the same day.  Two years later I
2 CONC sobel Elizabeth Hay was born in 1764, John Hay born 1766, Nathaniel Hay born 1769, William Hay born 1771, Robert Hay born 1774, James Hay born 1780 and Barbara Hay born 1785.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Riach was born on the 27th May 1741.  Kirkmichael was at the time of James and Anne  both the town and name of the greater parish of Banffshire County – containing the  highest elevated village in Scotland the village of  Tom
2 CONC intoul.  Banffshire’s total length being the grand stretch of just over 40 miles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mountainous ranges roll into hills and moors that undulate down to the coast. Valleys throughout this ancient land sit alongside the river Aven which has run from Loch Aven through the land for untold millennia.  A feature of Banf
2 CONC f has longbeen it’s clear and crystal waters flowing throughout the region in it’s many rivers and tributaries.  Sitting wedged into the Scottish highlands the parish area of Kirkmichael, for many generations remained a deeply rel
2 CONC igious area with astaunch cohort of Catholic inhabitants.  Along the coastal towns thriving fishing communities provided lucrative employment  for many of Banffshire’s inhabitants. Agriculture and farming also made up a significan
2 CONC t contingent of the areas considerable wealth.  Banffshire has been home to many well known distilleries over time and once had the title of malt-whiskey capital of the world.  . Likewise it is a county characterised by it’s many 
2 CONC churches and castles both in ruins andthose that remain to colour the landscape.
2 CONT
2 CONT The formal layout of the township of  Tomintoul happened in 1776 when it was designed and laid out by the Duke of Gordon.  The village previously had been a collection of turf  huts on modest farms (such as James and Anne would ha
2 CONC ve lived in). Tomintoul had sat on the line of an old military road that had been built during the 1745 Jacobite rebellion.  During this Jacobite rebellion (James would have only been a boy of 9 or so) a small garrison had been po
2 CONC sted in Timontoul tosuppress the rebels and capture and arrest reviers (cattle thieves) who were known to cross the Aven here with their heisted beasts.
2 CONT
2 CONT As to where and if and how James and his forbears fitted into the Clan Hay I cannot bear witness.  What I can say is that Banffshire, Morayshire, Nairnshire and Perthshire all incorporated the traditional lands of Clan Hay. Equall
2 CONC y these lands of Banffshire and Perthshire were the homelands of our branch of Hay’s. So it is highly likely that our Hay’s were a part of this Clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hay Tartan Hay Tartan
2 CONT
2 CONT Bearing in mind that the clan family incorporated not only the leaders and their families of the clan but usually their extended family and bannermen and their families and those living in the nearby villages and settlements who w
2 CONC ould oftentake on the name of the clan to whom they were attached.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the defeat of the Jacobites at Culloden in Inverness; In 1746 the unpopular  pacification act was enacted and banned the wearing of tartan. This was one of the many efforts by the British to bring the Scottish clans furt
2 CONC her underBritish rule and to break down the power of the clans.  These laws were not repealed until after the death of James in 1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne is recorded on the Perth, Scotland survey of inhabitants as being an ‘in-dweller’ of Perth.  (Meaning a resident). In this census there were 7,542 persons inhabiting Perth.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is 81 miles between Tomintoul and Perth.  A move from the highlands proper into what is often known as ‘the gateway to the highlands would have been a considerable distance.   The actual village of Kirkmichael where I suspect t
2 CONC he Hay’s had moved to was around 27 miles from Perth.  Even still, it was not a common thing for farming families or labourers to move longer distances.  Why the Hay’s did?  At this time I’m still trying to place them.  The only r
2 CONC eason I can come upwith that would fit within that time of history would be that maybe as part of the highland clearances?  Maybe they had to move to a city for work or employment?
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773 at the age of 37 James is recorded on the Perth Scotland Survey of Inhabitants as being a resident of Perth and I am fairly sure it states on the record “Mealler”.  A mealler, or mailler, paid farm rent in money or kind, n
2 CONC ot in labour provided.
2 CONT
2 CONT James dies aged 59 in Kirkmichael, Banffshire, Scotland in 1795. Possibly in Broomtown.  To date I have only the briefest of information on this branch of the family and as yet don’t know when Anne Riach died.
