2 CONT ==========================================
2 CONT Sir Hector Munro, 1st Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hector Munro, 1st Baronet of Foulis was a Scottish soldier, noble and clan chief of the highland Clan Munro.[1] He is also by tradition the 19th Baron and 22nd overall chief of the clan.[1] He is however the 12th chief of the 
2 CONC Clan Munro who can be proved by contemporary evidence.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro, 1st Baronet was the younger brother of Robert Munro, 18th Baron of Foulis (the Black Baron) who left only daughters and was therefore succeeded in the chieftaincy of his clan by his younger brother Hector. They were 
2 CONC both sons of Hector Munro, 17th Baron of Foulis.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide] 
2 CONT 1 Thirty Years' War 2 Baronetcy 3 Return to Germany 4 Family 5 See also 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT Thirty Years' War[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro, 1st Baronet was originally designated "of Clynes" indicating that he was bred for the church. However early in life he embraced a military career.[1] He was an officer of distinction in Sir Donald Mackay, 1st Lord Re
2 CONC ay's regiment, along with his brother Robert, 18th Baron.[1] Hector Munro served in the Thirty Years' War under Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden in his campaign in Germany.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Baronetcy[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of his older brother Robert Munro, 18th Baron of Foulis in 1633, Hector Munro succeeded in his estates, and returned to Scotland in 1634 to take possession of the family estates and assume his position as head of th
2 CONC e clan.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hector Munro was received by Charles I in London, and shortly afterwards was created a Baronet of Nova Scotia.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Royal patent, or diploma, conferring the title is dated 7 June 1634 and says the following:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT “ Domino Hector de Foulis, militi baronetto, terrarum baronie et regalitates de Foulis in regimme Novar Scotiae in America, et haeredibus suis masculis quibuscunque ” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to Germany[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Having arranged his family affairs Sir Hector Munro, 1st Baronet returned to Germany to resume his military career in the war that still carried on there.[1] He took shipping from Cromarty and landed safely in Hamburg in April 163
2 CONC 5 but died that same month in that town.[1] He was buried at "Buckstchood in the Old Land" on the river Elbe.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Hector Munro, 1st Baronet had married in July 1619 at Tongue, Sutherland, in Scotland. He married Mary Mackay, daughter of Hugh Mackay of Farr, Sutherland, chief of the Clan Mackay.[1] Mary was also the sister of Donald Mac
2 CONC kay, 1st Lord Reay'''.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hector Munro, 1st Baronet and Mary Mackay had four children: '''1.Sir Hector Munro, 2nd Baronet (successor as chief of the Clan Munro). 2.Jean Munro, who married her 2nd cousin Sir Robert Munro, 3rd Baronet. 3.Margaret Munro, 
2 CONC who married Rev. Hector Munro, minister of Loth, Sutherland, with issue - Lieutenant Hector Munro and the Rev. John Munro. 4.Catherine Munro, who married William Munro, 4th of Teanoird, with issue - three sons and one daughter'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Hector_Munro,_1st_Baronet
