1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "IV. <strong>Hector Munro</strong>, designated "of Coul." He also entered the army, accompanied the Baron of Fowlis to the German wars, and highly distinguished himself. Colonel Robert Munro, in his Expedition, recording the brave
2 CONC ry and " resolution of some particular soldiers " who were wounded in the service, says that " Major Hector Munro of Coul was shot through one of his feet, and that, on being requested to retire to the rear, answered that it was n
2 CONC ot time till first he emptied his Bandeliers against his enemies, before which time he was shot through the other foot also, and then was not able to come off alone, without help of others, and some of his comrades, which helped h
2 CONC im off, going further with him than he thought needful for his safety or their credit, he wished them to return and discharge their duties against the enemy, as they had sufificiently done towards him," He, however, recovered from
2 CONC  his wounds, returned home, and married Isobella, daughter of Andrew Ross of Balintore, with issue — 
2 CONT * Andrew, his heir and successor. 
2 CONT * Robert, who followed his father's profession, attained the rank of Captain, and died apparently without issue. 
2 CONT * John, who entered the Church, and was, before the 2nd of March, 1664, appointed colleague and successor to his grandfather the Rev. Robert Munro, minister of Farr. 
2 CONT * William, who died unmarried. 
2 CONT * Isobella, who married David Munro. 
2 CONT * Christian. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Hector was succeeded by his eldest son."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/historyofmunroso00mack#page/386/mode/2up History of the Munros of Fowlis: with genealogies of the principal families of the name to which are added those of Lexington and New England]; Alexander
2 CONC  Mackenzie; 1898; page 386
