1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born - Katewell Farm in Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.ebooksread.com/authors-eng/alexander-mackenzie/history-of-the-munros-of-fowlis-with-genealogies-of-the-principal-families-of-th-kca/page-40-history-of-the-munros-of-fowlis-with-genealogies-of-the-principal-familie
2 CONC s-of-th-kca.shtml Alexander Mackenzie. History of the Munros of Fowlis with genealogies of the principal families of the name: to which are added those of Lexington and New England. (page 40)]
2 CONT
2 CONT THE MUNROS OF KATEWELL
2 CONT
2 CONT n. George Munro, who received the lands of Katewell, and married, secondly, Euphemia, daughter of John 
2 CONT Munro, I. of Pittonachy, progenitor of Novar, with issue ...
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his '''third and only surviving son, David Munro''', who married Agnes, daughter of  the Rev. Alexander Munro, minister of Durness from 1620  to 1653, fourth son of Hector Munro, I. of Milntown of  Katewell, wi
2 CONC th issue — 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. George, his heir and successor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Alexander, who succeeded his brother George in the representation of the family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Andrew, who under his distinguished relative, General Sir Georg-e Munro, I. of Newmore, fought, with the rank of Major, at the battle of Preston, on the 17th of August, x1648, was taken prisoner there, and banished to Virginia,
2 CONC  America. Andrew managed to effect his escape and settled in Northumberland County, Virginia, where he  had several grants of land made to him, the first extending vto 200 acres, designated as one of the " Head Rights," being date
2 CONC d the 8th of June, 1650. He married, and had issue, bfrom whom, it is believed, President James Monroe of  of the United States of America was descended. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Robert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Hector, who died "without succession." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. John, who studied for the Church, and emigrated  to America — his brother Andrew being there before him —  where he was for several years minister of Pomunkie,  Virginia. He married there, with issue — several children,  whose 
2 CONC descendants, according to the late Alexander Ross,  lived and are still numerous in the United States. Probably one of his sons was the Rev. Andrew Monro, minister of the Established Church, Virginia, on record in 1696, and  named
2 CONC  after his uncle. Major Andrew. A Rev, John cMonro, appears as a rector in Northumberland County,  Virginia, in 1692. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Janet, who married Hugh Boggie, Fortrose, 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his eldest son,  IV. George Munro.
2 CONT
