1 NAME William /Middleton/
2 GIVN William
2 _MARNM Middleton
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} As Captain William Middleton he is men-  tioned in several deeds, 1669 and 1670. - Ibid. (Dab.), 20 December 1669 - Ibid. (Dundee), 18 June 1675
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 April 1672 he was admitted a Burgess of Aberdeen 'for love and favour.' M As Colonel William he was a  witness to baptisms in the Strathmore family up to  1701. It is possible that he is the William Middleton  of Kincardine O
2 CONC 'Neil whom Sir James Strachan of  Thornton designs his cousin-german in 1663. - Sheriff Clerks Deeds, Aberdeen, 17 July 1672
2 CONT
2 CONT The relationship may have been through Strachan's  mother, Marie Ramsay, who was a sister of Eliza-  beth Ramsay, mother of Robert Middleton of Cald-  ham. The date of William's death has not been ascertained. If he were the same 
2 CONC as the William  of Kincardine O'Neil his wife's name was Madeline  Sinclair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-Colonel - The History of Fettercairn: A Parish in the County of Kincardine (1899) - http://www.archive.org/details/historyfetterca00camegoog
1 FAMC @F6000000011791767244@
1 RFN geni:6000000013825423324
1 SUBM @S6000000013620847446@
1 CHAN
2 DATE 27 NOV 2016
3 TIME 12:41:59
0 @I6000000013825423327@ INDI
1 NAME James /Middleton/
2 GIVN James
2 _MARNM Middleton
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1672
1 OCCU Clergyman
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Knighted before 1662, probably the Cornet  James Middleton to whom in 1646 the Parliament  granted 600 merks for his pains and expenses for  carrying letters to and from the Lord-General to the  Parliament and Comm
2 CONC ittees. - Acta Parl. Scot., vi. pt. i.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a Lyon Office pedigree he is styled ' Tribunus Militaris ' in Spanish Flanders. - Reg of Gen. Lyon Office, i. 18.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clergyman in Montrose.
2 CONT * [http://www.archive.org/details/historyfetterca00camegoog The History of Fettercairn: A Parish in the County of Kincardine] (1899)
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES MIDDLETON, was a licentiate 21st Nov. 1650; ord. to Second  Charge, Montrose, 17th March 1659 ;  trans, and adm. 18th Dec. 1661 ; died 1672.  He marr. Elizabeth Murray, and had issue  — John ; Helen. — [St Andreivs Tests."]
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www20.us.archive.org/details/fastiecclesiaesc05scot Fasti ecclesiae scoticanae; the succession of ministers in the Church of Scotland from the reformation (1925)] (Recorded under Longforgan)
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES MIDDLETON, called June 1653; ord. 17th March 1659; trans, to Longforgan 18th Dec. 1661.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://books.google.no/books?ei=b4mJULXpCZDSsgag6YDACA&hl=no&id=pptbAAAAMAAJ&q=%22james+middleton%22#search_anchor Fasti ecclesiæ scoticanæ: the succession of ministers in the Church of Scotland from the reformation, Volum 5 (1
2 CONC 925)]
2 CONT
2 CONT Old and St Andrew's Church, Montrose, 1659-1661.
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://www.oldandstandrews.com/page0/page34/page35/page35.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Three witnesses attested regarding James Middleton, that, on his being rebuked by the minister, they heard him say that ‘he cared not for him, nor any minister in Scotland;’ and when the minister threatened to put him in the jougs
2 CONC , they heard him say that ‘neither he nor the best minister within seven miles durst do so much.’
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.electricscotland.com/history/domestic/vol2ch2c.htm Domestic Annals of Scotland]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Middiltoune. Settled, 1661.— Middiltoune was ordained at Montrose in 1659, whence he was translated to Longfonran in 1661. He died in 1672, in the thirteenth year of his ministry. His wife's name was Elizabeth Murray, and he
2 CONC  had a son John and a daughter Helen. " His books, with utencils and abulze-ments, were estimat at iic li. Frie geir, d. d., vc xxxiili. xivs. iiij d."
2 CONT
2 CONT The most interesting episode, perhaps, in the church records of the day is the baptism of Earl Patricks eldest son. A large company, including the High Commissioner's wife, Lady Middleton, was staying at the castle. The baptism to
2 CONC ok place on the 19th May 1663. The record tells how on that day John, Lord Lyon, eldest lawful son to the noble and potent Earl Patrick, procreat betwixt him and Dame Helen
2 CONT
2 CONT Middletone his spouse, was presented by the said noble Earl and baptized by Mr. J as. Middletone, our own minister, with all solemni-tyes requisite. Among those present were the Earls of Errol, Morton, Panmure, Sirs Geo. Kinnaird,
2 CONC  Hay of Megginch, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT One event of more public note in the parish occurred the year in which Middiltoune died. In 1672, Charles II. granted a charter ;n favour of Earl Patrick of Strathmore, erecting Longforgan into a free burgh of barony, " with power
2 CONC  to erect and constitute bailies, burgesses, clerks, officers, sergeants, etc., and to admit all kinds of trades to a variety of privileges ; to have a weekly market within the burgh, and to have two free yearly fairs , '. . each 
2 CONC to last three days." It is to be feared that Longforgan has never risen to its privilege of erecting and constituting bailies and burgesses. By the same charter the barony of Longforgan was erected into a lordship, to be called th
2 CONC e Lordship of Lyon. It is likely that it was at this time that the castle ceased to be known as Castle Huntly and came to be known as Castle Lyon, a name which it bore for over one hundred years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The feudal system was then in full force. Everyone in the village was subject to the castle. As much as three-fourths of the rent was paid in kind in 1667, and part of the rest in constant service.
2 CONT
2 CONT (High Commissioner's wife, [http://www.geni.com/people/Grizel-Middleton/6000000003634390611 Lady Middleton] would've been his sister-in-law, so perhaps it's not so strange that this happened in Longforgan..)
2 CONT * [http://www.electricscotland.com/history/longforgan/chapter09.htm The Parish of Longforgan: Chapter IX. A List of Ministers in Longforgan, with biographical notes and glimpses of parish life.]
2 CONT
2 CONT James was in the army of the King of Spain in the Netherlands, and in 1731 his great-grandson, James Francis Edward Middleton, obtained from the Lyon Office a certificate of his pedigree and arms, with a thistle in the paw of the 
2 CONC lion
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/details/scottisharmsbein02stoduoft Scottish arms : being a collection of armorial bearings, A.D. 1370-1678, reproduced in facsimile from contemporary manuscripts, with heraldic and genealogical notes (1881).]
2 CONC  (Page 358)
