1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Patrick Rose
2 CONT * Birth:	1650 - Kilravock,,Morayshire,Scotland
2 CONT * Death:	Mar 31 1727 - Kilravock,,Morayshire,Scotland
2 CONT * Parents:	Hugh Rose, Katherine Ord
2 CONT * Wife:	Margaret Isabel Tolloch
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilravock_Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rose family
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilravock Castle has been continuously tenanted by the Roses, a family of Norman origin, who arrived in Britain with William the Conqueror. They settled at Kilravock in 1293, since which date son has succeeded father without the i
2 CONC nterposition of a collateral heir, an instance of direct descent unique in Scottish history.[citation needed] Moreover, many Roses have borne the Christian name of Hugh, and only one attained to a higher social rank than that of l
2 CONC aird. The present chief of the Clan Rose, Anna Elizabeth Emily Guillemard Rose, 25th of Kilravock passed away peacefully, at Nairn on December 9, 2012, aged 88 years.[5] Some of the present descendents of the Clan Rose now reside 
2 CONC in the State of Maine, located in the United States; other descendants are located throughout Canada, many through southern Saskatchewan; many still remain in Scotland and some reside in the east of London.
1 FAMC @F6000000004226831642@
1 FAMS @F6000000004227081013@
1 RFN geni:6000000004227081006
1 SUBM @S6000000000638015714@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/e2/a7/9f/13/5344483a917cc2e2/58499f48b03f6eb6c1e53cd41878f00e_original.jpg?hash=8f4d7bc7b7ffd9e5dec999799c44df67998b69854e174ae0f05ebd73ab7925e9.1797148799
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Kilravock Castle 
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE Kilravock Castle... had a curse: if a gooseberry bush that grew at the top of their main tower died, so too would the male line. Why there was a bush on their roof in the first place is not clear. Maybe it was wind-seeded; maybe a
3 CONC  gardener was going for a Babylonian look. In the late 1980s, the bush started to wither. The owner, Elizabeth Rose... made numerous frantic attempts to get a cutting from the original to take root, but it failed each time. Replac
3 CONC ement with a brand-new gooseberry bush was apparently not valid in the small print of the curse. The bush died, the male line died soon after, and Kilravock became a Christian youth hostel.
