1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the father of https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-Burnett-of-Easthampton/6000000008631711147
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Attended Cambridge University and became a "Doctor of Physique" and "physician of eminence" practicing in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dr. Thomas Alexander Burnet Sr., M.A.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the website http://www.iment.com/maida/familytree/ancestraleducation.htm#burnet :
2 CONT
2 CONT Abt. 1570, Leys, Kincardineshire, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Aft. 1634
2 CONT
2 CONT + 1611
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Foys 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abt. 1590, London, Middlesex Co., England
2 CONT
2 CONT ?
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Thomas Alexander Burnet was the son of Alexander Burnet, 11th Lord of Leys, and Katherine Arbuthnot, a direct descendant of Robert II, King of Scotland and also Niall "of the nine hostages" King of Ireland. Alexander's son Dun
2 CONC can tells that one his father's ambitions was that his sons be men of learning and culture. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Son Thomas was born in Crathes Castle, Leys, and was educated about 1603 at the University of Cambridge. He was employed as a doctor in Cambridge and Braintree, England, and was described as a "physician of eminence." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother Duncan was another doctor, brother Robert a minister, and brother Gilbert a Professor of Philosophy at Basle. Brother Alexander, a close friend of the Chancellor of Scotland, succeeded his father as the 12th Lord of Leys. 
2 CONC It was by this Lord of Leys that the construction of Crathes Castle, begun by the 9th Lord, was finally finished. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop of Salisbury Gilbert Burnet was the grandson of Alexander, the 12th Lord, making him the grandnephew of Alexander's brother, Dr. Thomas Burnett. Bishop Gilbert's son, Governor William Burnet, was Dr. Burnet's great-grandnep
2 CONC hew. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT From The family of Burnett of Leys, with collateral branches from the mss. of the late George Burnett, by Burnett, George, 1822-1890, Published 1901.  Page 30.  < [https://archive.org/stream/familyofburnetto00burnuoft#page/30/mode
2 CONC /1up Archive.Org] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT III. THOMAS, M.A. of Cambridge, a physician of eminence, practising at Braintree in Essex at the time of the Visitation of 1634. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Jane, daughter of John Foys fof London, and had two sons,
2 CONT # Thomas, born 1612, and 
2 CONT # Alexander; and 
2 CONT #a daughter, Frances, who married the Rev. Thomas Templer, rector of Weston, Co. Northampton.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT s.p. - [Latin] sine prole; without offspring 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/LC3H-TVL
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://bolesbooksblog.wordpress.com/2019/01/06/40-the-scottish-background-of-the-southampton-burnet-family/
2 CONT
2 CONT 40. The Scottish Background of the Southampton Burnet Family
2 CONT January 6, 2019 dbboles ancestry, England, genealogy, New York, royalty, ScotlandAberdeenshire, Braintree, Burnet, Burnett, Crathes Castle, Essex, Gullett, Holbeach, Kincardineshire, Leys, Lincolnshire, Long Island, Southampton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that the Scottish portion of this post has been superseded by another post, namely “52. A Correction to the Ancestry of Thomas Burnet of Southampton, New York￼”.  '''It is concluded there that the Southampton family do
2 CONC es not in fact descend from the Burnet of Leys family.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Burnet descent is fully described and referenced in the book The Omnibus Ancestry: 619 Documented American and European Lines.  It is available for download through Lulu.
2 CONT
2 CONT The patriarch of the Burnet family of Southampton, Long Island, NY, was Thomas Burnet, first known as a settler who arrived at Scout’s Bay, western Long Island, in 1640/1, and who died in Southampton in 1684 [1].  My wife descends
2 CONC  from him by way of her Gullett ancestors.  Thomas’ great-great-great-granddaughter Jane Burnett (1820/1-1883) married in 1849, as her second husband, Joshua Gullett (1825-1863), in Washington county, Indiana [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT For a number of years it has been claimed that Thomas of Southampton was the son of Thomas Burnet, a physician in Braintree, co. Essex, England, and grandson of Alexander Burnet, 11th laird of Leys, co. Kincardine, Scotland, a con
2 CONC nection explicitly stated in E. A. Bailey’s Crannog to Castle (2000) [3].  The laird, whose wife was Katherine Arbuthnot, has extensive traceable ancestry that includes a descent from King Robert III of Scotland [4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Crathes Castle, co. Aberdeen, Scotland, home of the Burnetts of Leys.  Land granted 1323, castle begun 1553, completed 1596 [5].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grounds for Skepticism
2 CONT
2 CONT Much as I would like to trace a royal line of descent on behalf of my wife, I have been skeptical of the stated connection between the Southampton and Leys Burnetts.  There are three major grounds for skepticism, as follows.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. The reference given in Crannog to Castle for the Southampton to Leys connection was the “personal research notes” of a private individual. In absence of a description of the evidence, such notes cannot be assumed authoritative.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. There is little similarity between the names of the children of Thomas of Southampton (John, Lot, Hester, Joel, Aaron, Miriam, Priscilla, Daniel, Lois, Mordecai, and Matthias) and those of the children of Thomas of Braintree (T
2 CONC homas, Alexander, Frances), his wife (Jane), or his siblings (Alexander, Robert, Gilbert, Duncan, John, Janet, Margaret, and Elizabeth) [2]. It seems an important observation that the children’s names in the Southampton family app
2 CONC ear to be solidly English, while several names in the Braintree family are commonly Scottish. But in any case, the lack of resemblance between the two families is a reason to doubt that one closely derived from the other.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. There is also some question whether the chronology is consistent. The Braintree man did have a son named Thomas, who was b. 1612 [2]. However, the second child named in the Southampton man’s will, Lot, was m. 1675, and the thir
2 CONC d child, Hester, married a man b. 1649 [2].  These dates suggest that the first child, John (for whom no firm birth or marriage dates are otherwise known) was b. ca 1648? [6].  In turn that is suggestive that his father Thomas was
2 CONC  born later than 1612, possibly closer to 1622.
2 CONT
2 CONT While none of these points is definitive, together they provide ground for skepticism about the Southampton-to-Leys connection, at least as it has been described.
2 CONT
2 CONT Support from Y-DNA Testing
2 CONT
2 CONT A fly in the skepticism ointment, however, is that Y-DNA testing does in fact support a connection between the Southampton and Leys families.  Y-DNA comparisons have been made between two descendants of Thomas of Southampton on th
2 CONC e one hand, and on the other hand, a descendant of Alexander Burnet, 11th laird of Leys (b. ca 1540?, d. 1578) and a descendant of Alexander Burnet, 9th laird of Leys (b. 1500, d. 1574).  The former match the latter in either 32/3
2 CONC 7 or 62/67 markers depending on the pairing [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT These results appear definitive, establishing that there is in fact a genetic connection between Thomas of Southampton and the Burnett lairds of Leys.  But is the connection through the 11th laird?  The results indicate, on averag
2 CONC e across the outcomes, a 50% probability of convergence in 15.5 generations [7]. At 31 years a generation [7] and assuming an average birthyear of 1970 across the testees, that would indicate a common ancestor b. ca 1490? — i.e., 
2 CONC more likely in the person of the 9th than the 11th laird.  However, the error margin is very high, and the results are consistent with either a much earlier or a much later convergence, including one with the 11th laird. Hopefully
2 CONC  estimates will narrow as more tests are received.
2 CONT
2 CONT Others of the Name
2 CONT
2 CONT A final consideration is that there was no shortage of persons named Thomas Burnett in British records of the early 17th century.  Any of several could have been the American immigrant, instead of the son of Thomas of Braintree.  
2 CONC Thus those of the name born 1617-1627 in England include Thomas Burnett, chr. 1620 in Stepney, London, Eng, son of John and Elizabeth; Thomas Burnett, chr. and d. 1621 in Lincoln, co. Lincoln, son of John; Thomas Burnet, chr. 1622
2 CONC  in Fincham St. Martin with St. Michael, co. Norfolk, Eng, son of John; Thoms. Burnet, chr. 1623 in Otley, co. York, son of Thoms.; and Thomas Burnet, chr. 1625 in Adstock, co. Buckingham, son of Richard [2].  This list, of course
2 CONC , is only as complete as the church records from which it was compiled, and there were others of somewhat different spellings of the surname.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier, there were the intriguing christenings of Mathias Burnet (1594) and Joell Burnet (1599), both sons of Thomae (Thomas) Burnet, in Holbeach, co. Lincoln [2].  There was also a Danyell Burnett m. 1631 in Holbeach, whose bapt
2 CONC ism was not recorded there [8], but who could have been another son of Thomas.  Mathias, Joel, and Daniel are three of the unusual names in the Southampton family, possibly suggesting a relationship.  However Tho. [Thomas], the ap
2 CONC parent brother of Mathias and Joell (and possibly of Danyell), was chr. 1609 [2], raising the same chronological issue as the Leys connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusions
2 CONT
2 CONT That the ancestry of Thomas Burnet of Southampton, Long Island, ultimately traces to the Burnetts of Leys in Scotland appears indisputable, given the Y-DNA evidence.  However, there remain grounds for skepticism over whether the r
2 CONC elationship was as close as a descent from the 11th laird.  If the line instead went through one of the other English families of record, such as that of Thomas of Holbeach, co. Lincoln, there could have been any number of connect
2 CONC ing generations before convergence with the Leys family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, for now that keeps me from claiming a royal descent for my wife [9].  But perhaps it offers the prospect of one, following some future application of the genealogist’s craft.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
