1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir Andrew Ramsay, Lord Abbotshall, 1st Baronet</strong> (May 1619 – January 17, 1688), Privy Counsellor, was the first Lord Provost of Edinburgh and a judge of the Court of Session...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...Andrew Ramsay was the third son of the Reverend Andrew Ramsay (d.1659), Minister of the Old Kirk, Edinburgh, and from 1620 - 1626 Professor of Divinity and Rector at Edinburgh University, a younger son of David Ramsay, 1534 - 1
2 CONC 625, and Katherine Carnegie; and a great-grandson of William Ramsay of Balmain, 1510 - 1569. Sir Andrew's mother was Mary, daughter of Alexander Frazer, Laird of Dores."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'Andrew Ramsay, Lord Abbotshall', <em>Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia</em>, 23 July 2012, 23:52 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Andrew_Ramsay,_Lord_Abbotshall&oldid=503856601> [acc
2 CONC essed 4 September 2012]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * 'Ramsay, Andrew (1620?-1688) (DNB00)', Wikisource, The Free Library, <http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Ramsay,_Andrew_(1620%3F-1688)_(DNB00)>
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=grI2AAAAIAAJ An Historical Account of the Senators of the College of Justice: From Its Institution in MDXXXII]; by George Brunton, David Haig; 1832; Page 399-401
2 CONT
2 CONT == Notes ==
2 CONT * There are inconsistencies between <em>[http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n291/mode/2up Complete baronetage]</em> (Vol. IV; page 273) and Lord Abbotshall's entry in <em>Dictionary of National Biography</em> as to wh
2 CONC ether he was the 1st Baronet.  The <em>Complete baronetage</em> indicates it was his son that was created Baronet, while the <em>Dictionary of National Biography</em> indicates his son succeeded him as Baronet.  His entry in [http
2 CONC ://books.google.com.au/books?id=grI2AAAAIAAJ An Historical Account of the Senators of the College of Justice: From Its Institution in MDXXXII] (Page 399-401) makes no reference to him being created a Baronet.
2 CONT >"Andrew Ramsay, of Abbotshall, co. Fife, as also of to Waughton, co. Haddington, a. and h. ap. of Sir Andrew Ramsay, of Abbotshall, sometime (1654-57 and 1662-73) Lord Provost of Edinburgh, and (1671-73) a Lord of Session under t
2 CONC he style of lord Abbotshall, by Janet Craw, his wife, was bap. 24 Dec. 1648, at the Grey Friars church, Edinburgh ; was M.P. [S.] for North Berwick, 1669-74, and was cr. a Baronet [S.], 23 June 1669, with rem. to heirs male of the
2 CONC  body. He suc. his father (who d. aged 69), 17 Jan. 1688. He m. Anne, da. of Hugh (Montgomerie), Earl of Eglintoun [S.], by his 2d wife, Mary, da. of John (Leslie), Earl of Rothes [S.]. He d. s.p.s. 1709, and was bur. at Abbotshal
2 CONC l, when the Baronetcy became extinct." - [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n291/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 272
2 CONT > "...He was succeeded in the baronetcy and estates by his son Andrew." - 'Ramsay, Andrew (1620?-1688) (DNB00)', Wikisource, The Free Library, <http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Ramsay,_Andrew_(1620%3F-1688)_(DNB00)>
