1 NAME James /Drummond/ 4th Earl of Perth
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Drummond
2 NSFX 4th Earl of Perth
2 _MARNM Drummond
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paris
3 STAE Île-de-France
3 CTRY France
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 JUL 1648
2 ADDR
3 CITY Perth
3 STAE Scotland
3 POST PH2 6PL
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 MAY 1716
2 ADDR
3 CITY Saint-Germain-en-Laye
3 STAE Île-de-France
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Scottish politician 
1 TITL 4th Earl of Perth, Kt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>James, fourth Earl of Perth</strong>, was born in 1648, and was served heir to his father 1 October 1675. As a youth he studied at the University of St Andrews, and afterwards completed his education in France. On 10 Janu
2 CONC ary 1678 he admitted to the Privy Council, and ultimately joined in opposition to the Duke of Lauderdale, after whose fall he was, on 1 May 1682, appointed Lord Justice-General and one of the Extraordinary Lords of Session 16 Nove
2 CONC mber of the same year...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He was created a [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_of_the_Thistle Knight of the Thistle],  [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Knights_and_Ladies_of_the_Thistle 29 May 1687], on the institution or revival of that order...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He ultimately settled at St Germains, and was appointed by James, 19 July 1696, Governor to the Prince of Wales, and one of the Lords of the Bedchamber. He was also created Duke of Perth, Marquess of Drummond, Earl of Stobhall,
2 CONC  Viscount Cargill, and Baron Concraig. He also received from King Charles II. of Spain the Order of the Golden Fleece. After the death of James, in 1701, Louis XIV. confirmed to him the rank and privileges of a French Duke...
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at St Germains 11 May 1716, and was buried in the chapel of the Scots College in Paris. On his death the title of Earl of Perth and Lord Drummond should have devolved on his eldest son, but as he was forfeited, he was inca
2 CONC pable of succeeding to them. The Earl married, first, 18 January 1670, Jane, daughter of William, first Marquess of Douglas; secondly, Lilias, widow of James, fourth Earl of Tullibardine, and daughter of Sir James Drummond of Mach
2 CONC any. She died about 1685, and he married, thirdly, within a few months, Mary, widow of Adam Urquhart of Meldrum, and daughter of Lewis Gordon, third Marquess of Huntly. She died 13 March 1726, in her eightieth year, her heart bein
2 CONC g buried in the chapel of the Scots College in Paris."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/scotspeeragefoun07pauluoft#page/50/mode/2up The Scots peerage], Vol. VII, page 51-53
2 CONT
