1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===COLIN LINDSAY, EARL OF BALCARRES===
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/548/mode/1up The Scots Peerage I: 520]
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/scotspeeragefoun01pauluoft/page/549/mode/1up The Scots Peerage I: 521-22]
2 CONT
2 CONT == [http://thepeerage.com/p2045.htm#i20449 Biographical Summary by Wikipedoa] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Balcarres [1]
2 CONT *M, #20449, 
2 CONT *b. 23 August 1652, 
2 CONT *d. 1722
2 CONT *Last Edited=19 Feb 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=6.83%
2 CONT
2 CONT Colin Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Balcarres was baptised on 23 August 1652 at Kilconquhar, Fife, Scotland.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, firstly, Mauritia de Nassau, daughter of Louis de Nassau, Herr van der Leck and Beverwaerde and Elizabeth (?), circa 1670.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, secondly, Jean Carnegie, daughter of David Carnegie, 2nd Earl of Northesk and Lady Jean Maule, between 1672 and 1680.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, thirdly, Lady Jean Ker, daughter of William Ker, 2nd Earl of Roxburghe and Jean Ker, after 1680.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, fourthly, Lady Margaret Campbell, daughter of James Campbell, 2nd Earl of Loudoun and Lady Margaret Montgomerie, before 4 July 1689.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1722 at Balcarres, Fife, Scotland.[2] He was buried at Chapel, Balcarres, Fife, Scotland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Alexander Lindsay, 1st Earl of Balcarres and Lady Anne Mackenzie.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 3rd Earl of Balcarres [S., 1651] on 15 October 1662.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 4th Lord Lindsay of Balcarres [S., 1633] on 15 October 1662.[1] 
2 CONT *He succeeded to the title of 3rd Lord Lindsay and Balneil [S., 1651] on 15 October 1662.[1] 
2 CONT *In 1668 he was presented to King Charles II, who gave him a troop of horse and a life pension of £1,000 per year.[1] 
2 CONT *He fought in the Battle of Solebay on 28 May 1672, with the Duke of York.[1] 
2 CONT *He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) on 3 June 1680.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Sheriff of Fife in 1682.[1] 
2 CONT *He held the office of Commissioner of the Treasury on 3 September 1686, as one of the Council of Six in whom the Scottish administration was lodged.[1] 
2 CONT *He wrote the book An account of the affairs in Scotland relating to the Revolution, published 1688.[2] 
2 CONT *In 1690 he supported King James II, and joined him in exile at St. Germain.[1] In 1700 he returned to Scotland from exile.[1] 
2 CONT *In 1715 he joined in the Uprising of 1715, but having surrendered, was included in the indemnity.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT His grand-daughter described him as "one of the handsomest and most accomplished men of his time, a man of letters, but fond of pleasure, and pleasure's favourite."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Colin Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Balcarres and Jean Carnegie
2 CONT *1. Lady Anne Lindsay+[3] d. 3 Feb 1743
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Colin Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Balcarres and Lady Jean Ker
2 CONT *1. Lady Margaret Lindsay [3]
2 CONT *2. Captain Colin Lindsay, Lord Cummerland [3] b. bt 1680 - 1689, d. Nov 1708
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Colin Lindsay, 3rd Earl of Balcarres and Lady Margaret Campbell
2 CONT *1. Lady Eleanor Lindsay [3] d. 7 Aug 1735
2 CONT *2. Alexander Lindsay, 4th Earl of Balcarres [2] b. b 1690, d. 25 Jul 1736
2 CONT *3. James Lindsay, 5th Earl of Balcarres+[4] b. 14 Nov 1691, d. 20 Feb 1768
2 CONT
