1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Thomas Erskine second son of James Erskine son to umql James Erskine of Findlaystown who was brother to William Erskine of Pitodrie the father of Thomas Erskine late of Pitodrie, having it is supposed conceived som
2 CONC e idea that he may be called to the succession of the estate of Pittodrie as an heir male in presence to the minister to propose now to take out as brief from chancery for cognoseing William Erskine as fatuous,to have himself serv
2 CONC ed curator to him as nearest agnate. (MS 2524/9-10 - Memorial and queries of 1787 for the children of the marriage between Lt. Col. Henry Knight and Miss Mary Erskine of Pittodrie. 23 November 1787.  Images 1-9 – MS 2524/9,images 
2 CONC 10-13 – MS 2524/10)
2 CONT
2 CONT Uncle to the Laird of Pittodrie. Younger son of Thomas Erskine of Pittodrie who in 1643 married Helen, daughter of Sir William Auchinleck of Balmanno. Married a Mowat "Heiress of Balquholly". - Jacobites of 1715, North East Scotla
2 CONC nd: And, Jacobites of 1745, North East Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Balquholly is supposed to mean the "house in the Wood". This ancient feudal designation is now changed into the modern title of Hatton. The Castle Balquholly was formerly the seat of Mouat of Balquholly, the chief of that name, an
2 CONC d for many generations of considerable note in the north. It was also a seat of Sir Thomas Urquhart of Cromarty when he opposed the Covenanters in 1609. It then past into the hands of James A. Erskine, brother to the laird of Pitt
2 CONC odrie, and was sold in 1727 to Duff of Hatton. The original Castle of Balquholly was supposed to have been built about the year 1252. 
