2 CONT OBJE: _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;The earliest mention of any variant of the name Killough found by&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; professional genealogists of the Ulster Historic Foundation, and other&lt;b
2 CONC r&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONT CONC nbsp; genealogists hired, shows that there was a John Kellogh on Sir Thomas&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Phillips&amp;#39; 3000 acre estate in Kenaught Barony near Limavady, Co.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp; Londonderry, Irelan
2 CONT CONC d, in 1611-1616.&amp;nbsp; This was the only privately owned&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; estate in what, since 1922, is known as Northern Ireland.&amp;nbsp; Phillips, a&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Scot
2 CONC s soldier of fortune, h
2 CONT CONC ad received this estate for his service in&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Ulster when he raised a &amp;quot;company of foot&amp;quot; in 1600.&amp;nbsp; We can only surmise&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; why
2 CONC  John was there as a se
2 CONT CONC rvitor, a person receiving land in exchange&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; for military service.&amp;nbsp; He might have came to Ulster from Scotland with&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; waves of the McDonald
2 CONC s during the age of Eli
2 CONT CONC zabeth I and somehow linked&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; up with Phillips.&amp;nbsp; Hundreds of unemployed Clan Donald men swarmed into&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Ulster from Scotland about 1600 as me
2 CONC rcenaries for the Iris
2 CONT CONC h nobles.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; John Kellog, possessing a &amp;quot;sword and pike,&amp;quot; on Sir Thomas Phillips&amp;#39;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; estate in 1631 appears on a list of all p
2 CONC rotestant men fit to be
2 CONT CONC ar arms&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; in the local militia.&amp;nbsp; Nothing more is known of him.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The second John Killough to show up in records appears on the 165
2 CONC 9 census&lt;br&gt;&amp;
2 CONT CONC nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; of Drogheda, County Louth, Ireland.&amp;nbsp; He did not come to Ireland with&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Cromwell&amp;#39;s troops in 1649; he was born in Ireland in 1630.&amp;nbsp; He j
2 CONC oined&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbs
2 CONT CONC p;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; them and was later paid in Irish land in Louth.&amp;nbsp; In 1654, this John had&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; married Mary Hyde.&amp;nbsp; By about 1660, John and Mary, with son John (the&lt;
2 CONC br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONT CONC sp;&amp;nbsp; third), left County Louth, which was Catholic, to go north to the Bann&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; River valley in Ulster.&amp;nbsp; He probably sold his debenture to acquire cash&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; fo
2 CONT CONC r new land and a business.&amp;nbsp; Here they would be close to their&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; coreligionists.&amp;nbsp; John Killough of Drogheda died in Antrim about 1715.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&amp;nbsp; Their so
2 CONT CONC n John (the third), born in Louth in 1657, married Anne McNeil&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; and had at least three children, Robert, John (the fourth) and James.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They were li
2 CONC ving in the County Antr
2 CONT CONC im area. These and possibly other of&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; their children and other Killoughs coming from Scotland account for the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Killoughs found in Ireland presently
2 CONC .&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&a
2 CONT CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; In the early 1700&amp;#39;s, due to laws affecting religion in Ulster, the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; marriages of the Presbyterian Ulster Scots became invalid, their churches&lt;br&g
2 CONC t;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONT CONC amp;nbsp; became illegal, their ministers could not preach or hold office, and they&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; had to be buried by Episcopal prelates.&amp;nbsp; A severe drought and a smallpox&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; ep
2 CONT CONC idemic swept the land.&amp;nbsp; At the urging of their ministers and&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; accompanied by them, their congregations emigrated to Maine and New&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Hampshi
2 CONC re.&amp;nbsp; Three boa
2 CONT CONC tloads of them left Coleraine early in 1718.&amp;nbsp; Robert&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; and his wife, Margaret Finley, their sons, Finley (Finlay), David, John&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; and possibl
2 CONC y Allen, along with Rob
2 CONT CONC ert&amp;#39;s brother John and wife, Jean Young,&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; departed on the &amp;quot;William&amp;quot; and arrived in Boston August 4, 1718.&amp;nbsp; The&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 
2 CONC captain of this ship wa
2 CONT CONC s Archibald Hunter from Coleraine.&amp;nbsp; The families&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; on this ship had attended the Macosquin Church and followed their&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; minister, Rev. Thomas
2 CONC  Craighead, to America.
2 CONT CONC &amp;nbsp; Rev. Craighead made other&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; trips to America to bring Scottish Presbyterians.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Their brother James remained in County Antrim an
2 CONC d is believed to be the
2 CONT CONC &lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; ancestor of the Killoughs who lived at Gortahar, some of whom came to&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Canada in 1834.&amp;nbsp; Due to lack of old records, many Killoughs livin
2 CONC g in&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONT CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Northern Ireland and the Republic of Ireland do not now know their direct&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; ancestry or their relationship to one another.&amp;nbsp; They still have close&lt;br&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp
2 CONT CONC ;nbsp; ties to Scotland.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Since land had not been determined for them and the Boston Calvinists&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; would not tolerate them, they were force
2 CONC d to move to Worchester
2 CONT CONC ,&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Massachusetts, and then about ten miles northwest to Rutland,&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Massachusetts. In time, Robert is found in Portsmouth, New Hampshire,&lt;br&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONT CONC nbsp; involved in the oil and sturgeon trade until his business mysteriously&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; burned down.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The John Young family came to Portsmouth and 
2 CONC worked in the fishing&l
2 CONT CONC t;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; industry also.&amp;nbsp; The lack of land and attitude of the people toward them&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; made living in Massachusetts or New Hampshire a poor choice.&amp;
2 CONC nbsp; The Youngs&lt;br&
2 CONT CONC gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; and Robert Killough&amp;#39;s family traveled through Connecticut and down to&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Philadelphia by sea to await developments so to learn where they could&lt
2 CONC ;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONT CONC bsp;&amp;nbsp; settle.&amp;nbsp; It depended on the Indian situation, what grants could be&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; secured, etc.&amp;nbsp; There the Youngs participated in the drawing of lots for&lt;br&gt;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONT CONC sp; land in the Warren, Maine, area.&amp;nbsp; The Commonwealth of Massachusetts&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; included Maine at that time.&amp;nbsp; Their daughter Mary married Robert&amp;#39;s son&lt;br&gt;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONT CONC ; Finley,&amp;nbsp; who was only about 14 when their first child, David, was born&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; in Philadelphia in 1725.&amp;nbsp; His wife was eleven years his senior.&amp;nbsp; This&lt;br&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONT CONC ; may explain why he and his wife went north in 1735, to what was then&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; still the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, with the Youngs instead of&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; stayin
2 CONC g with the Killough fam
2 CONT CONC ily.&amp;nbsp; For several generations none of the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Maine Kellochs named their children after Finley&amp;#39;s father Robert,&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; indicating it likely
2 CONC  that a family disput
2 CONT CONC e was associated with this move.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Robert&amp;#39;s brother John&amp;#39;s two sons, Samuel and John &amp;quot;of Sherman&amp;#39;s Creek&amp;quot;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; were born i
2 CONT CONC n Massachusetts.&amp;nbsp; Later, Mary, Ann, and Allen were born to&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; John and Jean Killough.&amp;nbsp; The Presbyterians who remained in Worcester&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC  had to attend the Cong
2 CONT CONC regational church if they attended church any.&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They finally realized they would never be granted the religious liberty&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; they wanted if they contin
2 CONC ued to stay in Worceste
2 CONT CONC r.&amp;nbsp; John eventually&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; followed Robert to Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, and then on to&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Cumberland County.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Th
2 CONT CONC e Killough contingent established themselves near a series of springs&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; in what is now Cumberland County, Pennsylvania.&amp;nbsp; Big Springs, now the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp; town of Newville, w
2 CONT CONC as the center of this community.&amp;nbsp; The earliest&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; burials clustered around an old oak tree in the southeast portion of&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; the yard near the fi
2 CONC rst log church they bui
2 CONT CONC lt in 1737.&amp;nbsp; In one of these&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; graves lies Robert the immigrant.&amp;nbsp; The Rev. Thomas Craighead, the same&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; one who organized the Bann 
2 CONC migration, became the m
2 CONT CONC inister.&amp;nbsp; He dropped&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; dead in the pulpit shouting, &amp;quot;Farewell!&amp;nbsp; Farewell!&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp; By the late 1760&amp;#39;s&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC  the Killoughs pushed o
2 CONT CONC n to new frontiers of civilization in the&lt;br&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; midwestern and southern states.&amp;nbsp; The rest of the story follows.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;From the book:&amp;nbsp; &lt;u&gt;THE KILLOUGH/KELL
2 CONC OUGH FAMILY&amp;nbsp;I
2 CONT CONC N IRELAND, CANADA&amp;nbsp;AND THE UNITED STATES&lt;br&gt;&lt;/u&gt;http://killough.org/killough_book/kbk-intro.txt&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
