1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Served with distinction under Marlborough at Blenheim, 1704.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Master-General of the Ordnance 17 March 1704, Deputy Quartermaster-General 1708, Quartermaster-General 22 December 1712 - John Armstrong, 'The pedigree of the family of Armstrong' (National Library of Ireland Ms 5216).
2 CONT
2 CONT Founded the Royal Arsenal at Woolwich, 1716.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he died was Surveyor General of the Ordnance, Chief Engineer of England, Colonel of the 18th (Royal Irish) Regiment, Quartermaster-General of the Forces, and Lieutenant-Governor of the Tower of London - John Armstrong, 'The P
2 CONC edigree of the family of Armstrong', continued by the Rev. W. A. Armstrong (no imprint, 1813), p. 24.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wikipedia contributors. "<em>[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Armstrong_%28British_Army_officer%29 John Armstrong (British Army officer)]</em>." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia.
2 CONT * [Minorca] A Correct Map of the Island of Minorca By John Armstrong Esqr. Engineer in Ordinary to his Majesty with many Additions and Improvements From the late surveys. . . . 1794 < [https://www.raremaps.com/gallery/detail/71522
2 CONC /minorca-a-correct-map-of-the-island-of-minorca-by-john-a-laurie-whittle link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT This John Armstrong, according to Wikipedia and Burke, had nothing to do with the island of Minorca. It was another [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Armstrong/6000000037541660306 John Armstrong from County Offaly, Ireland, b. 170
2 CONC 6,] who was governor of Minorca and who created a map of the island.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalhera04burk/page/344/mode/1up Burke's Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Commoners of Great Britain and Ireland, "Armstrong of Ballycumber" p. 344]
