1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT William arrived in America in 1734 with wife Jane Gaffney, Brother James & wife Janey, Niece Jenny, Nephew William, and son William age 22 & his wife Mary Caldwell age 19 [Ed: see note below].
2 CONT
2 CONT Source-Citations include WLA Book, added after the history was written in 1901.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source-Chron-368, Margaret Armstrong of Mason Texas; NSSAR 93, Price.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source-From the Rutledge Files. The family settled in Augusta Co., Va.and resided there a few years, then William moved with his wife, to South Carolina and resided in Honea Path, Anderson Co., S.C. until his death in1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source-De Wolfe Miller manuscript states that Jany/Jenny is sister to William.
2 CONT Compiled by W.L.A.(AZ.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes about the above account===
2 CONT
2 CONT Almost certainly there are errors and assumptions underneath this account.
2 CONT
2 CONT For one thing, marriage at age 19 in Ireland was rare. 21 was the minimum age of marriage, and it was even written into the records this way. So that's Worry Number One. Then, died 1775?  Really?  He'd have to be born in 1669 or s
2 CONC o and that would make him over 100 at time of death. That's Worry Number Two.
2 CONT
2 CONT A will written in 1745 and proved in 1753 may be the will for "son William" described as being of "age 22" in 1734.  In that will it is clear that William (the deceased) was born around 1698 or so. This is because he had adult son
2 CONC s at the time his will was written in 1745 - two of them, William and John.  And he had a married daughter and a son-in-law John Thoptson as well.  Son Lancelot, by the description, was about age 20, so that pushes the timeframe b
2 CONC ack so that if this William had been on the boat in 1734 all of them would have been aboard.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no other will for the time period from 1734 - 1754 that would seem to apply.  What I'm not worried about is that that there was a 1734 journey and the principals as named were on board. I just question the ages mentioned 
2 CONC in the account above and wonder how they were obtained.
2 CONT
2 CONT These worries are compounded by the fact that "son William", supposedly born in 1712, is supposed to be the very same William who died in 1815 in Tennesee.  That William's wife, Mary Jane Caldwell, supposedly died in 1767.  Their 
2 CONC family, however, does not seem to begin until 1745 or so, rather much later than the supposed 1734 nuptial immigration journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT So what I am thinking here is that two generations have been collapsed: the William born c. 1722 from the will was the one who died in Tennessee in 1815, and the William aboard the boat wasn't accompanied by Mary Caldwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT
