1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Note about placement and children===
2 CONT
2 CONT The Clogher marriage records provide a number of contemporaneous Armstrong marriages to Johnstons between 1700 and 1730.  One of these Armstrongs was a Matthew Armstrong, who married Easter Johnston.  Of the whole line of Armstron
2 CONC gs established by pedigree in Ireland, there is only one other Matthew, and he's a grandson of John Joseph Armstrong.
2 CONT
2 CONT I therefore believe that the best placement for this family is within the family of John Joseph's father, especially given the number of interactions with the Johnston clan nearby. ~KDW January 2023
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew Armstrong's existence as the second son of John of Brookeborough comes from multiple lines of evidence.  Many Armstrong marriages were recorded at Clogher, County Tyrone, and a significant number of these were between an A
2 CONC rmstrong and a Johnston.  These hinted at a broader alliance between two families, and two families that lived not far from Clogher (which is just a few miles from Five Mile Town).
2 CONT
2 CONT One of these marriages was between a Matthew Armstrong and an Easter Johnson.  Matthew had heretofore been rare among Armstrongs in Ireland of the 1700 generation, but this was an Irish Armstrong near where John of Brookeborough l
2 CONC ived.  This Matthew was later found to have emigrated to Pennsylvania, and then to Mecklenburg County, NC.  He left a detailed will, attached to his profile here: https://www.geni.com/people/Matthew-Armstrong-of-Mecklenburg-County
2 CONC /6000000191442021886 .  This laid out the names of his children, in rough birth order.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Armstrong / Johnston marriage, that between https://www.geni.com/people/William-Armstrong-of-Bedminster/6000000007791959267 and Mary Johnston, turned out to belong to another couple who emigrated, but this time to the unus
2 CONC ual destination of Bedminster, Bucks County, Pennsylvania, in 1755.  This was after a well-known couple, James Hughes and https://www.geni.com/people/Rebecca-Hughes/6000000191583878830 , helped found Bedminster along with https://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/Joseph-Armstrong-Bedminster-founder/6000000191782481851 , in March of 1742.  It seemed clear that these Armstrongs were all closely related.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Further research showed that William and Mary were not alone in emigrating to Bedminster either -  another Armstrong / Johnston couple joined them there, although they did not leave much of a trace except for their children.  This
2 CONC  is the family of https://www.geni.com/people/George-Armstrong/6000000191419815858 and Elizabeth Johnston , who contributed two children to the Revolutionary War, one of whom lost their life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage records from Bedminster survive, and it was thus possible to put the children into families from these records.  Many were already known and had trees worked out for them rooted in Bedminster or in Bucks County.  Some wer
2 CONC e less known and seemingly had no children.  But at least one of the 1755 immigrants married a son of Joseph Armstrong.  It was a common practice in Ireland for cousins to marry, so this argued strongly that these families were li
2 CONC nked back in Ireland as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT DNA linked descendants of the Bedminster immigrants closely with known descendants of John Armstrong of Brookeborough, so it seemed that he had two children hitherto undescribed - one born around 1671, and the other being the Jose
2 CONC ph Armstrong who helped found Bedminster.  Furthermore, we could surmise that the Rebecca Armstrong who married James Hughes, and the William Armstrong who married Mary Johnston, and the George Armstrong who married Elizabeth John
2 CONC ston, were all siblings, and children of the 1671 Armstrong son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naming conventions in Ireland were quite strong at this time.  The convention for sons is that the first born son is named after his paternal grandfather.  The second born is named after his maternal grandfather.  The third is nam
2 CONC ed after his father.  The fourth is named after his father's eldest brother.  Applying this to the family so far, and assuming that this family was indeed children of a son of John of Brookeborough, was enough to conclude the foll
2 CONC owing:
2 CONT
2 CONT * The son was John of Brookeborough's second, after first son William
2 CONT * He was named after his mother's father
2 CONT * His first son would have been named John
2 CONT * His second son likely was the William Armstrong who emigrated to Bedminster
2 CONT * His third son likely was the Matthew Armstrong who married Easter Johnston and settled in Mecklenburg County, NC
2 CONT * He was likely called Matthew, as was his mother's father
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew of Mecklenburg County's family confirms this picture, and adds another detail: Matthew's wife was probably called Catherine.  This has been confirmed by analyzing the families of the other Armstrong / Johnston couples who 
2 CONC settled in Bedminster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once this picture was complete, and the corresponding work also done on the Johnston side, where the patriarch was named Samuel and his wife was Mary, it became possible to refine the Bedminster families.  In particular, because t
2 CONC here were two Armstrong/Johnston families that emigrated with children, there should be two Matthews and two Samuels.  For the Samuels, there were already two (although sometimes they were incorrectly merged).  One of the Samuels 
2 CONC had left Bedminster and gone to Abbeville, SC.  The missing Matthew turned out to be already known - he had settled in Pendleton, Anderson County, SC.
2 CONT
2 CONT The likely children of Matthew and Catherine include, at the time of this writing:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Catherine, b.c. 1692, married Thomas Johnston in 1714.  Was likely named after Matthew's wife's mother, which also happened to be Matthew's wife's name.
2 CONT # John, b.c. 1693, married Sarah Anderson; a son may have also emigrated to Bedminster before 1742
2 CONT # Jennet, b.c. 1694, fate unknown
2 CONT # Rebecca, b. 1695, married James Hughes, emigrated to Bedminster before 1742
2 CONT # William, b. 1697, married Mary Johnston, emigrated to Bedminster in 1755
2 CONT # Matthew, b. c. 1699, married Easter Johnston and later Lillian Beatty, emigrated to Chester County PA and then to Augusta County VA and then to Mecklenburg County, NC
2 CONT # James, b. .c. 1703, married Prudence Johnston, emigrated to Bedminster in 1755
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b.c. 1706, married James Johnston
2 CONT # Ann, b.c. 1709, married William Johnston
2 CONT # George, b.c. 1711, married Elizabeth Johnston, emigrated to Bedminster in 1755
2 CONT # Martin, b.c. 1713, married Dorothy Forster, a first cousin
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew likely died in Ireland, as did his wife Catherine.
