1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===History===
2 CONT
2 CONT The Armstrong clan of County Fermanagh had its origins when King James VI of Scotland ascended the throne of England as King James I, on 24 March 1603.  Several Borderlands clans, notably the Armstrongs and the Croziers, had made 
2 CONC their living as border reivers - taking advantage of the lawlessness of the region to build lives of petty thievery.  James the First, however, had no desire anymore to let that tradition continue, and he put an end to it - hangin
2 CONC g clan chiefs as needed until the point was made.  The best military men of the clans were given military commissions and sent to northern Ireland, after three Irish clans declared war on Scotland.  The Irish clans were defeated a
2 CONC nd their lands confiscated, and King James I saw to it that these lands were distributed to Scottish and English settlers, preferably Protestant, because he wanted to "break the grip of the Catholic Church on Ireland."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ulster Plantation, as it was called, existed in a state of near-continuous warfare from its founding in 1605 until the time when settlers were no longer welcomed from Britain, around 1690.  For the first part of this period, b
2 CONC uilding towns and villages was out of the question, and the settlers lived only in defensible places like moated forts or defensible islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gradually, however, the tensions eased.  By the time of the Battle of the Boyne, on 1 July 1690, where William of Orange was victorious, the Protestants were permanently encamped in the North of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT John Joseph Armstrong was born in 1683, a few years before the Battle of the Boyne.  He was a grandson of William Armstrong of Hightower - who had come from Scotland to County Fermanagh as an officer.  His father was John Armstron
2 CONC g of Brookeborough.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although many of John Joseph's brothers emigrated to via Pennsylvania to all over the United States, John did not, choosing instead to remain in Ireland and live near Five Mile Town, on the County Tyrone border, about five miles f
2 CONC rom the seat of the Clogher Diocese.  He married Susanna Johnson around 1704.
2 CONT
2 CONT (We know that John Joseph's wife was named Susanna, and the DNA evidence is strong that she was a Johnson, related to several Johnsons who emigrated to New Jersey in both the 1770s and in 1798.  See Susanna's profile for more deta
2 CONC ils.)
2 CONT
2 CONT We do not yet have a complete roster of John Joseph's children - we have the ones who emigrated to the New World, and DNA evidence of others.  As of this time, John's family looks like it includes:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Jane, b, c. 1705.  [DNA, Clogher records] Married an unknown Reilly first, then Thomas White.  Emigrated to Halifax County, Virginia.
2 CONT # John, b. c. 1706.  [DNA, Clogher records].  Married Jane Crozier, and emigrated to Morrisville NJ in 1742.
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b. c. 1707.  [DNA] Married Alexander McLaughlin, and emigrated to Cumberland County, PA.
2 CONT # Margaret, b. c. 1709. [Clogher record] Married first Hugh McConnell, then James Armstrong, emigrated to Bucks County PA in 1755.
2 CONT # Susanna, b. c. 1710.  [DNA] Married William Scott, and emigrated to Pennsylvania in 1734.
2 CONT # Joseph, b. 1711.  [DNA, historical account] Married Jennet Stewart, and emigrated to Cumberland County, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT # Mary, b. c. 1713.  [DNA] Married Samuel Wright.
2 CONT # Robert, b.c. 1714. [Clogher records] Married Isabella Robertson.
2 CONT # Rachel, b. 1715.  [DNA, historical account] Married Samuel Finley, and emigrated to Cumberland County, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT # Eleanor(?), b. c. 1717.  [DNA] Married an unknown Scoles.
2 CONT # Andrew, b. 1718.  [DNA, historical account] Married Ellinor Elliott, and emigrated to Pennsylvania.
2 CONT # Thomas, b. 1720.  [DNA, historical account] Married Sarah Gillespie, and emigrated eventually to Tennessee.
2 CONT # George, b. c. 1722.  [Historical account, Clogher record] Married Frances Barton, a Rosborough widow.
2 CONT # Isabella, b. c. 1724.  [DNA] Married Martin Armstrong, a first cousin, as his second wife.
