2 _MARNM Armstrong
1 NAME Thomas Armstrong of Moyaliffe
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1671
2 ADDR
3 CITY Armiger
3 STAE Mealiffe
3 CTRY Ireland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 JUL 1741
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ballycahill
3 STAE Tipperary
3 CTRY Ireland
1 OCCU HIgh Sheriff of Tipperary
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Armstrong of Farney Castle's brother was Thomas Armstrong of Moyaliffe.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “Landed families of Britain and Ireland - (182) Armstrong and Heaton-Armstrong of Farney Castle, Mount Heaton, Moyaliffe, and Chaffpool“ < [http://landedfamilies.blogspot.co.za/2015/09/182-armstrong-and-heaton-armstrong-of.html li
2 CONC nk] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT Armstrong family of Moyaliffe and Chaffpool
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Armstrong, Thomas''' (1671-1741). Younger son of Capt. William Armstrong (c.1635-90?) and his wife Alice, daughter of Sir Thomas Deane, born 1671. High Sheriff of Tipperary in the reign of Queen Anne, and said to have been an M
2 CONC P in the Irish Parliament. He married '''Mary''' (d. 1751), eldest daughter of '''Robert Carew of Castle Boro'''' (Wexford), whose sister married his brother, and had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT * (1) William Armstrong (1704-68) (q.v.);
2 CONT * (2) Andrew Armstrong (1706-40), born 1706; apprenticed to a merchant and mariner in Cork, and afterwards set up in business as a merchant by his father; he joined with others in purchasing or building a ship, the Armstrong, whic
2 CONC h he sailed himself to the West Indies with stores for the King's forces there in 1740; died unmarried on the return journey when the ship was lost with all hands;
2 CONT * (3) Rev. John Armstrong (1708-81) (q.v.);
2 CONT * (4) Rev. Robert Carew Armstrong (1709-90) of Corolanty (Offaly), born 1709; educated at Trinity College, Dublin (BA 1729; MA 1732); rector of Shinrone (Offaly); married Jane, fourth daughter of Anthony Atkinson MP of Cangort (Of
2 CONC faly) and had issue one son; died 1790;
2 CONT * (5) Peter Armstrong (b. 1711); died in infancy;
2 CONT * (6) Charles Armstrong (1713-31);
2 CONT * (7) George Armstrong (1716-39), born 1716; apprenticed to a banker in Cork; died 1739;
2 CONT * (8) Alice Armstrong; married, 1719, James Ellard of Newtown (Limerick) and had issue;
2 CONT * (9) Anne Armstrong (d. 1731);
2 CONT * (10) Juliana Armstrong; died in infancy;
2 CONT * (11) Margaret Armstrong (d. 1788); married, 1738, James Dexter, Marshal of the Four Courts, Dublin, of Brannockstown (Kildare), eldest son of John Dexter of Brannockstown and Dublin and had issue four sons and four daughters; di
2 CONC ed 14 March 1788 and was buried in St Patrick's Cathedral, Dublin;
2 CONT * (12) Elizabeth Armstrong; married Rev. John Smyth (d. 1749) and had issue two sons;
2 CONT * (13) Mary Armstrong; married Rev. Richard Lloyd of Castle Lloyd (Limerick), eldest son of Rev. Thomas Lloyd, and had issue;
2 CONT * (14) A daughter; married John Bettridge of Forest (Tipperary) and had issue two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT He acquired the Moyaliffe estate in about 1695.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died 20 July 1741. His widow died in 1751.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Armstrong (c.1635-90?) was also a soldier, but is of particular interest here as the man who laid the foundations of the family's Irish estates, through a series of grants from the Crown in the 1660s (recognising their los
2 CONC ses and service during the Civil War and Commonwealth), and purchases in the 1670s. He made his home at Farney Castle near Thurles (Tipperary), and when he died he left a life interest in it to the widow of his executed brother. S
2 CONC he died in 1693 and the estate passed to William's son, John Armstrong (d. 1707). John's heir was his eldest son, Col. William Armstrong (d. 1767)
2 CONT
2 CONT
