1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Edward-Armstrong/6000000121988392877?through=6000000000889250188
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Edward Armstrong of Terwinney, son of James of Brookboro, County Fermanagh, Ireland, brother of General John Armstrong of Carlisle, Pennsylvania, left Ireland about 1744 and was early identified in Pennsylvania with the French an
2 CONC d Indian War, being commissioned a Lieutenant in Captain Edward Ward's company May 22d, 1756. In July of that year his company was stationed at Fort Granville. On the 30th of the month Captain Ward marched from the fort with a det
2 CONC achment for the Tuscarora Valley, leaving Lieutenant Edward Armstrong in command. Soon after the departure of the troops the fort was surrounded by a hostile force of French and Indians, who after a siege of several days succeeded
2 CONC  in setting fire to the defences, killing Lieutenant Armstrong and several of his men, and capturing others who were subsequently burned at the stake, while the women and children were taken to the Ohio. An account of this transac
2 CONC tion is in volume 7 of the Minutes  of the Provincial Council. Edward left a son in Ireland who was called Gentle James" 
2 CONT
2 CONT - Chronicles of the Armstrongs, http://www.archive.org/stream/chroniclesofarms00arms#page/369/mode/2up
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT “1. Edward, the oldest, named for his grandfather, died in Ireland before his father, James. Some descendants came to America.4“
2 CONT
2 CONT However, he is also seen as killed in action, 1756:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://explorepahistory.com/hmarker.php?markerId=1-A-8F
2 CONT
2 CONT John Armstrong (1717-1795) was a surveyor from Carlisle who became a colonel in the Pennsylvania militia created after  Braddock's defeat. His brother, '''Lieutenant Edward Armstrong''', was among those provincial soldiers killed 
2 CONC when French-allied Delaware Indians took Fort Granville in July 1756. John Armstrong led the retaliatory raid against  Kittaning a few weeks later. Forty of his 300 Pennsylvania militiamen were killed or wounded in the attack, but
2 CONC  they killed the Delaware leader Captain Jacobs and recovered several English captives held in the village.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * [https://gardnerlibrary.org/journal/family-john-armstrong-sr-1717-1795-carlisle-pennsylvania “The Family of John Armstrong, Sr. (1717-1795) of Carlisle, Pennsylvania”] Journal Issue: 1991 Winter, Volume 8, Issue 2 Author: Bell, 
2 CONC Raymond Martin.  
2 CONT * Chronicles of the Armstrongs; Author.  Armstrong, James Lewis.  Page 375. [http://archive.org/stream/chroniclesofarms00arms#page/375/mode/2up Archive.Org] Page 373 [http://archive.org/stream/chroniclesofarms00arms#page/373/mode/
2 CONC 2up Archive.Org]
