1 OCCU Surveyor
1 TITL Major
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 4. William was a surveyor in Mifflin County, where his home near the site of Fort Granville by 1773 was a Presbyterian preaching point. He died late in 1784. Colonel John in 1755 referred to "my brother William. " 
2 CONC He later became a lieutenant in the militia.7 His wife was named Rebecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "William Armstrong, son of James of Brookboro ... left Ireland with his brother John. He took up a tract of 200 acres of land west of the Susquehanna on the 13th of January, 1737, on which he located. He seems to have served in th
2 CONC e defence of the frontiers, was commissioned Lieutenant May 10, 1756, and was on the expedition to the Kittanning. He was commissioned a Captain December 24th, 1757, and appears by the archives of Pennsylvania to have been an offi
2 CONC cer of considerable importance, especially in forwarding ammunition and provisions to the scattered and exposed outposts and block-houses. he was recommissioned Captain July 4th, 1763, and on July 4th, 1764, promoted to Major of t
2 CONC he Second Battalion of the Provincial regiment, accompanying Colonel Bouquet's army to the Muskingum. After the war he returned to his farm in Middleton township, where he died prior to December, 1770, leaving a wife Jean, and chi
2 CONC ldren John, William, Susannah, Charity, Elizabeth, Alexander" -Chronicles of the Armstrongs, http://www.archive.org/stream/chroniclesofarms00arms#page/369/mode/2up
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
