1 NAME George /Armstrong/
2 GIVN George
2 SURN Armstrong
2 _MARNM Armstrong
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bedford
3 STAE PA
3 POST 15522
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1719
2 ADDR
3 CITY Brookeborough
3 STAE Northern Ireland
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1789
2 ADDR
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 TITL Lt. Col.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''George Armstrong''',  son of James of Brookeboro ... came to Pennsylvania with his brother John, who settled in Carlisle, Pennsylvania. He assisted him in his surveying expeditions, and had warranted to himself 
2 CONC a number of valuable tracts of land. At the breaking out of the French and Indian War he was commissioner, May 22d, 1756, Captain in the Secpnd Battalion of the Provincial forces, and accompanied his brother, Colonel John Armstron
2 CONC g, afterward Major-General, on his expedition to the Kittanning. He was recommissioned Captain, December 12th, 1757, in Governor William Denny's Pennsylvania Provincial Regiment. Owing to some difficulty with Sir John Sinclair he 
2 CONC resigned his commission, but we find that shortly after, June 4th, 1758, he was promoted Major. On the 13th of April, 1760, he was commissioned Lieutenant-Colonel of the Third Pennsylvania Battalion, and for services rendered was 
2 CONC granted five tracts of land in the West Branch Officers' Survey. In 1756 he was directed to build Pomfret Castle where it had been laid out by Major James Burd as one of the projected forts of defence. He resided in Allen township
2 CONC , Cumberland County, Pennsylvania, and in 1782 was possessed of 230 acres of land. In a notice in Kline's 'Carlisle Gazette' of 1789 it is stated that his brother John was his 'heir at law'" - Chronicles of the Armstrongs, http://
2 CONC www.archive.org/stream/chroniclesofarms00arms#page/369/mode/2up
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. George Armstrong lived in Bedford in 1769. Colonel John mentioned "my brother George" as a militia captain in 1758 and "my late brother George" in 1785. He rose to the rank of colonel in the militia.6 In 1758 he was commissione
2 CONC d to lay out a road to Fort Duquesne. On 2 April 1766 he received a Pennsylvania license to many Martha Turner, probably a sister of his sister Rebecca's husband. George's last years seem to have been spent in Bedford
2 CONT
