1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.archive.org/stream/memorialsofearlo02rogeuoft/memorialsofearlo02rogeuoft_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Alexander, elder son of Claud Alexander  of Newtoun, born in April 1681, succeeded his father  in the estate of Newtoun in 1703. He married his  cousin Margaret, daughter of his uncle, Robert Alex-  ander, by whom he had a 
2 CONC son and daughter. The  daughter, Jean, married Robert Neilson of Paisley,  by whom she had issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Claud Alexander, only son of Robert Alexander of  Newtoun, was born in 1724, and succeeded to New-  toun in 1738. He married, in 1746, Joanna, daughter  of Alexander Cuninghame of Craigends (descended  from the noble House of Glen
2 CONC cairn), by his wife,  Anne, daughter of Sir John Houstoun, Bart, of that  ilk, and grand-daughter of John Drummond, Earl of  Melfort. Claud Alexander of Newtoun died in 1772,  leaving five sons and six daughters. The daughters  we
2 CONC re Catherine ; Margaret, born 1753 ; Anna, born  1754; Wilhelmina, Lockhart, and Lilias (Baptismal  Register of Abbey Parish, Paisley). Wilhelmina  Alexander is celebrated by Burns in his song, " The  Bonnie Lass o' Ballochmyle." 
2 CONC The poet had, by the  banks of the Ayr, chanced to encounter Miss Wilhel-  mina. There was no conversation or sign of recog-  nition, but the poet afterwards despatched to her a  copy of his song. She did not acknowledge it, but  
2 CONC her nephew, the proprietor of Ballochmyle, has  placed a bower on the spot where the poet saw her.  Miss Wilhelmina Alexander died unmarried in 1843,  at the age of eighty-eight. 
