1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Knox, esq. of Castlerea, in the county of Mayo, b. in 1630, whose name appears to the loyal addresses from that county to Charles II. in 1682, and 1683, m. first, Mary, daughter of Francis Palmer, esq. of F
2 CONC arrow, and had by her,
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Francis, of Moyne, in Mayo, his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. Arthur, ancestor of the present John Knox, esq. of Castlerea.
2 CONT
2 CONT He wedded secondly, the daughter and heir of Crofton, of Rappa Castle, and had by that lady, two sons and a daughter, viz.
2 CONT
2 CONT William, of Dublin, clerk of the crown for the counties of Mayo, Sligo, Roscommon and Leitrim, and for the town of Galway. He m. (licence dated in 1733), Mary, daughter of Henry Osborne, esq. of Dardistown, in Meath, and had issue
2 CONC ,
2 CONT
2 CONT John,
2 CONT
2 CONT William, who married Mary, daughter of Gillespie M'Donald, of Dublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane,
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy, married to Francis Darcy, esq. of Loftus Hall, in the county of Dublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, married to William Ryves, esq. of the county of Wicklow.
2 CONT
2 CONT John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, married in 1705, to Thomas Bell, esq. alderman of Dublin, and lord mayor in 1702.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Knox, whose will dated 30th July, 1705, was proved 20th November, 1707, was buried in the chancel of St. Audeons, Dublin, near his brother Sir John. His eldest son, Francis Knox, esq. of Moyne, in Mayo, served as sheriff o
2 CONC f that county in 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References:''' Notes from A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Commoners of Great Britain and ... By John Burke, pg 580 -581
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Links:''' http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Knox-650
