1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Evidence needed to support https://www.geni.com/people/Jean-Fergusson/6000000125275198856?through=6000000008049442312 as wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SOURCE 1: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n435/mode/2up Complete Baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 417'''
2 CONT
2 CONT "<strong>John Ferguson [or Fergusson], of Kilkerran</strong>, in the parish of Dailly, co. Ayr, s. and h. of Simon Fergusson, of Auchinwood, near Maybole, in that county, by Jean, da. of ( — ) Craufurd, of Balsarroch, was admitted
2 CONC  Advocate, 1 Feb. 1681, and, being successful in practice, purchased in 1700 the ancestral estate of Kilkerran from his cousin, Alexander Fergusson, and was cr. a Baronet [S.], as above, with rem. to heirs male of his body. 
2 CONT He m. in 1680, Jean, sister of Sir Adam Whitefoord, 1st Baronet [S. 1701], da. of James Whitefoord, of Dunduff, co. Ayr, by Isabel, da. of Sir Bryce Blair, of that ilk. 
2 CONT He d. at Kilkerran, Feb. 1729. Funeral escutcheon."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SOURCE 2: THE PEERAGE.COM at SOURCE 2: THE PEERAGE.COM at'''
2 CONT https://www.thepeerage.com/p40590.htm#i405896
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Fergusson of Kilkerran, 1st Bt.1 
2 CONT M, #405892, b. 1656, d. 14 February 1729
2 CONT Sir John Fergusson of Kilkerran, 1st Bt. was born in 1656.1 
2 CONT He was the son of Simon Fergusson and Jean Crauford.2 
2 CONT He married Jean Whitefoord, daughter of James Whitefoord of Dunduff and Isabel Blair, in 1684.1 
2 CONT He was a practising Advocate in 1681.1 
2 CONT He bought the Barclanachan estate next to Kilkerran.1 He moved the family seat from the old house of Kilkerran, now a ruin, to Barclanachan or Balmaclanachan, formerly a Kennedy property.1 
2 CONT On 7 February 1701 Kilkerran was erected into a free (territorial) Barony by charter.1 
2 CONT He was created 1st Baronet Fergusson, of Kilkerran [Nova Scotia] on 30 November 1703.1 
2 CONT He held the position of Chief of the Name and Arms of Fergusson from in 1719.1
2 CONT He died on 14 February 1729.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir John Fergusson of Kilkerran, 1st Bt. and Jean Whitefoord
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Ferguson b. 21 Jun 1673
2 CONT # Sir James Fergusson of Kilkerran, 2nd Bt.+2 b. 1688, d. 20 Jan 1759
2 CONT # Jean Fergusson+1 b. 1692, d. 1717.  Married Alexander Macdowall, 16th of Garthland
2 CONT # John Fergusson b. 6 Dec 1697
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1 [S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 1411. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 2 [S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''HISTORY OF THE FERGUSON DIASPORA''' (As added by the Geni Team in 2010 and updated 2013):
2 CONT
2 CONT FERGUSON - Logan records only two threads for the red stripe. D.C. 
2 CONT Stewart calls this Ferguson of Balquhidder to differentiate it from the Ferguson of Athol. 
2 CONT Chiefs of the Clan are the Fergusons of Kilkerran, descended from Fergus of Dalriada, who brought the 'Stone of Scone' to Scotland. The Fergusons of Perthshire were recognised as the principal Highland branch of the clan and the c
2 CONC hiefship belonged to 'MacFhearghuis' of Dunfallandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Fergus have spread across Scotland, from Ross-shire in the north to Dumfriesshire in the south-west. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gaelic patronymic, ‘Mac Fhearghuis', is translated alternatively as ‘son of the angry' or ‘son of the bold and proud'.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fergusons held the lands of Kilkerran, probably from the twelfth century, but the first certain record is John Ferguson of Kilkerran in 1464. He may have been descended from John, son of Fergus, one of the witnesses to a chart
2 CONC er of Edward Bruce signed at Turnberry shortly after the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1600 there were Fergusons all over the southern part of Carrick, all of whom acknowledged Kilkerran as their chief. They sided with the '''Kennedys''' in their feud with the Lairds of Bargany and were part of the Earl of Cassil
2 CONC lis's band in the skirmish around Maybole in 1601 when Bargany met his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Ayrshire Fergusons''' adopted the Protestant faith during the Reformation. 
2 CONT Sir John Ferguson of Kilkerran fought for the royalist cause in the civil war. 
2 CONT The Kilkerran estates fell heavily into debt and it fell to one of Sir John's grandsons to restore the family fortunes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Fergusson, born around 1653, became a distinguished lawyer and a member of the Faculty of Advocates in 1681. He was created a Baronet of Nova Scotia in November 1703. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, James, became a judge of the Supreme Court in 1735 with the title, ‘Lord Kilkerran'. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Sir Charles Ferguson of Kilkerran, seventh Baronet, served in the army for almost forty years. His early career took him in 1895 to Egypt and later to the Sudan. In 1914 he was the youngest major general on the army list. 
2 CONC He served throughout the First World War and was later military governor of occupied German territory. From 1924 to 1930 he was Governor General of New Zealand. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The present chief still lives at the splendid Kilkerran House in Ayrshire.
