1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Sir John Shaw [or Schaw], of Greenock</strong>, co. Renfrew, s. and h. of John Shaw, or Schaw, of the same, sometime M.P. [S.] for Renfrewshire, by Helen, da. of John Houston, of Houston, was in 1661 Lieut.-Col. of Dunfer
2 CONC mline's Horse, and distinguished himself in the Royal cause at the battle of Worcester, 3 Sep. 1651, where he was Knighted ; was M.P. [S.] for Renfrewshire, 1669-74, 1678, and 1681-82, and, having suc. his father in 1679, was cr. 
2 CONC a Baronet [S.], as above, the Privy Seal being dat. at Windsor, 28 June 1687, with rem. to heirs male of the body. He m. firstly, Jean (by whom he had six children), da. of Sir William Muir, of Rowallan. He m. secondly, Janet, da.
2 CONC  of ( — ) Scott. He d. at Edinburgh 16 and was bur 28 April 1693, in the Abbey Church of Holyrood. Funeral entry at Lyon Office. His widow, as " Dame Janet Scott, relict of Sir John Shaw, of Groomnoch, aged about 70," was bur. 8 F
2 CONC eb. 1719, at Grey Friars, Edinburgh."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n369/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 350-351
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT N/A	
2 CONT 1st Baronet of Greenock New Creation - 1693	
2 CONT Succeeded by
2 CONT John Shaw Bt. (abt.1650-1702)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Shaw-9577
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the son of John Shaw and Helen Houston.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted at the Battle of Worcester in 1651 and was created a Baronet in 1687.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married firstly, Jean, daughter of Sir WIlliam Mure of Rouwallan.[3] They had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Sir John, heir
2 CONT # Sarah, m. Sir Robert Dickson of Invereak w/o issue, m. Robert McDouall in 1678[4]
2 CONT # Ann, m. Tobias Smollet of Binhill and had issue
2 CONT He married secondly, Janet Elizabeth Scott on 9 May 1684.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT John died at Edinburgh on 16 April 1693 and was buried in Holyrood Abbey.[2] His testament was recorded on 31 Aug 1694.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
