1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Margaret-Henderson/6000000176066438953
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == MARGARET HENDERSON ==
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''11-13 July 1654: Extract disposition and renunciation by John Henryson of Fordell, William Henryson, his brother, Margaret Henryson, spouse of Sir Henry Wardlaw of Pitreavie (Pittreavie), bart, Jean Henryson, spouse of Thomas B
2 CONC ruce of Blairhall, and Elizabeth Henryson, in favour of James Henryson, their third brother, of all goods which might belong to them through the decease of George Henryson, their brother. Registered in Inverkeithing burgh court bo
2 CONC ok, 15 August 1654. [https://catalogue.nrscotland.gov.uk/nrsonlinecatalogue/details.aspx?reference=GD172%2f311&st=1&tc=y&tl=n&tn=n&tp=n&k=thomas+bruce+of+blairhall&ko=p&r=&ro=s&df=&dt=&di=n National Records of Scotland, Papers of 
2 CONC the Henderson Family of Fordell, reference GD172/311]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Henderson had **only two husbands**, both of whom were local Fife/Perthshire gentry. The other two husbands ("Robert Jack" and "Robert Hayes"), along with the burial in Cheshire, England, are total digital phantoms create
2 CONC d by automated algorithm matches.
2 CONT
2 CONT The anatomy of this profile smash-up breaks down clearly:
2 CONT
2 CONT === The Fact vs. Fiction Breakdown ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * **The Two Real Husbands (Fife Gentry):** Margaret Henderson married **firstly**, Sir Henry Wardlaw, 3rd Baronet, on June 9, 1653. He died *dsp* less than a year later. She married **secondly**, Peter Hay of Naughton (frequently 
2 CONC transcribed as Maughton or Meigans) on May 6, 1655, in Dalgety, Fife. These two marriages are fully documented in the Scottish Old Parish Registers (OPR).
2 CONT * **The Transatlantic Phantom ("Robert Hayes" / "George Hayes"):** In automated internet trees, users looking for the ancestry of early New England settlers (specifically George Hayes of Windsor, Connecticut) seized upon the surna
2 CONC me "Hay" in Fife. Algorithms automatically converted "Peter Hay" into "Peter Patrick Hayes" and fabricated a son named "George Hayes" to bridge the gap, dragging Margaret Henderson into an American colonial lineage where she does 
2 CONC not belong.
2 CONT * **The "Robert Jack" Identity:** This is a secondary algorithmic error where children named "William Jack" or similar were mis-attributed to her household due to a transcription error or an automated merge with a completely separ
2 CONC ate woman named Margaret Henderson who lived in Glasgow or England.
2 CONT * **The Cheshire Burial:** Margaret Henderson, Lady Pitreavie and Lady Naughton, lived her entire life within the Firth of Forth region of Scotland. She was never buried in Lower Peover, Cheshire. That burial belongs to a local En
2 CONC glish woman of a completely different family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT === Corrected Biographical Summary ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography ===
2 CONT Margaret Henderson was born in Fife, Scotland, around 1628, the daughter of Sir John Henderson, 5th of Fordell, and Margaret Menteith.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married firstly Sir Henry Wardlaw, 3rd Baronet of Pitreavie, on June 9, 1653, in Dunfermline, Fife. Her tenure as Lady Wardlaw was exceptionally brief; Sir Henry died without issue (''dsp'') before May 16, 1654. Following his 
2 CONC death, she executed a disposition and renunciation of her rights under her Wardlaw marriage contract, which was registered in the Inverkeithing burgh court books in August 1654.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married secondly Peter Hay of Naughton, an estate in the parish of Balmerino, Fife, on May 6, 1655, in Dalgety, Fife. She spent the remainder of her life as the Lady of Naughton, managing the household and establishing a subst
2 CONC antial family with her second husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Modern online databases frequently introduce severe identity conflations into her profile, falsely assigning additional husbands named "Robert Jack" and "Robert Hayes," and claiming a burial in Cheshire, England. These errors stem
2 CONC  from speculative 19th-century genealogical attempts to link the Scottish Hays of Naughton with the colonial Hayes families of New England. Primary records confirm she remained in Scotland throughout her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Immediate Family ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: Sir John Henderson, 5th of Fordell, and Margaret Menteith.
2 CONT * Husband 1: Sir Henry Wardlaw, 3rd Baronet of Pitreavie (married June 9, 1653, d. 1654). No issue.
2 CONT * Husband 2: Peter Hay of Naughton (married May 6, 1655).
2 CONT * Son: John Hay of Naughton
2 CONT * Daughter: Margaret Hay (baptized September 14, 1666, in Dunfermline).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dunfermline Parish Register, Marriages 1561-1700, marriage entry for Henry Wardlaw and Margaret Henderson, June 9, 1653.
2 CONT * Dalgety Parish Register, Marriages 1644-1700, marriage entry for Peter Hay and Margaret Henderson, May 6, 1655.
2 CONT * National Records of Scotland, Papers of the Henderson Family of Fordell, extract disposition and renunciation by Margaret Henryson, spouse of the late Sir Henry Wardlaw of Pitreavie, Bart., dated July 11–13, 1654 (GD172/311).
2 CONT * John Tod, The Wardlaws in Scotland: A History of the Wardlaws of Wilton, Torrie, and Pitreavie (Edinburgh: William Brown, 1912), page 222.
