1 NOTE {geni:about_me} His father, Robert Burnett, Lord Crimond (1592-1661) was a brother of Sir Thomas Burnett of Leys, 1st Baronet. He was an eminent member of the Scottish Bar and was so successful that he was able to purchase several
2 CONC  estates, including Crimond in Aberdeenshire, which became his residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to his opposition to the Covenant, he left Scotland on three occasions and once remained in exile for 5 years. On his final return, he lived in retirement on the Crimond estates until the restoration, at which time he was made
2 CONC  a Judge of the Court of Session, under the title of Lord Crimond, but he lived only a few months after his appointment. He married twice and had 5 sons and 2 daughters. '''Three of his sons died unmarried and the two others had n
2 CONC o male descendants, so the Burnetts of Crimond are extinct in the male line.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.burnett.uk.com/genealogy/
