1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT "<strong>Gilbert Burnett</strong> (18 September 1643 – 17 March 1715) was a Scottish theologian and historian, and <strong>Bishop of Salisbury</strong>. He was fluent in Dutch, French, Latin, Greek, and Hebrew. Burnet was respecte
2 CONC d as a cleric, a preacher, and an academic, as well as a writer and historian. He was associated with the Whig party...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...Burnett was born at Edinburgh, Scotland in 1643, the son of Robert Burnett, Lord Crimond, a Royalist and Episcopalian lawyer, who became a judge, and of Rachel Johnston, the sister of Johnston of Warristoun, a leader of the Cov
2 CONC enanters. His father was his first tutor until he began his studies at the University of Aberdeen, where he earned a Master of Arts in Philosophy at the age of thirteen. He studied law briefly before changing to theology, and earn
2 CONC ed his Doctor of Divinity by the age of eighteen. He did not enter into the ministry at that time, but travelled for several years. He visited Oxford, Cambridge, London, the United Provinces and France. He studied Hebrew under a R
2 CONC abbi in Amsterdam. By 1665 he returned to Scotland and was ordained in the Episcopal Church by the Bishop of Edinburgh...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...He married several times, once to Lady Margaret Kennedy, daughter of John Kennedy, 6th Earl of Cassilis. His sons included William Burnett and Gilbert Burnett. His daughter Elizabeth married Richard West a distinguished lawyer 
2 CONC who became Lord Chancellor of Ireland, and was the mother of Richard West the younger, the poet and friend of Thomas Gray."
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'Gilbert Burnett', <em>Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia</em>, 9 November 2012, 09:05 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gilbert_Burnet&oldid=522148206> [accessed 17 November 2012]
