1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Thomas Howard
2 CONT *M, #48973
2 CONT *Last Edited=11 Apr 2008
2 CONT *'''     Thomas Howard was the son of Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk and Lady Elizabeth Home.
2 CONT *'''Child of Thomas Howard
2 CONT **James Howard+ d. 1669
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p4898.htm#i48973 _____________________
2 CONT *'''Thomas HOWARD (Hon.)
2 CONT *'''Born: 1621
2 CONT *'''Died: BEF 10 Jun 1682
2 CONT *Father: Theophilus HOWARD (2º E. Suffolk)
2 CONT *Mother: Elizabeth HUME
2 CONT *'''Married: Werburge Van Den KERCKHOVEN (dau. of Johannes Polyander gn. Van Den Kerckhoven, Heer Van Kerckhoven, Heenvliet en Sassenheim and Anna Van Wesick) ABT 1649
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. James (John) HOWARD (b. 1649 - d. 1669)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/HOWARD4.htm#Thomas HOWARD (Hon.)1 _______________________
2 CONT *'''HOWARD, Hon. Thomas (1621-c.81), of Pall Mall, Westminster.
2 CONT *'''bap. 8 July 1621, 2nd s.''' of Theophilus Howard†, 2nd Earl of Suffolk, by Lady Elizabeth Home, da. and coh. of George, 1st Earl of Dunbar [S]. '''m. by 1649, Walburga, illegit. da. of Jan Polyander van der Kerchhove, lord of 
2 CONC Henvliet, Zeeland, 1s. d.v.p.1
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **'''Col. of horse (royalist) c.1643-5; master of horse to the Princess Royal by 1647-Dec. 1660; capt. of horse, Dutch army 1647-at least 1656; lt.-col. of ft. Holland regt. 1665-74.2
2 CONT **'''Freeman, Woodstock 1673; commr. for assessment, Berks. 1673-9, Oxon. 1677-9, recusants, Oxon. 1675.3
2 CONT *'''Howard’s father''', as warden of the Cinque Ports, '''nominated him as court candidate for Rye before he was 19, but he was not elected, and when his father died a few months later, he was left virtually unendowed.''' The 3rd 
2 CONC Earl sided with Parliament in the Civil War till he was impeached in 1647, but '''Howard fought as a Royalist. After the battle of Naseby he retired to Holland, where he was taken into the service of the Princess Royal and given a
2 CONC  commission in the Dutch army. He made periodic visits to England, one of them in the company of the royal mistress, Lucy Walter. He was suspected of sending information to his brother-in-law, Lord Broghill (Roger Boyle), and in 1
2 CONC 658 George Downing reported that he had ‘perfectly gained’ him. Sir Edward Hyde angrily denounced him as a spy, and his hopes of a peerage were dashed, but the King remarked only that he ‘earns his wages very easily, for all he in
2 CONC forms is of his own invention’. Certainly the letters which Downing obtained from him cannot have been of much use to the Protectorate Government. At the Restoration a pension of £500 was settled on his wife, from whom he was livi
2 CONC ng apart, but nothing was done for Howard himself until the outbreak of the second Dutch war, when he came over from Zeeland as second-in-command of the newly-formed Holland regiment. This was soon followed by the marriage of his 
2 CONC only son to the King’s natural daughter by Lady Shannon, and his future as a courtier seemed assured. With his younger brother he was granted the right to levy a shilling for every ton of ship’s ballast in any Irish port, though t
2 CONC he opposition of Dublin made this ineffective.4
2 CONT *'''Howard’s election at Woodstock in 1674 was probably procured by John Lovelace, his partner in obtaining a lease of crown property in Lincolnshire some four years before. On laying down his commission he received an excise pens
2 CONC ion of £300 p.a. He was not an active Member of the Cavalier Parliament, with only eight committees, none of which was of any political importance. He was noted as an official in 1675, perhaps by confusion with his cousins of the 
2 CONC Berkshire or Escrick families. But in August his pension was stopped until he had made good his soldiers’ pay, which he had detained. His name appeared on the working lists and Wiseman’s account, but Shaftesbury marked him ‘worthy
2 CONC ’, no doubt because of his elder brother’s politics and his own association with Lovelace. However, he was included in the court party in both lists of 1678, and as one of the ‘unanimous club’ probably did not stand for reelection
2 CONC . He died between 17 Jan. 1680 and 10 June 1682, when he was omitted from the entail on the Suffolk estates.5
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/howard-hon-thomas-1621-c81 _____________________________
2 CONT *Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk, KG (13 August 1584 – 3 June 1640) was an English nobleman and politician.
2 CONT *Born at the family estate of Saffron Walden, he was the son of Thomas Howard, 1st Earl of Suffolk, by his second wife, Catherine Knyvet of Charlton, and succeeded his father in 1626.
2 CONT *Sir Theophilus Howard was named in the Second Charter of Virginia made by King James I on 23 May 1609. The members of this extensive list were "incorporated by the name of The Tresorer and Companie of Adventurers and Planters of 
2 CONC the Citty of London for the Firste Collonie in Virginia."
2 CONT *He was elected MP for Maldon in a by-election in 1605 caused by the death of Sir Edward Lewknor and sat until he was ennobled in 1610 as Baron Howard de Walden by a Writ of Acceleration. [1]
2 CONT *Howard owned Framlingham Castle in Suffolk which he sold to Sir Robert Hitcham in 1635 for the sum of £14,000.
2 CONT *He died at Suffolk House, Charing Cross, London, and was buried on 10 June that year in Saffron Walden.
2 CONT *In March 1612, he married Elizabeth Home (d. 19 August 1633), daughter of George Home, 1st Earl of Dunbar. They had nine children:
2 CONT ** James Howard, 3rd Earl of Suffolk (c.1620–1689)
2 CONT **''' Thomas Howard
2 CONT ** Catherine Howard (d. 1650), married first George Stewart, 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny (d. 1642), second James Livingston, 1st Earl of Newburgh
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Howard (d. 11 March 1705), married on 1 October 1642 Algernon Percy, 10th Earl of Northumberland
2 CONT ** Margaret Howard, married Roger Boyle, 1st Earl of Orrery
2 CONT ** George Howard, 4th Earl of Suffolk (1625–1691)
2 CONT ** Henry Howard, 5th Earl of Suffolk (1627–1709)
2 CONT ** Anne Howard, married Thomas Walsingham
2 CONT ** Frances Howard (d. October 1677), married Sir Edward Villiers (d. 1689)
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theophilus_Howard,_2nd_Earl_of_Suffolk _________________________
