1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT In the 1640s Ireland was in a state of conflict, internally betweengroups competing for title to lands, and as a consequence of the CivilWar. A number of Scots were drawn to the country and remained withgrants of lands, especially
2 CONC  where they joined relatives who had becomeestablished. It is understood that he married Jean Hamilton whobrought him the lands of Tullydonnell or Tully O'Donnell, in theParish of Raphoe, by Convoy in Co. Donegal (old name Tyrconn
2 CONC el). <p></p> <p>Another source gives an Isabella O'Donnell, and it could be a case ofeither or both. In both cases they are said to be a daughter of Sir).Wilson. But he had only one daughter who died very young. His father,William
2 CONC  Wilson, however, had daughters, one of whom married a Hamiltonand another who married an O'Donnell. It is possible that JeanHamilton was a granddaughter of William's who married James Nesbittand brought lands by inheritance or pu
2 CONC rchase from an O'Donnell. <p></p> <p>William Willson had been an attorney from Clare in Suffolk who becamea major undertaker, with 3000 acres, in the plantation of Donegal in1610 under King James Stuart 1st of England and 6th of S
2 CONC cotland. Thelocation of his residence, Wilson's Fort, is a settlement on modernmaps. As an attorney of Clare in Suffolk, William had represented thepoor of the Parish in resisting attempts by landowners to enclose theCommon Land. 
2 CONC He was successful and the Common exists at the presentday. James Nesbitt had a son by his first marriage, also James, andtwo sons, Andrew and John by a second spouse who is unknown; hiseldest son succeeded him. <p>In the 1640s Ire
2 CONC land was in a state of conflict, internally betweengroups competing for title to lands, and as a consequence of the CivilWar. A number of Scots were drawn to the country and remained withgrants of lands, especially wherethey joine
2 CONC d relatives who had becomeestablished. It is understood that he married Jean Hamilton whobrought him the lands of Tullydonnell or Tully O'Donnell, in theParish of Raphoe, by Convoy in Co. Donegal (old name Tyrconnel). <p></p> <p>A
2 CONC nother source gives an Isabella O'Donnell, and it could be a case ofeither or both. In both cases they are said to be a daughter of Sir).Wilson. But he had only one daughter who died very young. His father,William Wilson, however,
2 CONC  had daughters, one of whom married a Hamiltonand another who married an O'Donnell. It is possible that JeanHamilton was a granddaughter of William's who married James Nesbittand brought lands by inheritance or purchase from an O'
2 CONC Donnell. <p></p> <p>William Willson had been an attorney from Clare in Suffolk who becamea major undertaker, with 3000 acres, in the plantation of Donegal in1610 under King James Stuart 1st of England and 6th of Scotland. Thelocat
2 CONC ion of his residence, Wilson's Fort, is a settlement on modernmaps. As an attorney of Clare in Suffolk, William had represented thepoor of the Parish in resisting attempts by landowners to enclose theCommon Land. He was successful
2 CONC  and the Common exists at the presentday. James Nesbitt had a son by his first marriage, also James, andtwo sons, Andrew and John by a second spouse who is unknown; hiseldest son succeeded him.
