1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===John Cunningham of Caprington & of Lambroughton Kilmaurs, 1st Baronet of Nova Scotia===
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cunningham, 1st Baronet, of Lambroughton (died November 1684), formerly of Brownhill, was a member of the Convention of the Estates of Scotland and of the Parliament of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with his father of the same name, this John was the son and heir of John Cunningham of Brownhill in Tarbolton and Geiss in Caithness who a member of the Committee of War, by his second wife Elizabeth the daughte
2 CONC r of Sir John Sinclair of Ratter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John was admitted an advocate and became a lawyer of some distinction. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John purchased the estate of Lambroughton soon after 1663
2 CONT
2 CONT John sold the estate of Brownhill in 1667.
2 CONT
2 CONT John purchased in 1683 the old family estate of Caprington in the parish of Riccarton, Ayrshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John was Shire Commissioner for Ayrshire in 1665 and again from 1681 to 1682.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was created a Baronet of Nova Scotia on 19 or 21 September 1669. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Margaret, daughter of John Murray of Touch Adam and Polmaise
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Margaret's son, William Cunningham 2nd Baronet, was baptized on 7 February 1664. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cunningham is known to have been buried on 20 November 1684.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE:     George Edward Cokayne, The Complete Baronetage, vol. IV (Exeter, 1904) p. 273.
2 CONT >SOURCE: [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524408#page/n291/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1904; Vol. IV; page 273
