0 @N6000000002185881023@ NOTE Notes for WILLIAM RN-17658 (COL) ARMSTRONG:  William Called
1 CONT 'Christie's Will'.   Also was called "The Younger of Barngleish
1 CONT & later Younger of Langholm, and Younger of Aughingill.
1 CONT Source-Brookeborough Parish Records in Fermanagh Co., Ireland
1 CONT Source-Armstrong House of Langholm Item 5. Source-Border
1 CONT Exploits edit 1812 p. 292.   It is well known that during the
1 CONT troubles of Charles I the Earl of Traquair continued
1 CONT unalterably fixed in his attachment to his unfortunate master,
1 CONT in whose service he hazarded his person and improverished his
1 CONT estate. On one occasion his lordship dispatched Christie's Will
1 CONT to London upon business of the highest importance to his
1 CONT Majesty. But the task was a difficult one, as the Parliamentary
1 CONT leaders used their utmost endeavors to prevent and
1 CONT communication between the king and his Scottish friends. Will
1 CONT arrived at London and delivered his papers in safety. In the
1 CONT meantime his embassy had taken air, and orders were dispatched
1 CONT by the enemy to intercept him.
1 CONT He passed skillfully from London to Carlisle, a distance
1 CONT of 265 miles, stopping at the latter place to refresh his
1 CONT horse, and then proceeded on his journey.  Crossing the bridge
1 CONT over the Eden just outside of Carlisle, he suddenly discovered
1 CONT Parliamentary soldiers springing up like magic at both ends of
1 CONT the bridge.  There was nothing left to do but take to the
1 CONT water, which was in high flood. Facing down stream, he patted
1 CONT his horse's neck, and with a touch of the spur gracefully
1 CONT cleared the parapet. Horse and rider were carried  by the swift
1 CONT current to a place called the Stanners, or Stanhouse, where he
1 CONT guided the horse up the wet bank but they slipped back into the
1 CONT river. Quickly cutting the loop that held his long wet cloak,
1 CONT he made for the bank again. With the noble effort the animal
1 CONT brought his master to firm ground. The soldiers, for a time
1 CONT struck with wonder, forgot to fire upon him. Two or three ran
1 CONT down the waterside intending to capture him, but Will pointed
1 CONT his wet pistol at them, which weapon, although useless, caused
1 CONT them to halt.
1 CONT From the Eden he was chased to the Esk, which he swam.
1 CONT Gaining the further side, he wheeled around and in true Border
1 CONT style called out to his pursuers to come through and drink with
1 CONT him. They declined, knowing he was in the neighborhood of
1 CONT friends.  After this taunt, he proceeded on the journey and
1 CONT faithfully accomplished his mission.   Source-Wilsons Tales of
1 CONT the Border edit. London vol. 2, p. 736, on perilous mission
1 CONT from Scotland to King Charles 1st, in London.  Source-Armstrong
1 CONT Surname Organization Salt Lake City, Utah
1 CONT In 1630 William kidnapped Sir Alexander Gibson, Lord Durie, a
1 CONT Judge and kept him prisoner for 3 months, in a Castle Dungeon
1 CONT until court decided in favor of his friend, then released the
1 CONT Judge at night. Judge & friends thought William had been
1 CONT spirited away by witchcraft.
1 CONT Source-(Preface of Forbe's Journal of the Sessions, Edinburgh
1 CONT 1714)
1 CONT Also In this year, reign of King Charles I, when the old
1 CONT Border practices were not entirely discontinued, the Tower of
1 CONT Gilknockie was occupied by William Armstrong called for
1 CONT distinction's sake Christie's Will, grandson to the famous John
1 CONT of Gilknockie executed by King James V.
1 CONT From Armstrong Clan Association News, dated April 1997, page 9.
1 CONT There is a story told of Christie's Will, that when
1 CONT confined in Jedburgh Jail, the Earl Traquair, who knew him
1 CONT well, got him released, in order to send him to Edinburgh, to
1 CONT aid his Lordship in terminating a law plea, which he proposed
1 CONT doing by getting the Counsel opposed to him out of the way.
1 CONT Christie's Will undertook to do this. He said he liked to
1 CONT do a "pawky trick," and engaged to remove the opposing lawyer,
1 CONT whom he accordingly inveigled away on the promise of giving him
1 CONT some "flowing fees," and when he succeeded in getting the
1 CONT advocate into his own free-booting domain, he incarcerated him
1 CONT in a tower till Traquair gained his suit. This Earl was perhaps
1 CONT Charles the First's friend, who fought with his son at Preston
1 CONT on behalf of his Sovereign, and fell into the hands of the
1 CONT rebels, when they were committed to Warwick, and imprisoned
1 CONT like the counsel, though for a much longer period, their
1 CONT captivity extending to four years. After the Earl's release and
1 CONT return, he suffered extreme poverty.  Soon after this
1 CONT Christie's Will joined the Army of King Charles I.
1 CONT William son of Christopher and grandson John Of Gilnockie
1 CONT left Scotland in  taking nephew Andrew with him and settleing
1 CONT in Northern Fermanugh Co., Ireland, where he became the founder
1 CONT of a numerious family whose branches flourished in those parts
1 CONT . This was some years after the death of Queen Elizabeth.
1 CONT Another source states William settled in Fermanagh Co., Ireland
1 CONT in 1604 Source-Armstrong Surname Organization  Armstrong
1 CONT families of Ireland, most all trace back to William & nephew
1 CONT Andrew in Brooksboro Ireland. Source-Chronicles of the
1 CONT Armstrongs p. 245, 309, 316 , 318 & 392.
1 CONT The Munro Clan had a long minority in the chiefship from
1 CONT 1635 to 1651 coincided with the period of Civil War. During
1 CONT these years General Robert Munro commanded the army sent by the
1 CONT Scottish Parliament to Ireland in 1642.
1 CONT Colonel William died in battle in King Charles I, British
1 CONT Army, during the period of the First Civil War with Ireland
1 CONT 1642-1646. Source-1635-1651 Civil War, (Abstract of acts of
1 CONT settlement & explanation, reign of King Charles 1, in Parliment
1 CONT at Westminister in 1640. Buried in the Old Churchyard of
1 CONT Agahvea a short distance from Brooksboro, Fermanagh Co.,
1 CONT Ireland.   Source-Sir Bernard Burke Source-Chart of the Ten
1 CONT Lairds of Mangerton Castle  Source-Clans & Tartans by
1 CONT Harper/Collins Publishers P.O. Box, Glasgow G4 ONB-1995(
1 CONT Mention of the Civil War on page 89, & 248)
1 CONT There is an old house in Brookboro with the Armstrong crest
1 CONT and motto, "Valida Manu", over the fireplace. It must have been
1 CONT a grand place in it's time, and now is used as a hotel. This
1 CONT house marks the spot of the first home of the Armstrongs in
1 CONT Ireland, that of Christie's Will's family, who went there from
1 CONT the Border in the early part of the seventeenth century.
1 CONT Christie's Will upon his death, his heirs were awarded a
1 CONT land grant in Fermanagh County, Northern Ireland.
1 CONT Source-Chronicles of the Armstrongs, p.p.203, 309, 310, 311,
1 CONT 312, 313, 316, 320,330 sent to author Dr. James L. Armstrong by
1 CONT Letter from Edwin E. Armstrong of Detroit, Michigan dated
1 CONT 12/6/1897.
1 CONT Source-"I Remain Unvanquished p.p.117,118,119  Compiled by
1 CONT W.L.A.(Az.)
1 CONT Called 'Christie's Will'.  Source-Armstrong House of Langholm
1 CONT Item 5.
1 CONT In 1630 William kidnapped a Judge and kept him prisoner for 3
1 CONT months, in a Castle Dungeon until court decided in favor of his
1 CONT friend, then released the Judge at night. Judge & friends
1 CONT thought William had been spirited away by witchcraft. Source-
1 CONT (Preface of Forbe's Journal of the Sessions, Edinburgh 1714)
1 CONT William son of Christopher and grandson of the famous John
1 CONT (Robin Hood )left Scotland in 1630 takeing nephew Andrew with
1 CONT him and settling in northern Fermanaugh Co., Ireland, where he
1 CONT became the founder of a numerious family whose branches
1 CONT flourished in those parts . This was some years after the death
1 CONT of Queen Elizabeth.  Armstrong families of Ireland, most all
1 CONT trace back to William & nephew Andrew in Brooksboro Ireland.
1 CONT Source-Chronicles of the Armstrongs p. 316 & 392. William died
1 CONT in battle in King Charles 1 Army about 1639 (Abstract of acts
1 CONT of settlement & explanation, reign of King Charles 1, in
1 CONT Parliment at Westminister in 1640.  Source-Chronicles General
1 CONT Listing 1/UKBC
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT If interested in more generations, contact CalOwen@attbi.com and I'llsend you what you want
