1 CONT "John came to Plymouth with the remnants of John Robinson's church.  Many of the pilgrims who came in the Mayflower, and the boats that followed later, lived in Holland before coming to Massachusetts.  Many lived in Leydon, which 
1 CONC is near Delft, where Rev. John Forbes (his father) took up his pastorate about the time the Mayflower left.  They apparently had to come though Delft to board the boat at Delft Haven.  It is natural to suppose that John Forbes the
1 CONC  younger was aquainted with the pilgrims who came in the Mayflower, and with those who were left in Delft.  The thirty five thousand in Holland who were forced out of Britain by religious intolerance had much in common and we may 
1 CONC assume that John Forbes chose to cast his lot with Rev. Robinson's flock at Leyden and to come over with the last load in 1636, two years after his father's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT "It is a matter of history that the pilgrims left Holland because their children were growing up to be more Dutch than British.  Many British had difficulty with the Dutch names, and the Dutch with the English.  Inter-marriages be
1 CONC came a worry.  The letters V and F are used much the same in Dutch.  After thirty years in Holland, Forbes became Vobes.  When he came to Plymouth or to Duxbury just across the bay, he was among people who knew him in Holland as V
1 CONC obes.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The following information is taken from Mitchell's History of Bridgewater, Winston's History of Duxbury and from Pioneers of Massachusetts.  John Fobes, one of fifteen young men who landed in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., Mass., in 1636
1 CONC  is the first of his family to land on American soil.  He was a tailor in Duxbury, and appears in the court records of 5 Aug 1636, and is put down as a proprietor.  In 1637 he was known to be at Powder Point, also at Green Harbour
1 CONC .  It was about this time that he married Constant Mitchell, sister of Experience Mitchell, who came from England in the third ship Ann in 1623, and who removed from Plymouth to Duxbury in 1631.  He was granted land at powder Poin
1 CONC t and built a house there and lived there five years.  His name appears among the list of men who were able to bear arms in 1643, was one of the original proprietors and became on of it's prominent citizens.  he died there 1661-2.
1 CONT
1 CONT "In the records of the colony of Plymouth there are two entries regarding John Fobes:
1 CONT
1 CONT 'In answare unto a request made to the court by the widow Vobes requesting some supply of land in respect unto the conditions of an indenture made betwixt Mr. Isacke Allerton and her husband John Vobes lately deceased the court gi
1 CONC ves liberty that any for her may looke out some land for her supply and a competency willbe granted and confirmed unto her.'
1 CONT (Vol III, p.200 - 13 Jun 1660)
1 CONT
1 CONT 'Captaine Willett is appointed by the court to purchase land of the Indians, which is granted unto such that were servants and to others that were ancient free men etc. Their names are as followeth; (the 24 names include John Vobe
1 CONC s)'
1 CONT (Vol IV, p.18 - 3 Jun 1662) "
0 @N6000000001579493254@ NOTE "Descendant of Robert the Bruce and Alfred the Great"; from Aberdeenshire, Scotland during 1636 with Reverend John Robinson of the Pilgrim Fathers; at Duxbury MA 1636, and later at Bridgewater; original proprietor there; William B
1 CONC rett and Arthur Harris deposed that being with Goodman Fobes of Bridgewater, lying sick and expecting his change, he deposed a part of his estate to his 2 eldest sons John and Edward and to his daughter Mary, and left the remainde
1 CONC r to be divided by his wife; estate inventory 31 Jul 1662; perhaps died in Sandwich, MA.
