1 CONT [http://www.cyberramp.net/~nathan/ClanRoss/history.html]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Clan Ross take their surname from the county of Ross, of which our chiefs' forefathers were the earls from about 1226. Their first chief, Ferchar, styled Macc in t' sacarit (' the Son of the Priest '), already held by inherita
1 CONC nce the vast district of Applecross in Wester Ross as heir of the O'Beolain hereditary abbots of Applecross. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1215, Ferchar ' the Son of the Priest ' brought his numerous warriors to assist the new king, Alexander II, against rival claimants to the throne who were powerful in the North of Scotland. The Chronicle of Melrose records his 
1 CONC prowess on this occasion: ' Maccintsacairt attacked them, and mightily overthrew the king's enemies; and he cut off their heads, and presented them as new gifts to the new king; on the seventeenth day before the Kalends of July. A
1 CONC nd because of this, the lord king appointed him a new knight '. A decade or so later, the same king entrusted him with the whole Earldom of Ross: so called after the ancient British word ros, meaning a moor--or possibly from the G
1 CONC aelic word ros, a headland, after the huge promontory formed by Easter Ross. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The first five chiefs were Earls of Ross. But on the death of William, Earl of Ross and Lord of Skye, in 1372, the earldom passed to his daughter; while the chiefship devolved on his younger half-brother Hugh Ross, lst of Balnagow
1 CONC n and 6th Chief of Clan Ross, whose inheritance in Easter Ross was so great that by 1632 the Rosses could raise I,000 men. After the death in 1711 of David Ross, I3th of Balnagown, Sheriff of Ross, the chiefship of the clan passed
1 CONC  to Malcolm Ross, 5th of Pitcalnie; but the great estate of Balnagown had been burdened with debt and passed to strangers, whose descendants evicted many of the clansmen in the ' progressive ' days of the Clearances. The Rosses of
1 CONC  Pitcalnie, however, continued to function of the chiefs of the clan in Ross, and the celebrated Simon, Lord Lovat (beheaded in 1747) wrote to the then Pitcalnie in 1740 as 'My Dear Brother Chieff'. The late 34th chief was ninety-
1 CONC nine-year old Miss Ross of Ross, who lived on the outskirts of the old royal burgh of Tain in Ross. Her appointed heir, David Ross of Ross, the new Chief, belonged to the Shandwick branch of the clan, and is heir male of Macc in t
1 CONC ' sacairt who became Earl of Ross seven and a half centuries ago.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Earls of Ross became the most powerful of the Highland chieftains in the 13th and 14th centuries. The Earldom was one of the most valuable in Scotland and Clan Ross led all other Highland clans in prestige and fighting power. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Our chief, David Ross of Ross and Shandwick, is the 34th hereditary Chief of the Clan, tracing his lineage in an unbroken male line to the first Earl of Ross. David has visited many Clan Ross gatherings in the U.S. His interest, e
1 CONC nthusiasm, and support of Clan Ross, USA is welcomed and appreciated by all clansmen and clanswomen. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [http://www.bcpl.net/~ellen/ross.html]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ross of New Castle, Delaware
1 CONT Index for Ward and Associated Families of Sassafras Neck, Cecil County, Maryland
1 CONT
1 CONT The progenitor of this family was the Reverend George Ross, the first rector of Immanuel Church, New Castle, Delaware, of which he assumed charge in the month of June 1705. He was one of the family of Ross of Balblair, in the pari
1 CONC sh of Fern, near the town of Fain, in the shire of Ross, in the south of Scotland, near the point called the Tarbot Ness, his father being David Ross, the second Laird of Balblair. The Balblair family is said to have been a branch
1 CONC  of the great Scotch family of Ross and is mentioned in the work entitled "The Earls of Ross and their Descendants" compiled by Francis Neville Reid and reprinted from "The Scottish Antiquity or Southern Notes and Queries" Volume 
1 CONC IV. Full abstracts were made by Duncan Veazey.
1 CONT
1 CONT There seems to have been no Earl of Ross whose family surname was Ross. When the title passed to descendants in the female line, the Lairds of Balmagown assumed the name as male representor lines of the Earls. The descent is under
1 CONC stood to be as stated following:
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferquhart, second Earl of Ross, founded the abbey of Fern. Balblair was the Parish of Fern. He was Knighted June 15, 1215 and created Earl Ross by Alexander the Second of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT William. Third Earl (son of Ferquhart). 1232, married Jean, daughter of William Cormyn, Earl of Buchan. Died in 1274 and his son,
1 CONT
1 CONT William, Fourth Earl died in 1322 and was succeeded by his son,
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh, fifth Earl. Hugh married as his second wife Margaret, daughter of Sir David Graham of Old Montrose, prior to 1329 by whom he had several children, among them Hugh Ross his third son,
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh Ross, Lord of Rariches and First Laird of Balmagown, one of the great landed estates of Scotland. He married Margaret de Barclay and died June 1371 and was succeeded by his son,
1 CONT
1 CONT William, Second of Balmagown. He married Christian, daughter of Lord Livingston and their son and heir,
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter, Third of Balmagown and Lord of Rariches 1398. He married Katherine, daughter of Paul MacTyre and had a son,
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh, Fourth of Balmagown. His second son was,
1 CONT
1 CONT William, who became Laird of Little Allan and married Grizal McDonald and had two sons, the younger was:
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter who became the First Laird of shandwick. He died in 1531 and was buried at Ferne Abbey, having married several times. By his wife Janet Tullosh he had four sons, the third of whom was:
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh Ross who became the First Laird of Balmachy, or Ballamuckie. His son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald, Second of Balmachy died in 1603 and his son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Walter, Third Laird of Balmachy. He married Jane Douglas and, dying in 1625 his son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh, fourth of Balmachy died 1649 and his son:
1 CONT
1 CONT George, fifth Laird of Balmachy married Margaret McCulloch, 1649, and his younger son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew Ross became First Laird of Balblair. Balblair in 1666 belonged to James Dallas of Balblair and Grissel Ross his spouse. In a letter of 1764 Ross of Balblair is described as "Cadet of the decayed house of Balmachy" [meaning 
1 CONC a line other than the senior line of descent. Thanks to Edwin Holcombe, Columbia, SC for this clarification]. Mr. Reid's book says that Andrew, son of George "very probably became Andrew First of Balblair. The letter referred to i
1 CONC s said to be still in existance and it is believed that no doubt exists at all as to the line of descent. In fact the letter was written by Hugh Ross, a descendant of the Balmachy family to Mr. John Ross of Philadelphia, a son of 
1 CONC the Reverend George Ross. This Andrew, First of Balblair had a son:
1 CONT
1 CONT David Ross, Second Laird of Balblair. Notary in the Parish of Fern, 1678. Married Margaret Stronach about July 8, 1681. Died about April 13, 1710. They had three sons and one daughter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew Ross who became Third of Balblair about 1710. A writer or lawyer in Edinburgh, Scotland. Married Margaret Gallio prior to 1710 and died before 1764 leaving a son.
1 CONT Andrew, who became Fourth of Balblair. He was a surgeon at Kingston, Jamaica and in London in 1730. In 1730 he sold Balblair to John Cruikshank, merchant of London, and died without issue. (His death without issue made the Rev. Ge
1 CONC orge Ross Fifth of Balblair by right of descent but of course the property was sold and the descent is of merely genealogical interest.
1 CONT George Ross
1 CONT Hugh Ross, of whom no further record.
1 CONT Elizabeth Ross, who married David Munro, Eighth Laird of Allan who appears to have lived at Daan House, in the Parish of Edderton. It is said that the Ross arms were painted or otherwise whown in one of the windows of Daan House i
1 CONC n compliment to her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Last Updated: June 7, 2003.
