0 @N6000000003615250050@ NOTE http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=PED&db=jweber&id=I10372&style=TEXT
1 CONT
1 CONT James Ross, a Royalist Soldier captured at Worcester. Transported from Gravesend to Boston on the "John&Sarah" 13 May 1652. He was one of the surviving soldiers of Charles II's scottish army made prisoners at the Battle of Worcest
1 CONC er 3 Sep 1651 by Cromwell. After the battle Cromwell, was greatly exercised to find sustenance for them, and to keep them under restraint as a potential source of danger. Many died of pestilence and many were sent to Boston as red
1 CONC emptioners on such terms that they soon acquired freedom. James Ross, the father of Mary Goodenow's bastard received heavy punishment of 39 stripes for "shameful abuse and violence towards his master". Served in King Philip's War,
1 CONC  1675, Narragansett Swamp Fight.
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT He, or the succeed, may have been that soldier, whose wife Mary, in the spring of 1676, says her husband went to Narraganset, under Capt. Mosely, of course in Dec. before was near 70 years old, was still in the service and his fam
1 CONC ily suffered so that she begged for his release. [Genealogical Dictionary of First Settlers of New England] (Note - he would have been about 56 in 1676, so maybe Mary was exaggerating?)
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The Sudbury Archives list a Mary Ross born to a James Ross and Mary Goodenow/Goodnow on 25 Dec 1656; the fact that they used Mary's maiden name implies that they were not married.
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The following information was contained in an e-mail from Debbie La Valley:
1 CONT
1 CONT Entry in ANNALS OF SUDBURY by Wayland that James Ross signed a petition concerning a 1678 battle between the settlers and the Pokonoket Indian known as "Philip" and his warriors. This book also noted that a Mary Ross was killed an
1 CONC d scalped by Indians in 1707. This could have been Mary Goodenow Ross ?
1 CONT
1 CONT (A Mary Ross is listed as dying in 1700 - hard to say if date is wrong and is the same Mary Ross. Bill Ross (Calif.) has read this as well and cannot verify either at this time.)
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Also contained in the same e-mail from Debbie La Valley, is the following, written by Stephen Ross, concerning prison time done by James:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bill, I found the reference on James Ross concerning his beating up James Ruddock. It was driving me nuts.
1 CONT
1 CONT In a nutshell, I found -
1 CONT
1 CONT James Ross was in prison at least from May 23, 1655 onward and was ordered released from prison when he petitioned the court with a 'humble apology' and promised to reform his behavior on August 31, 1655.
1 CONT
1 CONT I'd like to retain the source of this as it will be in my book, I'm trying to wind it up. I'm trying to run down when he was whipped or when the beating occurred or the fines for his fornication with Mary G.
1 CONT
1 CONT Steven C. Ross
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Supposedly the pedigree that I have for James Ross came from a Rear Admiral Maurice "James" Ross, who researched the Ross family and wrote at least one book, which I have never seen. The following e-mail from Debbie La Valley, and
1 CONC  accompanying e-mail from Stephen Ross, who has researched Ross family extensively, put some doubt as to the validity of the pedigree:
1 CONT
1 CONT From: Debbie La Valley
1 CONT To: jimweber@nwintl.com
1 CONT Sent: Wednesday, June 18, 2003 6:48 PM
