0 @N6000000003075790164@ NOTE [Brøderbund WFT European Origins Vol. E1, Ed. 1, Tree #0003, Date of Import: Dec 25, 1999]
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Boston Records NEG&HR vol 18 p 171
1 CONT NEG&HR vol 3 p 178 Samuel inherits everything in will from Samuel Crowers in 1646.  Vo 20 P 239 Samuel constable of Boston in 1652.  In Drake's Boston p 250 20 acres of land allotted to Samuel for 5 heads? 24 Feb 1640.  Vol 3 p. 1
1 CONC 88 Samuel Bidield, cooper 1648 with the rest of the soopers of Boston and Charlestowne were granted leave to form a guild for mutual protection, public benifit and to prevent abuses in trade.  Vol 10 p 83 Will of Samuel Bidfield 1
1 CONC 2 Mar 1659 "Igive unto my wife my dwelling house and yard with the out house in the yard during her lfie, p'rided shee stay in this Cuntry and keepe the dayde house in repare, and after her decease or going out of the Cuntrie into
1 CONC  England, my will is, saide house shall fall to Samuel Plumer, my grandchild, and to his heirs.  If hee dye with oute issue, it shall goe to the next sonn and his heirs, and soe to the next successively.  Unto my wife #40, and hal
1 CONC fe the househould goods; the other halfe to bee devided to my tow(2) dau's children to each of them Alike, that is to say, Samuel Plummer, John Plumer, Ephraim Plumer, Mary Plumer, John Stevens, Samuel Stevens ... to William Disda
1 CONC le, my Joynter, aces, oders and all my other working tooles with on coat and a pare of britches"  Who deposed 20 Sep 1660 Inventory taken 13 Jul 1660 Amt. #512 TAG 67:236-242 says Samuel Bitfield was apprenticed 4 Jun 1616 son of 
1 CONC Giles Bitfield, yeoman, deceased, late of Wrington, Somersetshire to John Goode, cooper and Lettice, his wife (abstract of Apprentice Books of the city of Bristol, 1600-1660).  On 27 mar 1622 he was discharged from his apprentices
1 CONC hip because his master was in prison.  (Briston Apprentice Books) Samuels baptism recorded in the parish registers of Wrington (FHL film #1,526,776) On 24 Feb 1639/40, Samuel Bitfield of Mount "Woollystone" (Wollaston) was, for fi
1 CONC rve heads, allotted twenty acres of land, apparently in that section of Boston, massachusetts (Boston Town Records).  On 2 Jun 1641 he was made freeman of Massachusetts Bay Colony.  On 7 Aug 1646 Sampson Lane registered a complain
1 CONC t against Nathaniel Duncan and four others, including Samuel Bitfield, for taking away pipes of wine, a ring and some money from his cellar.  They replied that they had been carrying out the orders of the general court.  Served as
1 CONC  a constable in Boston 8 mar 1651/2.
