0 @N6000000000401054531@ NOTE [3178510.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT [fish on webv67t1192.ftw]
1 CONT He was the first proprietor of the Manor of Livingst on in the provin
1 CONT ce of New York. He was the son of Rev. John Livingston, D.D.- -a descenda
1 CONT nt in the 30th generation of Egbert, the 1st Saxon King in all of Englan
1 CONT d; in the 28th generation of Alfred the Great; in the 20th generation o f Q
1 CONT ueen Margaret, wife of Malcolm III, King of Scotland; in the 14th generati
1 CONT on of Robert I (the Bruce of Bannock burn). Through his ancestress, Ali
1 CONT da W arren, wife of Henry, Prince of Scotland and Earl of Huntingdon in Eng
1 CONT land; w as descended from Gundred, 5th daughter of William the Conqueror a
1 CONT nd through his ancestress Matilda of Flanders, wife of William the Conquer
1 CONT or and her anc estress Judith, daughter of Charles the Bald, who married Ba
1 CONT ldwin, the 1st Co unt of Flanders--this Robert Livingston was a descenda
1 CONT nt in the 31st generati on of the Emperor Charlemagne and Hildegarde of Swa
1 CONT bia, his 2nd wife.
1 CONT By his will dated February 10, 1722, he left the principal part of his est
1 CONT ate to hi s son, Philip, and Clermont (called the lower manor) to his son R
1 CONT obert; and i n July 1722, he conveyed one segment of Saratoga to his younge
1 CONT st surviving so n, Gilbert.
1 CONT He had a small shipping business in the Netherlands, before emigra ti
1 CONT ng to America, in April 1673. He originally arrived from Rotterdam, via Sc
1 CONT otland, at Charlestown, MA, arriving Dec. 1673. He met with the powerful W
1 CONT in throps, who were connected to his father. He met John Hull, the silversm
1 CONT ith, who convinced him of the money to be made in the fur trade. He we
1 CONT nt to Albany ,NY 1674, where he became ingratiated with Rev. Nicholas Van R
1 CONT ensselaer.  He was the Secretary to town of Albany, Secretary to Rensselae
1 CONT rswyck.  He became the First Lord of the Manor of Livingston, 1686, creat
1 CONT ed by Gov. Thomas Dong an, originally 2000 acres(Nov. 4,1684), across the H
1 CONT udson from the Catskills, through which Roeliff Jansen's Kill flowe
1 CONT d.  He added two more small parcels , and mysteriously by July 22,1686, wh
1 CONT en the manor was granted, it had grown to 160,000 acres.  He was buri
1 CONT ed in the church, Albany, April 21,1725; Mayor of Albany 1710-1719. He des
1 CONT cended from William, 4th Lord Livingston of Callen dar, Earls of Linlithgo
1 CONT w.  Appointed secretary to the first Board of Indian A ffairs, then to to
1 CONT wn clerk. Collector of Excise, at Albany. Speaker, Provenci al Assembly, M
1 CONT ay 27, 1718. Family lands increased to about 1 million acres th rough purch
1 CONT ase and marriage by the end of the second generation.
1 CONT He was an o pponent of Leisler, and a leader of the anti-Leislerians, a pro
1 CONT minent force i n the Albany Convention, which defied Leisler, and the auth
1 CONT or of an official January 1690 letter denouncing the Leisler regime.  Aft
1 CONT er the massacre at Sch enectady, Feb. 9,1690, they reluctantly accepted Lei
1 CONT sler's troops for protect ion.  He was present at the hanging of Leisler a
1 CONT nd Milbourne in NYC, May 16,1 691.  Gov. Sloughter made him victualer of t
1 CONT he garrisons at Albany and NYC.
1 CONT He was a part owner of a brigantine, which returned in 1694, from a Caribb
1 CONT ean voyage with a 500% return on investment.  Gov. Fletcher ordered the sh
1 CONT ip sei zed, and the owners charged with violations of the Navigation Act (f
1 CONT or tradin g with the French).  The charges were dropped, when the Grand Ju
1 CONT ry refused to indict.The Grand Jury chairman, was Livingston's good frien
1 CONT d, Capt. William Kidd.  He had met the new Governor (1698), Lord Bellomon
1 CONT t, while in England a nd realizing that he sided with the Leislerians, Livi
1 CONT ngston changed sides and became a member of the Governor's Council, wh
1 CONT en he returned to NYC, along wi th Abraham DePeyster, Dr. Samuel Staats a
1 CONT nd Robert Walters (Nicholas Bayard a nd Willett being among those dismissed
1 CONT )House at State and Pearl Streets, Alba ny.  He bought Capt. Kidd's hou
1 CONT se on Dock St. in NYC, and later gave it Marga ret as a wedding presen
1 CONT t. He was a personal friend of Capt. Kidd, privateer a nd later pirate a
1 CONT nd had introduced him to Lord
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert Livingston the Elder (December 13, 1654 – October 1, 1728) was a New York colonial official, and first lord of Livingston Manor. He married Alida Schuyler van Rensselaer in 1679. He was the father of Philip Livingston (1686
1 CONC -1749) and Robert Livingston (1688-1775) and uncle of Robert Livingston the Younger.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in the village of Ancrum, near St Boswells, in Roxburghshire, Scotland, one of seven children of the Reverend John Livingston, a lineal descendant of the fifth Lord Livingston, ancestor of the earls of Linlithgow and C
1 CONC allendar, a minister of the Church of Scotland, who was sent into exile in 1663 due to his resistance to attempts to turn the Presbyterian national church into an Episcopalian institution. The exiled family were raised in Rotterda
1 CONC m, in the Dutch Republic, thus Robert Livingston was fluent in the Dutch language, which helped him greatly in his later career in the former Dutch colony of New York.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the death of his father in 1673, Robert Livingston returned to Scotland and then sailed for Boston to find his fortune in North America. Livingston moved to Albany, New York where he became wealthy in the fur trade, and 
1 CONC then obtained a patent to 160,000 acres (650 km²) that would become Livingston Manor in Columbia and Dutchess County.
1 CONT
1 CONT With his brother-in-law Peter Schuyler, he led the opposition in Albany to Leisler's Rebellion. He served as Secretary for Indian Affairs from 1695 until his death. In 1696, he backed Captain William Kidd's privateer voyage on the
1 CONC  Adventure Galley. He served as a representative to the New York provincial assembly in 1709–1711 and 1716–1725 and was elected speaker in 1718.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another relation was niece Catherine Schuyler married into the De Peyster family; her son was loyalist Arent Schuyler De Peyster. A nephew of Arent was Abraham De Peyster a loyalist Officer who served with the King's American Regi
1 CONC ment and was at Battle of King's Mountain; Abraham was married to Catherine Livingston a grandaugther of Philip Livingston {1686-1749 2nd Lord of the Manor}.
1 CONT
1 CONT A son of Robert was Gilbert Livingston-his wife was Cornelia Beekman (a granddaughter of Schenectady Mayor Wilhelm Beekman); their daughter married New York Lt. Governor Pierre Van Cortlandt and were the parents of Congressmen Phi
1 CONC lip Van Cortlandt and Pierre Van Cortlandt Jr; Philip and Pierre Van Cortlandt's brother-in-law was Albany Mayor Philip Schuyler Van Rensselaer. A sister-in-law of Gilbert Livingston married the great-grandson of Peter Stuyvesant—
1 CONC they were grandparents of New York Governor Hamilton Fish.
