0 @N6000000002457097519@ NOTE [Morison3.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT .John Stewart's second son, Gordon, was the last of the line to live in Drumin Castle, said to have been destroyed or abandoned after Culloden. Gordon was apparently prevented by ill health from going "out" in the '45 and died in 
1 CONC bed at Forres in 1746 - so was not, as often suggested, executed as a Rebel in London.
1 CONT  Although the eldest brother Charles does not seem to have been actively involved on either side, the youngest brother James was a Brigade Major
1 CONT in Cumberland' army (the family have his copy of the army marching orders from January 1745 to August 1746 - London to Culloden then to Fort Augustus).
1 CONT  The usual claim that the Jacobite brothers, Ludovick and Robert Stewart48B of John Roy Stewart's Edinburgh Regiment, were  of the Drumin family seems unlikely.   At best they might have been cousins, but in his "Lyon in
1 CONT Mourning", Bishop Forbes only refers to them as being "the representatives of Sir Walter  Stewart of Strathdoun and Glenlivet" - a description that has been applied to other Glenlivet  Stuarts who were not recognisably cadets of D
1 CONC rumin.As noted above, Charles died unmarried in
1 CONT 1790, while James was killed at the Siege of Havannah in 1762 when with the 56th Regiment of Foot - childless despite having eloped with the daughter of "General" (in fact, Col Charles, illegitimate son of General Charles) Churchi
1 CONC ll.[Morrison1.FTW]
1 CONT
