1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John Oliphant 
1 CONT Sex: M
1 CONT Birth: in Pencoatlard, Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 1 Janet Gilcrist
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     The first Oliphant known in Scotland was David Oliford, godson of
1 CONT     David I, acquired from the king lands in Roxburghshire probably after
1 CONT     1141, and appears as witness to several charters of that king.
1 CONT     "Tradition has it that John Oliphant had lost his property due to
1 CONT     heavy fines imposed upon him by the English because of his loyalty to
1 CONT     the Presbyterian cause. Very shortly after his son William was
1 CONT     sentenced to exile... his parents, John and Janet (Gilchrist) Oliphant
1 CONT     of Pencaitland (Scotland) indentured themselves and their minor
1 CONT     daughters, Janet and Margaret, to John Hancock of Edinburgh to serve
1 CONT     him for four years in New Jersey for their passage over." Ref.
1 CONT     Pusheck, p.82. Since the indenture was signed Aug. 19, 1685, and the
1 CONT     "Henry and Francis" sailed on Sept.5, 1685 with William Oliphant
1 CONT     aboard (see below), it is presumed that the entire Oliphant family
1 CONT     sailed together to New Jersey.
1 CONT     The following is from Mary Noles Triplett (mirriah@att.net),
1 CONT     34905 Emily Drive, Dade City, FL. She references "Genealogies of New
1 CONT     Jersey, Vol.I" by Joseph R. Kleh, pub.1996.
1 CONT     "The father of the family appears to have been John Oliphant, who
1 CONT     signed an indenture of service in Edinburg, Scotland, on August 19,
1 CONT     1685, in anticipation of being transported to New Jersy. By this
1 CONT     indenture "John Oliphant (residing in Pencartland), Janet Gilchrist
1 CONT     his wyfe and Margaret and Janet Oliphant his daughters doeth bind
1 CONT     themselves to servants to John Hancock residenter in Edinburg...for
1 CONT     the space of four years next efter their and his arryveall at the
1 CONT     province of East New Jersey in America, and the sd two daughters oblyg
1 CONT     them to serve until each of them attaine to the age of Twenty one
1 CONT     years compleat..." for which John Hancock is to provide them free
1 CONT     passage and bed, clothing, and all necessaries during their term of
1 CONT     indenture. At the completion of four years service John Hancock is to
1 CONT     grant John Oliphant "Twenty five acres of land in America..., one
1 CONT     horse, two sowes, and shall plough one acre of land, and give him
1 CONT     Corne to sow the same with...".
1 CONT     It has been assumed that the Oliphant family sailed to America
1 CONT     aboard the Henry and Francis, a ship of 350 tons and twenty guns,
1 CONT     under Richard Hutton, as master. This ship had been engaged as early
1 CONT     as May 1685 by George Scot, Lord of Pitlochie, to transport various
1 CONT     religious prisoners and interested emigrants to New Jersey. Almost 200
1 CONT     were on board, but only a partial list exists. This list, in William
1 CONT     A. Whitehead's `Contributions to the Early History of Perth Amber'
1 CONT     (published in 1856, page 29) contains names of those who embarked
1 CONT     involuntarily, being victims of religious persectuion confined at
1 CONT     Glasgow, Edinburg, and Sterling, who were banished to America, and who
1 CONT     were released to Scot on the provision that he transport them there.
1 CONT     The name "Wm. Oliphant" appears in this list. Since it is known that
1 CONT     all the above named Oliphants later appeared in New Jersey, it seems
1 CONT     likely that William was among the dissenters, while the other
1 CONT     Oliphants joined him voluntarily to seek a new life in America.
1 CONT     The Henry and Francis left Leith,Scotland, on September 5, 1685,
1 CONT     at which time the banished persons had already been aboard for some
1 CONT     time. Soon after the vessel left Lands End "a fever began to prevail
1 CONT     with virulence, particularly among the poorer ones, many of whom were
1 CONT     sick when they came on board, and the health of the others were
1 CONT     endangered by the condition of the provisions laid in by the Captain,
1 CONT     the meat, owing probably to the length of time which had elapsed since
1 CONT     the vessel was chartered, becoming offensive and uneatable. A month
1 CONT     elapsed, and the fever assumed a malignant type. Few escaped it, and
1 CONT     on some days as many as three and four elapsed, and the fever assumed
1 CONT     a malignant type. Few escaped it, and on some days as many as three
1 CONT     and four bodies were committed to the deep." Nearly seventy passengers
1 CONT     died, among them the Lair of Pitlochie and his wife. In addition to
1 CONT     sickness, the conduct of the captain was later described as cruel, the
1 CONT     ship twice sprang a leak, and calms and storms added to the distress
1 CONT     of the passengers. The ship finally arrived in New Jersey in
1 CONT     mid-December 1685."
1 CONT
